PSLE.subjects["english"] = {"name":"English","sections":{"p1":"Paper 1","a":"Paper 2 Booklet A","b":"Paper 2 Booklet B"},"topics":[{"id":"grammar","name":"Grammar","icon":"🔧"},{"id":"vocabulary","name":"Vocabulary","icon":"📖"},{"id":"vocabulary-cloze","name":"Vocabulary in Context","icon":"🔍"},{"id":"visual-text","name":"Visual Text Comprehension","icon":"🖼️"},{"id":"grammar-cloze","name":"Grammar Cloze","icon":"🧩"},{"id":"editing","name":"Editing: Spelling and Grammar","icon":"✂️"},{"id":"comprehension-cloze","name":"Comprehension Cloze","icon":"🕳️"},{"id":"synthesis","name":"Synthesis and Transformation","icon":"🔀"},{"id":"comprehension","name":"Comprehension (Open-ended)","icon":"📰"},{"id":"writing","name":"Writing","icon":"✍️"}],"papers":[{"id":"2025-prelim-tao-nan","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","count":76},{"id":"2026-prelim-acsj","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","count":75},{"id":"2026-prelim-ai-tong","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","count":75},{"id":"2026-prelim-catholic-high","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","count":77},{"id":"2026-prelim-chij-olgc","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","count":75},{"id":"2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","count":74},{"id":"2026-prelim-henry-park","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","count":77},{"id":"2026-prelim-mgs","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","count":77},{"id":"2026-prelim-nan-hua","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","count":76},{"id":"2026-prelim-nanyang","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","count":77},{"id":"2026-prelim-plmgs","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","count":75},{"id":"2026-prelim-raffles-girls","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","count":76},{"id":"2026-prelim-red-swastika","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","count":75},{"id":"2026-prelim-tao-nan","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","count":77}],"questions":[{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-p1-q1","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 1 Question 1","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"p1","number":1,"topic":"writing","subtypes":["situational-writing"],"format":"structured","marks":14,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Part 1: Situational Writing (14 marks)\nThe pictures below show a student and a librarian at Westview Library. Study the pictures carefully.\n[[img1]]\n++Your Task++\nImagine you are William Wong.\n**Write an email to the Manager of Westview Library to commend his staff for the excellent service.**\nYou are to refer to the pictures and information above for your email. The bullet point underlined below requires you to come up with your own information.\nIn your email, include the following key information:\n• the date of your visit\n• the name of the staff member and how she helped you\n• other than being helpful, give one other commendable quality she possesses\n• what you like the most about the reading corner\n• ++one suggestion you have for the reading corner++\nYou may reorder the points. Remember to write in complete sentences.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There is no single model answer. Check your writing against the points below."},"explanation":["Purpose: to commend (praise) a staff member for excellent service, and to share a suggestion for the reading corner. Say this clearly in the opening lines.","Audience: the Manager of Westview Library, an adult you do not know. Use a formal, polite tone: 'Dear Sir' or 'Dear Manager', no contractions, slang or emojis, and end with 'Yours faithfully' or 'Yours sincerely' and your full name, William Wong.","Context: you are William Wong, a student who visited the library and was helped by the librarian.","Point 1, the date of your visit: 18 February 2025.","Point 2, the name of the staff member and how she helped you: Miss Lim found a copy of the poetry book you had been looking for at Lagoon Library and asked for it to be transferred to Westview Library as soon as possible (because she knew you needed it urgently).","Point 3, one other commendable quality besides being helpful: infer it from the pictures, for example thoughtful or considerate (she knew you needed the book urgently), proactive or resourceful (she searched another library), or open to feedback (she asked for your ideas). Do not write 'helpful' again.","Point 4, what you like most about the reading corner: it is comfortable, a quiet little space where you can immerse yourself in the world of books.","Point 5 (underlined, your own idea), one suggestion for the reading corner: it must be sensible and make the corner more conducive, for example adding brighter reading lamps, more cushions or beanbags, a display shelf of recommended books, or plants.","Format and language: a subject line, short paragraphs, complete sentences and correct tenses (past tense for the visit, present tense for your opinion and suggestion). Close by thanking the Manager. 6 marks are for covering the task and 8 marks for language and organisation."]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-p1-q1-img1","alt":"Four comic panels at Westview Library. Panel 1 (caption: 18 February 2025, 2.00 p.m.): the librarian hands William a book over the counter. Librarian: \"Here you go, William. I found a copy of the poetry book you've been looking for at Lagoon Library and asked them to transfer it here as soon as possible. I know you need this urgently.\" William: \"Thanks, Miss Lim. Now I can start on my project. It's due in two days' time.\" Panel 2: they are at a bookshelf and an armchair under a sign 'Reading Corner'. Miss Lim: \"By the way, what do you think of our reading corner? It's been quite a while since we refreshed it and it could do with a new look. Do you have any ideas to make it more conducive?\" William: \"Well, it's rather comfortable. Nothing beats reading at this quiet little space where I can immerse myself in the world of books.\" Panel 3: William says, \"Anyway, I'm excited about the revamp and will give it some thought. I'll get back to you with my suggestion.\" Panel 4: William thinks, \"Librarians like Miss Lim are hard to come by. I'll write an email to commend her and also share a suggestion for the reading corner.\""}]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-p1-q2","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 1 Question 2","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"p1","number":2,"topic":"writing","subtypes":["continuous-writing"],"format":"structured","marks":36,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Part 2: Continuous Writing (36 marks)\nWrite a composition of ++at least 150 words++ about **an unexpected delivery**.\nThe pictures are provided to help you think about this topic.\nYour composition must be based on at least one of these pictures.\n[[img1]]\nConsider the following points when you plan your composition:\n• What was the delivery?\n• Why was it unexpected?\nYou may use the points in any order and include other relevant points as well.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There is no single model answer. Check your writing against the points below."},"explanation":["Understand the topic: a delivery is something brought or sent to someone. 'Unexpected' means nobody saw it coming: it was not ordered, it came from a surprising sender, it arrived at a strange time, or what was inside was a surprise. Both words must drive the story.","Picture 1 (box marked 'Handle with care'): a parcel nobody ordered arrives at the door. It could hold something fragile or even alive, a gift from a relative overseas, or a parcel meant for a neighbour.","Picture 2 (letter in an envelope): a letter arrives from someone you had lost touch with, such as an old friend, a former teacher or a grandparent, bringing news, an apology or thanks.","Picture 3 (entry tickets): tickets to a concert, match or theme park turn up, perhaps a prize you forgot you entered for, or a secret treat from a parent to reward your hard work.","Answer the two guiding points clearly: what the delivery was, and why it was unexpected. Use at least one picture; you may combine them (a box that holds a letter and two tickets).","Story arc: an ordinary day, the doorbell rings or the postman arrives (build curiosity), opening the delivery, the problem or surprise it brings (whose is it? should you keep it? who sent it?), the turning point, and how it is resolved.","Show feelings with actions and thoughts (puzzled, suspicious, thrilled, touched) and include some dialogue.","Ending: return to the topic. Reflect on what the unexpected delivery taught you or how it changed something, for example gratitude for someone who remembered you, or honesty in returning what was not yours.","Write at least 150 words (aim for 350 to 450) in paragraphs. 18 marks are for content and 18 for language and organisation."]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-p1-q2-img1","alt":"Three pictures. Picture 1: a sealed cardboard box labelled 'HANDLE WITH CARE'. Picture 2: a handwritten letter sticking out of an opened envelope. Picture 3: two tickets, each printed 'ENTRY TICKET'."}]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q1","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 1","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":1,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["subject-verb-agreement","tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The jury __________ unable to reach a verdict due to the fact that they had different opinions.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Tense: 'they **had** different opinions' is in the past, so the missing verb must be past tense. That rules out 'is' and 'are'.","'jury' is a collective noun. When the members act together as one unit it is singular; when they act as separate people it is plural.","Here the members had different opinions, and the sentence calls them 'they'. They are acting as individuals, so the verb is plural.","'The jury were unable to reach a verdict.' The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["is","are","was","were"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q2","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 2","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":2,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["tenses","subject-verb-agreement"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Mei Ling, along with her uncle, __________ chess regularly since she developed a love for the game.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'since she developed a love for the game' tells us the action started in the past and is still going on. 'since' needs a perfect tense, so 'plays' and 'is playing' are out.","'regularly' shows a repeated, continuing activity. The present perfect continuous (has been + verb-ing) stresses that it has kept going up to now, so it fits better than 'has played'.","Agreement: the subject is 'Mei Ling'. 'along with her uncle' is only extra information between commas, so the verb stays singular: 'has'.","'Mei Ling ... has been playing chess regularly since ...' The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["plays","is playing","has played","has been playing"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q3","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 3","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":3,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Mrs Rai looked on with pride as her son delivered a speech that he had prepared by __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'by + reflexive pronoun' means alone, without help.","The reflexive pronoun must match the person who did the action. Who prepared the speech? 'he', Mrs Rai's son.","The reflexive pronoun for 'he' is 'himself'. 'herself' is the trap because Mrs Rai is mentioned first, but she did not prepare the speech.","The answer is option (2), himself."]}],"options":["herself","himself","ourselves","themselves"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q4","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 4","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":4,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["question-tags"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Emily never forgets to remind her grandmother to take her medicine, __________?\" remarked Mother.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Rule: a positive statement takes a negative tag, and a negative statement takes a positive tag.","'never' makes the statement negative, even though there is no 'not'. So the tag must be positive. That rules out 'isn't she' and 'doesn't she'.","The verb 'forgets' is simple present, so the helping verb in the tag is 'does', not 'will'.","'Emily never forgets ..., does she?' The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["will she","isn't she","does she","doesn't she"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q5","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 5","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":5,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["conditionals","modals"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Attend to the guests promptly __________ they need assistance,\" the restaurant manager reminded his staff during his briefing.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["The blank joins an instruction to a condition: attend to the guests **if** they need assistance.","In formal English, 'should' can replace 'if' when the helping verb comes before the subject: 'should they need assistance' = 'if they should need assistance'.","'shall', 'must' and 'could' cannot be used in this way to mean 'if'.","The answer is option (4), should."]}],"options":["shall","must","could","should"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q6","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 6","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":6,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["comparatives"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The Long Island Project is by far __________ scheme to combat rising sea levels along Singapore's coastline.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'by far' means 'by a great amount'. It is used to strengthen a superlative: by far the best, by far the largest.","The superlative of 'significant' is 'the most significant'.","'a more significant' is a comparative and would need 'than ...' after it. 'the very significant' is not a superlative.","The answer is option (4), the most significant."]}],"options":["a significant","a more significant","the very significant","the most significant"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q7","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 7","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":7,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The government introduced electric buses __________ the high costs as they were committed to a greener public transport system.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Work out how the two ideas are linked. High costs are a reason **not** to introduce electric buses, yet the government did it anyway. That is a contrast.","'in spite of + noun' shows contrast: something happened even though there was an obstacle.","'owing to', 'because of' and 'resulting from' all give a cause. The buses were not introduced because of the high costs; the real reason comes later ('as they were committed to a greener ... system').","The answer is option (2), in spite of."]}],"options":["owing to","in spite of","because of","resulting from"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q8","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 8","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":8,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["relative-pronouns"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The tour guide, __________ company had conducted the Angkor Wat historical tour, has received excellent reviews.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["The blank is followed by a noun, 'company'. The company belongs to the tour guide.","'whose' is the relative pronoun that shows possession: the tour guide's company = whose company.","'who' is tempting because the tour guide is a person, but 'who company had conducted' is not grammatical. 'who' must be followed by a verb, not a noun that belongs to the person.","The answer is option (4), whose."]}],"options":["who","which","where","whose"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q9","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 9","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":9,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"If you have plans to visit this modern city, be sure to put __________ at least two hours for a complete experience.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'put aside' (also 'set aside') means to save or reserve time or money for a purpose.","'put away' means to keep something in its proper place. 'put behind' means to forget something unpleasant. 'put across' means to explain an idea clearly.","You reserve two hours for the visit, so 'put aside at least two hours' is correct.","The answer is option (2), aside."]}],"options":["away","aside","behind","across"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q10","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 10","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":10,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["quantifiers"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"There is __________ doubt that Janice will win the award given her outstanding performance.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'doubt' is an uncountable noun here, so 'few' (used with countable plural nouns) is wrong.","Meaning: her performance was outstanding, so people are almost sure she will win. There is hardly any doubt.","'little' means 'almost no' and goes with uncountable nouns: 'There is little doubt that ...' is a fixed expression. 'much doubt' gives the opposite meaning, and 'any' needs a negative ('There isn't any doubt').","The answer is option (3), little."]}],"options":["any","few","little","much"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q11","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 11","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":11,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"When the tsunami warning alarm went off, residents were told to __________ the area immediately and head for higher ground.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Clues: a tsunami warning, 'immediately' and 'head for higher ground'. People must leave a dangerous place for safety.","'evacuate' means to leave a place because it is dangerous. We say 'evacuate the area'.","'depart' also means to leave, but it is used for ordinary journeys and needs 'from' (depart from the area). 'survey' and 'observe' mean to look at something carefully.","The answer is option (4), evacuate."]}],"options":["depart","survey","observe","evacuate"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q12","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 12","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":12,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Many tourists are unaware of the __________ effects their visits can have on the heritage sites. A number have suffered irreparable damage.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Clue in the second sentence: 'irreparable damage' is damage that can never be repaired. The effects are extremely harmful.","'devastating' means causing very great damage or destruction.","'subtle' means hard to notice, which is tempting because the tourists are 'unaware', but it does not match damage that is beyond repair. 'temporary' (lasting a short time) is the opposite of irreparable.","The answer is option (4), devastating."]}],"options":["subtle","unlimited","temporary","devastating"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q13","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 13","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":13,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning","collocation"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The lack of proper shelter during heavy rain has become a major __________ for the school community.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Not having shelter in heavy rain is a problem that worries people in the school.","'concern' means a matter that causes worry. 'a major concern for ...' is a common word pair.","'lapse' is a small mistake or slip. 'obstruction' is something that blocks the way, and 'interference' is unwanted meddling. A lack of shelter is none of these.","The answer is option (2), concern."]}],"options":["lapse","concern","obstruction","interference"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q14","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 14","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":14,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"My little nephew __________ when he saw blood trickling down his grazed knee.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Some people faint at the sight of blood. 'passed out' means fainted, or lost consciousness.","'passed up' means chose not to take (a chance). 'passed off' means pretended that something was something else. 'passed over' means ignored or not chosen.","Only 'passed out' can stand alone without an object and fits the sight of blood.","The answer is option (3), passed out."]}],"options":["passed up","passed off","passed out","passed over"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q15","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 15","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":15,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The students were hesitant to speak __________ in front of the discipline master for fear of being reprimanded.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clue: 'for fear of being reprimanded' (scolded). The students were afraid that telling the truth openly would get them into trouble.","'candidly' means honestly and openly, without hiding anything.","'tactfully' means carefully, so as not to upset anyone. Nobody is scolded for speaking tactfully, so there would be nothing to fear. 'deliberately' means on purpose, and 'eagerly' means keenly.","The answer is option (3), candidly."]}],"options":["eagerly","tactfully","candidly","deliberately"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q16","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 16","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":16,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++realised++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["When a dream is 'realised', it becomes real: it comes true.","Clue: 'After months of planning, their dream was finally realised as they travelled to Kampong Thom'. The trip they dreamed of actually happened.","'materialised' means became real or actually happened, which matches.","'revealed' means made known, and 'manifested' means shown clearly. The dream was not shown to anyone; it came true. The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g1","options":["released","revealed","manifested","materialised"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q17","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 17","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":17,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++Entranced++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'Entranced' means filled with such wonder and delight that you give something all your attention, as if in a trance.","Clue: Sophea was entranced 'by the beautiful fields and the distant clamour of roosters crowing'. The scenery held her attention.","'Captivated' means attracted and held by something charming, which matches.","'Gratified' means pleased or satisfied, which is weaker and does not carry the idea of being spellbound. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g1","options":["Gratified","Energised","Captivated","Unaffected"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q18","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 18","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":18,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++laboriously++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'laboriously' means with a lot of effort and difficulty ('labour' means hard work).","Clue: 'perspiration dotting their brows'. The climb up the hill was hard work.","'strenuously' means with great effort and energy, which matches.","'tirelessly' is the trap: it means without getting tired, but they were clearly straining. 'hastily' means in a hurry. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g1","options":["hastily","tirelessly","strenuously","purposefully"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q19","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 19","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":19,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++to a great extent++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'to a great extent' means mostly, or in large part, but not completely.","The view matched the stories Kiri had told for the most part.","'mainly' means mostly, which matches.","'purely' means completely and only, which is too strong: 'a great extent' is not the whole. The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g1","options":["briefly","visibly","purely","mainly"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q21","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 21","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":21,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the poster in Text 1, the movie __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Check each option against the poster.","The poster says 'Based on a true story by Felicity Vale.' A film based on a true story is inspired by true events, so (2) is correct.","(1) Willow Park is the venue where the film is shown, not what the film is about. (3) Elliot Drake **produced** the film; the poster does not say he directed it. (4) Felicity Vale wrote the story; she is not one of the three actors listed.","The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g2","options":["features Willow Park","is inspired by true events","is directed by Elliot Drake","stars Leo Graham and Felicity Vale"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q22","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 22","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":22,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Based on the poster in Text 1, which of the following **best** fits the film that will be screened in the park?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Look at the tagline and the picture: 'An abandoned manor in the woods... Many mysteries to unravel... With every step deeper, the danger closes in... Will he escape?'","Mystery, danger and suspense are the marks of a thriller. The dark, spooky manor in the picture supports this.","'Documentary' is tempting because the film is 'based on a true story', but it has actors playing characters ('Leo Graham as Jamie'), so it is not a documentary. Nothing suggests cartoons or science.","The answer is option (1), Thriller."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g2","options":["Thriller","Animation","Documentary","Science Fiction"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q23","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 23","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":23,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Many mysteries to unravel... Will he escape?\" The image in the poster **best** supports this by giving the impression that the boy is __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Look at the image on the screen: a small figure stands alone, upright, facing a huge dark manor at night.","The tagline calls him 'One brave boy' who goes 'deeper' even as 'the danger closes in'. Standing squarely in front of the frightening manor shows he is ready to go in.","That gives the impression he is determined and fearless. He is not 'relaxed' (there is danger), and nothing shows him as bored, confused or hesitant.","The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g2","options":["curious and relaxed","bored and distracted","confused and hesitant","determined and fearless"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q24","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 24","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":24,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Ready for a magical night under the stars with your loved ones?\" Why did the writer ask the question in Text 1?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["The question speaks directly to the reader ('your loved ones') and describes the event: a night outdoors 'under the stars'.","A question like this at the top of a poster is a hook. It makes readers picture themselves enjoying the evening so that they will want to come.","(1) 'magical' here means wonderful, not about magic tricks. (2) The question is about the outdoor event, not the film's story. (3) The poster asks people to register for the event, not to join the club.","The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g2","options":["to spark people's interest in magic","to make people connect with the movie","to invite people to join Willow Film Club","to encourage people to attend the movie screening"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-a-q25","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 25","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":25,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference","purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Text 2 supports the message of the poster in Text 1 as it also __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Find what the two texts share. The poster invites people to enjoy a night 'with your loved ones'. Text 2 is about 'spending quality time together' as a family and its benefits.","So both texts are about spending meaningful time with family. 'fruitful time' means the same as 'quality time'.","(3) is the trap: Text 2 does mention a strong community, but the poster does not, so it is not something the texts share. (1) and (2) are only examples in Text 2.","The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g2","options":["promotes indoor and outdoor activities","encourages people to have more picnics","focusses on the need to build a strong community","highlights spending fruitful time with family members"]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q26","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 26","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":26,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (26). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"N","accept":["n","to"]},"explanation":["'native' is followed by the preposition 'to': a plant or animal is native **to** the place it comes from.","'Native to Singapore, the Tembusu Tree can grow up to 40 metres tall.'","The answer is (N) to."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q27","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 27","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":27,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (27). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"M","accept":["m","those"]},"explanation":["The blank is followed by 'who walk past'. We need a pronoun that stands for people and can take 'who'.","'those who ...' means 'the people who ...'. The plural verb 'walk' confirms a plural pronoun.","'some' cannot be used here because 'some who walk past' would mean only a few of them; the reminder is for everyone who walks past.","The answer is (M) those."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q28","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 28","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":28,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (28). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"B","accept":["b","an"]},"explanation":["Pattern: such + a/an + adjective + singular noun ('such a tall tree').","The next word, 'iconic', begins with a vowel sound, so the article is 'an', not 'a'.","'Few would expect such an iconic image to be ... real'. The answer is (B) an."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q29","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 29","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":29,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (29). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"L","accept":["l","still"]},"explanation":["The blank sits between 'also' and the adjective 'accessible', so it must be an adverb.","The tree on the five-dollar note is not just a drawing: it is real, and even today the public can go and see it. 'still' means 'even now'.","The answer is (L) still."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q30","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 30","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":30,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (30). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"C","accept":["c","behind"]},"explanation":["'the significance ___ the symbol': we need a preposition that links the meaning to the symbol.","We speak of the meaning, story or significance **behind** something, that is, the deeper meaning that lies under it.","The answer is (C) behind."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q31","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 31","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":31,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (31). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"J","accept":["j","since"]},"explanation":["'is believed to have existed' is a perfect form, showing something that began in the past and has continued.","The blank is followed by a point in time: when 'the Botanic Gardens was officially founded in 1859'. From a starting point until now, we use 'since'.","'while' would mean only during that moment, which does not fit a tree that is close to two centuries old.","The answer is (J) since."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q32","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 32","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":32,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (32). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"F","accept":["f","has"]},"explanation":["After the blank comes 'been admired', so we need a helping verb to make the perfect passive: has/had + been + past participle.","The tree is still admired today ('some of whom return'), so use the present perfect. 'this tree' is singular: 'has'.","'had' would place the admiration wholly in the past. The answer is (F) has."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q33","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 33","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":33,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (33). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Q","accept":["q","with"]},"explanation":["The sentence begins with a phrase that describes what the tree possesses: 'its thick, weathered trunk and an expansive canopy of branches'.","'With + noun phrase' at the start of a sentence means 'having': With its thick trunk ..., the tree represents ...","'having' is the trap: 'Having its thick trunk ...' is not natural English, while 'With its ...' is the usual pattern.","The answer is (Q) with."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q34","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 34","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":34,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (34). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"P","accept":["p","while"]},"explanation":["Two things are true at the same time: 'standing unshaken by storms' and 'remaining deeply grounded in its values'.","'while' joins two actions that go on together, and can be followed by a verb-ing form.","The answer is (P) while."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q35","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 35","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":35,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (35). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"D","accept":["d","being"]},"explanation":["After the dash, the sentence explains what Singapore takes pride **in**. A preposition ('in') is followed by a noun or a verb-ing form.","'anchored and forward-looking' are adjectives, so we need the -ing form of 'be' in front of them: 'being anchored and forward-looking'.","'having' does not work because we say someone **is** anchored, not **has** anchored.","The answer is (D) being."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q36","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 36","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":36,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (36) ++invovment++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"involvement"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The base word is 'involve' (in-volve). Add the suffix '-ment' without dropping the 'e': involve + ment.","Say it in syllables: in-volve-ment.","The correct word is 'involvement', which means taking part in something."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q37","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 37","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":37,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (37) ++litracy++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"literacy"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word comes from 'literate' (able to read and write).","Say it in four syllables so that no letter is lost: lit-er-a-cy. The unstressed 'e' is easy to miss.","The correct word is 'literacy'. 'Computer literacy' means knowing how to use computers."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q38","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 38","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":38,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (38) ++inntaitive++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"initiative"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word is linked to 'initial' and 'initiate' (to start something).","Spell it in parts: in-i-ti-a-tive. The 'sh' sound is spelt 'ti'.","The correct word is 'initiative', which here means a new plan or project."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q39","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 39","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":39,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (39) ++by++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"between"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong preposition.","A 'divide' is a gap that separates two groups. For two things we use 'between ... and ...': the divide between the young and old generations.","The correct word is 'between'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q40","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 40","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":40,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (40) ++emphatick++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"empathetic","accept":["empathic"]},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. Think about the meaning: volunteers who guide the elderly should understand how the elderly feel. That quality is 'empathy'.","The adjective from 'empathy' is 'empathetic': em-pa-thet-ic. It ends in '-ic', never '-ick'.","Do not confuse it with 'emphatic', which means said forcefully.","The correct word is 'empathetic'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q41","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 41","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":41,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (41) ++because++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"but","accept":["yet"]},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong connector.","Look at the two ideas. It is easy for **us** to take the skill for granted. For **someone who did not grow up with digital technology**, it is intimidating. The two ideas contrast; the second is not the reason for the first.","A contrast needs 'but' (or 'yet'), not 'because'.","The correct word is 'but'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q42","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 42","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":42,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (42) ++those++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"this","accept":["it","that"]},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the pronoun does not agree with what it refers to.","The pronoun points back to 'such a skill set' or 'digital technology', each of which is one thing. 'those' is plural and is used for many things.","A singular pronoun is needed: 'this can be rather intimidating'. ('it' or 'that' is also singular and correct.)","The correct word is 'this'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q43","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 43","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":43,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (43) ++applies++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"applying"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the verb form.","'By' is a preposition, so the verbs after it take the -ing form: 'By sharing your knowledge and ___ your skills'.","The two verbs joined by 'and' must match (parallel structure): sharing ... and applying ...","The correct word is 'applying' (apply + ing; keep the 'y')."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q44","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 44","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":44,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (44) ++comunikation++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"communication"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The base word is 'communicate', with double 'm' and a 'c' for the 'k' sound.","Drop the 'e' and add '-ion': com-mu-ni-ca-tion.","The correct word is 'communication'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q45","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 45","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":45,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (45) ++seen++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"seeing"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the verb form.","In 'look forward to', the word 'to' is a preposition, so it is followed by a verb-ing form, not the base verb or the past participle.","'We look forward to seeing you at the workshop!'","The correct word is 'seeing'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q46","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 46","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":46,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (46) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"collector"},"explanation":["We need a noun for a person, described as 'an enthusiastic toy ___'.","Clue further on: he set up the museum with 'his personal collection from more than forty countries'. A person who has a collection is a collector.","The answer is 'collector'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q47","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 47","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":47,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (47) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"walks"},"explanation":["'from all walks of life' is a fixed expression. It means people of every kind of job and background.","The words 'from all' before the blank and 'of life' after it are the clues.","The answer is 'walks'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q48","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 48","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":48,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (48) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"displaying","accept":["showcasing","exhibiting","featuring","showing","housing"]},"explanation":["After the comma we need a verb-ing form that tells what the museum does with 'his personal collection'.","A museum puts things on show, so 'displaying' fits. The next paragraph confirms it: 'the museum **displays** toys from the 1840s'.","'showcasing', 'exhibiting' and 'featuring' have the same meaning and are also acceptable.","The answer is 'displaying'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q49","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 49","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":49,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (49) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"like","accept":["including"]},"explanation":["'action figures, wind-up robots and miniature cars' are examples of toys.","To introduce examples after a noun we use 'like' ('such as' is two words, so it cannot be used).","The answer is 'like'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q50","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 50","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":50,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (50) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"alike"},"explanation":["'children and adults alike' is a common pattern: 'A and B alike' means both A and B equally.","The toys awe (amaze) both groups in the same way.","The answer is 'alike'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q51","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 51","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":51,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (51) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"wound"},"explanation":["Clues: 'up with a key in the winding slot'. The verb is 'wind up' (to turn a key so that a toy can move).","The toys do not wind themselves; they are wound by someone. After 'when' we can use the past participle: 'when (they are) wound up'.","The past participle of 'wind' is 'wound', not 'winded'.","The answer is 'wound'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q52","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 52","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":52,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (52) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"One"},"explanation":["Pattern: 'One of the + plural noun + singular verb'. Here: '___ of the highlights of the museum **is** the mechanical toy'.","The singular verb 'is' and the single item named ('the mechanical toy') show that just one highlight is meant.","The answer is 'One'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q53","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 53","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":53,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (53) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"pressing","accept":["pushing"]},"explanation":["The sentence lists three ways to power the toy, all in the -ing form: 'winding a key, ___ a button, or pushing a lever'. The missing verb must match (parallel structure).","The verb that goes with 'a button' is 'press'.","'pushing' is also possible for a button, but it is used for the lever straight after, so 'pressing' is the better choice.","The answer is 'pressing'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q54","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 54","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":54,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (54) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"instead"},"explanation":["'instead of' means 'in place of'. The blank is followed by 'of relying on batteries'.","The toy is powered by hand (a key, a button, a lever) in place of batteries.","The answer is 'instead'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q55","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 55","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":55,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (55) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"voice","accept":["tone","words"]},"explanation":["The blank comes after 'said the museum owner, his ___ carrying a note of nostalgia'.","'a note of' is used for the sound of feeling in someone's voice. What carries a note of nostalgia when a person speaks? His voice.","The answer is 'voice' ('tone' is also acceptable)."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q56","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 56","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":56,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (56) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"throughout","accept":["over","through","across"]},"explanation":["'how toys had gradually evolved ___ the years': we need a preposition for a period of time during which a slow change happened.","'throughout the years' and 'over the years' both mean during the whole of that time.","The answer is 'throughout' ('over' is equally correct)."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q57","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 57","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":57,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (57) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"that","accept":["how"]},"explanation":["'It is indeed amazing to note ___ these toy characters were part of the children's playtime'.","After 'to note', a clause that states a fact is introduced by 'that'.","The answer is 'that'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q58","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 58","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":58,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (58) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"before","accept":["until"]},"explanation":["Think about the order of events: first children played with these toys; later technology (screens and gadgets) 'took over'.","The toys were part of playtime earlier than that, so the connector is 'before'.","The answer is 'before'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q59","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 59","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":59,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (59) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"relate","accept":["connect"]},"explanation":["The blank is followed by 'to the past'. We need a base verb (after 'helps visitors') that takes 'to'.","Earlier the passage speaks of an 'emotional connection to the past'. To 'relate to' something is to feel a connection with it and understand it.","'connect to the past' has the same meaning and is also acceptable.","The answer is 'relate'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q60","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 60","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":60,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (60) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"life"},"explanation":["'come to life' is a fixed expression meaning to become lively, as if alive.","In the museum the toys move, make sounds and bring back memories, so the toys 'come to life'.","The answer is 'life'."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q61","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 61","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":61,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["no-matter-how"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Our relatives insisted on paying us a visit. It was raining heavily.\nNo matter how ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"No matter how heavily it was raining, our relatives insisted on paying us a visit.","accept":["No matter how heavily it rained, our relatives insisted on paying us a visit.","No matter how heavily it was raining our relatives insisted on paying us a visit.","No matter how heavy the rain was, our relatives insisted on paying us a visit.","No matter how heavy the rain was our relatives insisted on paying us a visit."]},"explanation":["'No matter how' is followed straight away by an adjective or adverb, then the subject and verb: No matter how + heavily + it was raining.","Move 'heavily' forward so that it comes right after 'how'. Do not write 'No matter how it was raining heavily'.","Put a comma, then the main clause unchanged: 'our relatives insisted on paying us a visit'.","Keep the past tense. You may also change the adverb to an adjective with a noun: 'No matter how heavy the rain was, ...'."]}]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q62","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 62","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":62,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["preference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The children do not like fried chicken wings. They like fruits and vegetables instead.\nThe children prefer ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The children prefer fruits and vegetables to fried chicken wings."},"explanation":["Pattern: prefer + A (what is liked more) + **to** + B (what is liked less).","What they like goes first: fruits and vegetables. What they do not like goes after 'to': fried chicken wings.","Use 'to', not 'than'. 'prefer ... than' is a common mistake. ('rather than' is possible, but 'to' is the safest.)","Drop 'do not like' and 'instead', because 'prefer' already carries that meaning."]}]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q63","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 63","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":63,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["reported-speech"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"\"What is your decision after hearing all the available options?\" my mother asked me.\nMy mother asked me ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"My mother asked me what my decision was after hearing all the available options."},"explanation":["This is a reported question. Keep the question word 'what', but change the question word order to statement order: subject first, then verb ('what my decision was', not 'what was my decision').","Change the pronoun to fit the speaker: 'your decision' becomes 'my decision'.","The reporting verb 'asked' is in the past, so move the tense back: 'is' becomes 'was'.","End with a full stop, not a question mark, and remove the quotation marks."]}]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q64","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 64","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":64,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["wish"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The team members were sorry about not running faster during the final event.\nThe team members wished that ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The team members wished that they had run faster during the final event."},"explanation":["A wish about the past (a regret) uses: wished + (that) + subject + **had + past participle**.","They regret 'not running faster', so they wished the opposite had happened: they had run faster. Remove 'not'.","The past participle of 'run' is 'run' (run, ran, run): 'had run', not 'had ran'.","Use 'they' for the team members and keep 'during the final event'."]}]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q65","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 65","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":65,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["noun-form"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The lifts are maintained regularly to ensure that they function properly.\nThe regular ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The regular maintenance of the lifts ensures that they function properly.","accept":["The regular maintenance of the lifts is to ensure that they function properly.","The regular maintenance of the lifts ensures they function properly."]},"explanation":["'The regular' must be followed by a noun, so change the verb 'maintained' to its noun form 'maintenance' (spelling: main-ten-ance). The adverb 'regularly' has become the adjective 'regular'.","Say what is maintained with 'of': 'The regular maintenance of the lifts'.","The subject is now 'maintenance', which is singular, so the verb is 'ensures' (not 'ensure').","Keep the rest: 'that they function properly'."]}]},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q66","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 66","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":66,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","factual"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"We can infer that the writer did not like his performance outfit. Give two reasons why he felt that way.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"First reason:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The rabbit costume was garish, with neon pink fur."},"explanation":["Find the description of the costume in paragraph 1: 'not the soft, storybook kind, but a garish ensemble of neon pink fur with ears that drooped like they had given up hope. Its oversized head sagged to one side.'","Each unattractive feature is one reason. First: it was garish (unpleasantly bright), made of neon pink fur.","Other acceptable reasons from the same lines: its ears drooped; its oversized head sagged to one side; it looked less presentable than everyone else's outfit. Give two different reasons, one on each line."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Second reason:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Its head was oversized and sagged to one side."},"explanation":["The second reason must be a different feature from the first.","'Its oversized head sagged to one side.' The head was too big and lopsided, so the costume looked silly.","You could also write that its ears drooped, or that everyone else's outfit 'looked far more presentable' than his."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q67","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 67","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":67,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Besides having to wear the costume, state two other reasons for the writer's anxiety about the performance.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"First reason:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"It was his debut performance, so he was worried about making a mistake."},"explanation":["Leave the costume out; the question says 'besides'. Look for what else made the event feel frightening.","Paragraph 1: 'This was it. My debut performance. My graduation day.' A debut is a first performance. He had never performed before, so he feared failing: in paragraph 3 he stumbles over a line and asks himself, 'was that failure?'","Key idea: it was his first time performing and he was afraid of making mistakes or failing."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Second reason:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"There would be an expectant audience watching, and he was worried that they would laugh at him."},"explanation":["Paragraph 1: 'A room with an expectant audience...'. Many people would be watching and expecting a good show.","His fear of the audience is shown later: at the rehearsal the giggles spread 'like wildfire', and on opening night he is relieved that the laughter was 'not mockery' and that he 'was not being laughed at'.","Key idea: he was afraid of being laughed at by the audience."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q68","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 68","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":68,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["character-and-feelings","inference"],"format":"short","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in each blank with **a word** that **best** describes the writer's feelings.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"\"A room with an expectant audience... and that was what I had to wear?\" (lines 5–6, paragraph 1)\nThis tells me that the writer was afraid of being ____________________.","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"text","value":"embarrassed","accept":["ridiculed","mocked","humiliated","laughed at","teased","shamed","judged"]},"explanation":["The writer pictures a room full of people looking at him in a costume he finds awful. The italics show his disbelief.","What does a person fear when he must look silly in front of a crowd? Being embarrassed, or being laughed at (ridiculed, mocked).","One word only. The answer is 'embarrassed' ('ridiculed', 'mocked' or 'humiliated' carry the same idea)."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"\"Somehow, everyone's outfit looked far more presentable than mine.\" (line 8, paragraph 1)\nThis tells me that the writer was ____________________ of his classmates' costumes.","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"text","value":"jealous","accept":["envious"]},"explanation":["He compares his costume with the others' and finds theirs 'far more presentable'. He wishes he had what they have.","Wanting what someone else has is being jealous or envious. Both words are followed by 'of', which fits the blank.","The answer is 'jealous' (or 'envious')."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q69","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 69","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":69,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["evidence-phrase"],"format":"short","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in the following table by identifying the correct words or sentence from lines 10–18 (paragraph 2).","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Which five-word phrase tells us that Leon did not mind his costume?","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"text","value":"standing tall all the same"},"explanation":["Find Leon's costume in paragraph 2: he was 'fiddling with the long frayed tail of his goat costume, looking as ridiculous as I felt, **yet standing tall all the same**'.","To 'stand tall' is to carry yourself proudly and confidently. 'all the same' means 'anyway'. So he looked ridiculous but was not bothered.","Count exactly five words: standing (1) tall (2) all (3) the (4) same (5). Leave out 'yet', which would make six.","The answer is 'standing tall all the same'."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Which sentence suggests that teamwork was valued?","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"text","value":"We're all in this together.","accept":["We are all in this together","Were all in this together"]},"explanation":["Look in Leon's speech for words about the group rather than one person.","'We're all in this together.' The words 'we', 'all' and 'together' show that everyone shares the task and supports one another. That is teamwork.","Copy the whole sentence exactly, and only that sentence.","The answer is 'We're all in this together.'"]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q70","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 70","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":70,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["referencing"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Look at the table below. What do the words in the left column refer to in the passage? Write your answers in the column on the right.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"scene (line 6, paragraph 1)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The costumes hanging in the closet, including the costumes that the writer's classmates would be wearing."},"explanation":["'Taking in the **scene**, I noticed the others – fluffy sheep, dignified goats, even cows'.","A 'scene' is a view, what you see in front of you. The writer had just opened the closet door, so the scene is what was inside: his rabbit costume and the other costumes hanging there.","'the others' are the other costumes, the ones his classmates would wear.","The answer is the sight of the costumes in the closet (the costumes that the writer and his classmates had to wear)."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"those (line 16, paragraph 2)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The long rehearsals, the endless retakes and the body aches."},"explanation":["'The long rehearsals, the endless retakes, the body aches. Had I forgotten all **those**?'","'those' is plural and points back to the list in the sentence just before it.","Name all three things: the long rehearsals, the endless retakes and the body aches. Together they are the hard work he had put in."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"It (line 39, paragraph 6)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Wearing the rabbit costume to perform on stage.","accept":["performing in the rabbit costume","wearing the rabbit costume to perform","performing on stage in the rabbit costume"]},"explanation":["'Thanks for talking me out of leaving. **It** isn't so bad after all,' I replied, tugging at the floppy ears.","He tugs at the costume's ears as he speaks and goes on to say 'This outfit still looks ridiculous.' So 'It' is the thing he had dreaded and wanted to run away from: performing in the rabbit costume.","Test by replacing: 'Performing in the rabbit costume isn't so bad after all.' It makes sense.","The answer is wearing the rabbit costume to perform."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q71","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 71","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":71,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["cause-and-effect","factual"],"format":"structured","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"\"Lying there, humiliation scorched my face.\" (line 28, paragraph 3). What made the writer feel that way?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"When the writer was exiting the stage after his part, his oversized foot caught on a mess of wires and he tripped and landed on his knees, and the audience burst into laughter."},"explanation":["Read the sentences just before the quotation: 'Exiting the stage after my part, my oversized foot caught on a mess of wires. Like a puppet with its strings cut, I tripped and landed on my knees. Laughter exploded.'","'Lying there' tells us he had fallen. 'Humiliation' is deep embarrassment, and it came from people laughing at his fall.","The marker looks for both ideas: he tripped (over the wires) and fell, and everyone laughed at him."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q72","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 72","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":72,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["true-false-with-reason"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on lines 19–36 (paragraphs 3 and 4), state whether each statement in the table below is true or false, then give one reason why you think so.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Initially, the laughter during the practice session made the writer want to give up acting.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. When the laughter spread, the writer doubted himself and thought that if there were a trapdoor, he would gladly vanish."},"explanation":["The 'practice session' is the full-dress rehearsal in paragraph 3. Find what the laughter did to him.","'Laughter. More laughter. The gnawing doubt clawed at me – was that failure? What a disaster! If there were a trapdoor, I would gladly vanish.'","Wanting to disappear through a trapdoor shows that he wanted to get off the stage and give up. So the statement is True.","'Initially' matters: later in the paragraph he changed his mind and decided to face his fears."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"On the performance night, the writer was not mentally prepared.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. His stomach churned with butterflies, but this time he welcomed them."},"explanation":["The performance night is the opening night in paragraph 4.","'My stomach churned with butterflies, but this time, I welcomed them.' He was still nervous, but he accepted the feeling instead of fearing it.","Welcoming his nerves shows that he was mentally ready. So the statement is False.","Further support: 'The fears were irrational. I had made peace with them.'"]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q73","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 73","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":73,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["cause-and-effect","inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Referring to lines 19–30 (paragraph 3), what made the writer change his mindset about facing his fears?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"As he lay on the floor feeling humiliated after tripping, the writer remembered Leon's words, \"We all start somewhere.\" They made him realise that even though people had laughed at him, he had to face his fears instead of running away."},"explanation":["Find the turning point near the end of paragraph 3: 'All of a sudden, something changed. I remembered Leon's words. \"We all start somewhere.\" I knew I had to face my fears.'","What caused the change (1 mark): he remembered Leon's words, 'We all start somewhere.'","What he then understood (1 mark): everyone is a beginner at first, so stumbling and being laughed at are normal, and he had to face his fears rather than run from them.","Quote Leon's words exactly and explain what they made him realise."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q74","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 74","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":74,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","purpose-and-audience"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Complete the table below. What is the purpose for each of the characters' actions?","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Writer: made silly twitches on the rabbit's ears","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"To make the audience laugh and add to their enjoyment of the show."},"explanation":["Paragraph 4: 'Every exaggerated movement, every silly twitch of my ears added to the joy ... my antics were making the guests laugh.'","'antics' are funny, playful actions. He twitched the ears on purpose to amuse the audience.","The purpose: to make the audience laugh (to entertain them)."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Leon: gave the writer a thumbs-up","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"To encourage the writer and show him that he was doing well (putting on a good show)."},"explanation":["Paragraph 4: 'Backstage, Leon flashed me a thumbs-up.'","A thumbs-up is a sign of approval. Leon gave it while the writer was performing well, and afterwards said, 'You did it! You're a natural.'","The purpose: to praise and encourage the writer, to show that he was doing a good job."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-b-q75","cite":"2025 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 75","year":2025,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":75,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["character-and-feelings","inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"What doubt did the writer have in the beginning and how had this attitude changed after the performance?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"In the beginning, the writer doubted that he could perform in his ridiculous costume without being laughed at, and he wanted to slip away. After the performance, he felt it was a success: he had learnt about courage and that the stage was not about perfection but about showing up, even when he was scared."},"explanation":["This needs two parts: the doubt at the start (1 mark) and the changed attitude at the end (1 mark).","Beginning (paragraph 1): his heart sank when he saw the costume, he feared facing 'an expectant audience' in it, and 'had the urge to slip away'. He doubted he could perform well and worried that people would laugh at him.","After the performance (paragraph 6): 'It isn't so bad after all ... I had learnt about courage and that the stage was not about perfection. It was about showing up, even when I was scared. My debut performance was a success.'","Use signal words such as 'In the beginning' and 'After the performance' so that the marker can see both parts."]}],"group":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":1,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["subject-verb-agreement"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Michael is most upset about the cancelled party plans and so __________ Peter.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Rule: to say that something is also true of another person, use so + helping verb + subject. The helping verb is the same one, in the same tense, as in the first half of the sentence.","The first half says 'Michael **is** most upset', which is present tense, so the helping verb is a present form of 'to be'. That rules out 'was' and 'were'.","The verb must agree with the subject that comes after it. 'Peter' is one person, so we need the singular 'is', not 'are'.","'...and so is Peter.' The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["is","are","was","were"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":2,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["question-tags"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Lisa has the entire series of the limited Minecraft Collection, __________ she?\" I asked.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Rule: a positive statement takes a negative question tag, and the tag uses the helping verb of the statement.","Here 'has' is the main verb meaning 'owns' (present simple), not a helping verb. There is no 'has + past participle'. A present simple main verb takes do/does in the tag.","'Lisa' is singular, so we need 'doesn't', not 'don't'.","'hasn't' is tempting, but it is used when 'has' is a helping verb, as in 'Lisa has collected the series, hasn't she?'. The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["can't","don't","hasn't","doesn't"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q3","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 3","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":3,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Ashvin, you only need to prepare for the presentation. Get your groupmates to complete the project by __________,\" Mrs Tan said.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Rule: a reflexive pronoun (-self, -selves) must match the person doing the action.","Who is to complete the project? 'Your groupmates'. They are plural and are being talked about (third person), so the pronoun is 'themselves'.","'yourselves' is tempting because Mrs Tan is speaking to Ashvin, but Ashvin is not the one completing the project. He only prepares for the presentation.","'...complete the project by themselves.' The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["himself","ourselves","yourselves","themselves"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q4","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 4","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":4,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["quantifiers"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"There is __________ hope of finding the missing wallet before the mall closes.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'Hope' is an uncountable noun here: the verb is the singular 'is' and there is no 's' on 'hope'.","'few' and 'many' are only used with countable plural nouns (few coins, many wallets), so they are out.","'any' is used in negative sentences and questions ('There isn't any hope'), not in a positive statement like this one.","'little' goes with uncountable nouns and means 'almost no'. 'There is little hope...' The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["any","few","little","many"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q5","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 5","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":5,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"__________ harsh your coach may sound, please be assured that he has the best interest of the team at heart.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Look at what comes right after the blank: an adjective ('harsh') and then the subject and verb ('your coach may sound').","The pattern However + adjective + subject + verb means 'no matter how'. 'However harsh your coach may sound' = 'No matter how harsh your coach may sound'.","'Although' is tempting because the meaning is close, but it needs the normal word order: 'Although your coach may sound harsh, ...'.","'Regardless' needs 'of how', and 'Nevertheless' starts a new sentence. The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["However","Although","Regardless","Nevertheless"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q6","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 6","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":6,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["subject-verb-agreement","tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"A number of students from that class __________ to be stationed at the booths for the upcoming fund-raising carnival.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Rule: 'A number of' means 'several', so it takes a plural verb. ('The number of' takes a singular verb.)","'volunteers' and 'is volunteering' are singular, so they are out.","The carnival is 'upcoming', so we are talking about something that has happened and still matters now. That is the present perfect: 'have volunteered'.","'had volunteered' (past perfect) is only used for an action that happened before another past action, and there is no other past action here. The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["volunteers","is volunteering","had volunteered","have volunteered"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q7","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 7","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":7,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["conditionals","modals"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Had Sally cut the pineapple earlier, we __________ lost so much time in preparing the dessert,\" Jenny lamented.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'Had Sally cut...' is another way of writing 'If Sally had cut...'. This is the third conditional: it imagines a different past.","Rule: If + had + past participle, ... would (not) have + past participle. It tells us the result that would have happened.","The imagined result is that time was not lost: 'we would not have lost so much time'.","'could not have' would mean it was impossible to lose time, and 'should not have' means it was wrong to. Neither is the plain result of the 'if'. The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["cannot have","could not have","would not have","should not have"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q8","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 8","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":8,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"I congratulated Yamal __________ his success winning three gold medals for swimming in the recent National School Games.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Some verbs always go with a particular preposition. We congratulate someone **on** something.","For example: 'She congratulated me on my results.'","'about' and 'with' are tempting because we say 'happy about' and 'pleased with', but 'congratulate' takes 'on'.","'I congratulated Yamal on his success...' The answer is option (2)."]}],"options":["in","on","with","about"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q9","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 9","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":9,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["infinitives-and-gerunds"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Wenhui saw her classmate __________ into the drain and alerted her teacher immediately.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Rule: after a verb of the senses (see, hear, watch, feel) + a person or thing, use the base form of the verb (or the -ing form). Example: 'I heard him shout.'","'saw' already shows the past tense, so the second verb is not put into the past again.","'fell' is tempting because the event is in the past, but 'saw her classmate fell' is wrong.","'Wenhui saw her classmate fall into the drain...' The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["fall","fell","has fallen","had fallen"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q10","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 10","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":10,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["comparatives"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Jolin would rather drink green tea __________ jasmine tea.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Rule: would rather + base verb + **than** ... It shows that one thing is chosen instead of another.","'to' is tempting because we say 'prefer green tea **to** jasmine tea', but 'would rather' pairs with 'than'.","'then' sounds like 'than' but is about time (first this, then that).","'Jolin would rather drink green tea than jasmine tea.' The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["to","over","then","than"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q11","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 11","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":11,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"We could smell a strong __________ stench coming from the heap of rubbish as there was a dead rat.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clues: a 'stench' is a very bad smell, and it comes from rubbish and a dead rat. We need a word that means disgusting.","'repulsive' means so unpleasant that it makes you want to move away.","'alluring' and 'enticing' both mean attractive and tempting, which is the opposite. 'aggressive' describes behaviour that is ready to attack, not a smell.","The answer is option (3), repulsive."]}],"options":["alluring","enticing","repulsive","aggressive"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q12","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 12","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":12,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["collocation","phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The students are very fearful of Mrs Lim, the discipline mistress, as they know she is one who should not be __________ with.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Clue: the students are 'very fearful' of her, so she must be treated seriously.","'to trifle with someone' means to treat them without respect, as if they do not matter. 'Not to be trifled with' is a set phrase for a strict or powerful person.","'meddled with' is tempting, but to meddle means to interfere in something that is not your business. 'dealt with' just means handled, and 'infringe' is used for breaking rules or rights (infringe on).","The answer is option (2), trifled."]}],"options":["dealt","trifled","infringed","meddled"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q13","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 13","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":13,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"My elderly uncle's health has __________ to the point that he is unable to get out of bed by himself.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clue: he cannot even get out of bed by himself, so his health has become much worse.","'deteriorated' means became steadily worse in condition. It is the usual word for health getting worse to a serious degree.","'depreciated' is tempting because it also means 'went down', but it is used for the value of money, cars or property. 'declined' is close in meaning, but 'deteriorated' is the more exact word for a condition worsening 'to the point that' something serious happens. 'denominated' is about units of money.","The answer is option (3), deteriorated."]}],"options":["declined","depreciated","deteriorated","denominated"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q14","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 14","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":14,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Mr Rogers needed time alone to __________ his decision before making the announcement.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Clue: he needed 'time alone' before announcing a decision, so he wanted to think about it carefully.","'mull over' means to think about something slowly and carefully.","'weigh down' is tempting because we 'weigh up' choices, but 'weigh down' means to be a heavy burden. 'sink in' means to be slowly understood (the news sank in), and 'revolve around' means to have as the main subject.","The answer is option (2), mull over."]}],"options":["sink in","mull over","weigh down","revolve around"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q15","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 15","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":15,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The steep climb to the summit of the mountain was __________ but the boys did not let that affect their spirit.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Clues: the climb was 'steep', and 'but ... did not let that affect their spirit' tells us it was something hard that could have discouraged them.","'arduous' means very difficult and tiring, needing a lot of effort.","'assiduous' looks similar but describes a person who works very hard and carefully, not a climb. 'ambiguous' means unclear, with more than one meaning. 'adventurous' would not be something that dampens their spirit.","The answer is option (1), arduous."]}],"options":["arduous","assiduous","ambiguous","adventurous"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q16","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 16","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":16,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++finality++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["To say something 'with finality' is to say it in a way that shows the matter is settled and will not be changed.","Clues in the passage: 'Father had made up his mind' and 'Nothing will change my mind!'","'decisiveness' means the quality of deciding firmly and not wavering, which matches.","'seriousness' is tempting because Father was serious, but a person can be serious and still be undecided. The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g1","options":["unfairness","uneasiness","seriousness","decisiveness"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q17","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 17","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":17,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++drastically++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'drastically' means in an extreme way that has a very big effect.","The boy sensed his life was about to change in a very big and harsh way, because his beloved Shu Ma was being sent away.","'severely' means very greatly or harshly, so it is the closest. 'subtly' is the opposite (in a small, hardly noticeable way).","'suddenly' is tempting, but it is about how fast a change happens, not how big it is. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g1","options":["subtly","strongly","severely","suddenly"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q18","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 18","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":18,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++confirmed++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["To 'confirm' something is to show that it is definitely true.","The boy already feared that Shu Ma would be sent away. When Father said 'Shu Ma will leave tonight', it showed that his fear was true.","'proved' means showed to be true, so it fits: Father proved my worst fears.","'accepted' is tempting, but Father did not agree to the boy's fears; his words showed they were real. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g1","options":["settled","proved","accepted","accustomed"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q19","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 19","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":19,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++outlived++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["To 'outlive your usefulness' is to go on existing after you have stopped being useful.","'outlive' means to live or last longer than something. 'outlasted' means lasted longer than, so it is the closest.","'outstayed' is tempting because of the phrase 'outstay your welcome', but that means staying in a place longer than you are wanted, which is a different idea.","'outlaid' is about spending money and 'outreached' about reaching further. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g1","options":["outlaid","outlasted","outstayed","outreached"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q20","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 20","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":20,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++sullenly++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'sullenly' means in a bad-tempered, gloomy and unfriendly way.","Clues: Father 'growled' and was 'less understanding' than Ellen, so he was in a bad mood.","'angrily' is the closest in meaning.","'calmly' is the opposite, and 'lowly' means humble or low in rank, not 'in a low voice'. The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g1","options":["lowly","boldly","calmly","angrily"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q21","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 21","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":21,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following statements is true about the launch?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Check each statement against the poster (Text 1).","The poster says 'A total of 88 pairs will be released.' A fixed, small number means the supply is limited, so statement (2) is true.","(1) is false: 'Reservations and online purchases are not available.' (3) is false: the launch is 'Exclusively for Sports House members'. (4) is false: it is only 'at the Vista City Outlet'.","The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g2","options":["Reservations for the shoes can be made online.","Only a limited number of shoes will be available.","Anyone can purchase the shoes on 12 December.","Falcon shoes will be sold at all Sports House outlets."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q22","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 22","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":22,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Although Linda has pain in her knees, she still enjoys her occasional runs at night. Based on Text 1, which two features would benefit Linda most?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Pick out Linda's two needs: she has knee pain, and she runs at night.","Running at night means low light. Feature D, Visibility: 'Reflective materials keep you visible in low light.'","Knee pain means she must avoid hurting her joints. Feature F, Support: 'Provides stability for injury-free running.'","B (Cushioned Comfort) is tempting, but the poster says it protects the **feet** from blisters, not the knees. She runs only occasionally, so E (Durability) matters less. The answer is option (4), D and F."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g2","options":["A and E","B and D","C and E","D and F"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q23","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 23","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":23,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"What kind of poster is shown in Text 1?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Ask: what does the poster want the reader to do?","It announces a 'sales launch', gives a price ($129.90), has a catchy slogan ('DON'T RUN, FLY!') and lists the shoe's selling points. It wants people to come and buy the Falcon.","A poster that advertises a product to get people to buy it is promotional.","'informational' is tempting because the poster gives a date and place, but those details serve the main aim, which is to sell. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g2","options":["educational","promotional","motivational","informational"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q24","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 24","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":24,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following statements is supported by Text 2?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["The first sentence of Text 2 says the right shoes are 'important for every runner whether they are experienced athletes or beginners'. So beginners benefit too, which supports (4).","(1) is too strong: the article says shoes help in 'preventing injuries' and lower the 'risk', not that all good shoes stop every injury.","(2) is contradicted: 'Good running shoes do more than keep the feet comfortable.' (3) is contradicted: choosing 'may seem like a simple decision but that is far from the truth'.","Watch out for words like 'all' and 'only'. The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g2","options":["All good running shoes prevent runners from getting injured.","The only thing running shoes do is provide runners with comfort.","Choosing a pair of good running shoes is a decision that comes easily to all.","The benefits of proper running shoes are not limited to experienced runners."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-a-q25","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet A Question 25","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"a","number":25,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"What message do both Text 1 and Text 2 convey about running shoes?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["The message must be found in both texts.","Text 1: 'Not every pair is made for the same purpose. Which features would work best for you?'","Text 2: 'Think about the features that will suit you best. Not every runner needs shoes with all sorts of fancy features.'","Both say the right shoe depends on the runner. (3) says the opposite of Text 2, and neither text says lightweight or reflective shoes are best. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g2","options":["Lightweight shoes are now the best choice for every runner.","Choosing the right running shoes depends on a runner's needs.","The more features a pair of shoes has, the better the shoes are for you.","Shoes with reflective materials are safer than shoes with better cushioning."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q26","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 26","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":26,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (26). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"N","accept":["n","was"]},"explanation":["The subject is 'ivory from the mammoth'. The words between the commas ('an extinct ancestor of the elephant') only add information.","After the blank is 'used to make household items'. Ivory does not use anything; it is used by people. That is the passive voice: be + past participle.","'Ivory' is singular and the Stone Age is in the past, so the verb is 'was'.","The answer is (N) was: 'ivory ... was used to make household items'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q27","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 27","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":27,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (27). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"L","accept":["l","to"]},"explanation":["'cool ___ the touch' is a fixed phrase.","We say something is cool, soft or smooth **to the touch**, meaning that is how it feels when you touch it.","The answer is (L) to."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q28","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 28","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":28,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (28). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"H","accept":["h","so"]},"explanation":["Look further along the sentence for a partner word: '___ precious **that** many people referred to it as \"white gold\"'.","The pattern so + adjective + that shows a result: it was so precious that people called it white gold.","'such' is tempting, but 'such' needs a noun after it (such a precious material that...).","The answer is (H) so."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q29","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 29","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":29,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (29). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"J","accept":["j","such"]},"explanation":["After the blank comes 'as' and then a list of examples: chopsticks, musical instruments and furniture.","'such as' introduces examples of the thing just mentioned ('useful and decorative objects').","The answer is (J) such."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q30","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 30","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":30,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (30). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"D","accept":["d","because"]},"explanation":["The second half of the sentence gives the reason for the first half.","Why did wealthy people continue to use ivory as ornaments? They 'regarded it as a symbol of wealth and prestige'.","A connector that introduces a reason is 'because'.","The answer is (D) because."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q31","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 31","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":31,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (31). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"A","accept":["a","after"]},"explanation":["'sought after' is a fixed phrase meaning wanted by many people. ('sought' is the past participle of 'seek'.)","As demand increased, ivory became 'highly sought after'.","The answer is (A) after."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q32","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 32","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":32,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (32). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"K","accept":["k","their"]},"explanation":["The blank comes before a noun phrase ('Asian counterparts'), so we need a word that shows belonging.","The counterparts belong to 'African elephants', which is plural, so the word is 'their'.","'their Asian counterparts' means the Asian elephants, which are the matching animals in Asia.","The answer is (K) their."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q33","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 33","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":33,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (33). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"G","accept":["g","on"]},"explanation":["The noun 'ban' takes the preposition 'on': a ban **on** something.","For example: a ban on smoking, a ban on plastic bags.","'an international ban on the ivory trade'. The answer is (G) on."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q34","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 34","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":34,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (34). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"M","accept":["m","unless"]},"explanation":["The sentence gives a condition and a feared result: stronger efforts to protect elephants / elephants could disappear.","The elephants will disappear only if stronger efforts are **not** made. 'Unless' means 'if ... not'.","'when' and 'after' do not work, because then making stronger efforts would lead to the elephants disappearing.","The answer is (M) unless."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q35","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 35","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":35,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (35). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Q","accept":["q","within"]},"explanation":["The blank comes before a period of time: 'the next twenty years'.","'within' means before that period of time is over.","'after' has already been used for blank (31), each word can be used only once, and 'within' fits the worry that it could happen soon.","The answer is (Q) within."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q36","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 36","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":36,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (36) ++begin++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"beginning"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. Look at the helping verb in front: 'She **was** ___ to understand'.","Rule: was/were + verb-ing makes the past continuous tense, for something that was happening at that time.","begin + ing: double the 'n' because the stress is on the last syllable: beginning.","The correct word is 'beginning'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q37","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 37","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":37,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (37) ++cabtivatted++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"captivated"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means held the full attention of someone because it is so interesting.","Say it in syllables: cap-ti-vat-ed. It comes from 'captive' (a prisoner), so it has a 'p', not a 'b'.","There is only one 't' before '-ed': captivate + d.","The correct word is 'captivated'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q38","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 38","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":38,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (38) ++nervous++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"nervousness"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error (wrong word form). 'nervous' is an adjective.","After a possessive word like 'her', and after 'give in to', we need a noun: give in to her ___.","Add the suffix -ness to turn the adjective into a noun: nervous + ness.","The correct word is 'nervousness'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q39","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 39","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":39,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (39) ++jescher++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"gesture"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The clue is 'a little with her hands': the word means to move your hands to help express what you are saying.","The 'j' sound at the start is spelt with a 'g' (like gentle), and the ending that sounds like 'cher' is spelt '-ture' (like picture, nature).","The correct word is 'gesture'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q40","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 40","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":40,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (40) ++Eloquent++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Eloquently","accept":["eloquently"]},"explanation":["This is a grammar error (wrong word form). 'Eloquent' is an adjective.","The word describes **how** she talked, and a word that describes a verb must be an adverb.","Add -ly to make the adverb. It starts the sentence, so keep the capital letter.","The correct word is 'Eloquently'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q41","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 41","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":41,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (41) ++daspparate++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"desperate"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means needing something very badly: her father badly needed to make ends meet.","Say it in syllables: des-per-ate. It has one 'p', and the middle is 'per' even though it sounds like 'pruht'.","Do not confuse it with 'separate', which does have '-par-' in the middle.","The correct word is 'desperate'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q42","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 42","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":42,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (42) ++foltor++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"falter"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The clue is the next sentence: she stammered and could not continue. The word means to lose confidence and hesitate or stop.","The 'ol' sound is spelt 'al' (like alter, salt, halt) and the ending is '-er'.","The correct word is 'falter'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q43","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 43","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":43,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (43) ++stammer++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"stammered"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error (tense). The whole passage is told in the past tense: 'When she **started**...', '...and **could** not continue'.","The verb must be in the simple past tense too.","stammer + ed. Do not double the 'r', because the stress is on the first syllable (STAM-mer).","The correct word is 'stammered'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q44","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 44","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":44,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (44) ++fimilair++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"familiar"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means known to you, seen before.","It comes from the word 'family': fam-il-i-ar. The first vowel is 'a', and the ending is '-iar', not '-air'.","The correct word is 'familiar'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q45","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 45","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":45,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (45) ++to++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"into"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error (wrong preposition).","The verb 'erupt' takes 'into' when a crowd suddenly starts making a loud noise: erupt into laughter, erupt into cheers, erupt into a roar.","'into' shows a change from one state (quiet) to another (roaring).","The correct word is 'into'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q46","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 46","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":46,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (46) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"limited","accept":["scarce"]},"explanation":["Look back at the first sentence: Singapore imports food 'because of its small land size'.","This sentence repeats that idea: the country's ___ land means farms cannot produce enough food. We need an adjective meaning 'not much'.","'limited' fits best. 'scarce' also carries the same meaning.","The answer is 'limited'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q47","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 47","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":47,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms","contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (47) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"imported","accept":["importing"]},"explanation":["'As a result' tells us this sentence is the result of local farms not producing enough food.","The first sentence says 'Singapore imports much of its food', and the blank is followed by 'food from many different countries'.","Before the noun 'food' we can use the past participle as an adjective: 'imported food' (food that has been brought in from other countries). 'depends on importing food' is also correct.","The answer is 'imported'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q48","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 48","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":48,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (48) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"However","accept":["however"]},"explanation":["The blank starts a sentence and is followed by a comma, so it is a connector linking this sentence to the one before.","Before: Singapore depends on food from many countries. After: unexpected events may interrupt food supplies. The second sentence brings in a problem, which is a contrast.","The connector for a contrast is 'However'. Remember the capital letter.","The answer is 'However'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q49","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 49","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":49,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (49) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"vulnerable","accept":["susceptible","prone"]},"explanation":["Extreme weather, conflicts and disrupted transport routes can cut off food supplies.","The word must go with 'to': 'more ___ to interruptions'. It should mean easily harmed or affected.","'vulnerable to' means exposed to the danger of something. 'susceptible to' and 'prone to' mean the same.","The answer is 'vulnerable'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q50","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 50","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":50,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (50) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"shortages","accept":["shortage"]},"explanation":["If food supplies are interrupted, there will not be enough food.","A situation where there is not enough of something is a 'shortage'. 'food shortages' is a common pairing.","There is no 'a' before 'temporary', and it happens in 'some parts of the world', so the plural 'shortages' fits best.","The answer is 'shortages'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q51","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 51","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":51,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (51) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"smarter","accept":["smart","innovative","modern","advanced","new","newer","efficient"]},"explanation":["The blank describes the 'farming methods' Singapore has invested in.","The clue is what these methods do: they 'make better use of every available space', like vertical farming with sensors. They are clever, new ways of farming.","The key's word is 'smarter' (cleverer than the old methods). Words such as 'innovative', 'modern' and 'advanced' give the same meaning and fit the grammar.","The answer is 'smarter'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q52","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 52","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":52,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (52) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"instead"},"explanation":["The sentence contrasts two ways of growing vegetables: 'on stacked shelves' and 'across wide fields'.","The blank is followed by 'of'. The phrase 'instead of' means 'in place of'.","Vegetables are grown on stacked shelves instead of across wide fields.","The answer is 'instead'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q53","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 53","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":53,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (53) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"amount","accept":["quantity","number","volume","yield"]},"explanation":["The pattern is 'a larger ___ of crops'. We need a noun for how much is produced.","Stacking the shelves lets farmers grow more while 'using much less land', so the contrast is between more crops and less land.","'amount' fits. 'quantity' and 'yield' (how much a farm produces) are also correct.","The answer is 'amount'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q54","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 54","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":54,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (54) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"sufficient","accept":["right","correct","required","necessary","exact","precise","optimal","appropriate","adequate","ideal"]},"explanation":["Sensors measure light, temperature and humidity. Why? So that the crops get just what they need.","The blank describes the 'amount of water and nutrients': not too little and not too much. The same sentence later mentions 'avoiding unnecessary waste'.","The key's word is 'sufficient' (enough). 'right' or 'correct' is the most natural choice after 'the' and is also accepted.","The answer is 'sufficient'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q55","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 55","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":55,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (55) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"increase","accept":["maximise","maximize","improve","boost","optimise","optimize","enhance","promote"]},"explanation":["'As a result' tells us this is the benefit of crops getting enough water and nutrients.","After 'can' we need a base-form verb, and its object is 'the growth of their crops'. With the right water and nutrients, crops grow more.","'increase' fits. 'maximise', 'improve' and 'boost' also work.","The answer is 'increase'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q56","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 56","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":56,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (56) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"conserve","accept":["save","preserve"]},"explanation":["The clue is 'recycle water and use it more efficiently'. Doing this means less water is used up.","After 'helping to' we need a base-form verb whose object is 'valuable natural resources'.","'conserve' means to use carefully so that it is not wasted. 'save' has the same meaning.","The answer is 'conserve'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q57","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 57","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":57,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (57) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"quickly","accept":["rapidly","swiftly","speedily","promptly"]},"explanation":["'Since the vegetables are grown locally' means they do not travel far, so they get to the supermarkets in a short time.","The blank comes after 'more' and describes the verb 'reach', so it must be an adverb (ending in -ly). 'more fast' and 'more faster' are wrong.","'more quickly' fits, and it explains the 'greater freshness' in the rest of the sentence.","The answer is 'quickly'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q58","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 58","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":58,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (58) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"over","accept":["across"]},"explanation":["The sentence compares local vegetables with vegetables brought from far away.","We need a preposition between 'transported' and 'long distances'. Things travel or are transported **over** long distances.","'across' is also acceptable.","The answer is 'over'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q59","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 59","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":59,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (59) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"demand","accept":["need"]},"explanation":["Look at the words around the blank: 'meet the entire ___ for food'.","'meet the demand for' is a common pairing. 'demand' means how much of something people want or need.","The passage earlier said local farms 'cannot produce enough food to feed the entire population', which is the same idea.","The answer is 'demand'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q60","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 60","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":60,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (60) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"sustainable","accept":["secure","stable","reliable","resilient","dependable"]},"explanation":["This sentence sums up the passage: local farms cannot grow everything, but they still play an important role.","How do they help? The passage is about 'food security' and coping with interruptions to supply. So local farms make Singapore's food supply one that can keep going in the future.","'sustainable' means able to continue for a long time. 'secure', 'stable' and 'reliable' also fit.","The answer is 'sustainable'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q61","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 61","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":61,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["neither-nor"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Dave does not turn up for morning exercise. His brothers do not turn up either.\nNeither ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Neither Dave nor his brothers turn up for morning exercise."},"explanation":["'Neither ... nor ...' joins two negative statements. It already means 'not', so drop 'does not', 'do not' and 'either'.","Put the two subjects into the pattern: Neither Dave nor his brothers ...","Agreement rule: with neither ... nor, the verb agrees with the subject nearest to it. The nearest subject is 'his brothers' (plural), so the verb is 'turn', not 'turns'.","Keep the present tense. Answer: Neither Dave nor his brothers turn up for morning exercise."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q62","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 62","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":62,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["preposition-gerund"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Min wants to perfect his pasta recipe. He also hopes to learn how to make bread.\nOther than ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Other than wanting to perfect his pasta recipe, Min also hopes to learn how to make bread.","accept":["Other than wanting to perfect his pasta recipe, Min hopes to learn how to make bread.","Other than wanting to perfect his pasta recipe, Min also hopes to learn how to make bread"]},"explanation":["'Other than' means 'besides' or 'in addition to'. It is a preposition, so the verb after it takes the -ing form (or is replaced by a noun).","Change 'wants' to 'wanting': Other than wanting to perfect his pasta recipe, ...","Put a comma after the opening phrase. Then name the person in the main clause ('Min', not 'He'), because the opening phrase has no subject of its own.","Keep both wishes exactly: 'wants to perfect' and 'hopes to learn'. Answer: Other than wanting to perfect his pasta recipe, Min also hopes to learn how to make bread."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q63","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 63","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":63,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["reported-speech"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"\"Where did you hide my toy last week?\" Lee asked his sister.\nLee asked ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Lee asked his sister where she had hidden his toy the previous week.","accept":["Lee asked his sister where she had hidden his toy the week before.","Lee asked his sister where she had hidden his toy the previous week"]},"explanation":["This is direct speech changed to reported speech. A reported question uses statement word order (subject before verb), has no 'did', and ends with a full stop, not a question mark.","Tense moves one step back: simple past 'did you hide' becomes past perfect 'had hidden'.","Pronouns change to fit the speaker: 'you' is his sister, so 'she'; 'my' is Lee's, so 'his'.","Time words change: 'last week' becomes 'the previous week' or 'the week before'.","Answer: Lee asked his sister where she had hidden his toy the previous week."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q64","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 64","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":64,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["preposition-gerund"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Tabitha called the police when she witnessed a robbery.\n____________________ upon ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Tabitha called the police upon witnessing a robbery.","accept":["Tabitha called the police upon witnessing a robbery"]},"explanation":["'upon' means 'immediately after' or 'at the moment of'. It replaces 'when'.","'upon' is a preposition, so it is followed by the -ing form of the verb (or a noun), and the subject 'she' is dropped: 'when she witnessed' becomes 'upon witnessing'.","'upon' is in the middle of the answer line, so the main clause comes first and stays the same: Tabitha called the police ...","Answer: Tabitha called the police upon witnessing a robbery."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q65","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 65","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":65,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["noun-form"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"She worked hard to pursue excellence in her studies.\nIn her ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"In her pursuit of excellence in her studies, she worked hard.","accept":["In her pursuit of excellence in her studies, she worked hard"]},"explanation":["After a possessive word like 'her' we need a noun, so the verb 'pursue' must change to its noun form: 'pursuit'.","The noun 'pursuit' takes the preposition 'of': 'to pursue excellence' becomes 'her pursuit of excellence'.","Keep the rest of the phrase ('in her studies'), put a comma, then write the main clause: she worked hard.","Answer: In her pursuit of excellence in her studies, she worked hard."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q66","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 66","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":66,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"structured","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on lines 1 to 8 (paragraph 1), why were visitors impressed by Trev?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Visitors were impressed by Trev because he had an extraordinary natural talent for sculpting."},"explanation":["Find the sentence about visitors in paragraph 1: 'Trev's extraordinary natural talent quickly earned him the admiration of visitors'.","'earned him the admiration of visitors' means the same as 'visitors were impressed by him', so the cause is his extraordinary natural talent.","The marker looks for: extraordinary natural talent (for sculpting). Do not write that he was proud; that was the result of the praise, not the reason for it."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q67","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 67","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":67,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["evidence-phrase"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Which six-word phrase in Paragraph 3 shows that Dev used his observations to guide his choice of sculpture?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Inspired by what he had seen","accept":["inspired by what he had seen","Inspired by what he had seen,","inspired by what he had seen,"]},"explanation":["In paragraph 3, Dev spends days 'observing ordinary people': labourers and parents caring for their children.","The next sentence links what he saw to what he chose to sculpt: 'Inspired by what he had seen, he decided to sculpt an elderly farmer carrying a tired child'.","Count exactly six words and copy them without changing anything: Inspired (1) by (2) what (3) he (4) had (5) seen (6). Writing more or fewer words loses the mark.","The answer is 'Inspired by what he had seen'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q68","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 68","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":68,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on lines 18 to 21 (paragraph 4, from \"As the weeks passed\" to \"smirking with pride\"), explain Trev's attitude towards his craft.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Trev was only concerned with how his sculpture looked and paid little attention to how well it was made or how long it would last. Whenever cracks or uneven surfaces appeared, he concealed them under carvings instead of repairing them properly."},"explanation":["An 'explain' question for 2 marks needs two things: what his attitude was, and the evidence from the lines given.","Attitude (1 mark): he cared only about the sculpture's appearance and about impressing people, not about its durability or doing the work properly. Evidence: 'Trev was only concerned with the sculpture's appearance. He paid little attention to its durability.'","Action that shows it (1 mark): he 'worked hastily', and when cracks or uneven surfaces appeared 'he concealed them under carvings instead of repairing them properly'.","Use the name 'Trev' and describe the attitude in your own words (careless about quality, took shortcuts) before giving the evidence."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q69","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 69","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":69,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["referencing"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Look at the table below. What do the words in the left column refer to in the passage? Write your answers in the column on the right.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"this (line 11, paragraph 2)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The competition that the King of Tiwana announced (to select a sculpture for the new Hall of Wisdom).","accept":["the competition","The competition","the competition that the King of Tiwana announced","The competition that the King of Tiwana announced"]},"explanation":["A word like 'this' points back to something already mentioned. Read the sentences just before it.","'While many sculptors saw it as a chance to showcase their skills, Trev saw **this** as an opportunity to earn fame.' Both 'it' and 'this' point back to what the King announced in the first sentence of the paragraph.","Test by replacing the word: 'Trev saw the competition as an opportunity to earn fame.' It makes sense.","The answer is the competition announced by the King of Tiwana."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"this (line 25, paragraph 4)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Visitors gravitating towards Trev's (spectacular warrior) sculpture."},"explanation":["Read the sentence just before 'Seeing **this**': 'When visitors entered the workshop to watch them work, they gravitated towards Trev's spectacular warrior.'","What Trev saw was the visitors being drawn to his sculpture rather than Dev's. That is why he mocked Dev straight after.","'this' here refers to a whole event, not one noun, so the answer must include who (the visitors) and what they did (gravitated or were drawn towards Trev's sculpture).","The answer is the visitors gravitating towards Trev's sculpture."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"his (line 38, paragraph 6)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The (elderly) farmer in Dev's sculpture.","accept":["the farmer","The farmer","the elderly farmer","The elderly farmer","the farmer that Dev sculpted","The farmer that Dev sculpted"]},"explanation":["'he noticed the warmth in the farmer's eyes, the careful shaping of **his** worn hands'.","'his' is tricky because Trev and Dev are also male. Ask: whose worn hands were carefully shaped? The hands are part of the sculpture, like the eyes mentioned just before.","The nearest matching noun before 'his' is 'the farmer'. Earlier, Dev 'carefully carved the farmer's weathered hands'.","The answer is the farmer that Dev sculpted (not Dev and not Trev)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q70","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 70","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":70,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"What did Trev mean by, \"Your sculpture is not getting you anything\" (line 25, paragraph 4)?","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Trev meant that Dev's sculpture would not win the competition (or earn him any fame or reward)."},"explanation":["Think about what there was to 'get': the competition winner would receive 'a royal title and a chest of gold', and Trev saw it as 'an opportunity to earn fame'.","Trev said this right after visitors gravitated towards his warrior, and he added that Dev's 'common man pales in comparison'.","So 'not getting you anything' means Dev's sculpture would not win him the competition, the prize or any recognition.","The marker looks for: Dev's sculpture would not win (the competition / the prize / attention)."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"How can you tell Dev was unaffected by what Trev said in (a)?","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Dev replied calmly that sometimes ordinary things are what matter most."},"explanation":["Look at how Dev reacted straight after Trev scoffed: '\"Sometimes,\" Dev replied calmly, \"ordinary things are what matter most.\"'","The key word is 'calmly'. A person who was hurt or angry would not answer calmly, and he also stood by his choice of an ordinary subject.","The marker looks for: Dev replied calmly (and what he said). Quote or retell the line accurately."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q71","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 71","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":71,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["vocabulary-in-context"],"format":"short","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose two words from paragraphs 4 and 5 which have similar meanings to the words below.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"magnificence","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"text","value":"grandeur","accept":["Grandeur"]},"explanation":["'magnificence' is a noun meaning the quality of being very grand and impressive. Look for a noun with that meaning.","Paragraph 5: 'Although his sculpture lacked **grandeur**, it moved everyone.' Dev's simple farmer was not grand like Trev's towering warrior.","Test by swapping: 'Although his sculpture lacked magnificence' makes sense. 'spectacular' in paragraph 4 is tempting, but it is an adjective, and the answer must be the same kind of word.","The answer is 'grandeur'. Copy the spelling exactly."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"battered","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"text","value":"weathered","accept":["Weathered"]},"explanation":["'battered' is an adjective meaning worn and damaged from long, hard use.","Paragraph 4: Dev 'carefully carved the farmer's **weathered** hands'. Weathered hands are rough and worn from years of work in the sun and rain.","'worn' means the same, but it is in paragraph 6, and the question limits you to paragraphs 4 and 5.","The answer is 'weathered'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q72","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 72","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":72,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Explain fully what Dev meant by \"ordinary things are what matter most\" in line 27 (paragraph 4).","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Dev meant that ordinary people and everyday things, like the elderly farmer carrying a tired child, may not look impressive, but they are more meaningful and important than grand, impressive things like Trev's towering warrior."},"explanation":["'Explain fully' for 2 marks means two ideas are needed, and you must say what 'ordinary things' are in this story.","Idea 1: 'ordinary things' are everyday people and scenes, like his elderly farmer carrying a tired child. They are simple and not impressive to look at. (Trev had just called it a 'common man'.)","Idea 2: 'matter most' means they are the most meaningful or valuable, more than grand and showy things such as Trev's towering warrior.","The ending proves him right: the sculpture 'lacked grandeur' but 'it moved everyone'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q73","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 73","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":73,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["character-and-feelings","evidence-phrase"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on evidence from the text, explain how Trev felt when his sculpture shattered. (lines 31 to 33, paragraph 5, from \"Moments later\" to \"across the ground\")","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Trev felt embarrassed (ashamed). His face turned beetroot red and he lowered his head as the broken pieces lay scattered across the ground."},"explanation":["This needs a feeling (1 mark) and the evidence for it (1 mark).","Evidence: 'Trev's face turned beetroot red and he lowered his head as the broken pieces lay scattered across the ground.'","A face turning red and a lowered head are signs of embarrassment or shame. It happened in front of the judges and a courtyard full of spectators, and his 'hidden flaws ... had finally been exposed'.","Name the feeling with a precise word (embarrassed, ashamed, humiliated), not just 'sad' or 'bad', then quote the evidence."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q74","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 74","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":74,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["true-false-with-reason"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on lines 28 to 46 (paragraphs 5 to 7), state whether each statement in the table below is true or false, then give one reason why you think so.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Part of Trev's sculpture crumbled to the ground during the inspection.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. A large fracture spread across the statue's chest before both of its arms broke away (and the broken pieces lay scattered across the ground)."},"explanation":["Find the inspection in paragraph 5: 'just as they were inspecting it, a faint crack echoed through the courtyard. Moments later, a large fracture spread across the statue's chest before both of its arms broke away.'","'Part of' matches 'both of its arms', 'crumbled to the ground' matches 'the broken pieces lay scattered across the ground', and 'during the inspection' matches 'just as they were inspecting it'.","So the statement is True. The reason must be evidence from the passage, not the statement repeated."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Trev's sculpture attracted more spectators than Dev's on the day of judgement.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. Both sculptures had their own set of admirers."},"explanation":["Paragraph 5 describes the spectators on the day of judgement: 'Both sculptures had their own set of admirers.'","Nothing says Trev's had more on that day. The trap is paragraph 4, where visitors 'gravitated towards Trev's spectacular warrior', but that was earlier, in the workshop.","So the statement is False, and the reason is that both sculptures had their own set of admirers."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"Trev finally understood the true meaning of the competition after Master Govin's reminder.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. Trev had already realised it when he studied Dev's sculpture, before Master Govin came into the workshop. Master Govin's words only reinforced that realisation; they did not cause it."},"explanation":["Look at the order of events in paragraph 6. First Trev studied Dev's sculpture: 'Seeing that, realisation finally dawned on him; it was never just about the end result.'","Only after that: 'Master Govin **then** came into the workshop and reminded him...'. The word 'then' shows the reminder came after the realisation.","So Trev understood before the reminder, not after it. The statement is False.","Reason: the realisation dawned on Trev when he studied Dev's sculpture; Master Govin's words came afterwards and only reinforced it."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-b-q75","cite":"2026 Prelim ACSJ Paper 2 Booklet B Question 75","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"ACSJ","section":"b","number":75,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"\"It was never just about the end result.\" (lines 39 to 40, paragraph 6)\nHow did Trev's view of Dev's sculpture change after the competition?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Initially, Trev believed that only appearance mattered, so he looked down on Dev's sculpture as ordinary and unimpressive. After the competition, he saw the warmth, careful work and quiet beauty in it, and realised that the patience and process (the journey) that went into a sculpture were important too."},"explanation":["A 'how did it change' question needs both sides: the view before (1 mark) and the view after (1 mark).","Before: Trev mocked Dev's 'ordinary subject' and said the 'common man pales in comparison to my towering warrior'. He believed only an impressive appearance and the end result mattered.","After: 'For the first time, he noticed the warmth in the farmer's eyes, the careful shaping of his worn hands and the quiet beauty that could only come from patience.' He realised 'it was never just about the end result': the process, patience and care mattered too.","Use signal words such as 'Initially ... After the competition ...' so the marker can see the change."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":1,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["conditionals","inversion"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"__________ the traffic not been heavy, we would have reached the airport on time.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Clue: 'we **would have reached**' tells us this is about something in the past that did not happen (the traffic was heavy, and we were late).","Rule: for an unreal past condition we use 'If + had + past participle'. The 'if' can be dropped by moving 'had' to the front: 'If the traffic had not been heavy' becomes 'Had the traffic not been heavy'.","The word after the blank is 'been', a past participle, so only 'Had' can go with it. 'Did', 'Might' and 'Should' are followed by the base form of a verb, not 'been'.","'Had the traffic not been heavy, we would have reached the airport on time.' The answer is option (2)."]}],"options":["Did","Had","Might","Should"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":2,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["question-tags"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"I heard Ryan is being awarded the Model Student Award. He's improved immensely in his behaviour, __________ he?\" Mrs Tan commented.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["First work out what 'He's' stands for. It is followed by 'improved', a past participle, so 'He's improved' means 'He **has** improved' (present perfect), not 'He is'.","Rule for question tags: use the same helping verb as the statement, and make a positive statement take a negative tag.","The statement is positive ('has improved'), so the tag is negative: 'hasn't he?'","'isn't' is the tempting wrong answer, because 's can also mean 'is'. The answer is option (4), hasn't."]}],"options":["is","has","isn't","hasn't"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q3","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 3","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":3,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Both of you should give __________ credit for working so well together despite your differences,\" praised Miss Chan.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["When the person doing the action and the person receiving it are the same, we use a reflexive pronoun (a '-self' or '-selves' word).","Miss Chan is speaking **to** two people: 'Both of you'. The reflexive pronoun for 'you' is 'yourself' for one person and 'yourselves' for more than one.","'Both' means two people, so we need the plural 'yourselves'. 'themselves' goes with 'they', and 'oneself' goes with 'one'.","The answer is option (3), yourselves."]}],"options":["oneself","yourself","yourselves","themselves"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q4","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 4","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":4,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Being declared the winners of the National Volleyball Championships was __________ our wildest dreams,\" the star player said.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'beyond our wildest dreams' is a fixed expression. It means far better than anything we had ever imagined or hoped for.","'beyond' means further than a limit. The win went further than even their wildest dreams.","'over' and 'above' also mean 'higher than', which makes them tempting, but they are not used in this expression.","The answer is option (4), beyond."]}],"options":["of","over","above","beyond"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q5","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 5","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":5,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["infinitives-and-gerunds","prepositions"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Krish insisted on __________ the puzzle before his dinner, refusing to leave it half done.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Rule: a verb that comes straight after a preposition (on, in, at, of, for ...) takes the -ing form.","'insisted **on**' ends with the preposition 'on', so the verb becomes 'completing'.","'completion' is a noun. It would need 'of' after it ('insisted on the completion of the puzzle'), so it does not fit here.","'Krish insisted on completing the puzzle.' The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["complete","completed","completing","completion"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q6","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 6","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":6,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Avery dislikes prawns. __________ that, she enjoys seafood.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Look at how the two sentences are linked. Prawns are a kind of seafood, so disliking prawns but enjoying seafood is a contrast, something we would not expect.","'In spite of that' means 'even though that is true'. It links two ideas that contrast with each other.","'As a result of' gives a cause, 'In addition to' adds a similar idea, and 'According to' tells us who said something. None of them shows contrast.","'Avery dislikes prawns. In spite of that, she enjoys seafood.' The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["As a result of","In addition to","According to","In spite of"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q7","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 7","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":7,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["quantifiers"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The scientists conducted __________ research on sea jellies before they published their detailed work last year.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'research' is an uncountable noun: we do not say 'a research' or 'researches'.","'many' and 'several' are used only with countable plural nouns (many books, several tests), so they are out.","'little' and 'much' both go with uncountable nouns, so use the meaning. The scientists published '**detailed** work', so they must have done a lot of research, not hardly any.","The answer is option (2), much."]}],"options":["little","much","many","several"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q8","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 8","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":8,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["subjunctive","tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Hoping to avoid the peak-hour crowd, the tour guide suggested that the tourists __________ at the lobby after breakfast.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Rule: after verbs such as suggest, recommend, insist and demand + 'that', the next verb stays in its base form, whatever the tense or subject. (It is short for 'should gather'.)","So we write 'suggested that the tourists gather', just as we would write 'suggested that he gather'.","'gathered' is tempting because 'suggested' is in the past tense, but the gathering had not happened yet. It was only a suggestion.","The answer is option (1), gather."]}],"options":["gather","gathered","had gathered","have gathered"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q9","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 9","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":9,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["subject-verb-agreement"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Neither of us __________ ready to leave the party now,\" whined Kim to her mother.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Rule: 'neither of' + plural noun or pronoun takes a singular verb, because it means 'not one and not the other'.","The word 'now' shows the present tense, so 'was' and 'were' are out.","The singular present form of 'to be' is 'is'. 'are' is tempting because 'us' is plural, but the subject is 'Neither'.","'Neither of us is ready to leave the party now.' The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["is","are","was","were"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q10","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 10","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":10,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["comparatives"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Working with Nora on this project was __________ than the requirements of the project itself,\" said Jill.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["The clue is the word 'than' right after the blank. 'than' is used when two things are compared.","Two things are compared here: working with Nora, and the requirements of the project. For two things we use the comparative form.","'challenging' is a long adjective, so its comparative is 'more challenging'. 'as challenging' needs 'as', not 'than'; 'most challenging' is for three or more things and needs 'the'.","The answer is option (3), more challenging."]}],"options":["challenging","as challenging","more challenging","most challenging"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q11","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 11","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":11,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The __________ of the football player was on full display as he successfully dodged the tackles from his opponents.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Clue: he 'dodged the tackles'. To dodge is to move quickly out of the way.","'agility' is the ability to move quickly and easily, changing direction fast. That is exactly what dodging needs.","'stamina' is the energy to keep going for a long time, 'strength' is power, and 'accuracy' is about hitting a target. None of these is shown by dodging.","The answer is option (4), agility."]}],"options":["accuracy","strength","stamina","agility"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q12","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 12","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":12,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"David stayed up late to revise for the examinations because he had __________ studying until the night before his first paper.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Clue: he had to stay up late because he had not studied 'until the night before'. He had delayed his studying.","'put off' means to delay something or leave it until later.","'put out' means to stop a fire or light, 'put up' means to build something or let someone stay, and 'put on' means to wear something or to stage a show.","The answer is option (2), put off."]}],"options":["put out","put off","put up","put on"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q13","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 13","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":13,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Although Lilian had won the Student Leader Award, she remained __________, rarely bringing up her achievements to her classmates.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Clue: she was 'rarely bringing up her achievements'. She did not boast. 'Although' also tells us her behaviour is a surprise after winning an award.","'modest' means not talking much about your own abilities or successes; humble.","'selfless' is tempting, but it means caring about other people's needs more than your own, which is not what the sentence describes. 'proud' is the opposite of what we need.","The answer is option (2), modest."]}],"options":["confident","modest","selfless","proud"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q14","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 14","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":14,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The detective searched the room __________ for fingerprints, looking into every nook and cranny for even the smallest clue.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Clue: 'every nook and cranny' and 'even the smallest clue'. The search was very careful and thorough.","'meticulously' means very carefully, paying attention to every small detail.","'vigorously' means with a lot of energy and force, which says nothing about care. 'hastily' means in a hurry, the opposite of careful.","The answer is option (1), meticulously."]}],"options":["meticulously","vigorously","hastily","calmly"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q15","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 15","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":15,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["collocation","word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"When Jessie and her team members were having a heated argument, Mrs Lim stepped in to __________ the tension before it worsened.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'defuse' means to make a tense or dangerous situation calmer. (It comes from removing the fuse of a bomb so that it cannot explode.) 'Defuse the tension' is a common word pair.","It fits best because Mrs Lim acted 'before it worsened', like stopping a bomb before it goes off.","'subside' means to become calmer by itself (the tension subsided); a person cannot 'subside' something. 'reduce' and 'minimise' make sense, but they are not the words normally paired with 'tension' in a heated argument, so they are not the best answer.","The answer is option (4), defuse."]}],"options":["minimise","subside","reduce","defuse"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q16","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 16","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":16,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++milestone++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["A 'milestone' is a very important event or achievement that marks a new stage.","Clue in the passage: these were 'the first-ever cubs of their kind to be born here'. Something achieved for the first time is a breakthrough.","'breakthrough' means an important achievement or success after much effort, which matches.","'anniversary' is tempting because the zoo 'celebrated', but an anniversary is the date on which something happened in an earlier year. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g1","options":["transformation","breakthrough","anniversary","incident"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q17","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 17","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":17,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning","phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++bring up++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Here 'bring up' means to raise a number: to make it go up.","Clue in the passage: the population is 'shrinking', with 'fewer than 800' left. The experts hope the new cubs will make the population bigger again.","'expand' means to make something larger in size or number. It is the opposite of 'shrink', so it fits best.","'multiply' is tempting, but it means to increase many times over, which is far more than three cubs can do. 'improve' means to make better, not bigger. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g1","options":["improve","multiply","expand","aid"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q18","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 18","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":18,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++thrive++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["To 'thrive' is to grow strong and healthy and do very well.","Clue in the passage: the cubs are described as 'healthy' and developing well.","'flourish' means to grow well and be healthy and successful, the same meaning.","'survive' is tempting, but it only means to stay alive, which is much weaker. 'excel' means to be very good at a skill. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g1","options":["excel","mature","flourish","survive"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q19","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 19","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":19,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++incredibly rewarding++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Something 'rewarding' gives you a deep feeling of satisfaction because your effort was worth it.","The curator looks after the animals, so seeing the cubs healthy made him feel that his work had been worthwhile.","'fulfilling' means making you feel satisfied and happy with what you have done, which matches.","'enjoyable' is tempting, but it only means fun or pleasant; it does not carry the sense of satisfaction from effort. The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g1","options":["enjoyable","valuable","inspiring","fulfilling"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q20","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 20","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":20,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++feisty++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["'feisty' describes someone who is full of energy and spirit and not easily pushed around.","Clue in the passage: the word 'while' contrasts the female cub with the males, who were 'laid-back' (relaxed and easy-going). The opposite of laid-back is energetic.","'lively' means full of energy, which matches.","'fierce' is tempting because leopards are wild animals, but fierce means violent and frightening, which is too strong and is not the opposite of 'laid-back'. The answer is option (1)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g1","options":["lively","fierce","playful","uptight"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q21","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 21","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":21,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Want to feel stronger and more energetic?\" The purpose of this question in Text 1 is to __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Look at what follows the question on the poster: the one-word answer 'Run!' and then a list of the benefits of running.","The question names something most readers want (to feel stronger and more energetic) and then offers running as the way to get it. Its job is to make readers want to start running.","(2) is tempting because the whole poster wants people to join the club, but this question says nothing about the club. The invitation comes later: 'we welcome you!' and 'Contact Jim ... to sign up'.","The poster does not expect readers to reply, so (4) is wrong, and the question does not explain what being healthy means, so (3) is wrong. The answer is option (1)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g2","options":["make readers want to pick up running","invite readers to join House of Runners","show readers what it means to be healthy","ask readers how they can become healthier"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q22","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 22","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":22,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Based on the poster in Text 1, which of the following would you least expect House of Runners to be?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["'Least expect' means: find the description that the poster supports the least. Check each one.","(2) is stated: members 'meet every Tuesday evening and Saturday morning'. (3) is stated: runners 'come together to exercise'. (4) is stated: 'Whether you are new to running or already enjoy it, we welcome you!'","(1) is never given as the purpose. The members are 'friendly' and 'support one another', but people join to run and get healthier ('Run Your Way to a Healthier Life'), not mainly to make friends.","The answer is option (1)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g2","options":["a club for people to make friends","a group of people who meet regularly","a way for people to exercise together","a club that people who like running can join"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q23","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 23","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":23,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience","inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following statements are true about how information on House of Runners is presented in Text 1?\nA: It expresses concern about readers' health issues.\nB: It emphasises how readers can benefit from an activity.\nC: It uses an inclusive and welcoming approach to attract readers.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["A is false. The poster never mentions illness or health problems, and it does not sound worried. It is cheerful and encouraging.","B is true. The heading 'Benefits:' is underlined and lists three benefits of running: 'Improves heart health', 'Strengthens muscles', 'Increases stamina'.","C is true. 'Runners of all levels come together' and 'Whether you are new to running or already enjoy it, we welcome you!' show that everyone is included.","Only B and C are true. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g2","options":["A and B","A and C","B and C","A, B and C"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q24","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 24","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":24,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"What is the **main** message Text 2 conveys about staying healthy?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["The main message is the idea the writer keeps coming back to, not a detail mentioned once.","Each paragraph ends on the same idea. Paragraph 1: 'What matters most is staying active regularly and enjoying the process.' Paragraph 2: 'regularly engaging in enjoyable activities is what helps people stay healthy in the long run.'","Regular + enjoyable = an enjoyable exercise routine, which is option (2).","(3) and (4) are in the text, but each is mentioned only once as extra advice ('In addition, ...', 'Health experts often recommend ...'). (1) is not said: the text says other activities also work, not that you must do many kinds. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g2","options":["Include a wide range of exercises.","Establish an enjoyable exercise routine.","Maintain a balanced diet and drink enough water.","Match the intensity of the activity to one's fitness level."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-a-q25","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet A Question 25","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"a","number":25,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Which idea is expressed in both Text 1 and Text 2?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["The idea must be found in both texts, so check each option against Text 1 and Text 2.","(1) Text 1 lists the 'Benefits' of running (heart health, muscles, stamina). Text 2 says 'Because of its health benefits ...'. Both agree.","(2) is wrong: Text 1 welcomes people who are 'new to running'. (3) is wrong: Text 2 says injuries are likely only if people run 'too often, too fast or for too long'. (4) is wrong: Text 2 says cycling or playing sports 'can also help improve heart health'.","The answer is option (1)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g2","options":["Running offers health benefits.","Running is not suitable for beginners.","Running regularly will result in injuries.","Running is the only way to improve heart health."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q26","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 26","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":26,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (26). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"E","accept":["e","by"]},"explanation":["'inspired' here is a past participle used in the passive: the entries were inspired ___ something.","In the passive, the thing that causes the action is introduced with 'by': 'inspired by local expressions and everyday speech'.","'from' is tempting, but the usual pattern is 'inspired by'. 'from' is also needed for blank (33).","The answer is (E) by."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q27","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 27","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":27,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (27). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"J","accept":["j","is"]},"explanation":["The sentence part 'one of the examples ___ \"lao hong biscuit\"' has no verb, so the blank needs one.","Rule: 'one of the' + plural noun takes a singular verb, because the subject is 'one'.","So we need 'is', not 'are'. The passage is in the present tense ('include', 'describes').","The answer is (J) is: 'one of the examples is \"lao hong biscuit\"'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q28","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 28","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":28,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (28). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"N","accept":["n","who"]},"explanation":["The words between the two commas give extra information about 'The creator', a person. That is a relative clause, and for a person it begins with 'who' or 'whom'.","After the blank comes the verb 'started'. The creator did the starting, so the pronoun is the **subject** of 'started'. Use 'who' for the subject.","'whom' is used for the object, for example 'the creator, whom many people admire'. It cannot be followed directly by a verb.","The answer is (N) who: 'The creator, who started the project as a personal passion, ...'"]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q29","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 29","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":29,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (29). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"C","accept":["c","and"]},"explanation":["Look at the two verbs on either side of the blank: 'wanted to **showcase** the uniqueness of Singlish ___ **highlight** how language reflects identity'.","He wanted to do both things, so we need a word that adds one action to another: 'and'.","'or' would mean he wanted only one of the two, which does not fit.","The answer is (C) and."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q30","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 30","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":30,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (30). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"A","accept":["a","a"]},"explanation":["'role' is a singular countable noun, so it needs an article: 'plays ___ unique role'.","Choose 'a' or 'an' by the **sound** that follows, not the letter. 'unique' begins with a 'yoo' sound, which is a consonant sound.","So it is 'a unique role', just like 'a uniform' and 'a university'. 'an' is the trap.","The answer is (A) a."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q31","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 31","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":31,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (31). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Q","accept":["q","with"]},"explanation":["The verb is 'communicate'. We communicate **with** other people.","'how Singaporeans communicate with others in daily life' makes sense.","The answer is (Q) with."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q32","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 32","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":32,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (32). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"F","accept":["f","for"]},"explanation":["'a platform ___ creative expression and cultural sharing' tells us the purpose of the platform, what it is meant to be used for.","'for' introduces a purpose: a platform for creative expression.","The answer is (F) for."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q33","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 33","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":33,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (33). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"G","accept":["g","from"]},"explanation":["'people from all walks of life' is a fixed expression. It means people of many different jobs and backgrounds.","Here it is 'online users from different walks of life': 'from' shows where the users come from.","The answer is (G) from."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q34","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 34","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":34,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (34). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"H","accept":["h","in"]},"explanation":["'grow in popularity' is a fixed pattern: grow in size, grow in number, grow in confidence.","'The dictionary has rapidly grown in popularity over time' means it has become more and more popular.","The answer is (H) in."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q35","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 35","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":35,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns","articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (35). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"K","accept":["k","its"]},"explanation":["The blank comes before the noun 'success', so we need a word that shows whose success it is: the dictionary's.","'its' (no apostrophe) is the possessive word for a thing: its success.","'it's' (with an apostrophe) is the trap. It is short for 'it is' or 'it has'; 'It is success has also encouraged' makes no sense.","The answer is (K) its: 'Its success has also encouraged others ...'"]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q36","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 36","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":36,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (36) ++opurtooniti++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"opportunity"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. Say the word aloud: op-por-tu-ni-ty. It means a chance to do something.","Break it into parts: op + por + tu + ni + ty. Remember the double 'p' and the ending '-ity'.","The correct word is 'opportunity'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q37","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 37","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":37,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (37) ++apeeling++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"appealing"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means attractive or interesting: was life overseas more attractive for them?","It comes from the verb 'appeal': double 'p', and the long 'ee' sound is spelt 'ea'. Then add -ing.","The correct word is 'appealing'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q38","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 38","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":38,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (38) ++shows++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"shown"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. Look at the helping verb in front: 'travelling around the world **has** ___ the family'.","Rule: has/have + past participle makes the present perfect tense.","The verb 'show' goes show, showed, shown. The past participle is 'shown'.","The correct word is 'shown'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q39","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 39","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":39,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (39) ++in++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"of","accept":["on"]},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong preposition has been used.","The fixed phrase is 'short **of** something', meaning not having enough of it: short of money, short of time.","'Singapore may be short of natural resources' means it does not have many.","The correct word is 'of'. ('short on' is also used in everyday English.)"]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q40","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 40","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":40,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (40) ++stable++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"stability"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong form of the word has been used.","'it offers safety and ___': the word 'and' joins two things of the same kind. 'safety' is a noun, so the second word must also be a noun.","'stable' is an adjective. Its noun is 'stability' (stable: drop the 'le', add 'ility').","The correct word is 'stability'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q41","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 41","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":41,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (41) ++bearnefical++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"beneficial"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means helpful or having a good effect.","It comes from 'benefit'. Break it up: ben + e + fi + cial. The 'shul' sound at the end is spelt '-cial', as in 'special'.","The correct word is 'beneficial'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q42","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 42","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":42,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (42) ++rekonaised++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"recognised","accept":["recognized"]},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means realised or accepted that something is true.","Break it up: rec + og + nise + d. There is a 'g' after 'reco' that is easy to forget, and the 'k' sound is spelt with a 'c'.","The correct word is 'recognised'. (The American spelling 'recognized' is also accepted.)"]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q43","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 43","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":43,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (43) ++great++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"greatest"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. Clues: 'the ___ gain **among them**'.","The son is being compared with everyone in the family (three or more people), and the word 'the' comes before the blank. That calls for the superlative form.","great, greater, greatest: the superlative is 'greatest'.","The correct word is 'greatest'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q44","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 44","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":44,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (44) ++advencheras++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"adventurous"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means willing to try new and exciting things.","It comes from 'adventure'. Drop the 'e' and add '-ous': adventur + ous. The 'cher' sound is spelt 'tur', and the ending 'us' sound is spelt 'ous'.","The correct word is 'adventurous'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q45","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 45","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":45,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (45) ++simple++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"simply"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong form of the word has been used.","In 'it is ___ a new season', the word does not describe a noun. It adds to the whole statement, with the meaning 'just' or 'only'. That is the job of an adverb.","'simple' is an adjective. Its adverb is 'simply' (drop the 'e', add 'y').","The correct word is 'simply'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q46","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 46","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":46,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","collocation"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (46) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"variety","accept":["range","selection","array","assortment"]},"explanation":["Clues: 'a wide ___ of food options to choose from'. We need a noun that goes with 'a wide ... of' and means many different kinds.","'a wide variety of' is a very common phrase meaning many different types.","'range' and 'selection' have the same meaning and also fit.","The answer is 'variety'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q47","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 47","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":47,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (47) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Meanwhile","accept":["however","conversely"]},"explanation":["Compare the sentences on either side. Before: for some students 'the answer is probably yes'. After: 'for others, their experiences may not be as positive'.","The two groups of students are being contrasted, and the blank starts a new sentence and is followed by a comma. We need a linking word that turns to the other side.","The key gives 'Meanwhile', which can mean 'on the other hand' when two situations are compared. 'However' shows the contrast even more clearly and is just as correct.","The answer is 'Meanwhile' (or 'However')."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q48","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 48","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":48,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["idioms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (48) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"piece"},"explanation":["'a piece of cake' is an idiom. It means something that is very easy to do.","'Addressing this issue may not be a piece of cake' means that solving the problem is not easy.","The answer is 'piece'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q49","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 49","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":49,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors","collocation"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (49) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"due","accept":["owing","attributed","attributable"]},"explanation":["The sentence gives the reason for the change: there are fewer and fewer canteen vendors.","The blank sits between 'largely' and 'to', so we need a word that makes a 'because of' phrase with 'to'.","'due to' means 'because of': 'This change is largely due to the decreasing number of canteen vendors.' ('owing to' means the same.)","The answer is 'due'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q50","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 50","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":50,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs","contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (50) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"cope","accept":["deal","contend"]},"explanation":["Clues: 'find it hard to ___ **with** the long working hours and rising operating costs'. We need a verb that is followed by 'with' and means to manage something difficult.","'cope with' means to manage a difficult situation successfully.","'deal with' is close in meaning and also fits.","The answer is 'cope'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q51","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 51","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":51,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (51) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"unwilling","accept":["reluctant","hesitant"]},"explanation":["'As a result' tells us this sentence is the effect of the long hours and rising costs. What would stallholders feel about running a stall?","They would not want to. The blank comes between 'are' and 'to run', so we need an adjective: 'are ___ to run food stalls'.","'despite the pressing need for canteen vendors' confirms it: stalls are needed, yet people do not want to run them.","The answer is 'unwilling' ('reluctant' has the same meaning)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q52","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 52","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":52,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (52) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"packed","accept":["packaged","placed","put","portioned"]},"explanation":["Clue: 'before being ___ **into bento boxes** and delivered'. What is done to meals to get them into boxes?","They are packed. After 'being' we need a past participle (passive voice), matching 'delivered'.","The answer is 'packed'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q53","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 53","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":53,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","collocation"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (53) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"solution","accept":["answer"]},"explanation":["Clue: 'a ___ **to the problem** of having a shortage of canteen vendors'.","The word that pairs with 'problem' is 'solution': a solution to a problem. Note the preposition 'to'.","The answer is 'solution'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q54","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 54","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":54,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","collocation"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (54) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"sparks","accept":["raises","causes","creates","triggers","generates","prompts"]},"explanation":["'While this seems like a solution ..., it also ___ concerns among parents and students.' 'While' sets up a contrast: there is a good side, but also a worrying side.","We need a verb meaning 'causes to begin'. The subject is 'it' (singular) and the passage is in the present tense, so the verb must end in -s.","The key gives 'sparks' (to spark is to set something off). 'raises concerns' is the most common word pair and is equally correct.","The answer is 'sparks' (or 'raises')."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q55","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 55","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":55,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (55) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"time","accept":["distance","period","duration"]},"explanation":["Clue: food 'transported over a long ___ may not always be as fresh as it was when first cooked'.","Food loses its freshness when a long time passes between cooking and eating. 'over a long time' fits.","'over a long distance' also makes sense, because a long journey takes a long time.","The answer is 'time' (or 'distance')."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q56","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 56","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":56,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["quantifiers","comparatives"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (56) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"fewer"},"explanation":["Clues: 'than before' shows a comparison, and 'only a limited menu' tells us there is not as much to choose from.","'choices' is a countable plural noun. For a smaller number of countable things we use 'fewer'; 'less' is for uncountable nouns (less food, fewer food choices).","The answer is 'fewer'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q57","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 57","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":57,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (57) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"ensure","accept":["guarantee"]},"explanation":["Why would central kitchens work with nutritionists? To make sure that the meals are healthy and tasty.","The blank needs one verb after 'to' that means 'make sure': 'ensure'. ('ensure (that) meals are healthy and tasty')","The answer is 'ensure'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q58","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 58","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":58,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["collocation"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (58) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"based","accept":["depending"]},"explanation":["Clue: 'changes to the menu can be made ___ **on** their food preferences'. The changes follow what the students like.","'based on' means using something as the reason or starting point.","The answer is 'based'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q59","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 59","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":59,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["collocation"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (59) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"worth"},"explanation":["'It is worth noting that ...' is a fixed expression used to point out an important fact.","After 'worth' the verb takes the -ing form: worth noting, worth trying, worth reading. The '-ing' in 'noting' is the clue.","The answer is 'worth'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q60","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 60","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":60,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (60) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"than"},"explanation":["'rather than' means 'instead of'. It is used to say what something is **not** meant to do.","The system is meant 'to serve as an option ..., rather than replace the traditional canteen completely'.","The answer is 'than'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q61","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 61","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":61,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["preposition-gerund"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Mrs Lim received the bad news. Mrs Lim fainted on the spot.\nOn ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"On receiving the bad news, Mrs Lim fainted on the spot.","accept":["On receiving the bad news, she fainted on the spot.","On receipt of the bad news, Mrs Lim fainted on the spot."]},"explanation":["'On' + verb-ing means 'at the moment when' or 'as soon as': the second action happened right after the first.","'On' is a preposition, so the verb after it takes the -ing form and the subject is dropped: 'Mrs Lim received' becomes 'On receiving'.","Put a comma after the opening phrase. Then name the person in the main clause, because the opening phrase has no subject of its own: '..., Mrs Lim fainted on the spot.'","Answer: On receiving the bad news, Mrs Lim fainted on the spot."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q62","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 62","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":62,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["noun-form"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Tina achieved her goal after months of training.\n____________________ of her goal came after ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Tina's achievement of her goal came after months of training.","accept":["Tina's achievement of her goal came after months of training"]},"explanation":["The words 'of her goal came after' show that the verb 'achieved' must be turned into a noun, which becomes the subject of 'came'.","The noun of 'achieve' is 'achievement' (achieve + ment; keep the 'e').","Tina was the one who achieved, so she becomes the owner of the noun: 'Tina' becomes 'Tina's' with an apostrophe and s.","The rest stays the same after 'came after': months of training.","Answer: Tina's achievement of her goal came after months of training."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q63","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 63","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":63,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["reported-speech"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"\"Why did you forget your homework?\" Mrs Gan asked Brother.\nMrs Gan asked Brother ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Mrs Gan asked Brother why he had forgotten his homework.","accept":["Mrs Gan asked Brother why he had forgotten his homework"]},"explanation":["This is direct speech changed to reported speech. A reported question keeps the question word ('why'), uses statement word order (subject before verb), has no 'did', and ends with a full stop.","Tense moves one step back: simple past 'did you forget' becomes past perfect 'had forgotten'.","Pronouns change to fit the speaker: 'you' is Brother, so 'he'; 'your' becomes 'his'.","Do not add 'if'. 'if' or 'whether' is only for yes/no questions; this question already has 'why'.","Answer: Mrs Gan asked Brother why he had forgotten his homework."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q64","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 64","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":64,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["passive-voice"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Passing the exam needs at least fifty marks.\nA minimum of ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"A minimum of fifty marks is needed to pass the exam.","accept":["A minimum of fifty marks is required to pass the exam.","A minimum of fifty marks is needed for passing the exam.","A minimum of 50 marks is needed to pass the exam.","A minimum of fifty marks is needed in order to pass the exam."]},"explanation":["'A minimum of' means 'at least', so 'at least fifty marks' becomes 'A minimum of fifty marks'. Do not write 'at least' again.","The marks are now the subject, but marks do not need anything; they are needed. So the verb becomes passive: 'needs' becomes 'is needed'.","Agreement: the subject is 'A minimum', which is singular, so use 'is needed', not 'are needed'.","'Passing the exam' moves to the end as the purpose: 'to pass the exam'.","Answer: A minimum of fifty marks is needed to pass the exam."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q65","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 65","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":65,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["infinitive-of-purpose"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The coach adjusted the training hours so that the pressure on students would be less.\nTo ____________________, the coach ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"To lessen the pressure on students, the coach adjusted the training hours.","accept":["To reduce the pressure on students, the coach adjusted the training hours.","To decrease the pressure on students, the coach adjusted the training hours.","To lower the pressure on students, the coach adjusted the training hours.","To ease the pressure on students, the coach adjusted the training hours."]},"explanation":["'so that ...' gives the purpose of an action. A purpose can also be given with 'To' + base verb at the start of the sentence.","After 'To' we need a verb, but 'would be less' has only the adjective 'less'. Change it to a verb with the same meaning: 'lessen' (or 'reduce').","The purpose phrase becomes 'To lessen the pressure on students', followed by the printed comma.","The main clause stays the same after 'the coach': adjusted the training hours.","Answer: To lessen the pressure on students, the coach adjusted the training hours."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q66","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 66","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":66,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["cause-and-effect","factual"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Explain how the English teacher made Nick's \"cheeks burn with embarrassment\" (lines 3–4, paragraph 1, from \"The stinging words\" to \"embarrassment\").","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The English teacher reprimanded Nick for forgetting to bring his homework, and her stinging words still echoed in his mind (he could not forget them)."},"explanation":["Find the phrase in paragraph 1 and read the sentence before it: 'In the morning, he had been reprimanded by his English teacher for forgetting to bring his homework.'","Then read the sentence it is in: 'The stinging words still echoed in his mind, **making** his cheeks burn with embarrassment.' The word 'making' shows cause and effect.","The question carries 2 marks, so give both points: (1) the teacher reprimanded (scolded) him for forgetting to bring his homework; (2) her stinging words still echoed in his mind, so he could not forget them.","'Echoed in his mind' is a figure of speech. It is safest to show you understand it: he kept remembering the words."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q67","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 67","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":67,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["evidence-phrase"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"From the first paragraph, pick out a two-word phrase that tells us Nick's suggestions were ignored.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"brushed aside"},"explanation":["Look in paragraph 1 for the part about Nick's suggestions (his 'ideas'): 'his classmates had barely acknowledged his presence and brushed aside his ideas'.","To 'brush aside' something is to refuse to consider it, like sweeping it away with your hand. That is the same as ignoring it.","'barely acknowledged' is also two words, but it is about his **presence** (they hardly noticed he was there), not his suggestions.","Copy exactly two words: 'brushed aside'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q68","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 68","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":68,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["cause-and-effect","character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The answer boxes on the paper are headed: Two effects the lady's act of kindness had on Nick.\n(Write one effect for each box, (a) and (b).)","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"First effect:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"It brightened his gloomy mood.","accept":["It brightened his gloomy mood","brightened his gloomy mood","It brightened his mood"]},"explanation":["The lady's act of kindness is sharing her umbrella in paragraph 2. Read the last two sentences of that paragraph for what it did to Nick.","'It was a simple gesture, yet it brightened his gloomy mood.' 'It' is the lady's gesture, so this is one effect: his gloomy mood became brighter (he felt more cheerful).","The two effects can be written in either box."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Second effect:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"A warmth stirred in his heart (he felt warm inside / touched).","accept":["A warmth stirred in his heart","a warmth stirred in his heart","A warmth stirred in Nick's heart"]},"explanation":["The other effect is in the sentence before: 'As they crossed the road together, a warmth stirred in Nick's heart.'","This means he felt warm inside, or touched by her kindness.","'Nick nodded gratefully' shows he was thankful, but the two effects the passage spells out are the warmth in his heart and his brightened mood."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q69","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 69","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":69,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["referencing"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Look at the table below. What do the words in the left column refer to in the passage? Write your answers in the column on the right.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"this (line 10, paragraph 2)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The sudden downpour (that began just as Nick stepped off the curb).","accept":["a sudden downpour","the sudden downpour","the downpour","the sudden rain"]},"explanation":["A word like 'this' points back to something already mentioned. Read the sentence just before it.","'Just as he stepped off the curb, a sudden downpour began. Caught off guard by **this**, Nick froze'.","What caught Nick off guard? The sudden downpour. Test by replacing the word: 'Caught off guard by the sudden downpour, Nick froze.' It makes sense.","The answer is the sudden downpour. Include 'sudden': that is why he was caught off guard."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"it (line 13, paragraph 2)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The friendly lady's umbrella.","accept":["the lady's umbrella","the friendly lady's umbrella","her umbrella","the umbrella"]},"explanation":["'Without waiting for a reply, she tilted **it** to shelter him from the rain.'","What can be tilted to shelter someone from rain? Look back: the lady asked, 'Would you like to share my umbrella?'","The tote bag is the nearest noun before 'it', which makes it the trap, but a tote bag slung on a shoulder cannot shelter anyone from the rain.","The answer is the (friendly) lady's umbrella. Say whose umbrella it is."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"discomfort (line 18, paragraph 3)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Nick's damp uniform clinging to his skin.","accept":["his damp uniform clinging to his skin","Nick's damp uniform clinging to his skin"]},"explanation":["'the discomfort' points back to something uncomfortable that was just mentioned. Read the sentence before it.","'... made his way onto the platform, his damp uniform clinging to his skin. Despite the **discomfort**, Nick patiently watched the train approach.'","The uncomfortable thing is the wet uniform sticking to his skin. Give the whole idea, not just 'his uniform'.","The answer is Nick's damp uniform clinging to his skin."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q70","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 70","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":70,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Why was Nick \"burdened\" (line 20, paragraph 3)?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Nick was burdened because he had a stack of assignments awaiting him and he was uncertain about being able to complete all that homework by that night."},"explanation":["Find 'burdened' in paragraph 3: 'trying to ease the uncertainty that filled his **burdened** heart'. A burden is a heavy load, so something was weighing on his mind.","The very next sentence tells us what it was: '\"How can I complete all that homework by tonight?\" he lamented softly.'","Link it back to paragraph 1: 'a stack of assignments awaited him, each one seeming more overwhelming than the last'.","The marker looks for: he was uncertain (worried) about whether he could complete all his homework by that night. Change 'I' to 'he' and 'tonight' to 'that night' when you use the quoted words."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q71","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 71","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":71,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["true-false-with-reason"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on lines 23–30 (paragraph 4), state whether each statement in the table below is true or false, then give one reason why you think so.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"The passengers were worried for the unwell commuter right after he vomited.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. The passengers recoiled in disgust, covering their noses and moving away."},"explanation":["Find what the passengers did right after the commuter vomited: 'Immediately, several passengers recoiled in disgust, covering their noses and moving away.'","'Immediately' matches 'right after he vomited'. To 'recoil in disgust' is to pull back because something is revolting. People who were worried would move towards him to help, not away.","So the statement is False. The reason is the evidence: they recoiled in disgust, covered their noses and moved away."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"The young man assisted the unwell commuter hesitantly.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. The young man stepped forward swiftly and calmly approached the commuter to render assistance."},"explanation":["'hesitantly' means slowly and unsurely, as if not certain whether to act.","The passage says the opposite: 'a young man stepped forward **swiftly**. **Calmly**, he approached the commuter and rendered assistance.' His actions were also 'steady and uncomplaining'.","So the statement is False. The reason must contain the words that show he did not hesitate: swiftly (and calmly)."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"The incident on the train caused Nick to view society more positively.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. Nick thought that perhaps Singaporeans were not as selfish as he had thought and that kindness still existed in society."},"explanation":["Look at Nick's thoughts at the end of the paragraph, after he 'watched in awe': 'Perhaps Singaporeans were not as selfish as he had thought, and kindness still existed in society.'","'not as selfish as he had thought' shows his opinion of people changed for the better, which is what 'view society more positively' means.","So the statement is True. Give his thought as the reason."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q72","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 72","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":72,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["character-and-feelings","inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The \"elderly lady was in distress\" (line 32, paragraph 5).","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"How did Nick feel when he saw the elderly lady in distress?","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Nick felt worried (concerned) for her.","accept":["Nick felt worried","He felt worried","worried","Nick felt concerned","He felt concerned","concerned","Nick felt worried for her","He felt worried for her","Nick felt concerned for her","He felt concerned for her"]},"explanation":["The passage does not name the feeling directly, so look for clues in paragraph 5.","He 'noticed a **concerning** sight'. A concerning sight is one that makes you feel concerned, or worried.","His action agrees: 'Without hesitation, Nick hurried over' to help.","The answer is that Nick felt worried or concerned."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Why did Nick feel this way?","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The elderly lady's electric wheelchair had run out of battery and she was stranded at the side of the path."},"explanation":["The reason for his worry is the trouble the lady was in. Read the sentence after 'an elderly lady was in distress'.","'Her electric wheelchair had run out of battery and she was stranded at the side of the path.'","The marker looks for both ideas: the wheelchair had run out of battery, and she was stranded (stuck and unable to move on)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q73","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 73","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":73,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual","cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Explain why it was difficult to push the wheelchair.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The wheelchair was heavy and its wheels resisted their efforts."},"explanation":["Find the pushing in paragraph 5. Two reasons are given in two different sentences.","First: 'a man who was struggling to push the **heavy** wheelchair forward'. The wheelchair was heavy.","Second: 'It was not easy, **as the wheels resisted their efforts**.' The word 'as' means 'because'. The wheels did not turn easily (the battery was flat, so the wheelchair could not move by itself).","The question carries 2 marks, so give both reasons: the wheelchair was heavy, and the wheels resisted their efforts."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q74","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 74","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":74,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"What impression did the elderly lady have of Nick in lines 38–39 (paragraph 5, from \"With gratitude in her eyes\" to \"You have a kind heart.\")?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"She thought he had a kind heart (he was kind-hearted).","accept":["She thought he had a kind heart","She thought he was kind-hearted","She thought he was kind","She thought that he had a kind heart","She thought Nick had a kind heart","She thought that Nick had a kind heart"]},"explanation":["An 'impression' is the opinion someone forms of a person.","Read what the old lady said to Nick: '\"Thank you, young man.\" Then, she added warmly, \"You have a kind heart.\"'","Her own words give her opinion of him. Turn them into reported speech: she thought he had a kind heart, or that he was kind-hearted.","Do not just write 'she was grateful'. That is how **she** felt, not what she thought of **Nick**."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-b-q75","cite":"2026 Prelim Ai Tong Paper 2 Booklet B Question 75","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Ai Tong","section":"b","number":75,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"What lesson did Nick learn about life that day?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Nick learnt that kindness can change one's outlook, and that although life will always have its challenges, one should face them positively."},"explanation":["The lesson is in the last paragraph, where Nick 'reflected on the events of the afternoon'.","Point 1: 'The kindness he had witnessed, and taken part in, had shifted his perspective.' Kindness can change the way a person sees things. (A day that starts poorly 'did not have to end that way'.)","Point 2: 'Life would always have its challenges', yet he was 'determined to face tomorrow with renewed positivity'. Challenges will always come, but we can choose to face them positively.","The question carries 2 marks, so give both points, and write them as a general lesson about life rather than retelling what happened to Nick."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-p1-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 1 Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"p1","number":1,"topic":"writing","subtypes":["situational-writing"],"format":"structured","marks":14,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Part 1: Situational Writing (14 marks)\nThe pictures below show Ricky and Julie discussing an idea during their school recess. Study the pictures carefully.\n[[img1]]\n++Your Task++\nImagine you are Ricky.\n**Write an email to your principal, Mdm Tan, to request for a reading corner to be set up.**\nYou are to refer to the pictures and information on page 1 for your article. The bullet point underlined below requires you to come up with your own information.\nIn your writing, include the following key information:\n- why you think a reading corner should be set up\n- where the reading corner can be set up\n- two ways you can contribute\n- how your schoolmates may contribute\n- ++another benefit of having the reading corner++\nYou may reorder the points. Remember to write in complete sentences.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There is no single model answer. Check your writing against the points below."},"explanation":["Purpose: to request (ask politely) that a reading corner be set up. State this clearly in the opening paragraph.","Audience: your principal, Mdm Tan. Context: you are Ricky, a pupil of the school, writing an email. Start with 'Dear Mdm Tan' and say who you are (name and class).","Point 1, why a reading corner should be set up: there are often no empty seats in the school library, and it is getting harder to find a quiet, comfortable place to sit and read during recess.","Point 2, where: in the vacant space between the library and the staircase.","Point 3, two ways you can contribute: provide an old bookshelf that is still in good condition (your parents were thinking of giving it away), and design some posters to decorate the reading corner.","Point 4, how schoolmates may contribute: they can donate their old books for others to read.","Point 5, another benefit (your own idea, and it must be different from point 1): for example, it will encourage more pupils to pick up the reading habit, or it lets pupils share and recycle books instead of throwing them away.","Format and tone: formal and polite, because you are writing to the principal. Write in the first person as Ricky ('I'), change the speech bubbles into your own complete sentences, thank Mdm Tan and say you hope she will consider your suggestion. End with 'Yours sincerely' or 'Regards' and your name, Ricky.","Language: 6 marks are for task fulfilment (all the points, purpose, audience, context) and 8 marks for language (grammar, spelling, punctuation, clear sentences)."]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-p1-q1-img1","alt":"Two comic panels showing Julie (a girl) and Ricky (a boy) from behind, standing outside the school LIBRARY. Through the library window two pupils can be seen reading at a table. On the right is a staircase. Panel 1. Julie: Ricky, there are no empty seats in the school library again! Ricky: It's getting harder for us to find a quiet, comfortable place to sit and read during recess. Hmm... Look over there, Julie. Don't you think the vacant space between the library and the staircase would be perfect for a reading corner? Julie: You're right! Panel 2. Ricky: I can provide an old bookshelf which is still in good condition. My parents have been thinking of giving it away. I can also design some posters to decorate the reading corner. Students can even donate their old books for others to read. Julie: Wow, those are great ideas! You should email our principal with your suggestion. Ricky: Thanks, Julie! I'll do that after checking with my parents."}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-p1-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 1 Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"p1","number":2,"topic":"writing","subtypes":["continuous-writing"],"format":"structured","marks":36,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Part 2: Continuous Writing (36 marks)\nWrite a composition of ++at least 150 words++ about **an apology**.\nThe pictures are provided to help you think about this topic.\nYour composition should be based on one or more of these pictures.\n[[img1]]\nConsider the following points when you plan your composition:\n- Why was an apology needed?\n- What happened in the end?\nYou may use the points in any order and include other relevant points as well.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There is no single model answer. Check your writing against the points below."},"explanation":["Understand the topic: an apology is saying sorry for something wrong that was done. The apology must be the heart of the story, not a one-line ending. Decide who apologises, to whom, and for what.","Picture 1 (a boy and a girl standing back to back with arms folded, both scowling): two friends or siblings have quarrelled and neither wants to say sorry first. Who gives in, and what makes them do it?","Picture 2 (a child's drawing of a house, sun and tree, smudged and spoilt): someone ruined another person's artwork, perhaps by spilling water or paint on it, by accident or out of jealousy.","Picture 3 (a bowl tipped over with noodles spilt out): someone knocked over a bowl of noodles, perhaps on a classmate in the canteen, on a stranger at a hawker centre, or a meal that a parent had lovingly cooked.","Use at least one picture and answer both guiding points: why the apology was needed, and what happened in the end.","Story arc: a short opening that sets the scene; the incident (what went wrong); the feelings afterwards (guilt, anger, pride, fear of owning up); the turning point where the character decides to apologise; the apology itself, with the words spoken and how the other person reacts.","Make the apology sincere: show the struggle before it (sweaty palms, rehearsing the words, avoiding eye contact) and an action that goes with it (helping to redo the drawing, buying another bowl of noodles, cleaning up).","Ending: return to the topic. What did the character learn about apologising, for example that saying sorry takes courage, or that a sincere apology can mend a friendship?","Marks: 18 for content (relevant, well-developed ideas) and 18 for language and organisation (paragraphs, varied sentences, vocabulary, grammar, spelling, punctuation). Write well over 150 words."]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-p1-q2-img1","alt":"Three pictures, one above the other. Picture 1: a boy and a girl standing back to back with their arms folded, both frowning angrily. Picture 2: a child's drawing of a house with a sun, a cloud, a tree and a flower; part of the drawing is smudged and spoilt as if something has been spilt on it. Picture 3: a bowl lying on its side with noodles spilt out of it."}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":1,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["question-tags"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"She said that she would attend the rehearsal for the concert later, __________ she?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Rule: a question tag checks the main clause of the sentence. A positive statement takes a negative tag, and the tag uses the helping verb of the main verb.","The main clause is 'She said'. The words 'that she would attend the rehearsal...' only tell us what she said.","'said' is the simple past with no helping verb, so the tag uses 'did'. The statement is positive, so the tag is negative: 'didn't she?'","'wouldn't' is tempting because 'would' is the nearest verb, but it belongs to the reported part, not the main clause. The answer is option (2)."]}],"options":["did","didn't","would","wouldn't"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":2,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Ronn wanted to attend the concert because his favourite singer Brono __________ there.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Think about the order of events. Ronn 'wanted to attend' the concert, so the concert had not happened yet. The performance was still in the future at that time.","Rule: to talk about the future as seen from a point in the past, change 'will' to 'would'. 'Brono will be performing' becomes 'Brono would be performing'.","'performed', 'had performed' and 'had been performing' all mean the performance was already over, so there would be nothing left to attend.","'...because his favourite singer Brono would be performing there.' The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["performed","had performed","had been performing","would be performing"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q3","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 3","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":3,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"__________ her parents' tireless care, she recovered quickly from her illness.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Work out how the two halves are linked. Her parents' tireless care is the reason she recovered quickly. This is cause and effect.","'Owing to' means 'because of' and is followed by a noun phrase ('her parents' tireless care').","'In spite of' shows a contrast (she recovered even though something was against her), which does not fit because good care helps recovery. 'In addition to' only adds another item, and 'Aligned to' means 'in line with'.","'Owing to her parents' tireless care, she recovered quickly...' The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["Owing to","In spite of","Aligned to","In addition to"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q4","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 4","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":4,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["relative-pronouns"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The boy with __________ I shared my lunch is my best friend.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["The blank comes straight after the preposition 'with' and refers to a person, the one who 'is my best friend'.","Rule: after a preposition, use the object form 'whom' for a person: with whom, to whom, for whom. ('I shared my lunch with him' becomes 'with whom I shared my lunch'.)","'who' is the subject form and cannot follow a preposition. 'which' is for things and animals. 'whose' shows belonging and must be followed by a noun (whose lunch).","'The boy with whom I shared my lunch is my best friend.' The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["who","which","whom","whose"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q5","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 5","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":5,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["infinitives-and-gerunds"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"After observing him __________ on the desk a few times, they realised that he was sending a coded message to someone in the room.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Rule: after a verb of the senses (see, hear, watch, observe, notice) + a person, use the base form of the verb for a completed action. Example: 'I watched him cross the road.'","Here the pattern is 'observing him ___'. The second verb does not take a tense or an 's', because 'him' is an object, not the subject of a new sentence.","'tapped' is tempting because the story is in the past, but 'observing him tapped' is wrong. 'taps' would need a subject ('he taps').","'After observing him tap on the desk a few times...' The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["tap","taps","tapped","had tapped"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q6","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 6","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":6,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["tenses","subject-verb-agreement"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Having received the teacher's approval, the prefect committee __________ ready to conduct the activity now.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["The time clue is 'now'. It tells us the sentence is about the present moment, so we need the simple present tense.","'The prefect committee' is one group, so it takes the singular verb 'is'.","'was' is past, and 'will be' is future; neither goes with 'now'. 'would be' is used for imagined situations.","'...the prefect committee is ready to conduct the activity now.' The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["is","was","will be","would be"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q7","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 7","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":7,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Other than Tom, Jerry and __________, no one else was there.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'Other than' works like a preposition (like 'except' or 'apart from').","Rule: after a preposition, use the object pronoun: me, him, her, us, them. We say 'except me', not 'except I'.","Test it by dropping the other names: 'Other than me, no one else was there' sounds right; 'Other than I' does not. 'myself' is only used when 'I' is already the subject of the same clause (I hurt myself), and 'mine' shows belonging.","'Other than Tom, Jerry and me, no one else was there.' The answer is option (2)."]}],"options":["I","me","mine","myself"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q8","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 8","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":8,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"We need to stop searching and rest. We __________ for more than twenty hours once the morning comes.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Look at the time clues: 'for more than twenty hours' (a length of time) and 'once the morning comes' (a point in the future).","Rule: to say how long an action will have gone on by a certain time in the future, use the future perfect continuous: will have been + verb-ing.","'have been searching' is tempting because of 'for more than twenty hours', but it measures up to now, and the sentence measures up to the coming morning. 'will search' would mean the search only starts in the future.","'We will have been searching for more than twenty hours once the morning comes.' The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["will search","have searched","have been searching","will have been searching"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q9","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 9","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":9,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["quantifiers"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Although there was __________ evidence to start with, the police managed to solve the case eventually.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'Evidence' is an uncountable noun (we cannot say 'evidences'), and the verb is the singular 'was'. So 'few' and 'many', which go with countable plural nouns, are out.","'Although' signals a contrast: the police solved the case even though something made it hard. Having almost no evidence makes it hard.","'little' means 'hardly any' and goes with uncountable nouns. 'much' would mean there was a lot of evidence, which gives no contrast with solving the case.","'Although there was little evidence to start with...' The answer is option (2)."]}],"options":["few","little","much","many"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q10","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 10","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":10,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The trouble __________ this storybook is that its language is too difficult to understand.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Some nouns always go with a particular preposition. We say 'the trouble **with**' or 'the problem **with**' something to introduce what is wrong with it.","Example: 'The trouble with this plan is that it costs too much.'","'about' is tempting because we complain about things, but the fixed phrase is 'the trouble with'. 'The trouble of' means the effort of doing something (the trouble of cooking).","'The trouble with this storybook is that its language is too difficult...' The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["of","in","with","about"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q11","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 11","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":11,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"After discussing the matter for over an hour, the judges voted __________ to disqualify the participant.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Clue: several judges 'voted' after a long discussion. We need a word that describes how a group votes.","'unanimously' means with every person agreeing. 'Voted unanimously' is a common pairing: all the judges voted the same way.","'mutually' is tempting because it is about two sides sharing a feeling (they mutually agreed), but it is not used with 'voted'. 'concurrently' means at the same time, and 'intuitively' means by instinct, without thinking it through, which does not fit an hour of discussion.","The answer is option (4), unanimously."]}],"options":["mutually","intuitively","concurrently","unanimously"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q12","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 12","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":12,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"After insisting for hours that he had not broken the vase, Ryan finally __________ that he had knocked it over while chasing the cat.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Clues: 'After insisting for hours that he had not' and 'finally'. Ryan changed from saying no to owning up.","'conceded' means admitted, often unwillingly, that something is true.","'denied' is the opposite; he had been denying it for hours. 'surrendered' is tempting because it means gave up, but we surrender to someone; we do not 'surrender that' something happened.","The answer is option (2), conceded."]}],"options":["denied","conceded","questioned","surrendered"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q13","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 13","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":13,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Corruption is often __________ in countries where laws are not strictly enforced.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Clue: where laws are not strictly enforced, a bad thing like corruption will spread unchecked.","'rampant' describes something bad that is spreading everywhere and is out of control.","'bountiful' also means plentiful, but it is used for good things (a bountiful harvest). 'stringent' means very strict and describes rules, and 'infamous' means well known for something bad, which describes a person or place rather than how common something is.","The answer is option (1), rampant."]}],"options":["rampant","bountiful","stringent","infamous"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q14","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 14","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":14,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The discipline master quickly __________ the fight between Eddie and Dylon before anyone got injured.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Clue: he acted 'before anyone got injured', so he stopped the fight.","'break up a fight' means to stop it by separating the people.","'broke off' means ended something suddenly, such as talks or an engagement, or snapped a piece off. 'broke down' means stopped working or started crying. 'broke away' means escaped from someone's hold.","The answer is option (1), broke up."]}],"options":["broke up","broke off","broke down","broke away"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q15","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 15","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":15,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"After the competition, the players from every team showed __________ by gathering together to sing at the closing ceremony.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Clue: players from every team, who had just been rivals, gathered and sang together. They were showing that they stood together as one.","'solidarity' means unity and support among people who share a feeling or purpose.","'consensus' is tempting, but it means a general agreement reached about a decision. 'tolerance' means putting up with people who are different, which is weaker than standing together. 'alignment' means being arranged in a line or in agreement with a plan.","The answer is option (1), solidarity."]}],"options":["solidarity","tolerance","alignment","consensus"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q16","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 16","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":16,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++assume++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["To 'assume' is to accept that something is true without checking or having proof.","In the passage, many people simply take it for granted that help will come quickly when they call an emergency number. They 'believe' it.","'infer' is tempting, but it means to work something out from clues or evidence, which is the opposite of taking it for granted. 'deem' means to judge something to be (deem it necessary), and 'reason' means to think something through step by step.","The answer is option (4), believe."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g1","options":["infer","deem","reason","believe"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q17","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 17","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":17,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++potentially++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["'potentially' means something could happen or could be true, but it is not sure.","Finding a crew 'can be a potentially lengthy process': it may take long, and the next sentence says the ambulance 'may take' two to three hours. 'possibly' matches.","'certainly' is too strong, because it means it is always lengthy. 'apparently' means 'as it seems' or 'as I have heard', and 'figuratively' means not in the real, exact sense.","The answer is option (1), possibly."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g1","options":["possibly","certainly","apparently","figuratively"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q18","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 18","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":18,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++compiles++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["To 'compile' is to collect information from different places and put it together.","The app 'compiles all the available information on ambulances onto a single platform'. 'collates' means gathers pieces of information and arranges them together, so it matches.","'connects' is tempting because an app links people to ambulances, but the word here is about gathering information, not joining things. 'constructs' means builds, and 'concentrates' means focuses or gathers in one small area.","The answer is option (1), collates."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g1","options":["collates","connects","constructs","concentrates"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q19","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 19","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":19,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++expertise++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'expertise' is the special skill and knowledge a person has in a particular field, gained through training and experience.","Users choose a service based on 'the expertise of the crew', that is, what the ambulance crew knows how to treat. 'knowledge' is the closest.","'talent' is tempting, but talent is a natural gift you are born with, while expertise is learnt. 'reputation' is what others think of you, and 'accomplishment' is something you have achieved.","The answer is option (3), knowledge."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g1","options":["talent","reputation","knowledge","accomplishment"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q20","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 20","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":20,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++navigate++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["To 'navigate' is to find a way through a difficult place skilfully.","The app helps ambulances 'navigate through the city's traffic jams'. 'manoeuvre' means to move skilfully and carefully around obstacles, so it matches.","'meander' is tempting because it also describes moving along a route, but it means to wander slowly with no hurry, which is the opposite of what an ambulance does. 'muddle' means to mix up or confuse, and 'manipulate' means to control or handle something, often unfairly.","The answer is option (4), manoeuvre."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g1","options":["muddle","meander","manipulate","manoeuvre"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q21","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 21","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":21,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"What is the main purpose of the webpage?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Look at the heading and the biggest boxes of the webpage (Text 1).","The heading says 'Enjoy watching them, but do not feed them', and the main box is titled 'Why we should not feed wildlife', with four reasons.","'refrain from' means to stop yourself from doing something, so option (4) says the same thing as the webpage. Options (1) and (2) are the opposite of its message, and the webpage does not say where wildlife can be found.","The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g2","options":["to teach people how to feed wildlife","to encourage people to keep wildlife as pets","to explain where wildlife can be found in Singapore","to explain why people should refrain from feeding wildlife"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q22","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 22","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":22,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference","purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The webpage shows images of a variety of wildlife to __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Ask why the designer drew so many different animals: a monitor lizard, an otter, monkeys, a tortoise, squirrels and a bird.","The webpage's message is 'do not feed them'. Showing many kinds of animals tells readers that 'them' means all wildlife, not just one animal such as monkeys.","Option (1) is tempting because the pictures do show animals found here, but the webpage never mentions parks, and showing animals is not its aim; its aim is the advice. Nothing on the webpage says which animals are friendly or which to avoid.","The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g2","options":["show the fauna of Singapore's parks","show which animals are the friendliest to visitors","help readers decide which animals they should avoid","remind readers that the advice applies to many kinds of wildlife"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q23","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 23","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":23,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the webpage, what may happen if people feed wildlife?\nA. Wildlife may help reduce pests such as rats.\nB. Animals may stop searching for their own food.\nC. Wildlife may become unwell from eating food from humans.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Check each statement against the box 'Why we should not feed wildlife' in Text 1.","B is true: 'Animals may stop searching for their own food.' C is true: 'Human food is not suitable for wildlife and can make them ill' ('ill' means unwell).","A is false: the webpage says 'Leftover food attracts pests such as rats', so feeding brings more pests, not fewer.","Only B and C are correct. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g2","options":["A and B","A and C","B and C","A, B and C"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q24","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 24","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":24,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"According to Ms Hazel Yoon's advice in Text 2, visitors should __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Find Ms Yoon's answer to 'What should visitors do when they come across wildlife?' in Text 2.","She says: 'If an animal appears injured or behaves in an unusual manner, report it to the relevant authorities so that trained officers can help.' 'Odd' means the same as 'unusual'.","(1) and (3) go against 'Observe the animals quietly from a safe distance and avoid touching or feeding them.' (2) is wrong because trained officers, not visitors, should help injured animals.","The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g2","options":["bring suitable food for wild animals","tend to injured animals themselves","move closer only if the animals seem friendly","report any odd animal behaviour to authorities"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-a-q25","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet A Question 25","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"a","number":25,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["factual","inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Which idea is found in both Text 1 and Text 2?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["The idea must appear in the webpage and in the interview.","Text 1: 'Let wildlife find its own food.' Text 2: 'Wild animals are healthiest when they continue to look for food in their natural environment instead of relying on humans to feed them.' Both say the same thing.","(2) is tempting, but only Text 1 mentions rubbish bins. (4) is wrong because Text 2 says to report only injured animals or unusual behaviour, not every sighting, and Text 1 does not mention reporting. Neither text says wildlife will attack people.","The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g2","options":["Wildlife will attack people if they are injured.","Throwing rubbish into bins keeps parks clean.","Wildlife should be allowed to find their own food.","Visitors should report all wildlife sightings to the authorities."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q26","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 26","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":26,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["infinitives-and-gerunds"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (26). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"N","accept":["n","to"]},"explanation":["Look at the verb before the blank: 'tend'. After the blank is a base verb, 'experience'.","Rule: 'tend' is followed by to + base verb (tend to forget, tend to be late). It means 'usually do'.","'People who read regularly tend to experience less stress.'","The answer is (N) to."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q27","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 27","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":27,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms","subject-verb-agreement"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (27). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"L","accept":["l","is"]},"explanation":["Find the subject of the sentence: 'The calming effect created from reading'. The words between the commas ('similar to relaxation exercises') only add information.","The sentence has no main verb yet, so the blank must be a verb: 'The calming effect ... ___ what helps our body'.","'effect' is singular and the passage is in the present tense, so we need 'is', not 'are'.","The answer is (L) is."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q28","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 28","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":28,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (28). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"K","accept":["k","in"]},"explanation":["This is a fixed phrase: to play a role (or a part) **in** something.","After a preposition, the verb takes -ing, which matches 'keeping' after the blank.","'Reading also plays an important role in keeping the brain active.'","The answer is (K) in."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q29","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 29","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":29,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms","modals"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (29). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"J","accept":["j","have"]},"explanation":["After the blank comes 'to remember'. 'have to' + base verb means 'must' or 'need to'.","The subject is 'they' (plural) and the passage is in the present tense, so we use 'have', not 'had'.","'As readers follow a story, they have to remember details...'","'had' is tempting because it also fits before 'to', but it is past tense and the passage states general facts in the present. The answer is (J) have."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q30","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 30","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":30,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (30). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"E","accept":["e","even"]},"explanation":["The sentence says reading keeps the brain flexible and sharp, and then adds something surprising: this is true ___ as people grow older, when the brain usually slows down.","'even' is used to stress something unexpected. 'even as' means 'at the very time that'.","'...reading helps the brain stay flexible and sharp, even as people grow older.'","The answer is (E) even."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q31","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 31","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":31,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (31). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"G","accept":["g","from"]},"explanation":["This is a fixed phrase: to see something **from** a perspective, or from a point of view.","A perspective is the place you look from, so the preposition is 'from'.","'...see situations from different perspectives.'","The answer is (G) from."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q32","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 32","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":32,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (32). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"M","accept":["m","this"]},"explanation":["The blank is the subject of a new sentence, and the verb 'improves' is singular.","We need a singular word that points back to the whole idea in the sentence before (understanding emotions and seeing situations from different perspectives). 'This' does that job.","'This improves empathy and social understanding.'","The answer is (M) this."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q33","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 33","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":33,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (33). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"D","accept":["d","by"]},"explanation":["The blank shows the way or method of 'staying mentally and emotionally grounded': how is it done? By reading widely.","Rule: by + verb-ing tells us how something is done (He learnt by practising).","'...staying mentally and emotionally grounded by reading widely can be beneficial.'","'with' is tempting, but 'with' is followed by a tool or a thing (with a pen), not by an action. The answer is (D) by."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q34","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 34","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":34,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (34). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"B","accept":["b","an"]},"explanation":["'amount' is a singular countable noun, so it needs an article in front of 'extended amount of time'.","The next word, 'extended', begins with a vowel sound, so we use 'an', not 'a'.","'...one does not need to read for an extended amount of time...'","The answer is (B) an."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q35","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 35","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":35,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (35). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"P","accept":["p","whether"]},"explanation":["After the blank there are two choices joined by 'or': 'through printed books **or** audiobooks'.","Rule: whether ... or ... means 'it does not matter which of the two'.","'Whether through printed books or audiobooks, reading remains a simple habit...'","The answer is (P) whether."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q36","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 36","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":36,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (36) ++helping++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"help"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. The pattern is 'insisted that Lily ___'.","Rule: after verbs that give an order or strong request (insist, demand, suggest, recommend) + that + person, use the base form of the verb, with no -s, -ed or -ing: 'She insisted that he go.' 'They demanded that she apologise.'","'helping' cannot stand alone as the verb of the clause; it would need 'was' in front.","The correct word is 'help': 'her grandmother always insisted that Lily help in the kitchen'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q37","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 37","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":37,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (37) ++irellevant++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"irrelevant"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error.","The word is made of the prefix ir- (meaning 'not') + relevant. Keep both r's: ir + relevant = irrelevant.","There is only one 'l': rel-e-vant.","The correct word is 'irrelevant', which means not connected or not useful to the matter."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q38","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 38","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":38,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (38) ++This recipes++. (Only one of the two underlined words is wrong. Type the corrected word.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"These","accept":["these recipes"]},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. 'recipes' is plural, and the verb 'carry' is plural too.","Rule: 'this' goes with one thing (this recipe); 'these' goes with more than one (these recipes).","So 'This' must change to 'These'. The noun 'recipes' is already correct.","The correct word is 'These': 'These recipes carry our family's history'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q39","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 39","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":39,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (39) ++telling++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"tells"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. The sentence 'Each ingredient ___ a story' needs a proper verb, and 'telling' alone is not one (it would need 'is').","Grandmother is stating a general truth, so use the simple present tense.","Rule: 'each' + singular noun takes a singular verb, so add -s: 'Each ingredient tells'.","The correct word is 'tells'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q40","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 40","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":40,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (40) ++rilaktantly++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"reluctantly"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. Sound it out: re-luc-tant-ly.","The word comes from 'reluctant' (unwilling). It starts with 're', has 'luc' in the middle with a 'c', not a 'k', and ends with -tant + ly.","The clue 'Lily rolled her eyes' shows she joined in unwillingly.","The correct word is 'reluctantly'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q41","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 41","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":41,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (41) ++genuwin++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"genuine"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word sounds like 'jen-yoo-in' but is spelt g-e-n-u-i-n-e.","There is no 'w', and it ends with a silent 'e', like 'engine' and 'imagine'.","'genuine interest' means real, sincere interest.","The correct word is 'genuine'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q42","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 42","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":42,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (42) ++blanned++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"bland"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word is an adjective describing how the food tasted, not a verb, so it does not end in -ed.","It is spelt as it sounds: b-l-a-n-d, with one 'n'.","'bland' means having little taste or flavour.","The correct word is 'bland'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q43","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 43","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":43,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (43) ++nostaljik++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"nostalgic"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word comes from 'nostalgia', which is spelt with a 'g' (nos-tal-gia).","The ending is -ic, as in 'magic' and 'allergic', not -ik.","'nostalgic' means longing for happy times in the past.","The correct word is 'nostalgic'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q44","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 44","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":44,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (44) ++Until++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"When","accept":["as","once","after"]},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong connector is used.","'Until' means 'up to the time that', which would mean the tears stopped when she tasted the dumplings. The story means the opposite: the tears came at the moment she tasted them.","For two things that happen at the same moment, use 'When'.","The correct word is 'When': 'When Lily tasted the dumplings, tears filled her eyes.'"]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q45","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 45","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":45,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (45) ++warm++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"warmth"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong word form is used.","After 'the' and before 'of', we need a noun: 'the ___ of home and love'. 'warm' is an adjective.","The noun form of 'warm' is 'warmth' (like strong and strength, deep and depth).","The correct word is 'warmth'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q46","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 46","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":46,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (46) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"needed","accept":["required"]},"explanation":["Read the sentence before: Singapore 'faced a serious problem' as air travel became more popular.","The problem was that the old airport was not big enough, so the country had to have 'a bigger airport to handle more planes and passengers'.","The passage is in the past tense ('faced', 'became'), so the verb is 'needed', not 'needs'.","The answer is 'needed'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q47","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 47","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":47,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms","modals"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (47) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"could","accept":["would","should"]},"explanation":["This is a question with the word order where + helping verb + they + base verb: 'where ___ they build it?'","'build' is in its base form, so the blank must be a modal verb. The story is in the past, so use a past form.","Singapore has 'limited land space', so the question is about where it was possible to build: 'where could they build it?'","The answer is 'could'. ('would' and 'should' also fit.)"]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q48","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 48","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":48,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (48) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"for"},"explanation":["The noun 'demand' takes the preposition 'for': the demand **for** something.","Examples: the demand for electric cars, the demand for water.","'the demand for air travel around the world rose dramatically'.","The answer is 'for'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q49","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 49","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":49,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (49) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"travelled","accept":["traveled","journeyed"]},"explanation":["The blank is a verb for what 'more people' did 'by air for business and holidays'.","We 'travel by air', 'travel by bus', and so on. The clause is in the past tense like 'rose', so add -ed.","In British spelling the 'l' is doubled: travelled.","The answer is 'travelled'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q50","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 50","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":50,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (50) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"keep","accept":["catch"]},"explanation":["Look at the words around the blank: 'struggled to ___ up with the increase in traffic volume'.","'keep up with' means to manage to go as fast as something that is growing or moving quickly.","After 'to' the verb stays in its base form.","The answer is 'keep': the old airport could not keep up with the growing number of flights."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q51","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 51","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":51,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (51) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Such"},"explanation":["After the blank is 'an airport'. Only a few words can come before 'a' or 'an' + noun: such, what, quite, many.","'Such an airport' means 'an airport like the one just mentioned', the new airport in Changi from the sentence before.","It begins the sentence, so it takes a capital letter.","The answer is 'Such'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q52","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 52","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":52,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (52) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"instead"},"explanation":["Two places are compared: planes would fly 'over water' and not 'over residential areas'.","The word after the blank is 'of'. 'instead of' means 'in place of'.","'planes would fly over water instead of over residential areas', so there would be less noise for people living nearby.","The answer is 'instead'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q53","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 53","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":53,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (53) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"addition"},"explanation":["The sentence gives one more benefit ('there was also the possibility of future expansion') on top of 'the earlier benefits mentioned'. The word 'also' is a clue.","The fixed phrase 'In addition to' means 'as well as' or 'besides'.","'In addition to the earlier benefits mentioned, there was also...'","The answer is 'addition'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q54","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 54","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":54,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (54) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"bigger","accept":["larger"]},"explanation":["The word 'than' after the blank tells us this is a comparison, so we need a comparative adjective ending in -er.","'five times ___ than the airport at Paya Lebar' compares size. The first paragraph already spoke of 'a bigger airport'.","'bigger' (or 'larger') fits.","The answer is 'bigger'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q55","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 55","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":55,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (55) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"build","accept":["construct"]},"explanation":["'To ___ the new airport, existing land had to be cleared and new land had to be reclaimed.' Clearing and reclaiming land are steps taken in order to put up the airport.","The paragraph begins with 'Building Changi Airport', which gives the word away.","After 'To' the verb is in its base form: build.","The answer is 'build' ('construct' is also right)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q56","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 56","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":56,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (56) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Despite"},"explanation":["The two halves contrast: the project was 'fraught with challenges' (full of problems), yet it 'still finished ahead of schedule'. The word 'still' is a clue.","The blank is followed by a verb-ing ('being'), so we need 'Despite', which takes a noun or verb-ing. 'Although' would need a subject and verb (Although it was fraught...).","'In spite of' means the same but is three words, and only one word is allowed.","The answer is 'Despite'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q57","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 57","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":57,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (57) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"off"},"explanation":["'The hard work paid ___' is a phrasal verb. 'pay off' means to bring good results after a lot of effort.","The proof of success follows: 'the move to Changi Airport happened in just one night'.","'The hard work paid off.'","The answer is 'off'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q58","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 58","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":58,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (58) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"started","accept":["began","commenced"]},"explanation":["The sentence gives the first day of the new airport: 'on 1 July 1981 with the arrival of' its first flight, 'marking the beginning of a new era'.","The blank is followed by a verb-ing ('operating'), and 'start' or 'begin' can be followed by -ing.","It happened in the past, so use the past tense: started or began.","The answer is 'started'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q59","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 59","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":59,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (59) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Since"},"explanation":["Look at the tense of the verb that follows: 'has expanded' (present perfect), which describes something from a past time up to now.","'Since then' means 'from that time until now', and it goes with the present perfect.","'Since then, Changi Airport has expanded significantly, from one terminal to four.'","The answer is 'Since'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q60","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 60","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":60,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (60) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"overcome","accept":["surmounted","conquered"]},"explanation":["What can be done to 'even the most difficult challenges' with 'foresight and perseverance'? They can be beaten or dealt with successfully.","The word that pairs with 'challenges' and 'difficulties' is 'overcome'.","After 'can be' we need the past participle (passive voice). The past participle of 'overcome' is 'overcome' (overcome, overcame, overcome), not 'overcame'.","The answer is 'overcome'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q61","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 61","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":61,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["no-sooner-than","inversion"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"As soon as the dog bit the stranger, its owner apologised profusely.\nNo sooner ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"No sooner had the dog bitten the stranger than its owner apologised profusely.","accept":["No sooner had the dog bitten the stranger than its owner apologized profusely."]},"explanation":["'No sooner ... than ...' means the second action happened immediately after the first, just like 'as soon as'.","When a sentence starts with 'No sooner', the helping verb comes before the subject (inversion): No sooner **had** the dog ..., not 'No sooner the dog had'.","The first action goes into the past perfect: had + past participle. The past participle of 'bite' is 'bitten' (bite, bit, bitten), not 'bit'.","Join the second action with 'than' (not 'then' or 'when') and keep it in the simple past: 'than its owner apologised profusely'. Drop 'As soon as'.","Answer: No sooner had the dog bitten the stranger than its owner apologised profusely."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q62","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 62","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":62,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["passive-voice"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The wind blew the papers all over the classroom.\nThe papers ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The papers were blown all over the classroom by the wind.","accept":["The papers were blown by the wind all over the classroom."]},"explanation":["The sentence now starts with the thing that received the action ('The papers'), so it must be in the passive voice: be + past participle + by + doer.","Tense stays the same: 'blew' is simple past, and 'papers' is plural, so the verb 'be' is 'were'.","The past participle of 'blow' is 'blown' (blow, blew, blown), not 'blew' or 'blowed'.","Put the doer at the end with 'by': 'by the wind'.","Answer: The papers were blown all over the classroom by the wind."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q63","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 63","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":63,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["noun-form","preposition-gerund"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"She considered the offer carefully for a few days before accepting it.\nAfter a few days of ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"After a few days of careful consideration, she accepted the offer.","accept":["After a few days of careful consideration of the offer, she accepted it.","After a few days of considering the offer carefully, she accepted it.","After a few days of carefully considering the offer, she accepted it."]},"explanation":["'of' is a preposition, so it must be followed by a noun (or a verb-ing), not by 'she considered'.","Change the verb 'considered' to its noun form 'consideration'. The adverb 'carefully' described the verb, so it becomes the adjective 'careful' to describe the noun: 'careful consideration'.","The order of events flips: 'before accepting it' becomes the main clause 'she accepted the offer', in the simple past. Say 'the offer' so that it is clear what she accepted.","Put a comma after the opening phrase.","Answer: After a few days of careful consideration, she accepted the offer. (Also correct: After a few days of considering the offer carefully, she accepted it.)"]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q64","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 64","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":64,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["reported-speech"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"\"Did you seek Mrs Tan's help in this matter?\" my mother asked me.\nMy mother asked me ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"My mother asked me if I had sought Mrs Tan's help in that matter.","accept":["My mother asked me whether I had sought Mrs Tan's help in that matter."]},"explanation":["This is direct speech changed to reported speech. A yes/no question is reported with 'if' or 'whether', uses statement word order, has no 'did', and ends with a full stop.","Tense moves one step back: simple past 'Did you seek' becomes past perfect 'had sought'. The past participle of 'seek' is 'sought' (seek, sought, sought).","Pronouns change to fit the speaker: 'you' is the person telling the story, so it becomes 'I'.","Pointing words move further away: 'this matter' becomes 'that matter'.","Answer: My mother asked me if I had sought Mrs Tan's help in that matter."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q65","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 65","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":65,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["inversion","preposition-gerund"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"You can succeed if you work hard and stay disciplined.\nOnly by ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Only by working hard and staying disciplined can you succeed."},"explanation":["'by' is a preposition, so the verbs after it take -ing: 'working hard and staying disciplined'. Change both verbs, and drop 'if you'.","When a sentence starts with 'Only by ...', the helping verb comes before the subject in the main clause (inversion): '**can you** succeed', not 'you can succeed'.","No comma is needed before 'can', and the sentence ends with a full stop, not a question mark, even though the word order looks like a question.","Answer: Only by working hard and staying disciplined can you succeed."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q66","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 66","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":66,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual","inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on the first paragraph, what tells you that the library is \"seriously old\" (line 4, paragraph 1)?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The paint on the towering bookshelves had flaked away in places, exposing the dark wood beneath, and cobwebs occupied the forgotten corners of the high ceiling."},"explanation":["The question is worth 2 marks, so give two pieces of evidence from paragraph 1, the sentences just before Sam says 'This place is seriously old'.","Evidence 1: 'The paint on the towering bookshelves had flaked away in places, exposing the dark wood beneath.' Paint only peels off after many years.","Evidence 2: 'Cobwebs occupied the forgotten corners of the high ceiling.' Cobwebs show the corners had been left untouched for a long time.","Do not use the scratched tables; they are in paragraph 2, and the question says 'based on the first paragraph'. The heavy wooden door, sunlight and tall windows do not show age."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q67","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 67","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":67,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"With evidence from lines 7-18 (paragraphs 2 and 3), state a difference between Maya's school library and the Seaside Library.\n(Write one answer for each library. The two answers must be about the same thing.)","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Maya's school library","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The tables in Maya's school library were clean and smooth."},"explanation":["Find a sentence in paragraph 2 that compares the two libraries. The word 'Unlike' signals a difference: 'Unlike the clean and smooth tables in the school library, the wooden tables here bore countless scratches and stains from decades past.'","For the school library, write the first half: its tables were clean and smooth.","Another correct pair from paragraph 3: at the school library Maya searched for books by typing into a search engine on a computer screen. Whichever point you choose, your Seaside Library answer must match it."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Seaside Library","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The wooden tables in the Seaside Library bore countless scratches and stains from decades past."},"explanation":["This is the other half of the same comparison: 'the wooden tables here bore countless scratches and stains from decades past'. 'here' means the Seaside Library.","The two answers must be about the same thing (tables with tables), so that they show a difference.","If you wrote about searching for books in (a), the matching answer is: the Seaside Library had no computers, and books were searched for with a card catalogue, drawer by drawer and card by card."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q68","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 68","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":68,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual","cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"According to Mrs Chen, what made the Seaside Library less popular?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Information could be found easily on the internet, so fewer people needed to visit the library."},"explanation":["Find what Mrs Chen says about visitors in paragraph 4: 'We don't get many visitors these days since information can be found easily on the internet.'","'don't get many visitors' means the same as 'less popular', and 'since' introduces the reason.","The marker looks for: information can be found easily on the internet. Do not write that the library was old or empty; that is not the reason Mrs Chen gives."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q69","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 69","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":69,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["evidence-phrase","character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"From the fourth paragraph, pick out a three-word phrase that tells us that Mrs Chen was saddened that fewer people visited the Seaside Library nowadays.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"eyes grew wistful","accept":["mrs chen's eyes grew wistful","mrs chens eyes grew wistful"]},"explanation":["Look in paragraph 4 right after Mrs Chen says 'We don't get many visitors these days'.","The next sentence is 'Mrs Chen's eyes grew wistful.' 'wistful' means sad and longing for something from the past.","The question asks for exactly three words, so copy only 'eyes grew wistful'. Copy the words exactly; do not change or add any.","'sighed in disappointment' is also three words about a sad feeling, but it describes Maya, not Mrs Chen."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q70","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 70","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":70,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["referencing"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Look at the table below. What do the words in the left column refer to in the passage? Write your answers in the column on the right.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"one (line 13, paragraph 3)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"one of the tiny drawers (of the huge wooden cabinet)"},"explanation":["Read the sentences around line 13: 'a huge wooden cabinet with hundreds of tiny drawers ... Mrs Chen slid one open and pulled out a small handwritten card.'","'one' stands for a noun mentioned just before. What can be slid open? A drawer.","The marker looks for: a tiny drawer, or one of the tiny drawers. Do not write 'the cabinet' or 'a card'."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"this (line 17, paragraph 3)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"searching for books using the card catalogue, drawer by drawer and card by card"},"explanation":["Read line 17 with the sentence before it: 'this was how everyone searched for books – drawer by drawer, card by card.\" Maya tried to imagine doing this every single day'.","'doing this' points back to the action just described: searching for books drawer by drawer, card by card.","The marker looks for the action of searching for books by going through the drawers and cards. Do not write 'typing into a search engine'; that is what she does 'instead'."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"them (line 30, paragraph 6)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"the local residents who wrote the letters during World War II"},"explanation":["Read line 30 with the sentence before it: 'Sam found a collection of handwritten letters from local residents during World War II ... He did not know any of them, had never heard their names before'.","'them' are people, because they have names and they wrote the words. So 'them' are the local residents who wrote the letters, not the letters themselves.","The marker looks for: the local residents (who wrote the letters during World War II)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q71","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 71","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":71,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["true-false-with-reason"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on lines 7-31 (paragraphs 2 to 6), state whether each statement in the table below is true or false, then give one reason why you think so.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Maya had planned to visit the Seaside Library during the school holidays. (Write True or False, then your reason.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. Maya stumbled across the library while exploring the older part of town, so she had not planned to visit it."},"explanation":["Find how Maya first came to the library, at the start of paragraph 2: 'Stumbling across this forgotten library while exploring the older part of town during the school holidays'.","To 'stumble across' something is to find it by chance. That is the opposite of planning.","The marker looks for: False, with the reason that she stumbled across (or upon) the library while exploring the older part of town. Both the True/False and the reason must be right to get the mark."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Maya knew what the huge wooden cabinet was for. (Write True or False, then your reason.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. Maya had a look of confusion when she paused in front of the cabinet, and Mrs Chen had to explain that it was a card catalogue."},"explanation":["Find the cabinet in paragraph 3: 'Maya paused in front of a huge wooden cabinet with hundreds of tiny drawers. Looking at Maya's look of confusion, Mrs Chen chuckled.'","A 'look of confusion' shows that she did not know what it was for. Mrs Chen then had to explain: 'This is a card catalogue.'","The marker looks for: False, with the evidence of Maya's look of confusion (or that Mrs Chen had to explain it to her)."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"The collection of handwritten letters that Sam found was well protected. (Write True or False, then your reason.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. The letters were carefully preserved in a glass case."},"explanation":["Find the letters in paragraph 6: 'a collection of handwritten letters from local residents during World War II, carefully preserved in a glass case'.","'carefully preserved' means kept safe from damage, and a glass case keeps hands and dust away. That matches 'well protected'.","The marker looks for: True, with the reason that they were carefully preserved in a glass case."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q72","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 72","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":72,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"What was the \"magic of holding a real book\" (line 36, paragraph 7)? Explain clearly how Maya's experience in the Seaside Library helped her understand that.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The magic of holding a real book was that one does not just hold the book but shares the memories, emotions and traces of the people who held it before. Maya understood this when she held the old books in the library and found herself wondering about the people who had held the same book before her, and she felt a warm sense of connection to them, such as when she saw Eleanor's name written in 1943."},"explanation":["There are two questions here, 1 mark each: what the 'magic' was, and how Maya's experience made her understand it.","What the magic was: go back to Mrs Chen's words in paragraph 4: 'You don't just hold the book, you share the memories, emotions and traces of the people who held it before.'","How Maya came to understand: in paragraph 7 she 'found herself wondering about the people who had held this same book before her'. In paragraph 5 she found 'Eleanor, 1943' in a book and 'felt a warm sense of connection'. She was now sharing in the traces of earlier readers herself.","The marker looks for: sharing the memories, emotions and traces of people who held the book before (1 mark); Maya wondered about, or felt connected to, the people who had held the book before her (1 mark)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q73","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 73","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":73,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual","cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Complete the diagram. The top box says \"Why the Seaside Library was closing\", and two arrows lead from it to two empty boxes, (a) and (b).\n(Write one reason in each box.)","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Reason (a)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"It was too expensive to maintain."},"explanation":["Find where the closing is mentioned, in paragraph 8: 'The council wants to close this library next month ... They say it's too expensive to maintain, and not enough people use it.'","The council gives two reasons joined by 'and'. The first is the cost: it was too expensive to maintain (to keep in good condition).","The two reasons can be written in either box."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Reason (b)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Not enough people used it."},"explanation":["The second reason in the same sentence: 'not enough people use it'.","This matches what Mrs Chen said earlier: 'We don't get many visitors these days.'","Write the two reasons in separate boxes; do not put both in one box."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q74","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 74","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":74,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["cause-and-effect","character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on lines 37-43 (paragraphs 8 and 9), explain clearly what caused Maya's heart to sink.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Mrs Chen told Maya that the council wanted to close the library the following month. Maya's heart sank because the thought of all the treasures she had found there, such as Eleanor's name, the old maps and the wartime letters, disappearing made her feel that she was losing something that could never be replaced."},"explanation":["'Maya's heart sank' means she suddenly felt very sad and dismayed. Look at what comes just before and just after those words in paragraph 8.","Before (what she heard): 'The council wants to close this library next month.'","After (why it upset her): 'The thought of all the treasures she found disappearing ... made her feel like she was losing something that could never be replaced.'","'Explain clearly' for 2 marks needs both: the library was going to be closed (1 mark), and she feared its treasures, which could never be replaced, would disappear (1 mark)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-b-q75","cite":"2026 Prelim Catholic High Paper 2 Booklet B Question 75","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Catholic High","section":"b","number":75,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Explain clearly why Mrs Chen said, \"Perhaps there's still a future for old places like this\" (lines 42-43, paragraph 9).","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Maya had promised to come back the next day and to bring her friends too. This gave Mrs Chen hope that more people would visit and use the library, so that it might not have to close."},"explanation":["Look at what happened just before Mrs Chen spoke: '\"We'll be back tomorrow,\" Maya promised. \"And we'll bring our friends too.\" Mrs Chen's face brightened with hope.'","Link this to why the library was closing: 'not enough people use it'. If Maya, Sam and their friends came, there would be more visitors.","So 'a future for old places like this' means the library might be able to stay open.","The marker looks for: Maya promised to return with her friends (1 mark), so Mrs Chen hoped more visitors would use the library and it could remain open (1 mark)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":1,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["question-tags"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"They've never been to the theatres in Singapore, __________?\" Aunt Mona asked.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Rule: the question tag uses the helping verb of the statement. 'They've' is short for 'They **have**', so the tag uses 'have', not 'did'.","Rule: a positive statement takes a negative tag, and a negative statement takes a positive tag.","'never' makes the statement negative, even though there is no 'not'. So the tag must be positive: 'have they'.","'haven't they' is tempting because the verb 'have' looks positive, but 'never' already carries the 'not'. The answer is option (2)."]}],"options":["did they","have they","didn't they","haven't they"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":2,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The detective was __________ the verge of giving up when he discovered the hidden clue in a dusty diary.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'on the verge of' is a fixed phrase. It means very close to doing something.","For example: 'She was on the verge of tears.'","A 'verge' is an edge, and we stand **on** an edge, which helps to remember the preposition.","'The detective was on the verge of giving up...' The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["in","by","on","with"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q3","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 3","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":3,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["infinitives-and-gerunds"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"When the old man dropped his groceries, several kind strangers helped him __________ them up from the pavement.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Rule: help + person + base form of the verb (or 'to' + base form). Example: 'She helped me carry the bags.'","'helped' already shows the past tense, so the second verb is not put into the past again.","'picked' is tempting because the event is in the past, but 'helped him picked' is wrong.","'...several kind strangers helped him pick them up...' The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["pick","picks","picked","picking"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q4","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 4","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":4,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"By the time the school bell rang, all the students __________ their art projects and placed them on display.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["There are two past actions: the bell rang, and the students finished their projects. 'By the time' tells us the finishing happened first.","Rule: for the earlier of two past actions, use the past perfect: had + past participle.","'had finished ... and (had) placed' fits. 'have finished' is present perfect and cannot go with the past 'rang'.","'finished' is tempting, but 'By the time' signals that one action was already complete before the other. The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["finish","finished","had finished","have finished"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q5","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 5","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":5,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"__________ the noise from the construction work nearby, the children managed to concentrate on their lessons.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Look at how the two halves are linked: there was noise, yet the children still managed to concentrate. That is a contrast, something unexpected.","'In spite of' + noun shows that something happened even though there was an obstacle.","'Owing to' and 'As a result of' both give a cause, but noise does not cause concentration.","'Contrary to' means 'opposite to what was said or believed' (contrary to popular belief), which does not fit. The answer is option (2)."]}],"options":["Owing to","In spite of","Contrary to","As a result of"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q6","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 6","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":6,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["quantifiers"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Even though he practised for weeks before the school concert, Marcus still had __________ confidence when he walked onto the stage to perform his solo.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'Even though' and 'still' signal a surprise: he practised a lot, but the result was not what we expect. So he had almost no confidence.","'confidence' is an uncountable noun. 'little' goes with uncountable nouns and means 'almost no'.","'few' has the same meaning but is used only with countable plural nouns (few friends).","'some' and 'much' would mean he was confident, which is no surprise after weeks of practice. The answer is option (2)."]}],"options":["few","little","some","much"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q7","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 7","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":7,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Emma giggled softly when she saw her grandfather alone in the garden, talking to __________ while watering the flowers.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Who was talking? Her grandfather. He was 'alone', so the only person he could be talking to was himself.","Rule: when the doer and the receiver of an action are the same person, use a reflexive pronoun (-self).","Grandfather is male, so the pronoun is 'himself', not 'herself'.","'her' is tempting because Emma is nearby, but he was alone and did not know she was watching. The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["her","him","herself","himself"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q8","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 8","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":8,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["infinitives-and-gerunds"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Jenna could not stop __________ when she saw her little brother trying to pull his hand out of the cookie jar.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Rule: stop + verb-ing means to end an action that is going on. 'She could not stop laughing' means the laughing went on and on.","'stop to laugh' has a different meaning: to pause what you are doing in order to laugh. That does not fit 'could not stop'.","'laugh' (base form) cannot follow 'stop' directly.","'Jenna could not stop laughing...' The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["laugh","to laugh","laughing","having laughed"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q9","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 9","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":9,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["comparatives"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Among all the books in the library, this mystery novel is definitely __________ one I have ever read.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clues: 'Among all the books' and 'I have ever read'. One book is being compared with many others, so we need the superlative.","Rule: the superlative of a long adjective is 'the most' + adjective: the most exciting.","'the more exciting' is the comparative, used when comparing only two things.","'...this mystery novel is definitely the most exciting one I have ever read.' The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["an exciting","a very exciting","the most exciting","the more exciting"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q10","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 10","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":10,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["subject-verb-agreement"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Where __________ the pair of glasses that I placed on the desk this morning?\" wondered Mrs Lim.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Find the subject: 'the pair of glasses'. The head word is 'pair', which is singular, so the verb is singular. That rules out 'are' and 'were'.","Tense: Mrs Lim is looking for the glasses now, so the question is in the present tense: 'Where is...?'","'was' is tempting because of 'placed' and 'this morning', but those describe when she put them down, not when she is asking.","'Where is the pair of glasses...?' The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["is","are","was","were"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q11","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 11","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":11,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"A fight __________ on the football field when two students started quarrelling over the football.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clue: the fight started when the two students began quarrelling.","'break out' means to start suddenly. It is used for fights, fires, wars and diseases.","'broke up' is tempting because we say a teacher 'broke up the fight', but that means stopped it. 'broke off' means stopped suddenly or snapped off, and 'broke down' means stopped working.","The answer is option (3), broke out."]}],"options":["broke up","broke off","broke out","broke down"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q12","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 12","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":12,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning","collocation"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"After months of practice, Maria became __________ playing the piano and could perform complex pieces without making mistakes.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clues: 'After months of practice' and 'could perform complex pieces without making mistakes'. She became very good at it.","'proficient at' means skilled at something through practice.","'familiar with' is tempting, but it only means knowing something fairly well, not being skilled at it. 'certified in' means holding a certificate, which practice alone does not give.","The answer is option (3), proficient at."]}],"options":["certified in","inspired by","proficient at","familiar with"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q13","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 13","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":13,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Sarah looked __________ at the tall diving board, worried about jumping into the deep water for the first time.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Clue: she was 'worried about jumping into the deep water for the first time'.","'apprehensively' means in a worried or nervous way about something that is going to happen.","'quizzically' means in a puzzled, questioning way. 'reproachfully' means in a way that shows blame, and 'cynically' means in a distrusting way.","The answer is option (4), apprehensively."]}],"options":["cynically","quizzically","reproachfully","apprehensively"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q14","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 14","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":14,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The firefighters worked hard on the __________ of the wildfire to stop it from spreading to nearby homes.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Clue: the firefighters wanted 'to stop it from spreading'. They were fighting to put the fire down.","'suppression' means stopping something by force. 'Fire suppression' is the usual term for putting out or holding back a fire.","'reduction' is tempting, but it only means making something smaller, and it is not the word used for fighting fires. 'restriction' is a rule that limits, and 'omission' means leaving something out.","The answer is option (4), suppression."]}],"options":["omission","reduction","restriction","suppression"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q15","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 15","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":15,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The storm began to __________ as the wind grew stronger and the rain became heavier throughout the afternoon.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Clue: 'the wind grew stronger and the rain became heavier'. The storm was getting stronger.","'intensify' means to become stronger or more extreme.","'accelerate' is tempting, but it means to go faster, and the clue is about strength, not speed. 'maximise' needs an object (maximise something), and 'progress' only means to move forward.","The answer is option (1), intensify."]}],"options":["intensify","progress","maximise","accelerate"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q16","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 16","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":16,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++formidable++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'formidable' describes something so strong or difficult that it is hard to deal with.","Clue in the passage: the opposing team 'had remained undefeated all season', so their defence was very hard to beat.","'tough' means strong and difficult to get past, so it is the closest.","'frightening' is tempting because a formidable opponent can seem scary, but the point here is that the defence was hard to beat, not that Wei Han was afraid. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g1","options":["fiery","tough","frightening","tremendous"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q17","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 17","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":17,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++conscientiously++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["'conscientiously' means carefully and with great effort, because you want to do something well.","Clue: he practised 'every afternoon under the scorching sun', which shows steady hard work.","'diligently' means in a hard-working and careful way, so it matches.","'precisely' means exactly, 'frantically' means in a rushed, panicky way, and 'haphazardly' (without any plan) is the opposite. The answer is option (1)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g1","options":["diligently","precisely","frantically","haphazardly"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q18","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 18","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":18,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++resilience++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'resilience' is the ability to keep going and bounce back when things are hard.","Clue: 'Despite his aching limbs', it 'kept him sprinting'. He refused to give up even though he was in pain.","'persistence' means continuing to try in spite of difficulty, so it is the closest. The last sentence agrees: 'determination was the key to success'.","'stamina' is tempting, but it is physical energy to keep going, and his limbs were already aching; what kept him going was his will. 'resistance' looks similar but means fighting against something. The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g1","options":["stamina","efficiency","resistance","persistence"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q19","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 19","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":19,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++noticed++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'noticed' means saw something and became aware of it.","'observed' means saw or noticed, so it can replace the word directly: 'he observed the goalkeeper leaning slightly to the left'.","'glimpsed' is tempting because it happened quickly, but a glimpse is a very short, incomplete look. 'spied' suggests watching secretly.","'detected' is used for finding something hidden or hard to sense (detect a smell, detect a fault). The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g1","options":["spied","detected","glimpsed","observed"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q20","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 20","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":20,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning","phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++going for++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["To 'go for' a shot is to try to make it, to carry it out.","'executing' means carrying out a planned move. 'Pretending he was executing a high shot' keeps the meaning: he acted as if he was making a high shot, then slid the ball low instead.","'pursuing' is tempting because 'go for' can mean 'chase after' (go for gold), but you do not chase a shot. We say 'strike a ball', not 'strike a shot'.","'launching' is close, but it means sending something off, while 'going for' is about attempting the move. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g1","options":["striking","pursuing","executing","launching"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q21","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 21","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":21,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The purpose of the statement \"Carry Everything in Style\" in Text 1 is to __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Split the slogan into its two ideas.","'Carry Everything' says the bag can hold all you need, so it is useful. 'in Style' says it looks good, so it is trendy.","Option (2) covers both ideas. Option (1) is tempting but covers only 'Carry Everything' and leaves out 'in Style'.","The slogan is not an order about looks, and it does not list what to bring. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g2","options":["emphasise the bag's storage capacity","highlight that the bag is useful and trendy","command students to care about how they look","remind students about what they must bring to school"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q22","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 22","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":22,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience","inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Ansel was quoted in Text 1 because __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Compare what Ansel says with what the advertisement says.","Advertisement: 'Fit your entire school day into one bag' and 'a specific space for everything'. Ansel: 'Everything I need for school fits comfortably into it. I can also find and take out any item I need easily!'","His words repeat the advertisement's claims in a real user's voice, which makes readers trust them. Advertisers use such quotes (testimonials) for this reason.","(2) is false because the bag is 'Suitable for students of all ages'. The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g2","options":["he appears to be a good student","all school bag users are around his age","his review was short enough to fit into the poster","what he said supports the claims made in the poster"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q23","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 23","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":23,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["factual","inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Based on Text 1, which of the following statements are true about the Peaks school bag?\nA: The bag is small and light.\nB: The bag comes in different colours.\nC: The bag has multiple compartments.\nD: The bag is only suitable for primary school students.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Check each statement against the advertisement.","B is true: 'Wide range of colours to choose from!' C is true: 'Get organised with a specific space for everything', which means the bag has many separate sections.","A is not supported: the bag fits 'your entire school day', so it is roomy, and nothing says it is light. D is false: it is 'Suitable for students of all ages!'","The true statements are B and C. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g2","options":["A and B","B and C","C and D","A and D"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q24","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 24","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":24,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"What is the **main** message of Text 2?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["The main message is the idea that the whole text builds up to, not one detail.","Paragraph 2 warns that 'Carrying too many items strains your back'. Paragraph 3 ends with 'you can protect your back and still have everything you need'.","Choosing a bag with wide straps, packing light and using a locker are all ways to do one thing: protect your back. That is option (1).","Option (4) is tempting, but it is only one example ('For example, store certain items in your school locker'). The answer is option (1)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g2","options":["Students should take measures to protect their backs.","Students should choose school bags with more features.","Students should express their personality and personal style.","Students should use lockers or desks to store their belongings."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-a-q25","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet A Question 25","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"a","number":25,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Text 1 and Text 2 express differing views on whether __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Look for a point on which the two texts disagree.","Text 1 encourages it: 'Fit your entire school day into one bag: all your books, stationery, lunch boxes and more!'","Text 2 advises against it: 'While it may seem convenient to fit everything into one bag, have you considered the weight?' and 'health experts recommend packing only the items needed for the day'.","Option (3) is tempting, but Text 2 never says a bag cannot hold everything; it says you **should** not carry everything. The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g2","options":["students can store certain items in school","school bags should look appealing to students","school bags can fit everything needed for school","students should fit everything into their school bags"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q26","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 26","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":26,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (26). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"J","accept":["j","is"]},"explanation":["The subject is 'The mystery behind fruit ripening'. The head word is 'mystery', which is singular.","After the blank is 'still not fully understood by scientists'. The mystery does not understand anything; it is understood by people. That is the passive voice: be + past participle.","'still' tells us this is true now, so we need the present tense 'is', not 'was'.","The answer is (J) is."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q27","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 27","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":27,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (27). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"G","accept":["g","if"]},"explanation":["The sentence gives a condition: fruits bruise easily in one situation, namely when they are left to ripen fully on the tree.","'if left to ripen' is a short form of 'if they are left to ripen'.","The connector for a condition is 'if'.","The answer is (G) if."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q28","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 28","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":28,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (28). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"E","accept":["e","has"]},"explanation":["After the blank is 'been known', so we need has/have to make the present perfect passive.","Find the subject: 'The flesh of these fruits'. The head word is 'flesh', which is singular. 'of these fruits' only describes it.","A singular subject takes 'has'. 'have' is tempting because 'fruits' is right next to the blank.","The answer is (E) has."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q29","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 29","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":29,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (29). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"L","accept":["l","the"]},"explanation":["The blank comes before the noun phrase 'ripening process', so it needs an article or determiner.","The passage has already been talking about fruit ripening, so the reader knows which process is meant. For something specific and already known, use 'the'.","'this' is tempting, but it is needed later, and 'the ripening process' is the phrase the passage repeats.","The answer is (L) the."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q30","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 30","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":30,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (30). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"P","accept":["p","where"]},"explanation":["The words after the blank ('the seeds develop') describe 'the parts'.","The parts are a place inside the fruit. To add information about a place, use 'where'.","'which' is tempting, but it would need a preposition: 'the parts in which the seeds develop'.","The answer is (P) where."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q31","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 31","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":31,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (31). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"D","accept":["d","can"]},"explanation":["The blank is between the subject 'workers' and the base verb 'slow'. A helping verb fits here.","'can' + base verb shows that the workers are able to do this.","No other helping verb in the list works: 'has' and 'have' need a past participle (slowed), and 'is' and 'was' do not fit a base verb.","The answer is (D) can."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q32","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 32","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":32,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (32). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"C","accept":["c","by"]},"explanation":["The words after the blank say how the workers slow down ripening: 'keeping fruits cool and reducing the amount of ethylene'.","Rule: by + verb-ing tells us the method used to do something.","For example: 'She saved money by walking to school.'","The answer is (C) by."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q33","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 33","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":33,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (33). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Q","accept":["q","which"]},"explanation":["The words after the blank ('have already released a lot of ethylene') describe 'a few ripe bananas'.","Bananas are things, so the relative pronoun is 'which'. ('who' is for people.)","The verb 'have' is plural because it agrees with 'bananas'.","The answer is (Q) which."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q34","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 34","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":34,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (34). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"B","accept":["b","an"]},"explanation":["The blank comes before 'unripe mango', one mango that has not been mentioned before, so we need 'a' or 'an'.","'unripe' begins with a vowel sound, so the article is 'an'.","'a' is used before consonant sounds (a ripe banana).","The answer is (B) an."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q35","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 35","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":35,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (35). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"K","accept":["k","of"]},"explanation":["'in the hope of' is a fixed phrase. It means wishing that something will happen.","It is followed by a verb-ing: 'in the hope of using this knowledge'.","The answer is (K) of."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q36","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 36","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":36,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (36) ++volentiering++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"volunteering"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means offering to do something without being forced.","Start from the base word 'volunteer': vol-un-teer. The middle is 'un', and the ending is '-eer' (like engineer, career).","Add -ing: volunteer + ing.","The correct word is 'volunteering'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q37","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 37","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":37,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (37) ++by++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"with"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error (wrong preposition).","'to be tasked **with** something' means to be given a job to do.","For example: 'The prefects were tasked with keeping the hall quiet.'","The correct word is 'with'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q38","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 38","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":38,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (38) ++enviromantle++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"environmental"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means to do with nature and our surroundings.","Start from the base word 'environment'. Remember the 'n' before 'ment': en-vi-ron-ment.","Add the suffix -al to make the adjective: environment + al. The ending is '-tal', not '-tle'.","The correct word is 'environmental'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q39","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 39","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":39,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (39) ++are++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"was"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error with two things to fix.","Agreement: 'everyone' is singular, so it cannot take 'are'.","Tense: the story is told in the past ('The principal had also made it clear that...'), so the verb must be past: was expecting.","The correct word is 'was'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q40","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 40","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":40,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (40) ++skrold++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"scrolled"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means moved through text or pictures on a screen.","The base word is 'scroll': the 'sk' sound is spelt 'sc' (like screen, scratch), and it ends in double 'l'.","Add -ed for the past tense: scroll + ed.","The correct word is 'scrolled'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q41","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 41","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":41,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (41) ++begin++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"begun"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error (verb form). Look at the helping verb in front: 'had ___'.","Rule: had + past participle makes the past perfect tense.","The verb changes like this: begin (present), began (past), begun (past participle). After 'had' we need 'begun', not 'began'.","The correct word is 'begun'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q42","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 42","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":42,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (42) ++sprauling++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"sprawling"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word describes something that spreads out over a large area.","The 'or' sound in the middle is spelt 'aw' (like crawl, shawl): sprawl.","Add -ing: sprawl + ing.","The correct word is 'sprawling'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q43","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 43","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":43,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (43) ++Althought++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"However"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error (wrong connector), not just a spelling slip.","The word starts a new sentence and is followed by a comma, and the sentence after it is complete on its own. 'Although' cannot be used this way, because it must join two clauses in one sentence.","The sentence is a contrast with the one before: Jia Wei wanted to find out the facts, but his friends cared more about posters. The connector that starts a contrasting sentence and takes a comma is 'However'.","The correct word is 'However'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q44","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 44","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":44,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (44) ++konsept++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"concept"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means an idea or plan.","Both hard and soft sounds are spelt with 'c': the first 'c' sounds like 'k' and the second sounds like 's' (con-cept).","Related words help: conception, accept, except.","The correct word is 'concept'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q45","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 45","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":45,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (45) ++what++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"how"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error (wrong word).","The word comes before the adverb 'easily'. To show degree before an adjective or adverb, we use 'how': how easily, how quickly, how tall.","'what' is used before a noun: what a pity, what a surprise.","The correct word is 'how'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q46","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 46","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":46,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (46) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"not"},"explanation":["Clue: Zubir 'grew up in a poor household'. In a poor home there is too little money.","The word 'but' sets up a contrast: money was lacking, but music was always there.","'money was always not enough' gives that meaning.","The answer is 'not'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q47","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 47","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":47,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (47) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"As"},"explanation":["The phrase '___ a teenager' tells us when he left home: at the time he was a teenager.","'As a teenager' means 'when he was a teenager'. Compare: 'As a child, I loved drawing.'","It starts the sentence, so use a capital letter.","The answer is 'As'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q48","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 48","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":48,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (48) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"ambition","accept":["dream","aspiration","goal","wish","hope"]},"explanation":["The sentence says what he wanted to be: 'to become a professional musician'.","We need a noun for something a person strongly wants to achieve. 'from childhood' shows he had held it for a long time.","'ambition' fits. 'dream' and 'aspiration' mean the same.","The answer is 'ambition'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q49","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 49","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":49,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (49) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"job"},"explanation":["The sentence describes paid work: 'playing the violin and piano in silent movie theatres'. The next sentence says 'The work was tiring and the pay was low'.","'The first ___ he had' needs a countable noun for a piece of paid work.","'work' is uncountable, so 'the first work he had' is wrong.","The answer is 'job'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q50","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 50","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":50,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","idioms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (50) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"life"},"explanation":["'bring something to life' is a fixed phrase. It means to make something lively and exciting.","Silent movies had no sound, so the live music made the film feel alive for the audience.","The answer is 'life'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q51","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 51","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":51,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (51) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"write","accept":["compose","create","produce"]},"explanation":["After 'invited him to' we need a base-form verb whose object is 'a special song'.","Zubir was a composer, and the same paragraph later says 'to write the perfect song'.","'write' fits. 'compose' means the same and is also correct.","The answer is 'write'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q52","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 52","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":52,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (52) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"accepted","accept":["undertook","embraced"]},"explanation":["He was invited to do a task. What did he do with the invitation? 'readily' means willingly and without delay.","To agree to take on a task is to 'accept' it. The story is in the past tense, so: accepted.","The next sentence shows he started work on it straight away.","The answer is 'accepted'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q53","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 53","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":53,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (53) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"this","accept":["it","that","they","these"]},"explanation":["The blank is the subject of 'would inspire him', so it must be a pronoun.","What did he hope would inspire him? Walking along the crowded streets and quiet kampungs. 'this' points back to that whole action.","'they' (the streets and kampungs) also makes sense.","The answer is 'this'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q54","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 54","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":54,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (54) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"wanted","accept":["intended","wished","meant","expected"]},"explanation":["Look at the pattern: He ___ the song **to capture** the aspirations of a young nation. This is verb + object + to-infinitive.","The sentence tells us his aim for the song. 'wanted the song to capture' fits the pattern and the past tense.","'hoped' is tempting, but it does not fit the pattern: we say 'hoped that the song would', not 'hoped the song to'.","The answer is 'wanted'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q55","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 55","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":55,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (55) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"anthem","accept":["melody","tune","song","piece","composition"]},"explanation":["The blank is the thing he 'composed', which people 'could remember and sing with pride'. It is a piece of music.","It is described as 'clear and rousing' (stirring), and the passage is about the national anthem.","'anthem' fits. 'melody', 'tune' and 'song' are also correct.","The answer is 'anthem'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q56","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 56","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":56,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (56) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"adopted","accept":["chosen","selected","declared","recognised","recognized","designated","picked"]},"explanation":["The song 'impressed the leaders', so afterwards it was officially taken as the national anthem.","'was later ___ as' is the passive voice, so we need a past participle.","'adopted' means formally chosen and accepted. 'chosen' and 'selected' also fit.","The answer is 'adopted'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q57","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 57","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":57,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (57) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"never"},"explanation":["'Although' signals a contrast between the two halves of the sentence.","Second half: he 'kept improving his musical technique by listening carefully to other composers'. He taught himself.","So the first half must say he did not have formal training: he never studied at prestigious music schools.","The answer is 'never'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q58","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 58","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":58,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (58) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"should","accept":["must"]},"explanation":["The blank is followed by base verbs: '___ not only **sound** grand but also **encourage** citizens'. A modal verb goes before a base verb.","The sentence gives Zubir's belief about what a national anthem ought to do. The modal for what is right or ideal is 'should'.","'must' also fits.","The answer is 'should'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q59","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 59","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":59,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (59) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"ceremonies","accept":["events","celebrations","occasions","festivities"]},"explanation":["'like the Primary 5 National Education (NE) Show' gives an example of the blank, so we need a plural noun for that kind of occasion.","The anthem is sung at formal national occasions.","'ceremonies' fits. 'events' and 'celebrations' are also correct. It must be plural because there is no 'a' and it is joined to 'assemblies'.","The answer is 'ceremonies'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q60","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 60","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":60,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (60) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"reminded","accept":["aware","mindful","appreciative","conscious"]},"explanation":["Why do students sing the anthem? 'so that they are ___ of the sacrifices and dreams that helped build Singapore'.","The word must go with 'of'. Singing the anthem brings those sacrifices back to mind: 'reminded of'.","'aware of' and 'mindful of' also fit.","The answer is 'reminded'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q61","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 61","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":61,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["none-of"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"None of the plates in the sink is clean.\nAll ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"All the plates in the sink are dirty.","accept":["All of the plates in the sink are dirty."]},"explanation":["'None ... is clean' means every single plate is not clean. Starting with 'All' turns the sentence from negative to positive, so the adjective changes to its opposite: clean becomes dirty.","Agreement: 'All the plates' is plural, so the verb changes from 'is' to 'are'.","Do not write 'All the plates in the sink are not clean'. That can also mean only some are not clean, so the meaning is no longer exact.","Answer: All the plates in the sink are dirty. ('All of the plates' is also accepted.)"]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q62","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 62","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":62,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["however-adjective"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The chef works very hard, but he cannot perfect the new recipe.\nHowever ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"However hard he works, the chef cannot perfect the new recipe.","accept":["However hard the chef works, he cannot perfect the new recipe."]},"explanation":["Here 'However' is not followed by a comma. The pattern is However + adjective or adverb + subject + verb, and it means 'no matter how'.","Take the adverb 'hard' and put it right after 'However'. Drop 'very' and 'but': However hard he works, ...","Put a comma, then the main clause. Make sure 'the chef' is named once so the reader knows who 'he' is.","Answer: However hard he works, the chef cannot perfect the new recipe. ('However hard the chef works, he cannot perfect the new recipe.' is also correct.)"]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q63","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 63","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":63,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["it-is-only"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The ice cubes are placed in the container after you break them up.\nIt is only ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"It is only after you break up the ice cubes that they are placed in the container.","accept":["It is only after you break the ice cubes up that they are placed in the container."]},"explanation":["The pattern is: It is only + time phrase + **that** + main clause. It stresses when something happens.","The time phrase is 'after you break them up'. It now comes first, so 'them' must be replaced by the noun: after you break up the ice cubes.","Add 'that', then the main clause, changing the noun to a pronoun: that they are placed in the container.","Keep the present tense. Answer: It is only after you break up the ice cubes that they are placed in the container."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q64","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 64","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":64,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["reported-speech","passive-voice"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Father reminded Lisa, \"Do your homework before meeting your friends.\"\nLisa was reminded ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Lisa was reminded by Father to do her homework before meeting her friends.","accept":["Lisa was reminded by her father to do her homework before meeting her friends."]},"explanation":["Two changes are needed: active to passive, and direct speech to reported speech.","Passive: Lisa is now the subject, so the doer goes after 'by': Lisa was reminded by Father ...","A command in reported speech becomes to + base verb: 'Do your homework' becomes 'to do her homework'.","Pronouns change to fit Lisa: 'your' becomes 'her' both times. No quotation marks are needed.","Answer: Lisa was reminded by Father to do her homework before meeting her friends."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q65","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 65","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":65,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["noun-form"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Many were absent from swimming lessons during the rainy season.\nThere was high ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There was high absenteeism from swimming lessons during the rainy season."},"explanation":["After the adjective 'high' we need a noun, so the adjective 'absent' must change to a noun form.","'Many were absent' is about how often or how many people stayed away. The noun for that is 'absenteeism', and it can be 'high' or 'low'.","'absence' is tempting, but we do not say 'high absence'. Check the spelling: absent + ee + ism.","Keep the rest of the sentence. Answer: There was high absenteeism from swimming lessons during the rainy season."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q66","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 66","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":66,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["evidence-phrase"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Pick out a four-word phrase from the first paragraph which tells us that Maya's grandfather cherished the vegetable garden.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"apple of his eye"},"explanation":["To 'cherish' something is to love it and care about it very much.","Paragraph 1: the garden 'was truly the **apple of his eye**'. This idiom means the thing a person loves most.","Count exactly four words and copy them without changing anything: apple (1) of (2) his (3) eye (4). Leave out 'the', or the phrase has five words.","The answer is 'apple of his eye'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q67","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 67","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":67,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual","cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Give two reasons why Maya's mother was concerned for her when Maya asked to take care of the garden.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Taking care of the garden would be a big responsibility, as plants need daily attention, and it would be an uphill (difficult) task for Maya."},"explanation":["Find Mother's reply in paragraph 2, right after 'Her mother gave her a concerned look'.","Reason 1 (1 mark): 'This would be a big responsibility, Maya. Plants need daily attention, you know?'","Reason 2 (1 mark): 'It'll be an uphill task for you.' An uphill task is a very difficult one.","Give both reasons clearly, and answer as a statement, not as Mother's question."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q68","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 68","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":68,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["character-and-feelings"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Circle one word that best describes how Maya felt when she saw her grandfather's plants wilting. DO NOT CIRCLE MORE THAN ONE WORD.\nfrustrated / guilty / dismayed / anxious\n(Type the word you would circle.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"dismayed"},"explanation":["Find the moment in paragraph 1: 'Maya's heart sank as she saw her dear grandfather's plants beginning to wilt'.","When your 'heart sinks', you suddenly feel sad and disappointed. 'dismayed' means upset and disappointed by something unpleasant.","'anxious' is tempting, but it means worried about what might happen; a sinking heart shows sadness. She had done nothing wrong, so she was not 'guilty', and nothing was blocking her efforts yet, so she was not 'frustrated'.","The answer is 'dismayed'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q69","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 69","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":69,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["sequencing"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write 1, 2 and 3 in the blanks below to indicate the order in which the events occurred in the story.\n_____ Maya's grandfather fell ill and was hospitalised.\n_____ Mrs Chen taught Maya how to take care of plants.\n_____ Maya decided to take care of her grandfather's plants.\n(Type the three numbers in the order of the blanks, from top to bottom.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"1, 3, 2","accept":["132","1 3 2","1,3,2"]},"explanation":["Find each event in the passage and note its paragraph.","Grandfather fell ill and stayed in hospital: paragraph 1. This is first.","Maya asked her mother if she could take care of the garden: paragraph 2. This is second.","Mrs Chen came and explained about watering: paragraphs 4 and 5. This is third.","Reading the blanks from top to bottom, the answer is 1, 3, 2."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q70","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 70","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":70,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["referencing"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Look at the table below. What do the words in the left column refer to in the passage? Write your answers in the column on the right.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"it (line 27, paragraph 6)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The aromatic herbs growing noticeably faster after Sandie and Maya talked to them."},"explanation":["Read the whole sentence: 'Sandie discovered that the aromatic herbs grew noticeably faster after they talked to them, though Maya suspected **it** was just a coincidence.'","A coincidence is two things happening together by chance. What did Maya think was only chance? The herbs growing faster after the girls talked to them.","'it' refers to a whole event, so the answer needs both halves: the herbs grew faster, and this was after Sandie and Maya talked to them. Writing only 'the herbs' is not enough.","The answer is the herbs growing faster after Sandie and Maya talked to them."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"her treatment (line 31, paragraph 7)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Maya sprinkling the soap solution that she had prepared onto the affected (lettuce) leaves."},"explanation":["Read the sentence before it: Maya 'prepared a soap solution which she sprinkled onto the affected leaves'.","Then: 'the tiny green invaders disappear completely after her **treatment**'. The treatment is what she did to get rid of the aphids.","Say what was done and to what: the soap solution sprinkled onto the affected leaves.","The answer is Maya sprinkling the soap solution onto the affected leaves."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q71","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 71","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":71,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual","cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":4,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on the information from lines 10–27 (paragraphs 3 to 6), fill in the following table. Write in complete sentences.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"What Maya did when she saw the lettuce leaves look droopy","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Maya called her best friend, Sandie, for advice."},"explanation":["The droopy lettuce is at the end of paragraph 3. Read on to see what Maya did next.","Paragraph 4: 'Feeling extremely discouraged, Maya called her best friend, Sandie, for advice.'","The marker looks for: called Sandie, for advice. Write a complete sentence."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"How Sandie responded","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Sandie offered to ask her grandmother for her opinion and call Maya back immediately."},"explanation":["Sandie's reply is in paragraph 4: 'My grandmother grows vegetables too! Let me ask her for her opinion and I will call you back immediately.'","Turn her spoken words into a statement: she offered to ask her grandmother and to call Maya back.","The marker looks for both actions: ask her grandmother, and call Maya back."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"What Mrs Chen taught Maya about watering plants","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Mrs Chen taught Maya that different plants need different amounts of water. (For example, the soil for lettuce should not be too wet.)"},"explanation":["Mrs Chen's lesson is in paragraph 5: 'different plants need different amount of water'.","She gave an example: 'the soil for lettuce should not be too wet'.","The marker looks for: different plants need different amounts of water."]},{"label":"d","prompt":"How Maya responded (to what Mrs Chen taught her)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Maya took Sandie's grandmother's advice to heart, taking special care not to overwater the plants, especially the lettuce."},"explanation":["Look at what Maya did after the lesson, in paragraph 6.","'Maya took Sandie's grandmother's advice to heart, taking special care not to overwater the plants, especially the lettuce.'","The marker looks for the action: she took special care not to overwater the plants."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q72","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 72","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":72,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Explain clearly what Mrs Chen meant when she said that \"Rome wasn't built in a day, and neither is a beautiful garden.\" (line 22, paragraph 5)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Mrs Chen meant that it takes time and patience to grow a beautiful garden, just as the great city of Rome took a long time to build."},"explanation":["'Rome wasn't built in a day' is a saying. Rome was a great city that took a very long time to build, so the saying means that good things cannot be rushed.","The clue is the sentence just before it: 'Gardening requires patience.'","Apply the saying to the garden: 'neither is a beautiful garden' means a beautiful garden also cannot be made quickly.","The marker looks for two ideas: time and patience (1 mark), and that these are needed to grow a beautiful garden (1 mark). Do not just repeat the saying."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q73","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 73","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":73,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["true-false-with-reason"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on lines 10–32 (paragraphs 3 to 7), state whether each statement in the table below is true or false, then give one reason why you think so.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Maya had no experience taking care of plants before looking after her grandfather's garden.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. Maya had watched her grandfather taking care of his garden countless times but had never done it herself."},"explanation":["Paragraph 3: 'Although she had watched her grandfather's practised movements countless times, doing it herself the first time felt like an arduous task.'","'the first time' shows she had only watched before and had never done the work herself. She was also 'not sure how much water each plant actually needed'.","So the statement is True. The reason must be evidence from the passage, not the statement repeated."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Maya solved the aphid problem with help from Sandie and Mrs Chen.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. Maya solved the problem on her own: she researched natural remedies online and prepared a soap solution which she sprinkled onto the affected leaves."},"explanation":["Paragraph 7 describes the aphids. Look at who did the actions: '**she** researched effective natural remedies online and prepared a soap solution which **she** sprinkled onto the affected leaves'.","Sandie and Mrs Chen are not mentioned in this paragraph at all. The trap is that they did help earlier, with watering and weeding.","So the statement is False, and the reason is that Maya solved it herself by researching remedies and making the soap solution."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"Mrs Chen was encouraging when she spoke to Maya.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. She told Maya that she was doing remarkably well."},"explanation":["Paragraph 5: Mrs Chen's first words were 'You're doing remarkably well, dear'.","She praised Maya before correcting her, and she 'explained patiently'. Praise like this gives a person confidence, which is what 'encouraging' means.","So the statement is True, and the reason is that she told Maya she was doing remarkably well."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q74","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 74","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":74,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","factual"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Maya's grandfather told her that she had done \"something wonderful\" (line 35, paragraph 8).\nWhat did Maya do that was wonderful?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Maya had taken care of her grandfather's garden while he was in hospital, keeping all the plants healthy and thriving and making the garden more beautiful than ever."},"explanation":["He said this while 'pointing at the plot of land', so the wonderful thing is about the garden.","What she did (1 mark): she took care of his garden while he was in hospital.","The result (1 mark): he returned 'to find his garden more beautiful than ever' and 'saw the healthy plants'. Paragraph 7 says the garden went 'from wilting to thriving under her dedicated care'.","Give both the action and its result."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-b-q75","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ OLGC Paper 2 Booklet B Question 75","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ OLGC","section":"b","number":75,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"How had Mother's opinion of Maya changed by the end of the story?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"At first, Mother doubted that Maya could take care of her grandfather's garden. At the end of the story, she was convinced that Maya was responsible and capable of taking care of the garden."},"explanation":["A 'how did it change' question needs both sides: the opinion before (1 mark) and the opinion after (1 mark).","Before (paragraph 2): Mother 'gave her a concerned look' and asked 'Are you sure you want to do it?', saying 'It'll be an uphill task for you.' She doubted that Maya could manage.","After (paragraph 10): when Grandfather asked Maya to be his gardening partner, 'Her mother nodded and gave her a thumbs-up.' She now approved and believed Maya was capable and responsible.","Use signal words such as 'At first ... At the end ...' so the marker can see the change."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-p1-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 1 Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"p1","number":1,"topic":"writing","subtypes":["situational-writing"],"format":"structured","marks":14,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The pictures below show Ricky and Emily discussing an accident that happened during their school recess. Study the pictures carefully.\n[[img1]]\n++Your Task++\nImagine you are Ricky from Primary 6E.\n**Write a report to your principal, Mdm Tan, to inform her about an accident that happened in school.**\nYou are to refer to the pictures and information on page 2 for your report. The bullet point underlined below requires you to come up with your own information.\nIn your report, include the following key information:\n- what happened to Avery Lim\n- what caused the accident\n- when the accident happened\n- where it happened\n- ++one suggestion you have to prevent such an accident++\n- why something must be done\nYou may reorder the points. Remember to write in complete sentences.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There is no single model answer. Check your writing against the points below."},"explanation":["Purpose: to inform the principal about the accident (and to suggest how to prevent another one). State the purpose in your opening sentence.","Audience: Mdm Tan, your principal. Context: you are Ricky from Primary 6E, a prefect, writing about an accident in school.","Point 1, what happened to Avery Lim: she slipped and fell (and was injured; Emily helped her to the General Office).","Point 2, what caused the accident: someone left the water cooler running, so there was a puddle on the floor.","Point 3, when it happened: on 12 August 2026, during recess.","Point 4, where it happened: at the water cooler near the library (beside the staircase), as shown in the pictures.","Point 5, your own suggestion (the underlined point): one sensible idea, for example putting up a reminder sign at the water cooler, placing a non-slip mat there, or having prefects check the cooler during recess.","Point 6, why something must be done: so that the school can be a safer place for all students and staff.","Format and tone: a formal report or email. Start with 'Dear Mdm Tan', use polite, formal language (no contractions or slang), write in complete sentences and paragraphs, and sign off with 'Yours sincerely' / 'Yours respectfully', then 'Ricky' and 'Primary 6E'.","Do not copy Emily's speech as if you saw the accident yourself: Ricky heard about it from Emily, so report it accurately in the past tense."]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-p1-q1-img1","alt":"Two comic panels dated 12 August 2026. A girl (Emily) and a boy (Ricky) stand near a water cooler with a puddle around it, between the school library and a staircase. Panel 1. Emily: Ricky, someone left the water cooler running again! Earlier in the day, during recess, our classmate, Avery Lim, slipped and fell because of a puddle on the floor. Ricky: That's terrible! What did you do to help? Emily: I turned off the water and helped Avery to the General Office so that the staff could see to her injury. Panel 2. Ricky: Something must be done. I may have an idea. Let me report this matter to our principal, and at the same time, make a suggestion so that our school can be a safer place for all our students and staff. Emily: You're such a proactive prefect! I hope things change for the better! Ricky: Thanks for the encouragement, Emily! I'll need your support!"}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-p1-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 1 Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"p1","number":2,"topic":"writing","subtypes":["continuous-writing"],"format":"structured","marks":36,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write a composition of ++at least 150 words++ about **showing respect**.\nThe pictures are provided to help you think about this topic.\nYour composition should be based on one or more of these pictures.\n[[img1]]\nConsider the following points when you plan your composition:\n- Why was there a need to show respect?\n- How was respect shown?\nYou may use the points in any order and include other relevant points as well.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There is no single model answer. Check your writing against the points below."},"explanation":["Understand the topic: 'showing respect' means treating someone (or their time, feelings, property or needs) as important. Your story must show clearly WHY respect was needed and HOW it was shown.","Picture 1 (watch): respecting other people's time, for example being punctual for a team practice or an appointment instead of keeping others waiting; or respecting something precious, such as a grandparent's old watch.","Picture 2 (loudspeaker and music): being considerate about noise, for example turning down loud music for a neighbour who is ill, a baby who is sleeping or a sibling who is studying; or staying quiet during a performance or ceremony.","Picture 3 (boy covering his face): respecting someone's feelings, for example not laughing at or teasing a classmate who is upset or embarrassed, giving him space, listening and comforting him.","Use at least one picture and make it important to the plot, not just mentioned in passing.","Story arc: an opening that sets the scene; a problem in which someone is NOT shown respect (or it is hard to show it); a turning point where the main character realises why respect is needed; the action that shows respect; the result.","Show feelings and thoughts with actions and dialogue ('my cheeks burned', 'I lowered the volume and knocked on his door') instead of just telling.","Ending: return to the topic with a reflection, for example what the character learnt about respect and how he or she behaves differently now.","Check: at least 150 words, paragraphs, consistent past tense, and both guiding points answered."]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-p1-q2-img1","alt":"Three pictures, one below the other. Picture 1: a wristwatch. Picture 2: a loudspeaker blasting loud music, with musical notes and lightning-shaped sound marks around it. Picture 3: a boy sitting with both hands covering his face."}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":1,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["question-tags"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the answer that best fits in the sentence.\n\"They've never been to the theatres in Singapore, __________?\" Aunt Mona asked.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Rule: a question tag uses the same helping verb as the statement. A positive statement takes a negative tag, and a negative statement takes a positive tag.","'They've' is short for 'They have', so the helping verb is 'have'. That rules out 'did they' and 'didn't they'.","'never' makes the statement negative, so the tag must be positive: 'have they', not 'haven't they'.","The answer is option (2), have they."]}],"options":["did they","have they","didn't they","haven't they"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":2,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the answer that best fits in the sentence.\nThe detective was __________ the verge of giving up when he discovered the hidden clue in a dusty diary.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'on the verge of' is a fixed phrase. It means very close to doing something.","The detective had almost given up when he found the clue.","The other prepositions do not go with 'the verge of'; 'in' is tempting because of phrases such as 'in the middle of', but the phrase is always 'on the verge of'.","The answer is option (3), on."]}],"options":["in","by","on","with"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q3","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 3","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":3,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["infinitives-and-gerunds"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the answer that best fits in the sentence.\nWhen the old man dropped his groceries, several kind strangers helped him __________ them up from the pavement.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Rule: help + someone + base form of the verb (with or without 'to'): 'helped him pick' or 'helped him to pick'.","The tense is already shown by 'helped', so the second verb does not change.","'picked' is tempting because the sentence is in the past, but only the first verb carries the past tense.","The answer is option (1), pick."]}],"options":["pick","picks","picked","picking"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q4","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 4","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":4,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the answer that best fits in the sentence.\nPaul giggled softly when he saw his grandmother talking to __________ while watching her favourite drama on television.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Rule: use a reflexive pronoun (-self) when the doer and the receiver of the action are the same person.","The grandmother was doing the talking, and she was talking to nobody else: she was talking to herself while watching 'her' favourite drama.","'her' would mean another woman, and 'himself' does not match 'grandmother', who is female.","The answer is option (3), herself."]}],"options":["her","him","herself","himself"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q5","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 5","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":5,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the answer that best fits in the sentence.\n__________ the noise from the construction work nearby, the children managed to concentrate on their lessons.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Look at the two ideas: there was noise, yet the children managed to concentrate. The second idea is a surprise, so we need a connector of contrast.","'In spite of' + noun means 'although there was'.","'Owing to' and 'As a result of' give a cause, but noise does not cause concentration. 'Contrary to' means 'opposite to what was said or expected', as in 'contrary to popular belief', and does not fit with 'the noise'.","The answer is option (2), In spite of."]}],"options":["Owing to","In spite of","Contrary to","As a result of"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q6","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 6","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":6,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["quantifiers"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the answer that best fits in the sentence.\nEven though he practised for weeks before the school concert, Marcus still had __________ confidence when he walked onto the stage to perform his solo.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'Even though ... still' signals a surprise: he practised for weeks, yet he did not feel confident.","'confidence' is uncountable. 'little' (without 'a') is used with uncountable nouns and means 'hardly any'.","'few' is only for countable nouns. 'some' and 'much' would mean he was confident, which is no surprise after weeks of practice.","The answer is option (2), little."]}],"options":["few","little","some","much"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q7","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 7","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":7,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the answer that best fits in the sentence.\nBy the time the school bell rang, all the students __________ their art projects and placed them on display.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Rule: 'By the time' + simple past is followed by the past perfect (had + past participle) for the action that was completed earlier.","The finishing happened before the bell rang, so: 'had finished'. ('placed' shares the same 'had': had finished ... and placed.)","'finished' is tempting, but it does not show that the action was already complete before the bell. 'have finished' is present perfect and cannot go with the past 'rang'.","The answer is option (3), had finished."]}],"options":["finish","finished","had finished","have finished"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q8","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 8","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":8,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["infinitives-and-gerunds"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the answer that best fits in the sentence.\nJenna could not help __________ upon seeing her little brother trying to pull his hand out of the cookie jar.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Rule: 'could not help' (meaning 'could not stop oneself') is followed by the -ing form of the verb.","Jenna could not stop herself from laughing: 'could not help laughing'.","'to laugh' is tempting because 'help to do' exists, but that means 'assist', which is a different meaning.","The answer is option (3), laughing."]}],"options":["laugh","to laugh","laughing","having laughed"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q9","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 9","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":9,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["comparatives"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the answer that best fits in the sentence.\nThis mystery novel is by far __________ one I have read this year.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'by far' is used to stress a superlative: it means 'by a great amount'.","The novel is compared with all the books read this year (more than two), so we need the superlative: 'the most exciting'.","'the more exciting' is used when comparing only two things.","The answer is option (3), the most exciting."]}],"options":["an exciting","a very exciting","the most exciting","the more exciting"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q10","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 10","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":10,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["subject-verb-agreement"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the answer that best fits in the sentence.\n\"Where __________ the pair of glasses that I placed on the desk this morning?\" wondered Mrs Lim.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Find the subject: 'the pair of glasses'. The head word is 'pair', which is singular, so the verb is singular. That rules out 'are' and 'were'.","Mrs Lim is wondering now where the glasses are at this moment, so the verb is in the present tense: 'is'.","'was' is tempting because 'placed' is in the past, but only the placing happened in the morning; the looking is happening now.","The answer is option (1), is."]}],"options":["is","are","was","were"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q11","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 11","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":11,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the answer that best fits in the sentence.\nA fight __________ on the football field when two students started quarrelling over the yellow card issued by the referee.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'broke out' means started suddenly. It is used for fights, fires, wars and diseases.","The fight started when the two students quarrelled, so 'A fight broke out'.","'broke up' means ended or separated (a teacher breaks up a fight), which is the opposite. 'broke off' means stopped suddenly or snapped off; 'broke down' means stopped working.","The answer is option (3), broke out."]}],"options":["broke up","broke off","broke out","broke down"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q12","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 12","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":12,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["collocation","word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the answer that best fits in the sentence.\nAfter months of hard work, Maria became __________ playing the piano and could perform complex pieces without making mistakes.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clues: 'months of hard work' and 'could perform complex pieces without making mistakes'. She became very skilled.","'proficient at' means skilled and able to do something very well.","'familiar with' is tempting, but it only means knowing something fairly well, not being highly skilled. 'certified in' means holding an official certificate, and 'inspired by' does not fit the result of hard work.","The answer is option (3), proficient at."]}],"options":["certified in","inspired by","proficient at","familiar with"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q13","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 13","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":13,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the answer that best fits in the sentence.\nSarah looked __________ at the tall diving board, worried about jumping into the deep water for the first time.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Clue: she was 'worried about jumping into the deep water'.","'apprehensively' means in a worried or nervous way about something that is going to happen.","'quizzically' means in a puzzled, questioning way; 'reproachfully' means in a blaming way; 'cynically' means in a distrusting way. None of these shows worry.","The answer is option (4), apprehensively."]}],"options":["cynically","quizzically","reproachfully","apprehensively"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q14","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 14","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":14,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning","collocation"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the answer that best fits in the sentence.\nSiti could hardly __________ her emotions upon hearing the devastating news.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'suppress' means to hold back a feeling so that it does not show. We 'suppress' emotions, tears, a smile or a yawn.","The news was devastating, so Siti could hardly hold back her feelings.","'restrict' is tempting, but it means to limit the amount or movement of something (restrict the number of visitors). 'omit' means leave out; 'reduce' means make smaller.","The answer is option (4), suppress."]}],"options":["omit","reduce","restrict","suppress"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q15","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 15","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":15,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the answer that best fits in the sentence.\nThe storm began to __________ as the wind grew stronger and the rain became heavier throughout the afternoon.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Clues: 'the wind grew stronger and the rain became heavier'. The storm became stronger.","'intensify' means to become stronger or more extreme.","'accelerate' is tempting, but it means to go faster, and the clues are about strength, not speed. 'advance' means move forward; 'maximise' means make as large as possible and needs an object.","The answer is option (1), intensify."]}],"options":["intensify","advance","maximise","accelerate"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q16","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 16","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":16,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++conscientiously++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["'conscientiously' means carefully and with great effort, because you want to do something well.","Clue in the passage: he practised 'every afternoon under the scorching sun' for hours.","'diligently' means in a hard-working and careful way, which matches.","'precisely' means exactly; 'frantically' means in a hurried, panicky way; 'haphazardly' means without any plan, which is the opposite. The answer is option (1)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g1","options":["diligently","precisely","frantically","haphazardly"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q17","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 17","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":17,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++formidable++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'formidable' describes something so strong or powerful that it is very hard to deal with.","Clue: the opposing team had 'exceptional skills' and was 'undefeated all season', so their defence was very hard to beat.","'tough' means difficult to beat or get through, which matches a defence best.","'frightening' is tempting because formidable things can cause fear, but the passage is about how strong the defence was, and Wei Han 'refused to back down'. 'tremendous' means very great in size or amount. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g1","options":["fiery","tough","frightening","tremendous"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q18","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 18","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":18,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++resilience++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'resilience' is the ability to keep going and not give up when things are hard.","Clue: 'Despite his aching limbs' he kept sprinting, and the last line says 'determination was the key to success'.","'persistence' means continuing to try in spite of difficulty, which matches.","'stamina' is tempting, but it is physical energy to keep going for a long time; his limbs were already aching, so it was his will, not his energy, that kept him going. The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g1","options":["stamina","efficiency","resistance","persistence"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q19","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 19","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":19,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++caught sight of++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["'caught sight of' means suddenly saw something, often for a moment.","He saw the goalkeeper leaning slightly to the left.","'noticed' means saw or became aware of, which matches.","'scanned' means looked over an area carefully to search for something, which is a longer, deliberate action. The answer is option (1)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g1","options":["noticed","scanned","guessed","concluded"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q21","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 21","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":21,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The purpose of the statement \"Carry Everything in Style\" in Text 1 is to __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Break the slogan into its two ideas. 'Carry Everything' says the bag can hold all you need, so it is useful. 'in Style' says it looks fashionable, so it is trendy.","The poster backs up both ideas: 'Fit your entire school day into one bag' and 'Say goodbye to boring school bags'.","(1) is tempting, but it covers only the 'Carry Everything' half and leaves out 'in Style'.","The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g2","options":["emphasise the bag's storage capacity","highlight that the bag is useful and trendy","command students to care about how they look","remind students about what they must bring to school"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q22","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 22","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":22,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience","inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Ansel was quoted in Text 1 because __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Advertisements quote users (a testimonial) to convince readers that what the advertisement says is true.","Match Ansel's words to the poster's claims: 'Everything I need for school fits comfortably into it' supports 'Fit your entire school day into one bag'; 'I can also find and take out any item I need easily' supports 'Get organised with a specific space for everything'.","(2) is false because the poster says the bag is 'Suitable for students of all ages'.","The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g2","options":["he appears to be a good student","all school bag users are around his age","his review was short enough to fit into the poster","what he said supports the claims made in the poster"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q23","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 23","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":23,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["factual","inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Based on Text 1, which of the following statements are true about the Peaks school bag?\nA. The bag is small and light.\nB. The bag comes in different colours.\nC. The bag has multiple compartments.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["A: the poster never says the bag is small or light. It says you can 'Fit your entire school day into one bag', which suggests a roomy bag. A is not supported.","B: 'Wide range of colours to choose from!' So B is true.","C: 'Get organised with a specific space for everything', and Ansel can 'find and take out any item' easily. Separate spaces for different things are compartments. So C is true.","The answer is option (3), B and C."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g2","options":["A and B","A and C","B and C","A, B and C"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q24","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 24","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":24,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"What is the **main** message of Text 2?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["The main message is the idea that runs through the whole text, not one detail.","Every paragraph of Text 2 comes back to the back: 'can protect your back', 'reduce the pressure on your back', 'you can protect your back and still have everything you need'.","(2) and (4) are true details, but each is only one way of protecting the back (choosing the bag wisely; packing light). (3) is the idea that Text 2 warns against.","The answer is option (1)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g2","options":["Students should take measures to protect their backs.","Students should choose school bags with padded backs.","Students should fit everything into one bag for convenience.","Students should use lockers and desks to store their belongings."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-a-q25","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet A Question 25","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"a","number":25,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Text 1 and Text 2 express differing views on whether __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'Differing views' means both texts talk about the same thing but do not agree.","Text 1 encourages it: 'Fit your entire school day into one bag — all your books, stationery, lunch boxes and more!'","Text 2 questions it: 'While it may seem convenient to fit everything into one bag, have you considered the weight?' and recommends 'packing only the items needed for the day'.","The other options are each mentioned in only one text, so the texts cannot disagree on them. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g2","options":["students should store certain items in school","school bags should look appealing to students","students should fit everything into their school bags","school bags should be designed with students in mind"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q26","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 26","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":26,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (26). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"J","accept":["j","is"]},"explanation":["The subject is 'The mystery behind fruit ripening'. The head word is 'mystery', which is singular.","After the blank is 'still not fully understood by scientists': this is the passive voice (be + past participle).","'still' shows the situation is true now, so the verb is present tense: 'is', not 'was'.","The answer is (J) is: 'The mystery ... is still not fully understood'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q27","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 27","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":27,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (27). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"G","accept":["g","if"]},"explanation":["The sentence gives a condition: the fruits bruise easily in one situation, namely when they are left to ripen on the tree.","'if left to ripen' is a short form of 'if they are left to ripen'. 'if' can be followed directly by a past participle in this way.","The answer is (G) if: 'they bruise easily if left to ripen fully on the tree'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q28","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 28","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":28,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (28). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"E","accept":["e","has"]},"explanation":["After the blank is 'been known': the present perfect passive needs has/have + been + past participle.","The subject is 'The flesh of these fruits'. The head word is 'flesh' (singular), not 'fruits', so the verb is 'has', not 'have'.","The answer is (E) has: 'The flesh of these fruits has been known to contain a gas'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q29","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 29","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":29,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (29). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"L","accept":["l","the"]},"explanation":["'ripening process' is a singular countable noun, so it needs a determiner in front.","It is a particular process that the reader already knows about (fruit ripening was introduced earlier), so we use the definite article 'the'.","'this' is tempting, but the process has not been pointed out in this sentence yet, and the passage uses the phrase 'the ripening process' again and again. 'a' does not fit because it is not just any process.","The answer is (L) the: 'a signal to begin the ripening process'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q30","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 30","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":30,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["relative-pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (30). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"P","accept":["p","where"]},"explanation":["The blank joins 'the parts' to 'the seeds develop'. 'the parts' are places inside the fruit.","To add information about a place, use the relative adverb 'where' (= in which).","'which' is tempting, but 'the parts which the seeds develop' would mean the seeds develop the parts. The seeds develop IN those parts.","The answer is (P) where: 'reaches the parts where the seeds develop'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q31","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 31","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":31,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["modals"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (31). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"D","accept":["d","can"]},"explanation":["After the blank is the base verb 'slow down' with the plural subject 'workers'. A modal verb fits before a base verb.","'can' shows what the workers are able to do.","The answer is (D) can: 'workers can slow down the ripening process'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q32","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 32","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":32,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (32). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"C","accept":["c","by"]},"explanation":["The words after the blank tell us HOW the workers slow the process down: 'keeping the fruits cool and reducing the amount of ethylene'.","Rule: by + verb-ing shows the method used to do something.","The answer is (C) by: 'by keeping the fruits cool'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q33","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 33","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":33,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["relative-pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (33). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Q","accept":["q","which"]},"explanation":["The blank joins 'a few ripe bananas' to 'have already released a lot of ethylene'. It stands for the bananas and is the subject of 'have released'.","For things, the relative pronoun is 'which' (or 'that').","'where' is for places and cannot be the subject of a verb, so it belongs in blank (30), not here.","The answer is (Q) which: 'a few ripe bananas which have already released a lot of ethylene'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q34","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 34","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":34,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (34). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"B","accept":["b","an"]},"explanation":["'unripe mango' is a singular countable noun mentioned for the first time, so it needs 'a' or 'an'.","'unripe' begins with a vowel sound (un-), so the article is 'an', not 'a'.","The answer is (B) an: 'put an unripe mango in a paper bag'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q35","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 35","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":35,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (35). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"K","accept":["k","of"]},"explanation":["'in the hope of' + verb-ing is a fixed phrase meaning 'wishing that something will happen'.","After the blank is 'using', an -ing form, which follows the preposition 'of'.","The answer is (K) of: 'in the hope of using this knowledge'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q36","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 36","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":36,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (36) ++volentiering++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"volunteering"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. Say the word in parts: vol-un-teer-ing.","The middle sound is 'un', spelt with a 'u', and the ending is '-teer' with a double 'e' (like engineer and pioneer).","After 'regretted', the -ing form is correct, so only the spelling changes.","The correct word is 'volunteering'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q37","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 37","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":37,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (37) ++by++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"with"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong preposition.","The verb 'task' takes 'with': to be tasked with (doing) something means to be given that job.","'by' would introduce the person who gave the task (tasked by the principal), but here what follows is the job itself: 'planning an exhibition'.","The correct word is 'with'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q38","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 38","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":38,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (38) ++enviromantle++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"environmental"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. Start from the base word 'environment': en-vi-ron-ment. Do not forget the 'n' before 'ment'.","Add the adjective ending '-al': environment + al = environmental.","The ending sounds like 'tle' but is spelt '-tal'.","The correct word is 'environmental'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q39","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 39","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":39,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (39) ++are++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"was"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error with two things to fix: agreement and tense.","Agreement: 'everyone' is always singular, so it cannot take 'are'.","Tense: the story is told in the past ('The principal also made it clear that ...'), so the verb is past tense.","The correct word is 'was': 'everyone was expected to do a good job'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q40","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 40","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":40,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (40) ++skrold++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"scrolled"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The base word is 'scroll': the /sk/ sound is spelt 'sc', and it ends in double 'l'.","Add '-ed' for the past tense: scroll + ed = scrolled.","The correct word is 'scrolled'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q41","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 41","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":41,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (41) ++begin++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"begun"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. Look at the helping verb in front: 'had ___'.","Rule: had + past participle makes the past perfect tense.","The verb forms are begin, began, begun. The past participle is 'begun' ('began' is the simple past and cannot follow 'had').","The correct word is 'begun'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q42","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 42","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":42,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (42) ++sprauling++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"sprawling"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The /aw/ sound in this word is spelt 'aw', as in 'crawl' and 'shawl'.","'sprawling' means spreading out over a large area, which describes huge shopping malls.","The correct word is 'sprawling'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q43","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 43","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":43,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (43) ++Although++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"However"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong kind of connector.","'Although' joins two clauses in one sentence and is never followed by a comma on its own.","Here the word starts a new sentence, is followed by a comma, and shows a contrast with the sentence before (Jia Wei cared about what the town might lose, but his friends cared about posters). That is the job of 'However'.","The correct word is 'However'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q45","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 45","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":45,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (45) ++what++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"how"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. The word is followed by the adverb 'easily'.","Rule: use 'how' + adjective/adverb to talk about degree: 'how easily', 'how quickly', 'how tall'.","'what' is followed by a noun (what a pity, what memories), not by an adverb.","The correct word is 'how': 'realised how easily a city's memories could disappear'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q47","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 47","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":47,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (47) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"As"},"explanation":["'___ a teenager, he left home' tells us what he was at the time he left.","'As a teenager' means 'when he was a teenager'. 'As' + noun is used for a role or stage of life.","The answer is 'As'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q48","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 48","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":48,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (48) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"dream","accept":["ambition","aspiration","goal","wish","hope"]},"explanation":["The sentence says what he had wanted 'from childhood': 'to become a professional musician'.","Something a person has long wished to become is a dream or an ambition.","The answer is 'dream'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q49","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 49","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":49,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (49) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"job"},"explanation":["The blank is described by what follows: 'playing the violin and piano in silent movie theatres'.","The next sentence says 'The work was tiring and the pay was low', so this was paid work: a job.","The answer is 'job'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q50","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 50","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":50,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (50) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"life"},"explanation":["'bring something to life' is a fixed phrase meaning to make something exciting and real.","Silent films had no sound, so his music made them come alive for the audience.","The answer is 'life'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q51","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 51","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":51,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (51) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"up"},"explanation":["'come up with' is a phrasal verb meaning to think of or produce an idea, a plan or a piece of work.","The City Council asked him to produce a special song: 'come up with a special song'.","The answer is 'up'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q52","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 52","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":52,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (52) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"accepted"},"explanation":["He was 'invited' to do something, and the sentence says he did so 'readily' (willingly, without delay).","The verb that goes with an invitation or a task you agree to do is 'accept'. The story is in the past, so 'accepted'.","The answer is 'accepted'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q53","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 53","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":53,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms","contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (53) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"seeking","accept":["finding"]},"explanation":["Why did he walk along the streets and kampungs? To look for ideas, that is, inspiration.","The blank follows a comma and has no subject, so it is an -ing form that describes what he was doing as he walked.","'seeking' means looking for, and it takes an object directly: 'seeking inspiration'. ('looking' would need 'for', and only one word is allowed.)","The answer is 'seeking'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q54","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 54","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":54,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (54) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"wanted","accept":["intended","wished"]},"explanation":["Pattern: verb + object + to-infinitive: 'He ___ the song to capture the aspirations of a young nation'.","'want something to do something' fits this pattern and tells us his aim. The story is in the past, so 'wanted'.","The answer is 'wanted'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q55","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 55","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":55,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (55) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"tune","accept":["melody","song"]},"explanation":["What does a composer compose that people can 'remember and sing'? A tune or melody.","'rousing' means stirring and exciting, which describes music.","The answer is 'tune' (or 'melody')."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q56","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 56","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":56,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (56) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"adopted","accept":["chosen","selected"]},"explanation":["Pattern: 'was ___ by the government as the national anthem'. This is the passive voice, so a past participle is needed.","To 'adopt' something means to choose it officially and start using it.","The answer is 'adopted'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q57","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 57","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":57,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (57) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"ever"},"explanation":["'than ever before' is a fixed phrase meaning 'than at any time in the past'.","He kept improving, so he was trying to be a better composer than he had been at any earlier time.","The answer is 'ever'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q58","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 58","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":58,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (58) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"should","accept":["must"]},"explanation":["After the blank are the base verbs 'sound' and 'encourage' with a singular subject ('a national anthem'). Without a modal verb they would need an -s, so a modal fits.","The sentence gives his belief about what an anthem ought to do. 'should' expresses what is right or expected.","The answer is 'should'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q59","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 59","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":59,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (59) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"events","accept":["celebrations","occasions"]},"explanation":["An example is given straight after the blank: 'like the Primary 5 National Education (NE) Show'.","A show like that is an organised happening for the nation: a national event. The blank is plural because it stands for many of them.","The answer is 'events'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q60","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 60","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":60,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (60) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"appreciation","accept":["gratitude","respect"]},"explanation":["Students sing the anthem to 'show their ___ for the sacrifices and dreams that helped build Singapore'.","What we feel and show for other people's sacrifices is thankfulness: appreciation or gratitude. Both take the preposition 'for'.","The answer is 'appreciation'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q61","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 61","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":61,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["none-of"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"None of the plates in the sink is clean.\nAll ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"All the plates in the sink are dirty.","accept":["All of the plates in the sink are dirty."]},"explanation":["'None ... is clean' means not a single plate is clean. Starting with 'All' turns the sentence round, so the describing word must change to its opposite: clean becomes dirty.","Agreement: 'All the plates' is plural, so the verb changes from 'is' to 'are'.","Do not write 'All the plates are not clean': that can also mean 'only some are clean', so the meaning is no longer the same.","Answer: All the plates in the sink are dirty."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q62","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 62","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":62,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["however-adjective"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The chef works very hard but he cannot perfect the new recipe.\nHowever ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"However hard the chef works, he cannot perfect the new recipe."},"explanation":["Here 'However' is not the connector meaning 'but'. 'However + adjective/adverb + subject + verb' means 'no matter how ...'.","Take the adverb 'hard' and put it straight after 'However'. Drop 'very' (the idea of degree is already in 'however') and drop 'but'.","Put a comma after the first clause, then write the main clause unchanged.","Name the chef in the first clause and use 'he' in the second. Answer: However hard the chef works, he cannot perfect the new recipe."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q63","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 63","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":63,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["reported-speech","passive-voice"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Father reminded Lisa, \"Do your homework before meeting your friends.\"\nLisa was reminded ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Lisa was reminded by Father to do her homework before meeting her friends.","accept":["Lisa was reminded by Father to do her homework before she met her friends.","Lisa was reminded by her father to do her homework before meeting her friends."]},"explanation":["Two changes are needed: active to passive voice, and direct to reported speech.","Passive: Lisa becomes the subject, and the doer follows 'by': 'Lisa was reminded by Father'.","A reported instruction uses 'to' + base verb: 'Do your homework' becomes 'to do her homework'.","Pronouns change to fit Lisa: 'your' becomes 'her' both times.","Answer: Lisa was reminded by Father to do her homework before meeting her friends."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q64","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 64","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":64,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["noun-form"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Children can benefit from regular exercise.\nExercising ____________________ for children.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Exercising regularly is beneficial for children.","accept":["Exercising regularly can be beneficial for children."]},"explanation":["The sentence now starts with the gerund 'Exercising' (a verb used as a noun), so the other words must change their forms to fit.","'regular' described the noun 'exercise'. To describe the verb form 'exercising', use the adverb: 'regularly'.","'benefit' was a verb. After 'is' (or 'can be') and before 'for children', use the adjective: 'beneficial'.","A gerund subject is singular, so the verb is 'is'. Answer: Exercising regularly is beneficial for children."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q65","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 65","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":65,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["cleft-sentence"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"You can place the ice cubes in the container after you break them up.\nIt is only after breaking up the ice cubes ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"It is only after breaking up the ice cubes that you can place them in the container."},"explanation":["The pattern 'It is only after ... that ...' stresses the condition that must come first.","After 'It is only after + -ing phrase', the main clause is introduced by 'that'.","'the ice cubes' has already been named in the starter, so use the pronoun 'them' in the main clause.","Answer: It is only after breaking up the ice cubes that you can place them in the container."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q66","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 66","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":66,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["evidence-phrase"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Pick out a four-word phrase from the first paragraph which tells us that Maya's grandfather cherished the lettuce garden.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"apple of his eye"},"explanation":["To 'cherish' something is to love it and treat it as precious.","Paragraph 1 says the garden 'was truly the apple of his eye'. The idiom 'the apple of someone's eye' means the thing or person that someone loves most.","Count exactly four words and copy them without changing anything: apple (1) of (2) his (3) eye (4). Leave out 'the', or the phrase has five words.","The answer is 'apple of his eye'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q67","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 67","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":67,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["character-and-feelings"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Circle one word that best describes how Maya felt when she saw her grandfather's plants wilting. DO NOT CIRCLE MORE THAN ONE WORD.\nfrustrated     guilty     dismayed     anxious\n(Type the word you would circle.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"dismayed"},"explanation":["Find the moment in paragraph 1: 'Maya's heart sank as she saw her dear grandfather's plants beginning to wilt'.","When your 'heart sinks', you suddenly feel sad and disappointed at something unpleasant. That is what 'dismayed' means.","'anxious' is tempting, but it means worried about what might happen; 'heart sank' shows sadness at what she saw. She had not done anything wrong, so she was not 'guilty', and she had not tried and failed yet, so she was not 'frustrated'.","The answer is 'dismayed'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q68","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 68","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":68,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual","cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"In paragraph 2, give two reasons why Maya's mother said it would be \"an uphill task\" when Maya asked to take care of the garden.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Maya's mother said so because taking care of the garden would be a big responsibility and because plants need daily attention."},"explanation":["An 'uphill task' is one that is very difficult. Look at what Mother said just before those words.","Reason 1 (1 mark): 'This would be a big responsibility, Maya.'","Reason 2 (1 mark): 'Plants need daily attention, you know?'","Give both reasons in a complete sentence. One reason alone earns only one mark."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q69","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 69","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":69,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["sequencing"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write 1, 2 and 3 in the blanks below to indicate the order in which the events occurred in the story.\n___ Maya's grandfather fell ill and was hospitalised.\n___ Mrs Chen taught Maya how to take care of plants.\n___ Maya decided to take care of her grandfather's plants.\n(Type the three numbers in the order of the statements, for example: 3, 1, 2.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"1, 3, 2","accept":["1 3 2","132","1,3,2","1-3-2"]},"explanation":["Find each event in the passage and note its paragraph.","Grandfather fell ill and stayed in hospital: paragraph 1. This is first (1).","Maya asked her mother if she could take care of the garden: paragraph 2. This is second (2).","Mrs Chen came and gave advice about watering: paragraph 5. This is third (3).","In the order of the statements, the numbers are 1, 3, 2."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q70","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 70","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":70,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["cause-and-effect","factual"],"format":"structured","marks":4,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on the information from lines 10–26 (paragraphs 3 to 6), complete the following table.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"How Maya felt when she saw the droopy lettuce leaves","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Maya felt deeply worried (and extremely discouraged).","accept":["Maya felt deeply worried.","Maya felt extremely discouraged.","She felt deeply worried."]},"explanation":["Find the droopy leaves in paragraph 3: 'she noticed the lettuce leaves looking droopy despite her efforts, making her feel deeply worried.'","Paragraph 4 adds: 'Feeling extremely discouraged, Maya called her best friend'.","The marker looks for the feeling: deeply worried / extremely discouraged."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"How Sandie responded upon hearing the problem","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Sandie offered without hesitation to ask her grandmother for her opinion, and within an hour she brought her grandmother, Mrs Chen, to Maya's garden to help."},"explanation":["Paragraph 4: '\"My grandmother grows vegetables too! Let me ask her for her opinion,\" Sandie offered without hesitation.'","She then acted on it: 'Within an hour, Sandie arrived breathlessly with her elderly grandmother, Mrs Chen'.","The marker looks for: she offered to ask her grandmother (and brought Mrs Chen over to help)."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"What Mrs Chen taught Maya about watering plants","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Mrs Chen taught Maya that different plants need different amounts of water, and that the soil for lettuce should not be too wet."},"explanation":["Paragraph 5 gives Mrs Chen's words: 'different plants need different amounts of water' and 'the soil for lettuce should not be too wet'.","Maya had been 'soaking the thirsty plants thoroughly', so she had been giving the lettuce too much water.","The marker looks for: different plants need different amounts of water / lettuce soil should not be too wet."]},{"label":"d","prompt":"How Maya responded","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Maya took Mrs Chen's advice to heart and took special care not to overwater the lettuce."},"explanation":["Paragraph 6: 'Maya took Sandie's grandmother's advice to heart, taking special care not to overwater the lettuce.'","'took the advice to heart' means she considered it seriously and followed it.","The marker looks for: she followed the advice and was careful not to overwater the lettuce."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q71","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 71","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":71,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","vocabulary-in-context"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Explain clearly what Mrs Chen meant when she said that \"Rome wasn't built in a day, and neither is a beautiful garden.\" (line 22, paragraph 5)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Mrs Chen meant that, just as the great city of Rome took a long time to build, a beautiful garden cannot be created quickly. It takes time, patience and steady care."},"explanation":["'Rome wasn't built in a day' is a proverb: great things take a long time to achieve.","Idea 1 (1 mark): a beautiful garden cannot be created immediately or in a short time.","Idea 2 (1 mark): it needs time, patience and continued care. The clue is the sentence just before: 'Gardening requires patience.'","Explain the meaning for the garden; do not just repeat the proverb."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q72","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 72","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":72,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["referencing"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Look at the table below. What do the word(s) in the left column refer(s) to in the passage? Write your answers in the column on the right.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"it (line 26, paragraph 6)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Sandie's discovery that the plants grew faster after she and Maya talked to them.","accept":["The plants growing faster after they talked to them.","The plants growing faster after Maya and Sandie talked to them."]},"explanation":["A word like 'it' points back to something already mentioned. Read the first half of the sentence.","'Sandie discovered that the plants grew faster after they talked to them, though Maya suspected **it** was just coincidence.'","What did Maya think was a coincidence? The plants growing faster after the girls talked to them.","Test by replacing the word: 'Maya suspected the plants growing faster after they talked to them was just coincidence.' It makes sense."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"that treatment (line 30, paragraph 7)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The soap solution (that Maya prepared and sprinkled onto the affected leaves).","accept":["the soap solution","soap solution","The soap solution that Maya prepared.","The soap solution Maya sprinkled onto the affected leaves."]},"explanation":["Read the sentences before 'that treatment' in paragraph 7.","'she ... prepared a soap solution. Then she sprinkled the treatment onto the affected leaves.' So the treatment is the soap solution.","Test by replacing the words: 'disappear completely after the soap solution' was sprinkled on. It makes sense.","The answer is the soap solution."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q73","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 73","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":73,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["true-false-with-reason"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on lines 10–31 (paragraphs 3 to 7), state whether each statement in the table below is true or false, then give one reason why you think so.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Maya had no experience taking care of plants before her grandfather was hospitalised.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. Although Maya had watched her grandfather's practised movements countless times, doing it herself for the first time felt like an arduous task."},"explanation":["Paragraph 3: 'Although she had watched her grandfather's practised movements countless times, doing it herself the first time felt like an arduous task.'","'the first time' shows she had only watched before and had never done it herself, so she had no experience.","So the statement is True. The reason must be evidence from the passage, not the statement repeated."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Mrs Chen praised Maya for her efforts.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. Mrs Chen told Maya, \"You're doing remarkably well, dear!\""},"explanation":["Paragraph 5 begins with Mrs Chen's words to Maya: 'You're doing remarkably well, dear!'","Telling someone she is doing 'remarkably well' is praise.","So the statement is True."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"Maya solved the aphid problem with help from Sandie and Mrs Chen.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. Maya researched natural remedies online, prepared a soap solution and sprinkled it onto the affected leaves by herself."},"explanation":["Paragraph 7 describes the aphid attack. Every action is Maya's own: 'she researched for natural remedies online and prepared a soap solution. Then she sprinkled the treatment onto the affected leaves.'","Sandie and Mrs Chen are not mentioned in this paragraph. They helped with watering and weeding earlier, not with the aphids.","So the statement is False."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q74","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 74","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":74,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","factual"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Maya's grandfather told her that she had done \"something wonderful\" (lines 34–35, paragraph 8). What did Maya do that was wonderful?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Maya took care of her grandfather's lettuce garden while he was in hospital. She restored the wilting, aphid-attacked plants to health so that the garden was more beautiful than ever, and she kept a detailed notebook filled with careful observations."},"explanation":["Look at what Grandfather saw just before he spoke (paragraph 8): 'his lettuce garden more beautiful than ever', 'the healthy plants and Maya's detailed notebook filled with careful observations'.","Point 1 (1 mark): she cared for the garden while he was away and brought the wilting plants back to health / made the garden more beautiful than ever.","Point 2 (1 mark): she kept a detailed notebook of careful observations.","Say what Maya DID (her actions), not just how the garden looked."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-b-q75","cite":"2026 Prelim CHIJ St Nicholas Paper 2 Booklet B Question 75","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"CHIJ St Nicholas","section":"b","number":75,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"How had Mother's opinion of Maya changed by the end of the story?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"At first, Mother was concerned that Maya would not be able to handle the big responsibility of taking care of the garden. By the end of the story, she was impressed by and approved of what Maya had done, as she nodded and gave Maya a thumbs-up."},"explanation":["A 'how did it change' question needs both sides: the opinion before (1 mark) and the opinion after (1 mark).","Before (paragraph 2): Mother 'gave her a concerned look' and said it would be 'a big responsibility' and 'an uphill task for you'. She doubted that Maya could manage.","After (paragraph 10): 'Her mother nodded and gave her a thumbs-up.' She now approved of Maya and believed she was capable and responsible.","Use signal words such as 'At first ... By the end ...' so the marker can see the change."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-p1-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 1 Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"p1","number":1,"topic":"writing","subtypes":["situational-writing"],"format":"structured","marks":14,"difficulty":2,"stem":"**Part 1: Situational Writing (14 marks)**\nThe pictures below show Vincent, Siti and Meera having a conversation. Study the pictures carefully.\n[[img1]]\n**Your Task**\nImagine you are Vincent.\n**Write an email to your teacher, Miss Hanisah, to propose the community service project that you and your classmates have discussed.**\nYou are to refer to the pictures and information above for your email. The bullet point underlined below requires you to come up with your own information.\n\nIn your email, include the following key information:\n• the name of the community service project\n• what your class will make and its purpose\n• the art materials required\n• the date by which you hope to get the materials\n• ++one value your class hope to learn from this project++\n\nYou may reorder the points. Remember to write in complete sentences.\n(Write your email on paper or type it, then check it against the points given.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There is no single model answer. Check your writing against the points below."},"explanation":["Purpose: to propose the class community service project to your teacher (and so ask for her approval and support). Say this in your opening sentence.","Audience: Miss Hanisah, your teacher. Write as Vincent ('I', 'my classmates and I', 'our class'). Context: your class has to do a community service project.","Point 1, the name of the project: 'Fold a Crane. Bring a Smile!' Meera says she likes Siti's **second** suggestion, so 'Project Paper Crane' is wrong.","Point 2, what the class will make and its purpose: fold 1,000 paper cranes to cheer up the patients at Greyway Hospital. Give both the number and the purpose.","Point 3, the art materials required: recycled coloured paper and some nylon string (to hang the paper cranes).","Point 4, the date by which you hope to get the materials: 10 July 2026. Vincent first said 15 July, but Siti corrected him, so 15 July is wrong.","Point 5, one value your class hopes to learn: this is your own idea. Name one value that fits and link it to the project, for example compassion or empathy (caring for people who are ill), teamwork (folding 1,000 cranes together) or perseverance.","Format and tone: a greeting ('Dear Miss Hanisah,'), a polite and respectful tone because you are writing to a teacher, complete sentences (no bullet points or short forms like 'pls'), a polite closing line such as 'I hope you will approve our proposal. Thank you.' and a sign-off ('Yours sincerely, Vincent' or 'Regards, Vincent').","Marks: 14 in total. In the PSLE format, 6 marks are for task fulfilment (all the points, the purpose, audience and context) and 8 marks are for language (grammar, spelling, punctuation and clear sentences). Do not copy the speech bubbles word for word; change 'we' and 'you' so that the sentences make sense in an email."]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-p1-q1-img1","alt":"Six speech bubbles in two rows of pictures showing three pupils talking in a library. Vincent: 'Siti, as part of our community service, shall we fold 1,000 paper cranes to cheer up the patients at Greyway Hospital?' Siti: 'Great idea, Vincent! We can call it 'Project Paper Crane' or 'Fold a Crane. Bring a Smile!'' Meera: 'I like your second suggestion, Siti. We can use recycled coloured paper and some nylon string to hang the paper cranes.' Vincent: 'I'll email Miss Hanisah to propose this as our community service project and tell her that we need to collect the materials by 15 July 2026.' Siti: 'No, we must collect all the materials by 10 July 2026 so that we will have enough time to fold the cranes.' Meera: 'You should also explain why our class is doing this as our community service project and what values we hope to learn from it.'"}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-p1-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 1 Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"p1","number":2,"topic":"writing","subtypes":["continuous-writing"],"format":"structured","marks":36,"difficulty":2,"stem":"**Part 2: Continuous Writing (36 marks)**\nWrite a composition of ++at least 150 words++ about **learning to respect others.**\nThe pictures are provided to help you think about the topic.\nYour composition should be based on one or more of these pictures.\n[[img1]]\nConsider the following points when you plan your composition:\n• What happened when the character(s) did not show respect?\n• How was respect shown after that?\n\nYou may use the points in any order and include other relevant points as well.\n(Write your composition on paper or type it, then check it against the points given.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There is no single model answer. Check your writing against the points below."},"explanation":["Understand the topic: 'learning to respect others' has two halves. Someone must first fail to show respect, and then **learn** to show it. A story where the character is respectful from the start does not fit. Respect can be for other people's peace and quiet, their belongings, their feelings or their opinions.","Picture 1 (loudspeaker blasting music): not respecting others' need for quiet, for example playing loud music late at night while a neighbour's baby sleeps, a sibling studies for an examination, or an elderly neighbour is ill.","Picture 2 (game console with a cracked screen): not respecting other people's belongings, for example taking a brother's or friend's console without asking and handling it roughly until it breaks.","Picture 3 (an angry boy and a sorry-looking boy): not respecting someone's feelings, or the quarrel that follows a disrespectful act. It can also be the turning point where the character sees how upset the other person is.","Use at least one picture and make it important to the story, not just mentioned in passing. You may combine them: a boy borrows his friend's console without permission (Picture 2) and the friend confronts him (Picture 3).","Plan a story arc. Beginning: who the character is and the careless attitude ('It's only a game. He won't mind.'). Build-up: the disrespectful act. Climax: the consequence (the cracked screen, the angry neighbour, the broken friendship). Turning point: the character realises how the other person feels, often with the help of a parent or teacher. Resolution: respect is shown in action, for example apologising sincerely, paying for the repair with saved pocket money, asking permission from then on, or turning the volume down.","Answer both guiding questions: what happened when the character did not show respect, and how respect was shown after that. The second one is easy to rush; give it a full paragraph.","Show feelings and thoughts, not only events: the other person's anger or hurt, and the main character's guilt ('My cheeks burned. I could not look him in the eye.'). Use some dialogue, but keep it short.","End by returning to the topic: what the character learnt about respect and how he or she behaves differently now. Avoid ending with only 'I learnt my lesson.'","Marks: 36 in total. In the PSLE format, 18 marks are for content (a clear, well-developed story that fits the topic) and 18 marks are for language and organisation (paragraphs, sentence variety, vocabulary, grammar, spelling and punctuation). Write well over 150 words; a developed story usually needs 350 words or more."]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-p1-q2-img1","alt":"Three pictures, one above the other. Picture 1: a loudspeaker blasting loud music, with music notes and jagged lightning-shaped sound marks around it. Picture 2: a handheld game console with a badly cracked screen and broken bits lying under it. Picture 3: two boys; one is angry, shouting with clenched fists, and the other looks sorry and embarrassed, scratching his head."}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":1,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["subject-verb-agreement"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"For each question from 1 to 10, choose the answer that best fits in the sentence(s).\nThe class monitor, together with a group of students, __________ very excited about the upcoming National Day celebration.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Find the real subject. Words added with 'together with', 'as well as' or 'along with' are extra information between commas; they do not change the subject.","Cover the part between the commas: 'The class monitor ___ very excited'. The subject is one person, so the verb must be singular.","'are', 'were' and 'have been' are all plural forms. They are tempting because 'a group of students' sits right before the blank.","'The class monitor ... is very excited.' The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["is","are","were","have been"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":2,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"For each question from 1 to 10, choose the answer that best fits in the sentence(s).\n__________ the difficult questions in the paper, Ahmad managed to complete the test on time.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Look at how the two ideas are related. Difficult questions would normally slow a person down, yet Ahmad finished on time. The result is unexpected, so we need a connector of contrast.","'In spite of' (like 'Despite') is followed by a noun phrase and shows that something happened even though there was an obstacle.","'Due to' and 'Because of' give a reason, but difficult questions are not the reason he finished on time. 'Instead of' means in place of something.","The answer is option (3), In spite of."]}],"options":["Due to","Instead of","In spite of","Because of"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q3","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 3","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":3,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["tenses","subject-verb-agreement"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"For each question from 1 to 10, choose the answer that best fits in the sentence(s).\nDue to the heavy downpour, the furniture __________ damp since last night.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clue 1: 'since last night'. 'Since' + a point in time tells us the situation started in the past and is still true now. That needs the present perfect tense (has/have + past participle), so 'is' and 'are' are out.","Clue 2: 'furniture' is an uncountable noun. It is always singular, even when it means many chairs and tables, so it takes 'has', not 'have'.","'the furniture has been damp since last night.' The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["is","are","has been","have been"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q4","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 4","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":4,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["question-tags"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"For each question from 1 to 10, choose the answer that best fits in the sentence(s).\n\"Let's work together for this project, __________?\" Lydia proposed.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["This is a question tag. Most tags repeat the helping verb of the sentence, but there are a few fixed ones to memorise.","Rule: a suggestion that begins with 'Let's' (let us) always takes the tag 'shall we?'.","Compare: an order such as 'Close the door' takes 'will you?'. 'will we' is tempting but is not used after 'Let's'.","\"Let's work together for this project, shall we?\" The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["shall we","may we","can we","will we"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q5","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 5","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":5,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["question-tags"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"For each question from 1 to 10, choose the answer that best fits in the sentence(s).\nThere's been a decrease in the amount of food wastage, __________?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["First work out what 'There's' stands for. It is followed by 'been', a past participle, so it is 'There **has** been', not 'There is been'.","Rule for question tags: use the same helping verb, and switch positive to negative (or negative to positive). The sentence is positive ('has been'), so the tag is negative: 'hasn't'.","The tag keeps 'there' as its subject. 'isn't there' is the trap for anyone who reads 'There's' as 'There is'.","'There's been a decrease ..., hasn't there?' The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["is there","isn't there","has there","hasn't there"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q6","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 6","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":6,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"For each question from 1 to 10, choose the answer that best fits in the sentence(s).\nPlease keep this secret between you and __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'between' is a preposition. Rule: a pronoun that follows a preposition must be in the object form (me, him, her, us, them).","Test with another preposition: we say 'for me' and 'with me', never 'for I'. So it is 'between you and me'.","'I' is the subject form and is the most common mistake here because 'you and I' sounds polite. 'myself' is only used when the subject is also 'I' ('I hurt myself'). 'mine' shows ownership.","The answer is option (2), me."]}],"options":["I","me","mine","myself"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q7","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 7","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":7,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"For each question from 1 to 10, choose the answer that best fits in the sentence(s).\nBy the time I got to school this morning, Candice __________ waiting there for me for 30 minutes.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["There are two past events: Candice started waiting, and later I got to school. 'By the time' + simple past ('got') tells us the other action began earlier and went on up to that moment.","Rule: for an action that continued up to a point in the past, with a length of time ('for 30 minutes'), use the past perfect continuous: had been + verb-ing.","'was waiting' cannot go with 'for 30 minutes' in this pattern. 'has been' and 'have been' connect to the present, but the whole sentence is about this morning, which is over.","'Candice had been waiting there for me for 30 minutes.' The answer is option (2)."]}],"options":["was","had been","has been","have been"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q8","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 8","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":8,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["subject-verb-agreement"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"For each question from 1 to 10, choose the answer that best fits in the sentence(s).\nNeither Jeremy nor his teammates __________ to school every morning.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Clue 1: 'every morning' describes a habit, so the verb is in the simple present tense. That rules out 'has walked' and 'have walked'.","Clue 2: with 'neither ... nor' (and 'either ... or'), the verb agrees with the subject nearest to it.","The nearest subject is 'his teammates', which is plural, so the verb has no 's': 'walk'.","'Neither Jeremy nor his teammates walk to school every morning.' The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["walk","walks","has walked","have walked"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q9","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 9","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":9,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["conditionals"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"For each question from 1 to 10, choose the answer that best fits in the sentence(s).\nHad I known about the traffic jam, I __________ a different route.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'Had I known' is another way of saying 'If I had known'. The 'if' is dropped and 'had' moves to the front.","This is a conditional about the past that did not happen: I did not know, so I did not take a different route.","Rule: If + had + past participle, ... would have + past participle.","'would take' is tempting, but it goes with 'If I knew' (an imagined present). 'Had I known ..., I would have taken a different route.' The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["will take","would take","will have taken","would have taken"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q10","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 10","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":10,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["participle-phrase"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"For each question from 1 to 10, choose the answer that best fits in the sentence(s).\n__________ the good news, my entire family celebrated with joy.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["The first part of the sentence has no subject of its own. It is a participle phrase that shares the subject of the main clause: 'my entire family'.","Two actions happened one after the other: first the family received the news, then they celebrated. Rule: use 'Having + past participle' for the earlier action done by the same subject.","'Received the good news' would be passive and would mean the family was received. 'To receive' shows purpose, and 'Receive' is a bare verb that cannot start the phrase.","'Having received the good news, my entire family celebrated with joy.' The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["Receive","Received","To receive","Having received"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q11","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 11","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":11,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"For each question from 11 to 15, choose the answer that best fits in the sentence(s).\nThe teacher __________ the students' artwork on the wall.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Clue: 'artwork on the wall'. We need a phrasal verb that means to fix something on a wall so that people can see it.","'put up' means to display or hang something (put up a poster, a notice, a picture).","'set aside' means to keep something for later use. 'turn down' means to reject or to lower the volume. 'brought about' means caused something to happen.","The answer is option (1), put up."]}],"options":["put up","set aside","turn down","brought about"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q12","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 12","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":12,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"For each question from 11 to 15, choose the answer that best fits in the sentence(s).\nHe __________ disobeyed the rules, knowing that his actions would have serious consequences.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Clue: 'knowing that his actions would have serious consequences'. He was fully aware and still chose to break the rules.","'wilfully' means deliberately, on purpose, even though you know it is wrong.","'unwittingly' is the opposite: without knowing or realising. 'impulsively' means suddenly, without thinking first, and 'blindly' means without thinking or questioning; neither fits someone who knew the consequences.","The answer is option (2), wilfully."]}],"options":["blindly","wilfully","impulsively","unwittingly"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q13","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 13","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":13,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["idioms"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"For each question from 11 to 15, choose the answer that best fits in the sentence(s).\nBeing a kind-hearted girl, Matilda would offer to help anyone __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clue: 'kind-hearted'. A kind-hearted girl helps readily, without needing to be asked twice.","'at the drop of a hat' means immediately, without hesitating.","'once in a blue moon' means very rarely, which is the opposite of what a kind girl would do. 'jump the gun' means to act too soon, and 'cut to the chase' means to get to the main point; these are verb phrases and do not fit after 'help anyone'.","The answer is option (3), at the drop of a hat."]}],"options":["jump the gun","cut to the chase","at the drop of a hat","once in a blue moon"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q14","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 14","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":14,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"For each question from 11 to 15, choose the answer that best fits in the sentence(s).\nThe residents were __________ when they saw that their house was completely burnt down by the fire, leaving them homeless.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clues: 'completely burnt down' and 'leaving them homeless'. This is a terrible loss, so we need the strongest word for sadness and shock.","'devastated' means extremely upset and shocked, as if your world has been destroyed.","'dejected' (low in spirits) and 'disappointed' (sad because a hope was not met) are far too mild for losing a home. 'disturbed' means worried or unsettled.","The answer is option (3), devastated."]}],"options":["dejected","disturbed","devastated","disappointed"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q15","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 15","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":15,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning","collocation"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"For each question from 11 to 15, choose the answer that best fits in the sentence(s).\nThe torrential rain began to __________ to a light drizzle by evening.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["All four words have something to do with becoming less, so think about which one is normally used for weather.","'subside' means to become calmer or less violent. It is the word used for storms, rain, floods, winds, pain and anger: 'the storm subsided'.","'reduce' needs a doer and an object (you reduce something). 'decrease' and 'lessen' are used for amounts and numbers going down, not for heavy rain calming into a drizzle.","'The torrential rain began to subside to a light drizzle.' The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["lessen","reduce","subside","decrease"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q16","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 16","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":16,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++cluster++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["A 'cluster' is a group of things close together.","The workshop was a cluster 'of tubes, levers and spinning wheels': many different things gathered in one place.","'collection' means a group of things gathered together, which matches. 'display' is tempting, but a display is arranged for people to look at, and nobody even knew what these things did.","The answer is option (4), collection."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g1","options":["unit","section","display","collection"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q17","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 17","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":17,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning","idioms"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++dead on his feet++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'dead on his feet' is an idiom. It describes someone so tired that he can hardly stay standing.","'exhausted' means extremely tired, which matches.","'wary' (careful because of possible danger) is tempting because Pip froze at the door, but that happened after he was described as dead on his feet. 'deprived' means lacking something needed.","The answer is option (4), exhausted."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g1","options":["wary","clumsy","deprived","exhausted"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q18","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 18","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":18,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++just barely++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'just barely' means only just, almost not at all.","The 'fraying wires' were 'just barely holding together': they were close to falling apart.","'scarcely' means hardly or only just, which matches. 'merely' means only or simply ('merely a child'); it does not carry the meaning of 'almost not'.","The answer is option (3), scarcely."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g1","options":["purely","merely","scarcely","carefully"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q19","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 19","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":19,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++reluctant++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'reluctant' means not wanting to do something.","Pip asked 'is that safe?' and did not want 'to step any closer' to the machine.","'unwilling' means not willing, which matches. 'unable' is the trap: Pip could step closer, he simply did not want to. 'unafraid' and 'undeterred' (not discouraged) are the opposite of how he felt.","The answer is option (3), unwilling."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g1","options":["unable","unafraid","unwilling","undeterred"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q20","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 20","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":20,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++dangerous++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'dangerous' means likely to cause harm.","The clue follows straight after: 'where's the fun if there's no risk?' The machine carries a risk.","'hazardous' means risky or dangerous (a hazard is a danger). 'averse' means strongly disliking something; it looks like 'adverse' but describes a person's feelings.","The answer is option (3), hazardous."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g1","options":["averse","unusual","hazardous","complicated"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q21","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 21","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":21,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"According to Text 1, what is the main purpose of the poster titled \"I CAN ACT AGAINST SCAMS!\"?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["The main purpose is what most of the poster is about, not one small part of it.","The title, the slogan 'Together, We Can Be Scam-Smart!', 'The 3-Step Guide' (Sense, Check, Act) and the Defence Tip all make people aware of scams and tell them what to do. That is option (4).","(2) is tempting because the workshop is on the poster, but the poster does not ask for volunteers and the workshop is only one section. (1) is wrong: there are no stories. (3) is wrong: the steps are what **the reader** should do, not what the organisations do.","The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g2","options":["to share stories of scam cases in Singapore","to recruit volunteers for the Young anti-Scam Ambassador Workshop","to show how the crime prevention organisations help the public deal with scams","to raise awareness about scams and teach people how to protect themselves from them"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q22","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 22","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":22,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"In the slogan \"Together, We Can Be Scam-Smart\", the use of the word \"Together\" primarily suggests that __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["'Together' means with one another. With 'We', it tells readers that everyone has a part to play.","'collective effort' means many people working as one, which is what 'Together, We' suggests. The poster title says '**I** can act' and the slogan adds that all of us acting makes everyone scam-smart.","(2) and (3) read too much into the word: nothing mentions the same training or living in the same place. (4) is wrong because the QR code is only for a quiz and lucky draw.","The answer is option (1)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g2","options":["collective effort is required to effectively fight scams","everyone must undergo the same training programme","scams can only be stopped if people live in the same community","the organisers and the public will deal with the scams by scanning the QR code"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q23","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 23","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":23,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience","inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"In Text 1, how does the design of the poster **most effectively persuade** the public to support the \"I CAN ACT AGAINST SCAMS!\" initiative?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["The key word is 'persuade'. People support a campaign when they trust the people behind it.","Right under the slogan are the words 'Jointly organised by' and the logos of the Singapore Police Force, the National Crime Prevention Council and SafeGuard. Seeing these official bodies makes readers believe the initiative is genuine, so they are willing to support it. That is option (2).","(3) is tempting, but big capital letters only catch attention; they do not give a reason to support the initiative. (1) is inaccurate: the guide is about sensing, checking and acting, and only the last step is about reporting. (4) is not true of the poster.","The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g2","options":["It includes a guide to explain the step-by-step process of reporting a scam.","It places the logos of authoritative organisations near the top to establish trust.","It uses bold, capitalised letters in the heading to demand the reader's immediate attention.","It uses a variety of font styles and sizes to distinguish between information meant for children and adults."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q24","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 24","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":24,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"In Text 2, what does Ms Sarah Lee suggest when she says that \"students are a crucial line of defence\"?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Read the whole sentence in Text 2: 'students are a crucial line of defence in protecting their families from the latest scam tactics.'","A 'line of defence' is something that protects, and 'crucial' means very important. So students can do something important to protect their families. That is option (2).","(1) goes too far: 'a' line of defence is not 'the only' one. (3) is contradicted later, when she says the campaign is not only for tech-savvy students. (4) also goes too far with 'all' and is not what she says.","The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g2","options":["Students are the only ones capable of identifying modern scam tactics.","Students can play an active role in protecting their family members from scams.","Students need to be tech-savvy to participate in the \"I Can Act Against Scams\" event.","Students should be the ones to report all suspicious activities to the Singapore Police Force."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-a-q25","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet A Question 25","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"a","number":25,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Which idea is expressed in both Text 1 and Text 2?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["The idea must appear in **both** texts, so check each option against each text.","Text 1 gives 'The 3-Step Guide': Sense, Check, Act, and says 'Be wary'. In Text 2, Ms Lee says they practised how to 'Sense, Check, and Act', and advises 'Always verify!' and 'staying vigilant'. Both tell people to stay alert and follow the steps. That is option (3).","(2) is tempting, but only Text 2 says role-playing was the most impactful part; Text 1 just lists it as an activity. (4) is the opposite of Text 2 ('open to everyone'). (1) is in neither text.","The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g2","options":["Scams are always easy to identify and avoid.","Role-playing is the most effective part of anti-scam workshops.","People should be alert and act against scams by following simple steps.","Only people who are good with technology can learn how to avoid scams."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q26","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 26","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":26,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (26). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"N","accept":["n","those"]},"explanation":["The sentence compares two sets of personalities: the dolphins' personalities and the personalities of humans.","To avoid repeating a plural noun, we use the pronoun 'those': 'much like those of humans' means 'much like the personalities of humans'.","For a singular noun we would use 'that' ('the brain of a dolphin is like that of a human'). 'personalities' is plural, so 'those'.","The answer is (N) those."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q27","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 27","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":27,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (27). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"E","accept":["e","from"]},"explanation":["Look at the verb: 'rescuing swimmers ___ shark attacks'.","The fixed pattern is rescue (or save, protect) someone **from** a danger.","The answer is (E) from: 'dolphins rescuing swimmers from shark attacks'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q28","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 28","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":28,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (28). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Q","accept":["q","who"]},"explanation":["The blank comes after a noun ('researchers') and before a verb ('study'). It starts a clause that tells us more about the researchers.","Rule: use the relative pronoun 'who' for people when it is the subject of the clause.","The answer is (Q) who: 'many researchers who study these creatures'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q29","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 29","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":29,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (29). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"F","accept":["f","however"]},"explanation":["The blank starts a sentence and is followed by a comma, so it is a linking word between two sentences.","Before: dolphins live in pods and rely on one another (their own kind). After: this close bond 'is not limited to their own species'. The second sentence adds something different and surprising, which is a contrast.","The connector for contrast that stands before a comma is 'However'. 'and' and 'unless' cannot be followed by a comma like this.","The answer is (F) however."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q30","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 30","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":30,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (30). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"A","accept":["a","across"]},"explanation":["'swimming ___ oceans just to interact with boats' needs a preposition of movement.","'across' means from one side to the other of a wide area. It stresses how far the dolphins travel 'just to' meet boats.","'into' does not fit because dolphins already live in the ocean. 'in oceans' would only say where they swim and would lose the idea of distance; 'in' is also needed elsewhere.","The answer is (A) across."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q31","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 31","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":31,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (31). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"B","accept":["b","and"]},"explanation":["'We should admire them from afar ___ avoid disrupting their natural habits.' There are two verbs after 'should': 'admire' and 'avoid'.","Two actions of the same kind, both things we should do, are joined with 'and'.","The answer is (B) and: 'We should admire them from afar and avoid disrupting their natural habits.'"]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q32","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 32","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":32,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (32). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"L","accept":["l","their"]},"explanation":["The blank comes before the noun 'habitats', so it needs a word that shows whose habitats they are.","The habitats belong to the dolphins (plural, 'them' in the sentence before), so the possessive is 'their'.","'its' is for one thing only. It is used later in the same sentence for 'pollution and its harmful effects', but it does not fit here.","The answer is (L) their."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q33","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 33","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":33,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (33). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"P","accept":["p","unless"]},"explanation":["The sentence has a condition and a result: '___ we take action to keep the oceans clean, these magnificent creatures will face a decline in population.'","The bad result happens if we do **not** take action. 'Unless' means 'if ... not'.","Check: 'If we do not take action ..., these creatures will face a decline.' It makes sense.","The answer is (P) unless."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q34","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 34","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":34,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (34). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"K","accept":["k","of"]},"explanation":["The fixed phrase is 'the responsibility **of** someone to do something'.","'It is the responsibility of everyone to ensure that dolphins continue to thrive.'","The answer is (K) of."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q35","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 35","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":35,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (35). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"D","accept":["d","every"]},"explanation":["The blank comes before a singular noun, 'effort', and the verb is 'will benefit'.","'Every' + singular noun means each one, without exception: every effort, however small, helps.","The answer is (D) every: 'Every effort to protect the oceans will benefit the world's marine life.'"]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q36","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 36","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":36,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (36) ++conducts++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"conduct"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. Look at the word in front: 'wanted **to** ___'.","Rule: after 'to', use the base form of the verb (the infinitive), with no -s, -ed or -ing.","The correct word is 'conduct': 'They wanted to conduct a study'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q37","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 37","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":37,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (37) ++ekuipment++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"equipment"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The sound /kw/ in English is almost always spelt 'qu' (quick, equal, require).","The word comes from 'equip' + 'ment'.","The correct spelling is 'equipment'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q38","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 38","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":38,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (38) ++tempreture++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"temperature"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. People often say the word quickly and drop a syllable.","Say it slowly in four parts: tem-per-a-ture. There is an 'e' after the 'p' and an 'a' before 'ture'.","The correct spelling is 'temperature'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q39","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 39","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":39,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (39) ++breadthtaking++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"breathtaking"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. Something so beautiful that it 'takes your breath away' is breath + taking.","'breadth' is a different word that means width. There is no 'd' in 'breath'.","The correct spelling is 'breathtaking'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q40","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 40","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":40,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (40) ++silence++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"silently"},"explanation":["This is a grammar (word form) error. The word describes **how** the icebergs floated.","Rule: a word that describes a verb is an adverb, and most adverbs end in -ly. 'silence' is a noun and 'silent' is an adjective.","The correct word is 'silently': 'Huge icebergs floated silently in the dark blue ocean'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q41","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 41","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":41,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (41) ++deestence++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"distance"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The scientists saw the bears far away: 'in the distance'.","The word is related to 'distant'. It starts with 'dis' and ends with 'ance': dis-tance.","The correct spelling is 'distance'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q42","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 42","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":42,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (42) ++behave++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"behaviour","accept":["behavior"]},"explanation":["This is a grammar (word form) error. Look at the word in front: 'observed **their** ___'.","Rule: after a possessive word (my, his, their), we need a noun. 'behave' is a verb; its noun is 'behaviour'.","The correct word is 'behaviour' (the American spelling 'behavior' means the same, but use 'behaviour' in school)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q43","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 43","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":43,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (43) ++far++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"further","accept":["farther"]},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. The clue is 'than': 'travel much ___ **than** usual'. 'than' shows that two things are being compared.","Rule: use the comparative form before 'than'. The comparative of 'far' is irregular: far, further (or farther), furthest.","The correct word is 'further'. 'farther' is also correct for distance."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q44","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 44","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":44,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (44) ++worry++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"worried"},"explanation":["This is a grammar (tense) error. The whole passage is about last month and uses the past tense: 'discovered', 'was melting'.","The subject 'This' (the fact that the ice was melting faster) did something to them in the past, so the verb must be in the simple past tense.","Change the 'y' to 'i' and add 'ed': the correct word is 'worried'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q45","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 45","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":45,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (45) ++successfuly++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"successfully"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The adjective is 'successful' (double c, double s, one l).","To make the adverb, add -ly to the whole word: successful + ly. That gives a double 'l'.","The correct spelling is 'successfully'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q46","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 46","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":46,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (46) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"live","accept":["survive"]},"explanation":["Look at what follows: 'for over 3,000 years'. That is a length of time, so the blank is about how long the trees stay alive.","After 'can' we need the base form of a verb. 'live for over 3,000 years' fits; 'survive' means the same here.","The answer is 'live'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q47","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 47","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":47,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (47) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"heights"},"explanation":["The clue is the measurement: 'of more than 80 metres'. For a tree, 80 metres is how tall it is.","The pattern is 'grow to heights of ...'. There is no 'a' before the blank, so the noun must be plural: 'heights', not 'height'.","The answer is 'heights'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q48","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 48","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":48,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (48) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"ability","accept":["capacity"]},"explanation":["'their incredible ___ to survive forest fires': after the adjective 'incredible' we need a noun, and it is followed by 'to' + verb.","The pattern is 'the ability to do something'. Surviving a fire is something these trees are able to do.","The answer is 'ability'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q49","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 49","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":49,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (49) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"protects","accept":["shields","guards"]},"explanation":["The clue is 'armour'. Armour keeps what is inside safe, and the pattern is 'protect something **from** damage'.","The subject 'This natural armour' is singular, and the passage states facts in the present tense, so the verb needs an 's'.","The answer is 'protects'. 'protect' without the 's' would be marked wrong."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q50","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 50","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":50,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (50) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"on","accept":["upon"]},"explanation":["The verb 'depend' is always followed by the preposition 'on' (or 'upon').","'giant sequoias actually depend on forest fires to reproduce.'","The answer is 'on'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q51","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 51","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":51,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (51) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"spread","accept":["release","scatter","disperse","drop"]},"explanation":["The heat 'causes their cones to open and ___ thousands of tiny seeds'. Think about what an opened cone does with its seeds: it lets them out so that they land on the ground around the tree.","'cause something **to** open and ___' means the blank is a base-form verb, matching 'open'.","The key's word is 'spread'. 'release', 'scatter' and 'disperse' also fit the meaning and the grammar.","The answer is 'spread'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q52","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 52","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":52,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (52) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"out"},"explanation":["'wipes ___ competing plants' is a phrasal verb. The fire destroys the other plants completely.","'wipe out' means to destroy or remove something completely.","The answer is 'out'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q53","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 53","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":53,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (53) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"deep","accept":["deeper","down","downwards","downward"]},"explanation":["The clue is the contrast 'spreads horizontally **instead of** growing ___ underground'. The opposite of spreading sideways is going far down.","The next sentence confirms it: the roots 'extend only about two metres down'. So they do not grow deep.","The answer is 'deep'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q54","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 54","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":54,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (54) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"in"},"explanation":["The fixed phrase is '**in** all directions' (also 'in every direction', 'in the opposite direction').","'they can spread over 30 metres in all directions.'","The answer is 'in'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q55","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 55","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":55,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (55) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"through","accept":["during","in"]},"explanation":["'helps the massive trees stay upright ___ storms and strong winds': we need a preposition that links staying upright with the time of the storms.","'through' means from the beginning to the end of something difficult: the trees stay standing all the way through a storm.","'during' also fits and is accepted.","The answer is 'through'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q56","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 56","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":56,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (56) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"never"},"explanation":["The clue is 'However', which signals a contrast with the sentence before: the trees grow 'incredibly slowly'.","The contrast to growing slowly is that the growing does not end: 'throughout their entire lifespan'. So they ___ stop growing must mean they do not stop at all.","The answer is 'never': 'they never stop growing throughout their entire lifespan'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q57","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 57","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":57,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (57) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"weigh"},"explanation":["The clue is the unit: 'as much as 2,000 tonnes'. Tonnes measure how heavy something is.","The subject 'Some of the largest specimens' is plural and the passage is in the present tense, so the verb has no 's'.","The answer is 'weigh'. Check the spelling: w-e-i-g-h ('weight' is the noun)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q58","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 58","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":58,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (58) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"that"},"explanation":["'Scientists believe ___ climate change must be slowed down': the part after the blank is a full clause that tells us what the scientists believe.","Rule: a clause that reports what someone believes, thinks, says or knows is introduced by 'that'.","The answer is 'that'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q59","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 59","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":59,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (59) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"threat","accept":["danger","risk"]},"explanation":["The clues are the verb 'poses', the adjective 'serious' and the preposition 'to': 'poses a serious ___ to these ancient giants'.","The fixed pattern is 'pose a threat to someone or something'. A threat is something likely to cause harm.","The answer is 'threat'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q60","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 60","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":60,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (60) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"harder","accept":["difficult","hard","tougher","tough","impossible"]},"explanation":["Higher temperatures and changing rainfall are bad for the trees, so the blank needs a negative idea: it will not be easy for young sequoias to survive.","The pattern is 'make it + adjective + for someone to do something'.","'harder' (more difficult than now) fits best. 'difficult' is also accepted.","The answer is 'harder'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q61","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 61","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":61,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["so-that"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The Environment Monitors put up eye-catching posters. Every student will remember to recycle their paper waste.\n____________________ so that ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The Environment Monitors put up eye-catching posters so that every student will remember to recycle their paper waste.","accept":["The Environment Monitors put up eye-catching posters so that every student would remember to recycle their paper waste."]},"explanation":["'so that' joins an action to its **purpose**. The action comes first, and the purpose comes after 'so that'.","Ask: which sentence is the action, and which is the aim? The monitors put up posters (action) in order to make every student remember (purpose).","Write the first sentence, then 'so that', then the second sentence with a small letter for 'every'. Do not swap the order, or the meaning changes.","Answer: The Environment Monitors put up eye-catching posters so that every student will remember to recycle their paper waste."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q62","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 62","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":62,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["whereas"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Cindy prefers designing digital animations. Her cousins are fond of building Lego structures.\n____________________ whereas ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Cindy prefers designing digital animations whereas her cousins are fond of building Lego structures.","accept":["Her cousins are fond of building Lego structures whereas Cindy prefers designing digital animations."]},"explanation":["'whereas' joins two statements that are in contrast. It means the same as 'while' or 'but' when comparing two people or things.","Keep both statements exactly as they are and put 'whereas' between them. Change the capital 'H' of 'Her' to a small letter.","Do not add 'but' or 'however'; 'whereas' already shows the contrast.","Answer: Cindy prefers designing digital animations whereas her cousins are fond of building Lego structures."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q63","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 63","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":63,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["reported-speech"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"\"You must complete the prototype by this Monday!\" Mr Chen informed the students.\nMr Chen informed the students ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Mr Chen informed the students that they had to complete the prototype by that Monday."},"explanation":["This is direct speech changed to reported speech. Start the reported part with 'that' and drop the quotation marks and the exclamation mark.","Pronoun: 'You' is the students, so it becomes 'they'.","Verb: the reporting verb 'informed' is in the past, so the verb moves back. 'must' (for an order) becomes 'had to'.","Time and pointing words change: 'this Monday' becomes 'that Monday'.","Answer: Mr Chen informed the students that they had to complete the prototype by that Monday."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q64","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 64","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":64,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["noun-form"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The Parents Association was very generous. More students benefited from the financial assistance provided.\nThe Parents Association's ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The Parents Association's generosity enabled more students to benefit from the financial assistance provided.","accept":["The Parents Association's generosity allowed more students to benefit from the financial assistance provided.","The Parents Association's generosity resulted in more students benefiting from the financial assistance provided.","The Parents Association's generosity led to more students benefiting from the financial assistance provided."]},"explanation":["The starter ends with an apostrophe-s, so the next word must be a **noun**. Change the adjective 'generous' to its noun: 'generosity'. (Spelling: gener-o-s-i-t-y, with no 'u'.)","'very' cannot describe a noun, so drop it. You may write 'great generosity', but it is not needed.","The generosity is the cause, and more students benefiting is the result. Link them with a verb such as 'enabled', 'allowed' (+ more students **to benefit**) or 'resulted in', 'led to' (+ more students **benefiting**).","Keep the past tense. Answer: The Parents Association's generosity enabled more students to benefit from the financial assistance provided."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q65","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 65","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":65,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["passive-voice","reported-speech"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Mrs Ling told her students that they would be late for dismissal if they did not pack their bags quickly.\nThe students ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The students were told by Mrs Ling that they would be late for dismissal if they did not pack their bags quickly."},"explanation":["The sentence now starts with 'The students', who received the action of telling. That is the passive voice: be + past participle.","'told' is in the past tense and 'The students' is plural, so the verb is 'were told'.","The doer comes after 'by': 'by Mrs Ling'. 'her students' becomes the subject 'The students'.","The rest of the sentence is already reported speech, so keep it unchanged.","Answer: The students were told by Mrs Ling that they would be late for dismissal if they did not pack their bags quickly."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q66","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 66","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":66,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"structured","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"What was Aoto doing when the police officer found him?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Aoto was wandering near the highway when the police officer found him."},"explanation":["Find the police officer's words in paragraph 1: 'I found him wandering near the highway.'","The answer needs the action (wandering) and the place (near the highway).","Do not write only 'He seemed a little lost'; that is how he looked, not what he was doing."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q67","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 67","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":67,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","factual"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"\"Kenji nodded sympathetically at his mother's struggles.\" (lines 15-16, paragraph 3). Explain fully what the \"struggles\" refer to.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Kenji's mother cared for Aoto (her father) every day, and on many nights she forced herself to stay awake so that she could stop him from leaving his bed and wandering away."},"explanation":["'Explain fully' and 2 marks mean two points are needed. Read the sentences just before the quoted line in paragraph 3.","Point 1: 'She had been caring for her father every day'.","Point 2: 'many nights she forced herself to stay awake, listening carefully in case he tried to leave his bed again' because 'he might wander off'.","Make clear who 'her father' is (Aoto, Kenji's grandfather). Writing only that she was 'exhausted' gives the result of the struggles, not the struggles themselves."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q68","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 68","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":68,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["true-false-with-reason"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on lines 7 - 44 (paragraphs 2 to 8), state whether each statement in the table below is true or false, then give one reason why you think so.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Aoto's illness caused him to forget what time it was.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. The illness (Alzheimer's disease) often caused Aoto to forget where he was or what time it was."},"explanation":["Paragraph 2: 'Aoto suffered from Alzheimer's disease. The illness often caused him to forget where he was or what time it was.'","'forget ... what time it was' matches the statement directly, so it is True.","The school's key gives a different reason: Aoto sometimes got out of bed and wandered around the house in the middle of the night. That shows the effect of not knowing the time, so it is accepted as well. The sentence quoted above is the most direct evidence."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Kenji got the idea for his invention while visiting his parents' laboratory.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. Kenji got the idea while checking on his grandfather (Aoto) one evening, when he saw his grandfather's feet press against the floor."},"explanation":["Paragraph 5: 'One evening, while checking on his grandfather, Kenji noticed something important ... his feet pressed against the floor. Suddenly, an idea formed in Kenji's mind.'","The laboratory (paragraph 4) is where his love for science and technology grew, not where the idea came. That is the trap.","So the statement is False. The reason must say where or how he really got the idea."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"Kenji tested the invention on his father while he slept.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. Kenji tested the invention on Aoto, his grandfather, at bedtime."},"explanation":["Paragraph 8: 'That night, Kenji tested the invention. He helped Aoto put on the sock at bedtime.'","Aoto is Kenji's grandfather, not his father. His father only 'tried it on' earlier (paragraph 7) and felt how comfortable it was; that was not the test of whether it worked.","So the statement is False, and the reason is that the test was on his grandfather, Aoto."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q69","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 69","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":69,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["referencing"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Look at the table below. What do the words in the left column refer to in the passage? Write your answer on the right.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"there (line 19, paragraph 4)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"His (Kenji's) parents' laboratory.","accept":["his parents' laboratory","Kenji's parents' laboratory","his parents laboratory","Kenji's parents laboratory","the laboratory of Kenji's parents"]},"explanation":["'there' points back to a place already mentioned. Read the sentences before it in paragraph 4.","'Kenji loved visiting his parents' laboratory ... Many hours were spent **there**, experimenting with small gadgets.'","Test by replacing the word: 'Many hours were spent at his parents' laboratory.' It makes sense.","The answer is his parents' laboratory."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"he (line 34, paragraph 6)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Kenji's father.","accept":["Kenji's father","Kenjis father","the father of Kenji"]},"explanation":["Read the sentence before: 'Some days, Kenji's father would find him poring over his notes, shoulders slumped. \"Stuck again?\" **he** would ask.'","Two males are in the sentence, Kenji and his father. The one asking the question is the one who found Kenji stuck, and Kenji is the one who replies 'Not for long.'","The answer is Kenji's father. Do not write only 'his father'; say whose father."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"prototype (line 37, paragraph 7)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Kenji's sock with a pressure sensor attached to the bottom.","accept":["the sock with a pressure sensor attached to the bottom","a sock with a pressure sensor attached to the bottom","Kenji's sock with a pressure sensor attached to the bottom"]},"explanation":["A prototype is the first working model of an invention. Here the word points forward: the next sentences say what it was.","'To his father, it looked just like a regular sock. What he did not know was that it was a sock with a pressure sensor attached to the bottom.'","Give the full description. 'A sock' alone is not enough, because the sensor is what makes it the invention.","The answer is the sock with a pressure sensor attached to the bottom."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q70","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 70","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":70,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on the passage, fill in the blanks below to show the cause-and-effect relationship in Kenji's invention process.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Cause: ____________________\nEffect: Kenji spent a lot of time experimenting with small gadgets.","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Kenji loved science and technology.","accept":["Kenji loved science and technology","Kenji loved visiting his parents' laboratory","He loved science and technology","He loved visiting his parents' laboratory"]},"explanation":["Find the effect in paragraph 4: 'Many hours were spent there, experimenting with small gadgets.'","The cause is in the sentences just before: 'Kenji loved visiting his parents' laboratory. This greatly influenced his love for science and technology.'","Because he loved science and technology (and loved visiting the laboratory), he spent a lot of time experimenting. Either way of putting it gives the cause."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Cause: The sensor was very thin.\nEffect: ____________________","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The sock was comfortable to wear.","accept":["The sock was comfortable to wear","The sock was comfortable","The sock felt comfortable","The sock was very comfortable"]},"explanation":["Find the cause in paragraph 7. Kenji says, 'That's because the sensor is so thin!'","'That' points back to the sentence before: 'His father tried it on and was amazed at how comfortable it felt.'","So the thin sensor is the cause, and the sock feeling comfortable is the effect."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q71","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 71","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":71,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["evidence-phrase","character-and-feelings"],"format":"short","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in the following table with two-word phrases from Paragraph 6 that show Kenji's emotions when he faced challenges with his invention.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"discouraged","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"text","value":"shoulders slumped"},"explanation":["Paragraph 6 is the one that begins 'Excited by his idea'. Look for body language, because feelings are often shown through how a person looks.","'Kenji's father would find him poring over his notes, **shoulders slumped**.' Drooping shoulders show a person who is losing heart, which is discouraged.","Copy exactly two words: 'shoulders slumped'."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"stressed","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"text","value":"brows furrowed"},"explanation":["'Hardly glancing up, his **brows furrowed**, Kenji would reply'. Furrowed brows are eyebrows drawn tightly together, a sign of worry, strain or deep concentration, which is stressed.","'Stuck again?' is also two words, but those are his father's words, not something that shows Kenji's emotion.","Copy exactly two words: 'brows furrowed'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q72","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 72","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":72,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"When Kenji started to work on his prototype, why did he find that \"turning his idea into reality was not easy\" (line 28, paragraph 6)?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"He needed to find a sensor that was small and comfortable enough to wear, which took a long time, and he had to create a system that could (reliably) send a wireless signal to a smartphone, but the signal sometimes did not reach the phone."},"explanation":["2 marks: give the two difficulties that follow the quoted words in paragraph 6.","Difficulty 1: 'He needed a sensor that was small and comfortable enough to wear. It took a long time to find the perfect one.'","Difficulty 2: 'Then, there was another uphill battle - he had to create a system that could send a wireless signal to a smartphone. The signal sometimes did not reach the phone'.","'another uphill battle' tells you there are two separate problems. Give both: the sensor and the wireless signal system."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q73","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 73","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":73,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["character-and-feelings","evidence-phrase"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Refer to lines 27 - 41 (paragraphs 6 and 7).","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"(a) Tick ONE positive trait Kenji possesses: courageous, empathetic, generous, resilient.\n(Type the word you would tick.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"text","value":"resilient"},"explanation":["Know the four words. 'resilient': able to keep going and recover after difficulties. 'courageous': brave in the face of danger. 'empathetic': able to understand how others feel. 'generous': willing to give.","Paragraphs 6 and 7 are about the problems Kenji met while building the sock and how he kept working: 'another uphill battle', 'Stuck again?', 'Not for long', 'He refused to give up', 'After many months of experimenting'.","That is resilience. 'empathetic' is tempting because Kenji felt for his mother, but that is in paragraph 3, outside the lines given. There is no danger (courageous) or giving (generous) in these lines.","The answer is 'resilient'."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"(b) Support your answer with evidence from the passage.","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"He refused to give up despite the difficulties (when he was stuck and the signal sometimes did not reach the phone) and kept experimenting for many months until he created a working prototype."},"explanation":["The evidence must match the trait chosen in (a) and come from lines 27 to 41.","Best evidence: 'Stuck again?' ... 'Not for long.' He refused to give up. And: 'After many months of experimenting, Kenji finally created a working prototype.'","A good answer says what the difficulty was and what Kenji did about it: he kept trying and did not give up."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q74","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 74","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":74,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"How do we know that Kenji was humble enough to continue to make his product better?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Kenji thanked the caregivers for their feedback (that some of the elderly did not like wearing socks to sleep), and even after receiving several awards for his invention, he continued to improve it."},"explanation":["Look in the last paragraph. A humble person does not think his work is already perfect and is willing to listen to others.","Evidence 1: when caregivers told him some of the elderly did not like wearing socks to sleep, 'Kenji thanked them for their feedback.' He was not offended by the criticism.","Evidence 2: 'Even though Kenji later received several awards for his invention, he continued to improve it.' Awards did not make him proud.","Link the evidence to 'humble': he accepted feedback and kept improving in spite of his success."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-b-q75","cite":"2026 Prelim Henry Park Paper 2 Booklet B Question 75","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Henry Park","section":"b","number":75,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Explain clearly why Kenji thought that \"his small idea had made a big impact\".","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"His idea was a small and simple one, a sock with a sensor that alerted caregivers whenever an elderly person got out of bed. Yet it helped to keep many elderly people and patients safe, and it allowed their caregivers (such as his mother, other families and elderly care centres) to rest more peacefully."},"explanation":["2 marks: explain both halves of the quotation, the 'small idea' and the 'big impact'.","Small idea: it began with one simple observation (his grandfather's feet pressing on the floor) and became something as ordinary as a sock with a sensor, which sends a message to a phone when the wearer steps out of bed.","Big impact: it helped 'many people's lives'. His mother 'could rest more peacefully at night', and 'Caregivers from other families and many elderly care centres approached Kenji to make his special socks for their family members or patients.'","Say who was helped (the elderly and their caregivers, many of them) and how (kept safe from wandering off; caregivers could rest)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":1,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["infinitives-and-gerunds"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Ahmad saw a robber __________ a neighbour's house while he was out for a walk.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Rule: after a verb of the senses (see, hear, watch, feel) + a person or thing, use the base form of the verb (or the -ing form): 'I saw him **leave**', 'We heard her **sing**'.","'saw' already carries the past tense, so the second verb does not take a tense. That rules out 'enters', 'entered' and 'has entered'.","'entered' is tempting because the sentence is in the past, but 'saw a robber entered' is wrong.","'Ahmad saw a robber enter a neighbour's house.' The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["enter","enters","entered","has entered"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":2,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The team __________ for hours when the coach called for a break.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Two past events: the training started first and was still going on when the coach called for a break.","Rule: for an action that had been going on for some time ('for hours') up to another past moment, use the past perfect continuous: had been + verb-ing.","'has been training' is the present form and does not match the past verb 'called'. 'had trained' is tempting, but it does not show that the action continued over those hours until the break.","'The team had been training for hours when the coach called for a break.' The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["train","had trained","had been training","has been training"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q3","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 3","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":3,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["question-tags"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"He's been an actor in a play, __________ ?\" said Lisa.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["First work out what 'He's' stands for. It is followed by 'been', a past participle, so it is 'He **has** been', not 'He is'.","Rule: a question tag repeats the helping verb of the statement. A positive statement takes a negative tag.","Helping verb 'has', positive statement, so the tag is 'hasn't he'.","'isn't he' is the trap for pupils who read 'He's' as 'He is'. The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["isn't he","was he","hasn't he","wasn't he"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q4","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 4","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":4,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"I was not sure if Henry and Betty could complete the difficult task by __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'by + reflexive pronoun' means alone, without help. The reflexive pronoun must match the people doing the action.","Who would complete the task? 'Henry and Betty', two people who are not the speaker, so the pronoun is 'they' and its reflexive form is 'themselves'.","'myself' is tempting because the sentence begins with 'I', but 'I' is only the one who was not sure, not the one doing the task.","The answer is option (4), themselves."]}],"options":["myself","himself","ourselves","themselves"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q5","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 5","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":5,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["subject-verb-agreement","tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The flock of crows __________ becoming a nuisance, which forced my father to call the authorities.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Find the real subject. In 'The flock of crows', the head word is 'flock', which is one group, so the verb is singular. 'crows' only tells us what kind of flock. That rules out 'are' and 'were'.","Now the tense. The second half says 'which **forced** my father', which is past, so the first verb must be past too.","Singular and past gives 'was'. 'were' is the trap for pupils who make the verb agree with the nearest noun, 'crows'.","'The flock of crows was becoming a nuisance.' The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["is","are","was","were"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q6","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 6","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":6,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["quantifiers"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"There was __________ warning before the rollercoaster suddenly plunged downwards, causing my sister to scream.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'warning' here is an uncountable noun (we say 'There **was** ... warning'), so 'few', which goes with countable plural nouns, is out.","Meaning: the rollercoaster plunged 'suddenly' and the sister screamed, so there was hardly any warning.","'little' means almost none and goes with uncountable nouns. 'much' and 'enough' would mean there was plenty of warning, which does not fit 'suddenly'.","'There was little warning.' The answer is option (2)."]}],"options":["few","little","much","enough"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q7","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 7","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":7,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Surrounded by strangers at the party, the shy little girl hid __________ her parents the whole time.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["The girl was shy and surrounded by strangers, so she used her parents as a shield. To hide **behind** someone is to stay at the back of them so that others cannot see you.","'hid from her parents' would mean she did not want her parents to find her, which does not fit a child frightened of strangers.","'beside' means next to, where she would still be seen. 'among' is for a group of three or more.","The answer is option (2), behind."]}],"options":["from","behind","beside","among"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q8","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 8","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":8,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The man was arrested for driving __________ the influence of alcohol.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["This is a fixed phrase: **under the influence of** something means affected or controlled by it.","We say 'driving under the influence of alcohol' and 'under the influence of his friends'.","'with', 'on' and 'against' do not go with 'the influence of' to give this meaning.","The answer is option (3), under."]}],"options":["on","with","under","against"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q9","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 9","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":9,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["passive-voice"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Did the famous director choose all the movies __________ at the film festival last year?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["The blank describes 'the movies'. The full form is 'all the movies **that were shown** at the film festival'.","Movies do not show anything; they are shown by people. That is the passive, which needs the past participle. When 'that were' is dropped, only the past participle stays: 'the movies shown'.","The past participle of 'show' is 'shown'. 'showed' is the simple past form, and 'showing' would mean the movies were doing the showing.","The answer is option (2), shown."]}],"options":["show","shown","showed","showing"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q10","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 10","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":10,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["connectors","conditionals"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"I realised that I would have failed __________ my mother's good advice. It was a good thing I listened to her.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["'would have failed' talks about something that did **not** happen. The second sentence confirms it: the speaker listened to the advice, so he did not fail.","'if not for' means 'if it had not been for' or 'without': without my mother's advice, I would have failed.","'because of' would mean the advice caused the failure. 'in spite of' would mean he failed even though he had good advice. Both go against the second sentence.","The answer is option (1), if not for."]}],"options":["if not for","in spite of","except for","because of"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q11","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 11","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":11,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Most shopping malls in Singapore are __________ by MRT or bus.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clue: 'by MRT or bus' tells us how people get to the malls.","'accessible' means able to be reached or entered: a place is accessible by train, by road or on foot.","'available' means ready to be used or bought, and is not used with 'by MRT'. 'attainable' is for goals that can be achieved. 'approachable' describes a friendly person who is easy to talk to.","The answer is option (3), accessible."]}],"options":["available","attainable","accessible","approachable"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q12","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 12","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":12,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Arun is a/an __________ team member. When his group did not have enough materials for their Science project, he came up with the idea of using plastic bottles to complete their model.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Clue: the group lacked materials and Arun 'came up with the idea of using plastic bottles'. He solved a problem with what was at hand.","'resourceful' means good at finding clever ways to deal with difficulties, especially when you have little to work with.","'industrious' means hardworking, but nothing says he worked hard. 'collaborative' means working well with others, and 'exceptional' means outstanding in general; neither points to finding a clever solution.","The answer is option (1), resourceful."]}],"options":["resourceful","industrious","exceptional","collaborative"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q13","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 13","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":13,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"While we cannot say for sure, the time needed to recover after a serious case of food poisoning is __________ one week,\" added the doctor.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Clue: 'While we cannot say for sure'. The doctor is giving a usual figure, not a precise one.","'generally' means usually, in most cases.","'exactly' and 'specifically' both mean precisely, which contradicts 'cannot say for sure'. 'constantly' means all the time, without stopping.","The answer is option (2), generally."]}],"options":["exactly","generally","constantly","specifically"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q14","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 14","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":14,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"After applying some ointment on my injured elbow, the pain slowly __________. I was able to bend my arm again after some time.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Clue: the writer could bend the arm again, so the pain went away bit by bit.","'wear off' is used for a feeling or an effect that gradually disappears: the pain wore off, the medicine wore off.","'wear on' means time passes slowly ('as the day wore on'). 'wear out' means to become useless from use, or to make someone very tired. 'wear away' is for a surface that becomes thinner through rubbing, like stone steps.","The answer is option (2), wore off."]}],"options":["wore on","wore off","wore out","wore away"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q15","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 15","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":15,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning","collocation"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Marine biologists are studying the long term __________ of a tsunami on coastal habitats.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Clues: 'long term' and 'on coastal habitats'. We need a word for the effects that something is likely to have on something else in the future.","'implications' means the possible effects or consequences of an event. It is used in the pattern 'the implications of X on/for Y'.","'results' and 'outcomes' are tempting, but they name how something finally turns out (the result of a match, the outcome of a meeting) and are not followed by 'on' something. 'inferences' are conclusions a person works out from clues.","The answer is option (4), implications."]}],"options":["results","outcomes","inferences","implications"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q16","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 16","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":16,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++entangled++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["To be 'entangled' in something is to be caught and twisted up in it so that you cannot get free, like a turtle caught in a fishing net.","In the passage, sea animals 'become entangled in plastic waste', which harms them because they cannot escape.","'trapped' means caught and unable to get away, which matches.","'buried', 'hidden' and 'concealed' are all about being covered or out of sight, not about being unable to move. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g1","options":["buried","hidden","trapped","concealed"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q17","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 17","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":17,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++alarming++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'alarming' means causing worry or fear.","The writer heard that 'millions of tonnes of plastic enter the oceans every year', news that is worrying and upsetting.","'distressing' means causing worry or upset, which matches.","'surprising' is tempting, but a surprise can be pleasant and carries no worry. 'stunning' usually means very impressive, and 'depressing' means making you feel sad and hopeless. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g1","options":["stunning","surprising","distressing","depressing"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q18","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 18","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":18,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++took up++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["To 'take up' a habit or a hobby is to start doing it.","The writer and Ben 'took up simple habits, such as bringing reusable water bottles': they started these new habits after the talk.","'adopted' means started to use or follow something new (to adopt a habit), which matches.","'practised' is tempting, but it means doing something again and again, not starting it. 'utilised' means made use of a thing. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g1","options":["utilised","adopted","practised","developed"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q19","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 19","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":19,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++important++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'important' here means mattering a lot, having a big effect.","Just before this, the teacher said that 'every small action mattered'. These changes matter because they 'would help reduce plastic waste'.","'significant' means important and having a noticeable effect, which matches.","'essential' is tempting, but it is stronger: it means absolutely necessary, something you cannot do without. 'prominent' means well known or easily seen. The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g1","options":["lasting","essential","prominent","significant"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q20","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 20","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":20,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++consistently++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'consistently' means in the same way every time, without changing.","'Since then, I have consistently avoided single-use plastics': the writer has kept the habit up all the time since the talk.","'regularly' means happening again and again as a habit, which is closest.","'intermittently' is the opposite: stopping and starting. 'deliberately' means on purpose. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g1","options":["properly","regularly","deliberately","intermittently"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q21","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 21","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":21,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Ready for our school's Park Run?\" Why did the writer ask this question in Text 1?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["The question is the big heading of Text 1. A heading that speaks directly to the reader in a lively way is there to catch attention and build excitement.","(1) is wrong because the purpose is given separately under 'Objectives of the run'.","(3) is tempting, but pupils are not being invited: Text 1 gives a reporting time and tells Primary 3 to 6 pupils where to assemble, so the run is for all of them. There is nothing to sign up for.","The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g2","options":["to share the purpose of the run","to get pupils excited before the run","to invite pupils to take part in the run","to mentally prepare pupils for the run"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q22","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 22","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":22,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following statements is true of the run?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Check each statement against Text 1.","(1) is false: it is the '5th Annual Park Run'. (2) is false: pupils assemble in school and then set off for 'Elizabeth Gardens', where the run is held. (3) is false: 'Primary 1 and 2 pupils will remain in school for the Sports Carnival'.","(4) is true: the route map shows two start lines (one for Primary 3, one for Primary 4-6) but only one 'FINISH LINE'.","The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g2","options":["This is the first year it is being held.","The venue is West Coast Primary School.","Pupils from Primary 1 to 6 will participate in it.","All pupils will complete the run at the same point."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q23","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 23","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":23,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Based on text 2, which of the following is ++not++ mentioned as a benefit of taking part in the park run?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Look for each option in Text 2.","'we are building our endurance and growing stronger, both physically and mentally' covers (1) and (2). 'feel a sense of pride' covers (4).","Medals and prizes are never mentioned. In fact the writer says 'Winning is not the only goal.'","The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g2","options":["building endurance","growing mentally stronger","winning medals and prizes","feeling a sense of pride for the school"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q24","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 24","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":24,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"In text 2, what does the writer mean by \"part of something big and exciting\"?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Read the whole sentence: 'Running with our classmates makes us feel like we are part of something big and exciting.' Earlier the writer says the run 'is about coming together as a school community'.","So 'something big' is the event shared by many people, and being 'part of' it means belonging to that community.","(1) is wrong on two counts: the writer says it 'is not just about running' and winning is not the only goal, so it is not simply a race, and Primary 1 and 2 do not run, so it is not the full school. (3) is about the size of the park, which is not what 'big' refers to.","The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g2","options":["Pupils are part of a race as a full school.","Pupils are excited to run with their friends.","Pupils are running in a famous park that is big.","Pupils are taking part in an exciting event as a community."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-a-q25","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 25","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"a","number":25,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Which idea is expressed in ++both++ Text 1 and Text 2?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["The idea must appear in both texts, so start with Text 1, which says less. Its objectives are 'to promote the physical well-being of pupils' and 'to foster school spirit'.","Text 2 says the same two things: 'it's a great way to stay fit' (fitness) and 'coming together as a school community', 'we learn to support one another' (bonds).","(1), (2) and (4) are found only in Text 2: cheering one another on, 'memories that will last a lifetime' and 'a sense of achievement'.","The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g2","options":["Pupils should encourage peers who are slow runners.","Running with friends helps to create lasting memories.","Running as a school improves fitness and builds bonds.","Pupils get a sense of achievement from completing a run."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q26","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 26","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":26,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (26). (Type its letter, A to R.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"B","accept":["b","an"]},"explanation":["'revamp' is a singular countable noun mentioned for the first time, so it needs 'a' or 'an'.","The article goes with the sound of the very next word. The next word is 'extensive', which begins with a vowel sound, so we use 'an'.","The answer is (B) an: 'the museum underwent an extensive revamp'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q27","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 27","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":27,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (27). (Type its letter, A to R.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Q","accept":["q","who"]},"explanation":["The blank begins a clause that tells us more about 'the prisoners of war (POWs) and civilians'.","They are people, and they are the subject of 'were held', so the relative pronoun is 'who'. 'which' is for things and animals.","The answer is (Q) who: 'civilians who were held in the Changi area'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q28","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 28","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":28,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (28). (Type its letter, A to R.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"E","accept":["e","by"]},"explanation":["'was built' is in the passive voice. The doer of the action in a passive sentence is introduced with 'by'.","Who built the chapel? The POWs. The next sentence confirms it: they 'used whatever materials they could find ... to construct a place of worship'.","'for' is tempting, but it would mean someone else built it for them. The answer is (E) by: 'built by POWs'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q29","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 29","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":29,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (29). (Type its letter, A to R.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"C","accept":["c","could"]},"explanation":["After the blank is the base verb 'find', so the blank needs a modal verb. The modals in the list are 'could' and 'should'.","The sentence is about the past ('provided') and about what the prisoners were able to do there, so we need the past form of 'can'.","'should' shows advice or duty and is needed for blank (34). The answer is (C) could: 'where prisoners could find comfort'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q30","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 30","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":30,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (30). (Type its letter, A to R.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"F","accept":["f","for"]},"explanation":["The blank links 'a quiet space' to 'reflection', which is what the space is used for.","'for' shows purpose: a room for meetings, a space for reflection.","The answer is (F) for: 'a quiet space for reflection'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q31","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 31","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":31,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (31). (Type its letter, A to R.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"N","accept":["n","to"]},"explanation":["This is a fixed phrase: a place is **home to** something when that thing is found or kept there.","For example: 'The zoo is home to more than 300 kinds of animals.'","The answer is (N) to: 'is home to a massive collection'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q32","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 32","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":32,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (32). (Type its letter, A to R.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"J","accept":["j","its"]},"explanation":["The blank comes before 'own story', so it needs a possessive word.","The owner is 'Every artefact'. 'Every' makes the noun singular, and an artefact is a thing, so the possessive is 'its'.","'their' is tempting, but it is for plural owners. The answer is (J) its: 'Every artefact has its own story'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q33","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 33","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":33,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (33). (Type its letter, A to R.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"R","accept":["r","p","which"]},"explanation":["The blank begins a clause that tells us more about the 'story of resilience': the story ___ has been preserved.","A story is a thing, so the relative pronoun is 'which'. 'who' is for people and is needed for blank (27).","The answer is 'which': 'its own story of resilience which has been preserved for future generations'.","In the word list this word is printed with the letter (R). The school's answer key calls it P, so type R (P is accepted too)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q34","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 34","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":34,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (34). (Type its letter, A to R.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"L","accept":["l","should"]},"explanation":["After the blank is the base verb 'make', so the blank needs a modal verb.","The writer is recommending the museum to history lovers. 'should' is used to give advice or a recommendation.","'could' is already used for blank (29) and only says it is possible. The answer is (L) should: 'Anyone who is interested in history should make a visit'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q35","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 35","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":35,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (35). (Type its letter, A to R.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"D","accept":["d","between"]},"explanation":["Look at what follows: 'the painful past **and** the peaceful present'. Two things joined by 'and'.","A gap is always 'between' two things: 'bridge the gap between A and B'.","The answer is (D) between."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q36","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 36","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":36,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (36) ++turnind++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"turning"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. Say the sentence aloud: 'As he was ___ twenty-one'. The word we hear is 'turning'.","Rule: was/were + verb-ing makes the past continuous. The ending is spelt -ing.","turn + ing = turning. 'To turn twenty-one' means to reach the age of twenty-one.","The correct word is 'turning'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q37","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 37","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":37,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (37) ++documentary++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"document"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. Look at the word in front: 'to ___ the day'. After 'to' we need a verb in its base form.","'documentary' is a noun (a film about real events). The verb is 'document', which means to record something in writing, photographs or film.","'She borrowed a video camera to document the day.'","The correct word is 'document'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q38","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 38","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":38,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (38) ++sellerbrait++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"celebrate"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. Sound it out: sel-uh-brayt. The word is what people do on a birthday.","It begins with a soft 'c' (as in 'cell'), not 's', and ends in '-ate' (as in 'gate').","c-e-l-e-b-r-a-t-e. A related word helps: celebr**ation**.","The correct word is 'celebrate'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q39","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 39","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":39,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (39) ++aknowleged++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"acknowledged"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means showed that he had noticed and was thankful for the gift.","Build it from 'knowledge': ac + knowledge + d. Two letters are easy to miss: the 'c' before the 'k', and the 'd' before the 'g'.","a-c-k-n-o-w-l-e-d-g-e-d.","The correct word is 'acknowledged'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q40","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 40","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":40,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (40) ++jescher++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"gesture"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. Sound it out: jes-cher. A gesture is an action done to show a feeling, here the kind act of giving a ten-dollar note and an apple.","The 'j' sound is spelt with a soft 'g' (as in 'gentle'), and the 'cher' sound at the end is spelt '-ture' (as in 'picture', 'nature').","g-e-s-t-u-r-e.","The correct word is 'gesture'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q41","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 41","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":41,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (41) ++By++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"In"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong preposition.","The fixed phrase is **in unison**, which means all together at the same time.","'In unison, they sang 'Happy Birthday' for the camera.' Start with a capital letter because it begins the sentence.","The correct word is 'In'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q42","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 42","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":42,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (42) ++explain++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"explanation"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. Look at the word in front: 'Andrea's ___'. After a possessive (Andrea's, his, my) we need a noun.","'explain' is a verb. Its noun form is 'explanation'.","Watch the spelling: the 'i' of 'explain' is dropped. expla**n**ation, not 'explaination'.","The correct word is 'explanation'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q43","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 43","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":43,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (43) ++reseet++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"receipt"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. Sound it out: ri-seet. It is the slip of paper a shop gives to show that money has been paid.","Three things to remember: a soft 'c', the rule 'i before e except after c' (so 'cei'), and a silent 'p' before the 't'.","r-e-c-e-i-p-t.","The correct word is 'receipt'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q44","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 44","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":44,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (44) ++generous++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"generosity"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. Look at the word in front: 'thanking him for his ___'. After 'his' we need a noun.","'generous' is an adjective. Its noun form is 'generosity'.","Watch the spelling: the 'u' is dropped. gener**os**ity, not 'generousity'.","The correct word is 'generosity'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q45","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 45","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":45,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (45) ++bezdar++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"bizarre"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. Sound it out: bi-zar. It means very strange or unusual. Giving away money and apples for someone else's birthday was a very unusual idea.","One 'z', double 'r', and a silent 'e' at the end.","b-i-z-a-r-r-e.","The correct word is 'bizarre'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q46","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 46","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":46,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (46) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"that"},"explanation":["After the reporting verb 'found' comes a whole clause that says what was found: 'about one in 11 individuals ... has dementia'.","The word that introduces such a clause is 'that': found that, said that, showed that.","The answer is 'that'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q47","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 47","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":47,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (47) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"has","accept":["have","had"]},"explanation":["The clause 'about one in 11 individuals aged 60 and above ___ dementia' has no verb yet. We say a person 'has' a condition.","The head of the subject is 'one' (one in 11), which is singular, so the verb is 'has'. The words 'individuals aged 60 and above' sit in between and must not decide the verb.","The present tense is used because this is still true today.","The answer is 'has'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q48","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 48","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":48,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (48) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"only","accept":["just","merely","simply"]},"explanation":["Look at the second half of the sentence: 'but it can **also** lead to changes in behaviour and personality'.","'but ... also' tells us the first half limits dementia to one thing: many people think memory loss is all it causes.","The word that limits is 'only' (only ... but also).","The answer is 'only'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q49","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 49","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":49,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (49) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"feel","accept":["become","be","get","seem","appear"]},"explanation":["After the modal 'may' we need a verb in its base form. After the blank are three adjectives: 'restless, suspicious or easily upset'.","A verb that links a person to an adjective describing a state is needed: feel, become, be.","'feel' fits best because these are emotions. 'become' also makes sense because the next sentence calls them 'changes'.","The answer is 'feel'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q50","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 50","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":50,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (50) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"way","accept":["manner"]},"explanation":["'behaves in a ___ that seems unusual': the blank is a singular noun after 'a'.","The fixed pattern is 'to behave in a way that ...' or 'in a manner that ...'.","The answer is 'way'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q51","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 51","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":51,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (51) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"trying","accept":["attempting","struggling","seeking"]},"explanation":["'the person is often ___ to communicate a need': after 'is' and before 'to + verb', we need a verb ending in -ing.","The idea is that the unusual behaviour is the person's effort to tell us something.","'trying to communicate' gives that meaning.","The answer is 'trying'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q52","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 52","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":52,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (52) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"As","accept":["because","since"]},"explanation":["The sentence has two halves: 'dementia affects the brain' and 'they may find it difficult to express themselves'.","The first half is the reason for the second. A connector of reason that can start a sentence is needed: As, Because or Since.","Remember the capital letter at the start of the sentence.","The answer is 'As'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q53","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 53","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":53,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (53) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"only","accept":["solely","just","purely","clearly","properly","well","effectively","adequately","fully"]},"explanation":["Read the sentences before it: a person with dementia who behaves in an unusual way is often 'trying to communicate a need or feeling'.","So behaviour is being used to say what words cannot. The sentence explains why: it is difficult for them to express themselves with words alone.","'only with words' gives this meaning. An adverb that says how well they express themselves, such as 'clearly', also makes good sense here.","The key's answer is 'only'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q54","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 54","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":54,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (54) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"already","accept":["just"]},"explanation":["'even though they have ___ eaten': the blank sits between 'have' and the past participle 'eaten'.","'even though' shows a contrast: they ask for food again when the meal is over. The adverb that shows something is done before now is 'already'.","The answer is 'already'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q55","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 55","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":55,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (55) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"recall","accept":["remember"]},"explanation":["After 'cannot' we need a base verb, and after the blank is 'having their meal'.","Why would someone ask for food straight after eating? Because the memory of the meal is gone. Paragraph 1 says dementia causes problems with memory.","'recall' and 'remember' both mean to bring a memory back, and both can be followed by a verb ending in -ing.","The answer is 'recall' (or 'remember')."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q56","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 56","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":56,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (56) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"awake","accept":["up"]},"explanation":["'some may stay ___ at night and feel sleepy during the day': the second half is the result of the first.","People feel sleepy in the day when they have not slept at night. 'stay awake' means not to sleep.","'stay up' means the same.","The answer is 'awake'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q57","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 57","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":57,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (57) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"right","accept":["correct","appropriate","suitable","exact","proper"]},"explanation":["The sentence before says a person with dementia 'may use the **wrong** word'.","So what they are trying to find is the opposite: the right words.","'to find the right words' is a common phrase. 'correct' means the same.","The answer is 'right'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q58","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 58","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":58,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (58) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"attention"},"explanation":["This is a fixed phrase: to **pay attention to** something means to notice it and think about it carefully.","'paying ___ to the person's emotions': the verb 'pay' and the preposition 'to' on either side of the blank are the clues.","The answer is 'attention'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q59","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 59","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":59,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (59) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"their"},"explanation":["The blank comes before the noun 'concerns', so it needs a possessive word. Whose concerns? Those of people with dementia.","The rest of the sentence uses 'they' and 'their' for them: 'how **they** feel' and '**their** unmet needs'.","Keep the pronoun the same throughout.","The answer is 'their'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q60","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 60","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":60,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (60) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"needed","accept":["necessary","required","possible","appropriate"]},"explanation":["'offer reassurance when ___.' The sentence ends at the blank, so this is a short form of 'when it is ___'.","Caregivers cannot reassure someone every second; they do it at the moments the person is upset or confused, that is, when it is needed.","'when needed' and 'when necessary' are common short forms.","The answer is 'needed'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q61","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 61","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":61,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["noun-form"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Jade admired her grandfather as he was wise.\n____________________ because of ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Jade admired her grandfather because of his wisdom."},"explanation":["'because of' must be followed by a noun or noun phrase, not a clause. 'because of he was wise' is wrong.","Change the adjective 'wise' to its noun, 'wisdom'.","Change the subject 'he' to the possessive 'his' to show whose wisdom it was: 'his wisdom'.","Keep the first part as it is. Answer: Jade admired her grandfather because of his wisdom."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q62","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 62","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":62,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["neither-nor"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Both the bicycle shops in the park are closed today.\nNeither of ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Neither of the bicycle shops in the park is open today."},"explanation":["'Neither of' is negative: it means 'not one and not the other'. To keep the meaning, the rest of the sentence must change to its opposite: 'closed' becomes 'open'. ('Neither ... is closed' would mean both are open.)","After 'Neither of' use 'the' + plural noun: 'Neither of the bicycle shops'.","Agreement rule: 'Neither of ...' takes a singular verb in formal writing, so 'are' becomes 'is'.","Answer: Neither of the bicycle shops in the park is open today."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q63","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 63","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":63,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["preposition-gerund"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The electrician fixed the lights. They now work perfectly.\n____________________ as a result of ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The lights work perfectly now as a result of the electrician fixing them.","accept":["The lights now work perfectly as a result of the electrician fixing them.","The lights work perfectly now as a result of the electrician's fixing them.","The lights now work perfectly as a result of the electrician's fixing them.","The lights now work perfectly as a result of the electrician having fixed them.","The lights work perfectly now as a result of the electrician having fixed them."]},"explanation":["'as a result of' is followed by the **cause**, so the result comes first: the lights work perfectly (result) as a result of the fixing (cause).","Replace 'They' with 'The lights', because the lights have not been mentioned yet in the new sentence.","'as a result of' ends in a preposition, so it must be followed by a noun or a verb ending in -ing, not a clause. 'as a result of the electrician fixed them' is wrong; change 'fixed' to 'fixing'.","Use 'them' to avoid repeating 'the lights'. Answer: The lights work perfectly now as a result of the electrician fixing them."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q64","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 64","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":64,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["reported-speech"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"\"Did you remember to return the library book?\" Jill asked Jack.\nJill asked Jack if ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Jill asked Jack if he had remembered to return the library book."},"explanation":["This is direct speech changed to reported speech. A reported yes/no question uses 'if', statement word order (subject before verb), no 'did', and a full stop instead of a question mark.","Tense moves one step back: simple past 'Did you remember' becomes past perfect 'had remembered'.","Pronouns change to fit the people: 'you' is Jack, so 'he'.","'to return the library book' stays the same. Answer: Jill asked Jack if he had remembered to return the library book."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q65","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 65","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":65,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["noun-form"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"My aunt was upset about the rude staff. She complained to the manager.\n____________________ and made ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"My aunt was upset about the rude staff and made a complaint to the manager."},"explanation":["The given words 'and made' need an object: you 'make' a thing. So the verb 'complained' must become its noun, 'complaint'.","'complaint' is a singular countable noun, so it needs an article: 'made **a** complaint'.","Both actions have the same subject, 'My aunt', so 'She' is dropped after 'and'.","Watch the spelling: complain (verb), complain**t** (noun). Answer: My aunt was upset about the rude staff and made a complaint to the manager."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q66","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 66","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":66,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["cause-and-effect","inference"],"format":"structured","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on paragraph 1, why did Darto cause trouble and chase chickens?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"He was bored because life in the quiet village moved too slowly for him and his imagination."},"explanation":["Find the sentence about Darto in paragraph 1: 'Life moved slowly in their quiet village—far too slowly for Darto, whose imagination could power a small city.'","A boy with a huge imagination in a place where nothing happens gets bored, so he made his own excitement by causing trouble and chasing chickens.","The marker looks for the cause: life in the village was too slow or quiet for Darto (he was bored). Do not just repeat that he liked chasing chickens."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q67","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 67","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":67,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"What were two things that Darto did to make sure he was not seen by Uncle Banu?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"He crept quietly into Uncle Banu's yard, and he made his move only when he saw the lights in the house go out."},"explanation":["Look at paragraph 4, where Darto goes to Uncle Banu's yard at night.","First thing: he 'crept quietly into Uncle Banu's yard'. To creep is to move slowly and silently so as not to be noticed.","Second thing: after the lights came on he 'kept an eye on the house and made his move only when he saw the lights go out'. He waited until Uncle Banu was no longer up and about.","One mark for each action. 'Darto froze' on its own is weaker, because freezing was a reaction of fright; the waiting for the lights to go out is the action that kept him from being seen."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q68","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 68","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":68,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on the information from lines 11-12 (paragraph 4, from \"He kept an eye\" to \"as harmless as possible\"), complete the following table.\n(For each action, write its purpose.)","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Action: hands stretched out towards it\nPurpose:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"To catch (grab) Putih.","accept":["to catch putih","to grab putih","to catch the chicken"]},"explanation":["The sentence is 'Darto walked slowly towards Putih, his hands stretched out towards it, trying to appear as harmless as possible.'","Why would he reach out with his hands? His whole plan was to catch the chicken ('Catching Putih will be easy!'), and hands are what you grab with.","The purpose is to catch Putih."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Action: trying to appear as harmless as possible\nPurpose:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"So that he would not frighten Putih away."},"explanation":["'harmless' means not likely to hurt anyone. Darto wanted to look as if he meant no harm.","If Putih sensed danger it would run off before he got close. The next paragraph shows exactly that: 'as though realising his true intentions, Putih bolted'.","The purpose is to stop Putih from being frightened and running away (so that he could get close enough)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q69","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 69","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":69,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":4,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in the table below with how Darto felt and explain why he felt that way. The first row has been filled in for you.\n\nWhen Darto first learnt about Putin — How Darto felt: **Excited**. Why he felt that way: **If he caught Putin, everyone would admit he was the best chicken chaser in the village.**\n(The table on the paper prints \"Putin\"; it means Putih, the chicken.)","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"When the lights in Uncle Banu's house turned on\n(Write how Darto felt, then why he felt that way.)","marks":2,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Frightened. He feared that Uncle Banu would spot (catch) him in the yard."},"explanation":["Find the moment in paragraph 4: 'Just then, the lights in Uncle Banu's house turned on. Darto froze, his heart beating so fast he thought it might burst.'","Freezing and a pounding heart are signs of fear. Words such as frightened, afraid, scared or terrified fit.","Why? The lights meant Uncle Banu might be awake and might see him sneaking in the yard at night.","One mark for the feeling and one for the reason."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"After he failed to catch Putin the first time\n(Write how Darto felt, then why he felt that way.)","marks":2,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Embarrassed. He had boasted that catching Putih would be easy, but he failed and his friends laughed at him."},"explanation":["Find the moment in paragraph 6, just after the failed chase: Arif and Siti 'were laughing so hard they could barely breathe. Cheeks burning, Darto glared at his friends'.","'Cheeks burning' shows a red face, a sign of embarrassment (humiliated or ashamed also fit).","Why? In paragraph 3 he had boasted, 'Catching Putih will be easy!' Now he had failed in front of his friends, and Siti shouted, 'I told you he couldn't do it!'","One mark for the feeling and one for the reason (he had boasted but failed, and his friends laughed at him)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q70","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 70","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":70,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","evidence-phrase"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"In what way was Putih cleverer than Darto? Find evidence from lines 13-18 (paragraph 5) to support your answer.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Putih outsmarted Darto by deliberately leading him into a muddy ditch and then through a thorny bush, which left him exhausted, bleeding and coated in mud (while Putih got away)."},"explanation":["Find 'cleverer' in paragraph 5: 'the chicken was faster—and far cleverer. It **led him** into a muddy ditch, then through a thorny bush that left his arms covered in scratches.'","'led him' shows Putih chose where to run. It picked places that were easy for a small bird but hard and painful for a boy.","The result proves it worked: 'By the time he emerged, he was exhausted, bleeding and coated in mud', and Putih had not been caught.","The marker looks for: (1) the way it was cleverer: it tricked or outsmarted Darto by leading him through difficult places; (2) the evidence: the muddy ditch and the thorny bush, and the state Darto ended up in."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q71","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 71","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":71,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["evidence-phrase"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Which 2-word phrase from lines 19-23 (paragraph 6) shows that Putih knew it had beaten Darto?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"perched smugly","accept":["perched smugly on"]},"explanation":["In paragraph 6, find where Putih is described: 'then at the Putih, who was **perched smugly** on the roof of Uncle Banu's goat shed'.","'smugly' means in a way that shows you are very pleased with yourself, usually because you have won or done better than someone else.","A chicken sitting calmly up on a roof, looking pleased with itself, shows it knew it had won.","Copy exactly two words: perched (1) smugly (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q72","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 72","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":72,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["true-false-with-reason"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on the lines 24-44 (paragraphs 7 to 13), state whether each statement in the table below is true or false, then give one reason why you think so.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Putih attacked and hurt Darto.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. Putih launched itself at Darto with its claws outstretched, but Darto dodged and ran, so he was not hurt."},"explanation":["Paragraph 8: 'with a loud squawk, it launched itself at Darto, claws outstretched. Darto **dodged** and ran for his life.'","The statement has two halves. 'attacked' is right, but 'hurt' is not: to dodge is to move quickly out of the way, so the claws never touched him. After that the chicken only 'chased him'.","When one half of a statement is wrong, the whole statement is False.","Reason: Darto dodged Putih's attack (and ran away), so he was not hurt."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Darto did not expect the crowd to be there.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. Word of his mission had spread through the village without Darto knowing (\"Unknown to Darto\"), and a crowd had gathered."},"explanation":["Paragraph 7: 'he saw a crowd of children. **Unknown to Darto**, word of his mission had spread through the village thanks to Arif and Siti.'","'Unknown to Darto' means he had no idea that people had been told, so he could not have expected a crowd.","The statement is True. Reason: he did not know that word of his mission had spread through the village."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"Darto gave up on catching Putih completely.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. Darto made several more attempts to catch Putih."},"explanation":["Paragraph 13: 'Trying to catch Putih became something of a tradition among the children after that. Even Darto made **several more attempts**.'","The trap is paragraph 9, where Darto 'was finally ready to admit defeat'. That was only for that day. The word 'completely' in the statement makes it false, because he tried again later.","The statement is False. Reason: Darto made several more attempts to catch Putih."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q73","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 73","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":73,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["sequencing"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write 1, 2 and 3 in the blanks below to indicate the order in which the events occurred in the story.\n\n____ Putih chased Darto.\n____ Darto felt nervous.\n____ Arif and Siti told everyone about Darto's mission.\n\n(Type the three numbers in the order of the statements above, from top to bottom, for example 1, 2, 3.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"3, 2, 1","accept":["321","3 2 1","3,2,1","3, 2, 1"]},"explanation":["Find each event in the passage, then put them in time order. Be careful: the order in which the passage mentions things is not always the order in which they happened.","Arif and Siti told everyone: paragraph 7 says 'word of his mission **had spread** through the village thanks to Arif and Siti'. 'had spread' shows this happened before Darto reached the yard and saw the crowd. This is event 1.","Darto felt nervous: also paragraph 7, 'Darto's stomach fluttered at the sight of so many eyes on him.' He felt this when he saw the crowd. This is event 2.","Putih chased Darto: paragraphs 8 and 9, after he threw the net and missed. This is event 3.","Reading the statements from top to bottom, the numbers are 3, 2, 1."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q74","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 74","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":74,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["referencing"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Look at the table below. What do the words in the left column refer to in the passage? Write your answers in the column on the right.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"it (line 19, paragraph 6)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Catching Putih.","accept":["catching putih","catch putih","to catch putih","catching the chicken"]},"explanation":["Line 19 is Siti's shout: 'I told you he couldn't do **it**!'","'it' points back to the thing Darto had set out to do. In paragraph 3 he boasted, 'Catching Putih will be easy!', and paragraph 5 describes how he failed.","Test by replacing the word: 'I told you he couldn't catch Putih!' It makes sense.","The answer is catching Putih. Here 'it' is an action, not the chicken itself."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"his audience (line 28, paragraph 7)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The crowd of children (and Uncle Banu) who had come to the chicken yard to watch him.","accept":["the crowd of children and uncle banu","the crowd of children"]},"explanation":["Line 28: 'However, Darto waved to **his audience**'. An audience is a group of people watching something.","Look back in the same paragraph for who was watching: 'he saw a crowd of children', 'They cheered him on', 'Even Uncle Banu was there', 'so many eyes on him'.","The answer is the crowd of children, and Uncle Banu, who had gathered at the chicken yard to watch Darto. Writing only 'Arif and Siti' is not enough."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-b-q75","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 75","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"b","number":75,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Explain clearly why Putih was considered a legend in the village.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"No one in the village could catch Putih, not even Darto after several attempts, and trying to catch it became a tradition among the village children."},"explanation":["A legend is someone or something famous for being extraordinary, that people keep talking about. Look for what made Putih extraordinary and what shows it became famous.","Extraordinary: nobody could catch it. Arif calls it 'a chicken that cannot be caught', it was 'no ordinary chicken', and Darto 'never did catch Putih'. It was also very old: Uncle Banu's grandfather 'raised it from an egg'.","Famous: 'Trying to catch Putih became something of a tradition among the children after that.'","The marker looks for two points: (1) no one could catch Putih; (2) trying to catch it became a tradition among the children of the village."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-p1-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 1 Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"p1","number":1,"topic":"writing","subtypes":["situational-writing"],"format":"structured","marks":14,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The pictures below show Tom, Siti and Devi having a conversation. Study the pictures carefully.\n[[img1]]\n++Your Task++\nImagine you are Tom.\n**Write an email to your teacher, Miss Hanisah, to share about the class community service project you and your classmates have discussed.**\nYou are to refer to the pictures and information above for your email. The bullet point underlined below requires you to come up with your own information.\n\nIn your email, include the following key information:\n• the name of the community service project\n• what your class will make and its purpose\n• the art materials required\n• the date by which you hope to get the materials\n• ++one value your class can learn from this project++\n\nYou may reorder the points. Remember to write in complete sentences.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There is no single model answer. Check your writing against the points below."},"explanation":["Purpose: to tell Miss Hanisah about the class community service project and to ask for her help in getting the art materials. Say this clearly near the start of the email.","Audience and tone: Miss Hanisah is your teacher, so the email is formal and polite. Begin 'Dear Miss Hanisah', use full sentences, and avoid short forms and slang.","Context: you are Tom, writing for your class after discussing the project with Siti and Devi. Write as 'I' and 'we'.","Point 1, name of the project: 'Fold a Crane, Bring a Smile'. Devi chose Siti's **second** suggestion, so 'Project Paper Crane' is wrong.","Point 2, what the class will make and its purpose: 1,000 paper cranes, to cheer up the patients at Rainbow Hospital. Give both the item and the purpose.","Point 3, art materials required: coloured paper and nylon string (to hang the paper cranes). Do not ask for glue; Devi said it is not needed.","Point 4, date: by 10 September, so that there is enough time to fold the cranes. Tom's '15 September' was corrected by Siti.","Point 5, your own information: one value the class can learn, with a short reason, for example compassion (caring for people who are ill), teamwork (working together to fold 1,000 cranes) or perseverance.","Format: a greeting, a short opening that states the purpose, the points in a sensible order, a polite closing line such as thanking her for her help, then 'Yours sincerely' or 'Regards' and the name Tom.","Marks: 14 in all, for task fulfilment (all five points, correct details, purpose, audience and context) and for language (grammar, spelling, punctuation and organisation)."]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-p1-q1-img1","alt":"Two rows of pictures showing Tom, Siti and Devi talking in a library. Top row. Tom: After our examinations, we should organise a class community service project. Let's fold 1,000 paper cranes to cheer up the patients at Rainbow Hospital! Siti: Great idea, Tom! We can call it 'Project Paper Crane' or 'Fold a Crane, Bring a Smile'! Devi: Let's use Siti's second suggestion. We'll need coloured paper. We won't need glue, but we will need some nylon string to hang the paper cranes. Bottom row. Tom: I'll email Miss Hanisah to tell her about our class community service project and ask her to help us get the art materials by 15 September. Siti: No. We must get the materials by 10 September so that we will have enough time to fold the cranes. Devi: You should explain why our class is doing this as our community service project and the values we can learn from this project."}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-p1-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim MGS Paper 1 Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"MGS","section":"p1","number":2,"topic":"writing","subtypes":["continuous-writing"],"format":"structured","marks":36,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write a composition of ++at least 150 words++ about **making the best of a situation**.\n\nThe pictures are provided to help you think about this topic.\nYour composition should be based on one or more of these pictures.\n[[img1]]\nConsider the following points when you plan your composition:\n• What was the situation?\n• How did you make the best of the situation?\n\nYou may use the points in any order and include other relevant points as well.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There is no single model answer. Check your writing against the points below."},"explanation":["Understand the topic: 'making the best of a situation' means something disappointing or difficult happened that you could not change, and you chose to do something good with it instead of sulking. The story needs both halves: the bad situation and the positive response.","Picture 1 (boy at a rainy window): rain spoils an outing, a match or a picnic. Making the best of it could be an indoor picnic, board games with a grandparent, or baking together.","Picture 2 (event cancelled sign): a concert, camp, party or competition is called off. Making the best of it could be holding your own small version, using the time to help someone, or finding something new to enjoy.","Picture 3 (no internet connection): the Wi-Fi goes down during the holidays or before an online activity. Making the best of it could be rediscovering books, playing outside with neighbours, or really talking to your family.","Use one or more pictures. One picture developed well is better than three squeezed in.","Story arc: an opening that shows what you were looking forward to; the problem (the let-down and how you felt); a turning point where you, or someone who inspired you, decided to make the best of it; what you did; the result.","Show the change in feelings: disappointment or frustration at first, then determination, then surprise at how well things turned out. Use actions, speech and thoughts to show them.","Ending: return to the topic. Reflect on what you learnt, for example that you cannot control what happens but you can control how you respond.","Check: at least 150 words, paragraphs, a consistent past tense, varied sentence openings and precise vocabulary. Marks: 36 in all, for content and for language and organisation."]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-p1-q2-img1","alt":"Three pictures, one above the other. Picture 1: a boy with a sad face leaning on a window sill, looking out at heavy rain. Picture 2: a sign hanging from a hook that says 'SORRY, THIS EVENT IS CANCELLED'. Picture 3: a hand holding a mobile phone whose screen shows a crossed-out Wi-Fi symbol and the words 'NO INTERNET CONNECTION'."}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-p1-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 1 Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"p1","number":1,"topic":"writing","subtypes":["situational-writing"],"format":"structured","marks":14,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The pictures below show Elsie visiting to a fire station. Study the pictures carefully.\n[[img1]]\n++Your Task++\nImagine you are Elsie.\n**Write an article for the class noticeboard to tell your classmates about your experience visiting a fire station.**\nYou are to refer to the pictures and information above for your article. The bullet point underlined below requires you to come up with your own information.\n\nIn your writing, include the following key information:\n- why you visited a fire station\n- how you prepared for the visit\n- two things you were allowed to do during the visit\n- one other detail about the visit\n- ++one way you have benefitted from the visit++\n\nYou may reorder the points. Remember to write in complete sentences.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There is no single model answer. Check your writing against the points below."},"explanation":["Purpose: to tell your classmates about your experience visiting a fire station. Audience: your classmates. Context: an article for the class noticeboard, written as Elsie. Write 'I', not 'Elsie'.","Format: an article needs a title (for example 'My Visit to City Fire Station') and your name as the writer. It is not a letter or an email, so no 'Dear classmates' and no sign-off.","Point 1, why you visited: City Fire Station was having an open house and your mother asked if you would like to attend it.","Point 2, how you prepared: you checked the open house programme and planned how you and your mother would spend your time there.","Point 3, two things you were allowed to do: try wearing a firefighter's helmet, mask and uniform; get into the fire engine. Give both.","Point 4, one other detail: you watched the firefighters demonstrate how they respond to various emergencies (or: the visit was at City Fire Station; you went with your mother; you found the firefighters very brave).","Point 5, one way you have benefitted (your own idea, it is not in the pictures): for example, you now know what to do in an emergency, or you appreciate how hard firefighters work to keep us safe.","Tone: friendly and enthusiastic, suitable for classmates, but in complete, correct sentences. Use the past tense for what happened. 6 marks are for covering all the points for the task; 8 marks are for language and organisation."]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-p1-q1-img1","alt":"Four numbered pictures. Picture 1: Elsie's mother holds a phone and says, 'Elsie, City Fire Station is having an open house. Would you like to attend it?' Elsie raises her fist happily and replies, 'That sounds interesting! I'll check the open house programme and plan how we'll spend our time there.' Picture 2: A firefighter hands Elsie a helmet and says, 'You can try wearing a firefighter's helmet, mask and uniform!' Her mother stands beside her. Picture 3: Elsie, now wearing a firefighter's helmet and uniform, stands by a fire engine with its door open. A firefighter asks, 'Would you like to get into the fire engine?' and she answers, 'Yes, please!' Picture 4: Elsie and her mother walk away from a building with the sign CITY FIRE STATION. Elsie says, 'I enjoyed watching the firefighters demonstrate how they respond to various emergencies, Mum. They are so brave!'"}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-p1-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 1 Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"p1","number":2,"topic":"writing","subtypes":["continuous-writing"],"format":"structured","marks":36,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write a composition of ++at least 150 words++ about **showing respect**.\nThe pictures are provided to help you think about this topic.\nYour composition should be based on one or more of these pictures.\n[[img1]]\nConsider the following points when you plan your composition:\n- Why was there a need to show respect?\n- How was respect shown?\n\nYou may use the points in any order and include other relevant points as well.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There is no single model answer. Check your plan and your story against the points below."},"explanation":["The topic is 'showing respect'. The whole story must lead to a moment where someone shows respect (or learns to), and you must use at least one picture as an important part of the story, not just mention it.","Picture 1, the watch: respecting other people's time. For example, a character who is always late keeps a team, a coach or a grandparent waiting, sees the trouble it causes, and learns to be punctual.","Picture 2, the loudspeaker: respecting other people's need for peace and quiet. For example, blasting music while a neighbour's baby sleeps or a sibling studies for an exam, then turning it down and apologising.","Picture 3, the dining table: respecting elders, hosts or other cultures at a meal. For example, waiting for the elders to start eating, putting the phone away, or trying a dish that a host has taken hours to prepare.","Answer both guiding points clearly: why respect was needed (who was affected and how they felt) and how it was shown (a clear action, not just 'I was respectful').","Story arc: a setting and characters, a problem where respect is missing or tested, a turning point where the character realises it, the respectful action, and the result.","Ending: return to the topic. Say what the character learnt about showing respect and how it changed the way he or she behaves. 18 marks are for content and 18 for language and organisation, so check tenses, paragraphing and vocabulary."]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-p1-q2-img1","alt":"Three pictures in a column. Picture 1: a wristwatch showing the time. Picture 2: a loudspeaker blasting loud music, with musical notes and lightning-shaped sound lines around it. Picture 3: a dining table laid for a meal with plates of food, cutlery, glasses and a bottle, and chairs around it."}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":1,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["infinitives-and-gerunds"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"I am really looking forward to __________ the new science centre during our next learning journey,\" exclaimed Michelle.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Rule: in 'look forward to', the word 'to' is a preposition, not part of a to-infinitive. A verb that follows a preposition takes the -ing form.","So we say 'looking forward to visiting', just like 'looking forward to seeing you'.","'visit' is the tempting wrong answer because 'to visit' looks right, but 'look forward to visit' is wrong.","The answer is option (4), visiting."]}],"options":["visit","visits","visited","visiting"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":2,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["pronouns","subject-verb-agreement"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"You spent two hours watching videos? __________ unacceptable!\" Dad said sternly.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Dad is talking about one thing: the fact that the child spent two hours watching videos. One thing needs a singular word, so 'These are' and 'Those are' are out.","Rule: use 'that' to point back to something that has just been said or has already happened. 'This' points to something here and now, or something you are about to say.","The two hours of videos are already over and have just been mentioned, so Dad says 'That is unacceptable!'","The answer is option (2), That is."]}],"options":["This is","That is","These are","Those are"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q3","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 3","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":3,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["subject-verb-agreement"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The captain of the badminton team, together with his team members, __________ hard every afternoon for the upcoming competition.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Rule: words added with 'together with', 'as well as' or 'along with' are not part of the subject. The verb agrees with the first noun only.","Cover up 'together with his team members'. The subject is 'The captain', which is singular.","'every afternoon' shows a habit, so use the simple present: singular subject + trains. 'training' cannot stand alone without 'is'.","'train' and 'have trained' are plural forms; they are tempting only if you count the team members. The answer is option (2), trains."]}],"options":["train","trains","training","have trained"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q4","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 4","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":4,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["quantifiers"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Since the talk ended late, there is __________ time left for us to go to the playground,\" Sally told her friends.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'time' cannot be counted, so 'few' and 'many' (for countable nouns) are out.","The talk ended late, so there is hardly any time left. 'little' means 'almost none' and is used with uncountable nouns.","'much' would mean a lot of time, which does not fit 'ended late'. Also, we do not normally say 'there is much time' in a positive sentence.","The answer is option (2), little."]}],"options":["few","little","much","many"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q5","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 5","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":5,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Learning a new language __________ easy so far, but I am enjoying the challenge,\" said Mr Tan.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clue: 'so far' means from some time in the past up to now. That signals the present perfect tense: has/have + past participle.","The subject 'Learning a new language' is one activity, so it is singular: 'has not been'.","'had not been' (past perfect) is for something before another past event, but Mr Tan is still learning ('I am enjoying'). 'is not' and 'was not' do not go with 'so far'.","The answer is option (3), has not been."]}],"options":["is not","was not","has not been","had not been"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q6","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 6","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":6,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["connectors","conditionals"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"You will not be able to join the dance team __________ you pass the audition next Tuesday.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["The sentence gives a condition: passing the audition is the only way to join the team.","'unless' means 'if ... not'. 'You will not be able to join unless you pass' = 'You will not be able to join if you do not pass'.","'since' gives a reason, 'although' shows contrast and 'whereas' compares two things. None of them sets a condition.","The answer is option (2), unless."]}],"options":["since","unless","although","whereas"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q7","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 7","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":7,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["question-tags"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"She'll be representing our school in the storytelling competition, __________?\" asked the principal.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Rule: a question tag repeats the helping verb of the statement. A positive statement takes a negative tag.","'She'll' is short for 'She will'. The helping verb is 'will', and its negative is 'won't'.","'wouldn't she' would match 'She'd' (she would), and 'isn't she' would match 'She's'. Read the short form carefully.","The answer is option (2), won't she."]}],"options":["isn't she","won't she","hasn't she","wouldn't she"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q8","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 8","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":8,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["connectors","prepositions"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The football match was postponed __________ the thunderstorm warning.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["The thunderstorm warning is the reason the match was postponed, so we need a phrase that means 'because of'.","'in view of' means 'because of' or 'considering'.","'in line with' means following a rule or matching something, 'on behalf of' means representing someone, and 'with reference to' means 'about' (used in letters). None of them gives a reason.","The answer is option (1), in view of."]}],"options":["in view of","in line with","on behalf of","with reference to"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q9","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 9","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":9,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["modals"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Madam Tan reprimanded her son, \"You __________ left home earlier to avoid being caught in the heavy rain on your way to school.\"","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'reprimanded' means scolded. Madam Tan is telling her son what the right thing to do was, which he did not do.","Rule: 'should have' + past participle is used for something that was the right thing to do but was not done.","'could have' only says it was possible, which is too weak for a scolding. 'must have' means you are sure it happened, but he did not leave early.","The answer is option (4), should have."]}],"options":["must have","could have","would have","should have"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q10","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 10","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":10,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["infinitives-and-gerunds"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"I witnessed the boy __________ the road before the traffic light turned green,\" the security guard reported.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Rule: after verbs of the senses (see, watch, hear, notice, witness) + a person, use the base form of the verb for a complete action: 'I saw him cross the road.'","'witnessed' already carries the past tense. The second verb does not take a tense, so 'crosses', 'crossed' and 'had crossed' are all wrong.","'crossed' is the tempting one because the event is in the past, but only the first verb shows the tense.","The answer is option (1), cross."]}],"options":["cross","crosses","crossed","had crossed"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q11","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 11","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":11,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning","collocation"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"After years of volunteering at the animal shelter, Jane has developed a strong __________ with the staff and the rescued animals.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["'rapport' means a close, friendly relationship in which people understand each other. We 'develop a rapport **with**' someone.","Years of volunteering would build that kind of bond with both the staff and the animals.","'familiarity' is tempting, but it only means knowing something well, and we do not say 'a strong familiarity'. 'influence' goes with 'on' or 'over', and a 'reputation' is what others think of you, not something you have 'with the animals'.","The answer is option (1), rapport."]}],"options":["rapport","influence","familiarity","reputation"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q12","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 12","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":12,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Even when faced with an angry and demanding customer, the manager remained __________ and calmly addressed the complaints.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Clue: 'calmly addressed the complaints'. We need a word that means calm and in control of your feelings.","'composed' means exactly that: calm and not showing anger or nervousness.","'cautious' means careful to avoid danger, 'courageous' means brave, and 'comforting' means making someone feel better. None of them matches 'calmly'.","The answer is option (2), composed."]}],"options":["cautious","composed","comforting","courageous"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q13","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 13","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":13,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Before submitting his final writing draft, Josh checked his work __________, scrutinising every word to ensure there were no grammatical mistakes.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Clue: 'scrutinising every word'. To scrutinise is to examine very closely.","'meticulously' means very carefully, paying attention to every small detail.","'briefly' (for a short time) is the opposite. 'routinely' means as a regular habit and 'confidently' means feeling sure; neither tells us he checked every word.","The answer is option (4), meticulously."]}],"options":["briefly","routinely","confidently","meticulously"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q14","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 14","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":14,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"When the fire alarm rang, the security guard immediately __________ the situation to determine if it was a real emergency.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["The guard wanted 'to determine if it was a real emergency', so he investigated.","'look into' means to investigate or find out the facts about a situation or problem.","'look over' means to check something quickly, such as a piece of work. 'look through' means to read or search through papers or a bag. 'look for' means to search for something lost.","The answer is option (2), looked into."]}],"options":["looked for","looked into","looked over","looked through"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q15","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 15","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":15,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["collocation","word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Samuel knew he had to __________ the opportunity and sign up for the leadership camp as the slots were limited.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clue: 'the slots were limited', so he had to act quickly before the chance was gone.","'seize the opportunity' is a fixed expression that means to grab a chance eagerly and quickly.","'miss' is the opposite of what he had to do. The opportunity already existed, so he did not need to 'find' or 'create' it.","The answer is option (3), seize."]}],"options":["find","miss","seize","create"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q16","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 16","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":16,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++constraints++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'constraints' are things that limit what you can do. 'Financial constraints' means not having enough money to do what you want.","'limitations' has the same meaning: financial limitations.","'burdens' is tempting because money problems are heavy to bear, but a burden is a load or duty, not a limit. 'setbacks' are events that delay progress.","The answer is option (3), limitations."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g1","options":["burdens","setbacks","limitations","boundaries"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q17","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 17","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":17,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++cease++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["To 'cease' is to stop completely. The vendors were forced to close their stalls.","'discontinue' means to stop doing something for good.","'pause' is tempting, but a pause is only a short stop before carrying on again. The passage says there is a 'severe lack' of vendors, so they have stopped for good.","The answer is option (4), discontinue."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g1","options":["delay","pause","reduce","discontinue"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q18","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 18","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":18,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++seamlessly++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["A seam is a join. 'Seamlessly' means with no breaks, hitches or problems.","Collecting pre-ordered food from a machine with one tap of a smart card happens 'smoothly'.","'systematically' means following a fixed step-by-step method, and 'independently' means on your own. Those are about how the students act, not about how trouble-free the collection is.","The answer is option (2), smoothly."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g1","options":["carefully","smoothly","systematically","independently"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q19","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 19","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":19,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++dietary++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'dietary' comes from 'diet', the food a person eats. 'Dietary needs' are needs about what a person should or should not eat.","'nutritional' is about food and the nourishment it gives, so it is the closest.","'medical' is tempting because some food needs come from allergies, but dietary needs are about food in general, not about illness and treatment.","The answer is option (4), nutritional."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g1","options":["medical","physical","personal","nutritional"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q20","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 20","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":20,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++promptly++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["'promptly' means quickly and without delay.","Clue: the next sentence says this 'will reduce the time needed to wait in queues'. So the point is time, and 'on time' is the closest.","'with ease' means without difficulty, which is closer to 'seamlessly' in question 18, not to 'promptly'.","The answer is option (1), on time."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g1","options":["on time","with ease","as planned","on their own"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q21","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 21","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":21,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Carry Everything in Style.\" The purpose of this statement in Text 1 is to __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Break the slogan into two halves. 'Carry Everything' says the bag is useful: it holds all your things. 'in Style' says the bag is trendy and looks good.","Only option (2) covers both halves: useful and trendy.","Option (1) is the tempting one, but storage capacity explains only 'Carry Everything' and leaves out 'in Style'. Option (4) covers only 'Style'.","The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g2","options":["emphasise the bag's storage capacity","highlight that the bag is useful and trendy","remind students of what they must carry to school","encourage students to care about how they look"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q22","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 22","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":22,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience","inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Why was Ansel featured in Text 1?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["A poster quotes a happy user to make its claims more believable. This is called a testimonial.","Match his words to the poster: 'Everything I need for school fits comfortably into it' supports 'Fit your entire school day into one bag'. 'I can also find and take out any item I need easily' supports 'a specific space for everything'.","Option (2) is tempting because he is 12, but the poster says the bag is 'Suitable for students of all ages', not only his age.","The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g2","options":["He appears to be a good student.","The school bag is designed for users of his age.","His review was short enough to fit into the poster.","What he said supports the claims made in the poster."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q23","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 23","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":23,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["factual","inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Based on Text 1, which of the following statements are true about the Peaks school bag?\nA: The bag is small and light.\nB: The bag comes in different colours.\nC: The bag has multiple compartments.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["A: The poster never says the bag is small or light. It says you can 'Fit your entire school day into one bag', which suggests a roomy bag. A is not supported.","B: 'Wide range of colours to choose from!' So B is true.","C: 'Get organised with a specific space for everything!' A separate space for each item means the bag has many compartments. So C is true.","B and C are true. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g2","options":["A and B","A and C","B and C","A, B and C"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q24","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 24","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":24,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"What is the **main** message of Text 2?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["The main message is the idea that runs through the whole article, not one detail. Text 2 moves from 'bags look good' to 'have you considered the impact on your health?' and keeps returning to your back: 'fits comfortably on your back', 'reduce the pressure on your back', 'protect your back'.","So the writer wants students to put comfort and health before style when choosing and packing a bag. That is option (1).","Option (4) is tempting, but lockers and desks are introduced with 'For example'. They are one example of how to keep the bag light, not the main message. Option (3) is what many students do, not what the writer advises.","The answer is option (1)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g2","options":["Students should prioritise comfort when choosing a bag.","Students should choose school bags with more features.","Students should express their personality and personal style.","Students should use lockers or desks to store their belongings."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-a-q25","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet A Question 25","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"a","number":25,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Text 1 and Text 2 express differing views on whether __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Find a point where the two texts say opposite things.","Text 1 encourages it: 'Fit your entire school day into one bag, all your books, stationery, lunch boxes and more!'","Text 2 advises against it: 'health experts recommend packing only the items needed for the day' and 'leaving out unnecessary items'.","Option (3) is the trap. It is about whether a bag **can** fit everything, and Text 2 does not say it cannot. The disagreement is about whether students **should**. The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g2","options":["students can store certain items in school","school bags should look appealing to students","school bags can fit everything needed for school","students should fit everything into their school bags"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q26","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 26","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":26,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (26). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"J","accept":["j","is"]},"explanation":["Find the subject: 'an astonishing **amount** of plastic waste'. The words 'enough to fill 2,000 garbage trucks' only describe the amount.","The waste does not dump anything; it is dumped by people. That is the passive voice: be + past participle ('dumped').","'amount' is singular and 'Every day' shows something that happens regularly, so use the present singular 'is'. 'are' is tempting because 'trucks' is right before the blank, but trucks are not the subject.","The answer is (J) is."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q27","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 27","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":27,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (27). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"P","accept":["p","up"]},"explanation":["'turn up' is a phrasal verb that means to appear, often unexpectedly.","'Surprisingly' tells us the plastic appeared somewhere unexpected: on the backs of hermit crabs.","The answer is (P) up: 'plastic waste has now turned up on the backs of hermit crabs'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q28","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 28","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":28,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (28). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Q","accept":["q","which"]},"explanation":["The blank joins 'soft, curly bodies' to more information about them: '___ leave them vulnerable to predators'.","Rule: use the relative pronoun 'which' to add information about a thing. ('who' is for people.)","The answer is (Q) which: 'soft, curly bodies which leave them vulnerable to predators'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q29","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 29","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":29,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (29). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"B","accept":["b","because"]},"explanation":["The sentence has two halves: 'their tails are fragile' and 'they must constantly look for empty shells'.","The first half is the reason for the second, so we need a connector of reason that can start a sentence.","The answer is (B) because: 'Because their tails are fragile, they must constantly look for empty shells'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q30","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 30","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":30,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (30). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"M","accept":["m","themselves"]},"explanation":["Who is being protected? The crabs are protecting the crabs. The doer and the receiver are the same.","Rule: when the subject and the object are the same, use a reflexive pronoun. 'they' pairs with 'themselves'.","The answer is (M) themselves: 'to protect themselves from hungry birds and fishes'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q31","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 31","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":31,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (31). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"F","accept":["f","have"]},"explanation":["After the blank is 'been observed', so we need has/have to make the present perfect passive.","The subject is '10 out of 16 land species', which is plural (10 species), so use 'have', not 'has'.","The answer is (F) have: '10 out of 16 land species have been observed wearing plastic waste'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q32","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 32","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":32,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (32). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"C","accept":["c","even"]},"explanation":["The list goes from an ordinary item (bottle caps) to a surprising one (broken lightbulbs).","'even' is used to add something surprising or extreme to what was said before.","The answer is (C) even: 'such as bottle caps, and even broken lightbulbs'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q33","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 33","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":33,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (33). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"H","accept":["h","in"]},"explanation":["'result in' is a fixed pair: to result in something means to cause it.","Fewer sea snails cause fewer natural shells. ('result from' would give the cause, which is the wrong way round here.)","The answer is (H) in: 'resulting in fewer natural shells for the crabs'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q34","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 34","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":34,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (34). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"L","accept":["l","over"]},"explanation":["To 'choose A over B' means to pick A instead of B.","The crabs pick plastic materials instead of traditional shells because plastic is lighter.","The answer is (L) over: 'crabs choose plastic materials over traditional shells'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q35","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 35","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":35,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (35). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"G","accept":["g","if"]},"explanation":["'it is still unclear ___' is followed by something scientists are not sure about: are the crabs endangered or not?","Rule: use 'if' (or 'whether') to introduce a yes/no question inside a sentence.","The answer is (G) if: 'it is still unclear if hermit crabs are directly endangered by this behaviour'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q36","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 36","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":36,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (36) ++sequanse++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"sequence"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means the order in which things happen.","Spell it in two parts: se + quence. The ending is '-ence', as in 'sentence' and 'silence', and the 's' sound is spelt with a 'c'.","The correct word is 'sequence'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q37","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 37","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":37,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (37) ++accidental++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"accidentally"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. The word describes how the object 'hit' the main character. A word that describes a verb is an adverb.","'accidental' is an adjective (an accidental hit). Add -ly to make the adverb: accidental + ly = accidentally. Keep both l's.","The correct word is 'accidentally'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q38","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 38","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":38,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (38) ++spreading++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"spread"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. 'she quickly ___ her panic' needs a main verb in the simple past, like 'followed' and 'began' earlier in the paragraph. An -ing word cannot be the main verb without 'was'.","'spread' is an irregular verb: the past tense is also 'spread', not 'spreaded'.","The correct word is 'spread'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q39","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 39","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":39,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (39) ++thurruhly++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"thoroughly"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means completely or very much.","It is built from 'thorough' + ly. Remember the silent 'ough' at the end of 'thorough', as in 'borough'.","The correct word is 'thoroughly'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q40","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 40","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":40,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (40) ++seens++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"scenes"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The parts of a play are its 'scenes'.","'scene' has a silent 'c' after the 's'. Do not mix it up with 'seen', the past participle of 'see', which sounds the same.","The correct word is 'scenes'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q41","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 41","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":41,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (41) ++dielog++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"dialogue","accept":["dialog"]},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means the lines that the characters speak to each other.","It starts with 'dia-' (as in 'diagram') and, in British spelling, which Singapore schools use, it ends with '-logue' (as in 'catalogue').","The correct word is 'dialogue'. The answer key gives the shorter American spelling 'dialog', which is also accepted."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q42","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 42","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":42,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (42) ++jump++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"jumping"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. Look at the word in front: 'the importance **of** not ___'. 'of' is a preposition.","Rule: a verb that follows a preposition takes the -ing form. The word 'not' in between does not change this.","The correct word is 'jumping'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q43","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 43","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":43,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (43) ++valued++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"valuable"},"explanation":["This is a word-form error. 'valued' means that someone treasures it (a valued friend). A lesson that is very useful and worth a lot is a 'valuable' lesson.","Form the adjective from 'value': drop the 'e' and add -able.","The correct word is 'valuable'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q44","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 44","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":44,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (44) ++rekkomend++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"recommend"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means to tell someone that something is good.","Think of it as re + commend: one 'c' and double 'm'. There is no 'k' in it.","The correct word is 'recommend'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q45","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 45","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":45,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (45) ++enjoying++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"enjoys"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. 'anyone who ___ a fun story' needs a complete verb. An -ing word needs a helping verb ('is enjoying'), and there is none.","'anyone' is singular and the sentence states a general fact, so use the simple present singular: enjoys.","The correct word is 'enjoys'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q46","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 46","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":46,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (46) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"until","accept":["till","before"]},"explanation":["The boxes are sealed, so buyers find out what is inside only at the moment they open them.","'not ... until' means something does not happen before a certain point: 'do not know what they will get until they open them'.","'till' and 'before' give the same meaning.","The answer is 'until'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q47","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 47","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":47,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (47) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"new","accept":["novel","original"]},"explanation":["'Although' signals a contrast: blind boxes **feel like** a modern trend, but in fact the idea is old.","The next sentence proves it: something similar was already common 'In the 1950's'. So 'the idea is not new'.","The answer is 'new'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q48","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 48","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":48,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (48) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"known"},"explanation":["'also known as' is a fixed phrase that introduces another name for something.","Mystery prize tickets had another name, Tikam-Tikam. Note that 'called' does not fit, because we say 'also called Tikam-Tikam' without 'as'.","The answer is 'known'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q49","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 49","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":49,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (49) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"actions","accept":["acts","behaviour","behaviours","measures","lengths"]},"explanation":["The example after the blank is 'shoplifting', which is something people did. We need a plural noun for things people do.","The next sentence refers back to the same idea with 'such acts'. So the craze led people to extreme actions.","'acts', 'behaviour', 'measures' and 'lengths' also fit.","The answer is 'actions'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q50","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 50","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":50,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (50) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"recent"},"explanation":["The passage moves from the 1950's to the present: 'such acts **have also been** reported by shops selling blind boxes here'.","The present perfect tense ('have been reported') goes with a period of time leading up to now. 'In recent months' means in the last few months.","The answer is 'recent'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q51","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 51","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":51,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (51) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"why"},"explanation":["The sentence is a question, and the next sentences answer it with 'One reason is ...' and 'Another reason is ...'.","A question that is answered with reasons begins with 'why'.","The answer is 'why'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q52","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 52","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":52,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (52) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"limited","accept":["small","low"]},"explanation":["The next sentence talks about 'a rare piece'. Rare items are ones that there are not many of.","Something that there is not much of is 'limited in quantity'.","The answer is 'limited'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q53","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 53","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":53,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (53) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"task","accept":["mission","quest","challenge"]},"explanation":["Look at the words around the blank: 'a difficult ___ has been successfully completed'. We need a noun for something that can be difficult and can be completed.","Finding the rare piece feels like finishing a hard job: a difficult task. 'mission', 'quest' and 'challenge' also fit.","The answer is 'task'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q54","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 54","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":54,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (54) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"experience","accept":["experiences","collections","collection","purchases","finds"]},"explanation":["What do collectors share 'online through posts and live videos'? The next sentence tells us: others watch them 'unbox rare items'.","They are sharing what happened to them when they bought and opened the boxes: their experience.","'experiences', 'collections', 'purchases' and 'finds' also fit.","The answer is 'experience'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q55","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 55","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":55,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (55) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"follow","accept":["join"]},"explanation":["Seeing others unbox rare items makes more people want to do the same thing.","The usual verb with 'trend' is 'follow': to follow a trend is to do what many others are doing. 'join the trend' is also used.","The answer is 'follow'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q56","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 56","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":56,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (56) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"similar","accept":["akin","comparable"]},"explanation":["The writer is comparing the excitement of blind boxes with gambling. In both, you pay without knowing what you will get.","The blank is followed by 'to', and 'similar to' means 'like'.","The answer is 'similar'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q57","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 57","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":57,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (57) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"hopes","accept":["hope","anticipation","search","pursuit"]},"explanation":["'in hopes of' (or 'in the hope of') is a phrase that means 'wishing to get'.","People keep buying because they wish for a rare item, 'only to end up with duplicates'.","'hope', 'search' and 'pursuit' also fit the pattern 'in ___ of getting'.","The answer is 'hopes'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q58","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 58","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":58,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (58) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"stop","accept":["break","end","resist","escape","quit","control"]},"explanation":["Clue: 'the behaviour may even become addictive'. Something addictive is very hard to give up.","So the cycle of buying again and again is 'hard to stop'. We also say 'break a cycle', so 'break' is just as good.","The answer is 'stop'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q59","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 59","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":59,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (59) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"sprees","accept":["spree","habits","patterns","behaviour","behaviours"]},"explanation":["The words after the blank explain it: 'where people frequently spend more than intended or buy repeatedly'.","A 'spending spree' is a short period when someone buys a lot without control. The blank needs the plural: sprees.","'spending habits' and 'spending patterns' also fit, because the passage says it happens 'frequently'.","The answer is 'sprees'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q60","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 60","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":60,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (60) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"restrictions","accept":["regulations","laws","measures","policies","bans","guidelines","limits"]},"explanation":["The blank is joined to 'rules' by 'and', so we need a plural noun with a similar meaning.","The purpose is 'to better protect consumers', and the example is 'the ban on Tikam-Tikam'. A ban is a kind of restriction: a limit on what people may do.","'regulations' and 'laws' also fit.","The answer is 'restrictions'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q61","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 61","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":61,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["reported-speech"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"\"Did you paint this picture?\" Mr Shelby asked his daughter.\nMr Shelby asked his daughter if ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Mr Shelby asked his daughter if she had painted that picture."},"explanation":["This changes a direct question into reported speech. After 'if', use statement word order (subject before verb), drop 'did' and end with a full stop, not a question mark.","Pronoun: 'you' is his daughter, so it becomes 'she'.","Tense: move one step back. The simple past 'did ... paint' becomes the past perfect 'had painted'.","Pointing word: 'this' becomes 'that'. Answer: Mr Shelby asked his daughter if she had painted that picture."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q62","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 62","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":62,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["inversion","cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The prey never heard a sound because of the silent movement of the tiger.\nSo silently did ____________________ the prey never heard a sound.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"So silently did the tiger move that the prey never heard a sound."},"explanation":["The pattern is 'So + adverb + did + subject + base verb + that + result'. Starting with 'So silently' turns the word order around, like a question.","The noun phrase 'the silent movement of the tiger' must become a subject and a verb: the tiger moved silently. 'silent' has already become the adverb 'silently'.","After 'did', use the base form 'move', not 'moved', because 'did' already shows the past tense.","Join the result with 'that'. Answer: So silently did the tiger move that the prey never heard a sound."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q63","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 63","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":63,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["preposition-gerund"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Mike ignored the warning sign. He could have caused an accident.\nBy ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"By ignoring the warning sign, Mike could have caused an accident."},"explanation":["'By' is a preposition that shows how something happened. A verb after a preposition takes the -ing form: 'ignored' becomes 'ignoring'.","Rule: the person doing the -ing action must be the subject of the main clause. So name 'Mike' right after the comma, in place of 'He'.","Keep 'could have caused an accident' exactly as it is, and put a comma after the 'By' phrase.","Answer: By ignoring the warning sign, Mike could have caused an accident."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q64","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 64","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":64,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["noun-form"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Polly contributed generously to the local charity.\nPolly made a ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Polly made a generous contribution to the local charity."},"explanation":["'made a' must be followed by a noun, so the verb 'contributed' changes to the noun 'contribution'.","The adverb 'generously' described the verb. Now it has to describe a noun, so it changes to the adjective 'generous'.","The adjective goes before the noun: a generous contribution. Keep 'to the local charity'.","Answer: Polly made a generous contribution to the local charity."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q65","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 65","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":65,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["not-only-but-also","inversion"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Besides being long, the movie was also boring.\nNot only was ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Not only was the movie long, but it was also boring.","accept":["Not only was the movie long, it was also boring."]},"explanation":["'Besides being long ... also boring' means the movie was both long and boring. 'Not only ... but also' says the same thing.","When 'Not only' starts the sentence, the verb comes before the subject: 'Not only **was the movie** long'.","The second half goes back to normal word order and needs its own subject: 'but it was also boring'.","Answer: Not only was the movie long, but it was also boring."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q66","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 66","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":66,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"structured","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Why did Ryan \"lay sleepless in the dark\" (line 3, paragraph 1)?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Ryan lay sleepless because it was his first time staying away from home and the thin, hard mattress made him miss his own comfortable bed."},"explanation":["Read on from 'Ryan lay sleepless in the dark'. The very next sentence gives the reason: 'This was his first time staying away from home, and the thin, hard mattress made him miss his own comfortable bed.'","The marker looks for two ideas for the 1 mark: he was away from home (for the first time), and his discomfort (he missed his own bed because the mattress was thin and hard).","Do not write only that the quiet forest made his heart beat faster. That describes how he felt, but the passage gives being away from home as the reason."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q67","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 67","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":67,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual","inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on lines 5-10 (paragraph 2), which two pieces of information about Marcus tell us that he was a suitable group leader?\n(Write two points, I and II.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"I) He was the only one who had mastered all the safety knots during the practice sessions in school.\nII) He checked every classmate's harness with a sharp gaze."},"explanation":["Paragraph 2 says Marcus's appointment 'was no surprise', and the dash introduces the first reason: 'he was the only one who had mastered all the safety knots'. Keep the word 'only'; it shows he was the most skilled. (1 mark)","The second piece of information is what he did as leader: 'he checked every classmate's harness with a sharp gaze'. This shows he was responsible and careful about everyone's safety. (1 mark)","Each point must be a fact about Marcus from lines 5-10. 'He climbed the wall with ease' is from paragraph 3, so it does not count."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q68","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 68","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":68,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["cause-and-effect","character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Explain clearly what caused Ryan to feel 'embarrassed' (line 14, paragraph 3).","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Ryan froze at the rock wall and Marcus shouted at him from above to start climbing, and the other students were watching him tremble."},"explanation":["'Explain clearly' for 2 marks means two causes are needed. Both are in paragraph 3, around the word 'embarrassed'.","Cause 1, what happened at the wall: Ryan 'took one look at the sheer height of the wall and froze', and Marcus shouted at him from above, 'Don't just stand there! Start climbing!' (1 mark)","Cause 2, who saw it: 'the other students watched him tremble'. Being watched by his classmates while he shook with fear is what made it embarrassing. (1 mark)","Writing only 'Marcus shouted at him' gets 1 mark. Add that his classmates were watching him tremble."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q69","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 69","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":69,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["character-and-feelings","inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on information from lines 17-21 (paragraph 4, from \"minded will to be the first group\" to \"kept his distance\"), fill in the following table.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Marcus: What do his actions tell us about how he felt?","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Marcus felt angry (or: annoyed / irritated / impatient / agitated).","accept":["angry","annoyed","irritated","impatient","agitated","He felt angry","Marcus felt angry","He was angry","Marcus was angry"]},"explanation":["Marcus's actions in these lines: he 'snatched' Ryan's water bottle, 'shouted', 'shoved' the bottle into his own bag and 'stomped off'.","'snatched', 'shoved' and 'stomped' are all rough, forceful actions. They show he was angry, and impatient with Ryan for slowing the group down.","The marker accepts: angry, agitated, impatient, irritated or annoyed. Give a feeling word, not a description of what he did."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Ryan: What does his facial expression tell us about how he felt?","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Ryan felt shocked (or: surprised / caught off-guard).","accept":["shocked","surprised","caught off-guard","caught off guard","He felt shocked","Ryan felt shocked","He was shocked","Ryan was shocked","He felt surprised","He was surprised"]},"explanation":["The question asks about Ryan's **facial expression**. Find the look on his face: 'Ryan stared wide-eyed at Marcus's back.'","Eyes open wide show shock or surprise. He did not expect Marcus to snatch his bottle.","The marker accepts: surprised, shocked or caught off-guard. It must be an unpleasant kind of surprise, so do not write 'amazed' or 'excited'. 'Sad' (from 'felt his eyes moisten') is not what the key takes, because the wide-eyed stare is the facial expression."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q70","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 70","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":70,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["true-false-with-reason"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on lines 15-23 (paragraphs 4 and 5), state whether each statement in the table below is true or false, then give one reason why you think so.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Marcus has a competitive nature.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. Marcus had a single-minded will to be the first group to reach the checkpoint."},"explanation":["A competitive person badly wants to win or be first. Look for that in paragraph 4.","'Marcus had a single-minded will to be the first group to reach the checkpoint.' 'Single-minded' means thinking about one aim only, and his aim was to be first.","So the statement is True. The reason must be this evidence, not the statement repeated."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Ryan could not control his emotions well.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. Ryan felt his eyes moisten (well up with tears), but he forced himself to keep walking in silence."},"explanation":["Find the moment Ryan's feelings were tested: after Marcus's outburst, 'Ryan felt his eyes moisten, but he forced himself to keep walking in silence.'","He was close to tears, but the word 'but' shows he held them back and carried on quietly. That is good control of his emotions.","So the statement is False. Give both halves: his eyes welled up, yet he forced himself to keep walking in silence."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"Ryan was still upset with Marcus during dinner.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. Ryan was in a sullen mood and sat alone at the edge of the campsite during dinner. (Or: He was still muttering that all Marcus cared about was that silly trophy.)"},"explanation":["Paragraph 5 describes dinner: 'By evening, Ryan was in a sullen mood. During dinner, he sat alone at the edge of the campsite.' 'Sullen' means silent and bad-tempered.","He also muttered, 'All Marcus cares about is that silly trophy', which shows that his unhappiness was about Marcus.","So the statement is True. Either piece of evidence earns the mark."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q71","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 71","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":71,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["referencing"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Look at the table below. What do the words in the left column refer to in the passage?","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"it (line 32, paragraph 7)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"text","value":"the brass compass","accept":["the compass","compass","brass compass","the brass compass that belonged to Marcus's brother","Marcus's compass","Marcus' compass","his brother's compass","the compass Marcus was holding","the brass compass Marcus was holding","his compass"]},"explanation":["'Marcus jumped and tried to hide **it**.' Ask: what was Marcus holding that he would want to hide?","Look back to paragraph 6: 'Marcus was holding the brass compass so tightly that his knuckles were white.' Later, in paragraph 7, he 'held up the compass'.","So 'it' refers to the (brass) compass. Extra detail, such as that it belonged to his brother, is fine."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Herculean task (line 36, paragraph 7)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Winning 'Best Camper' like his brother did (being just like his brother, as his parents expected).","accept":["winning Best Camper","Marcus winning Best Camper","winning 'Best Camper'","Marcus winning 'Best Camper'"]},"explanation":["Read the sentence before the phrase: 'He had won 'Best Camper' three years ago. My parents expect me to be just like him, but it's a Herculean task.'","A Herculean task is one that needs enormous effort. Here the task is what his parents expect: to be just like his brother, that is, to win 'Best Camper' too.","The marker looks for: (Marcus) winning 'Best Camper'. Writing only 'a very difficult task' explains the meaning of the phrase but does not say what it refers to."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q72","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 72","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":72,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on information from lines 24-31 (paragraph 6), fill in the following table.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Cause: Ryan saw Marcus sobbing and shaking.\nEffect: __________","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Ryan's anger towards Marcus began to soften. (Ryan became less angry.)","accept":["Ryan's anger began to soften.","Ryan became less angry.","Ryan's anger faded.","His anger began to soften."]},"explanation":["Find the sentence that matches the cause: 'However, as he watched Marcus shaking, his anger began to soften.'","The word 'as' links the two: watching Marcus sob and shake is the cause, and Ryan's anger softening is the effect.","The marker accepts: Ryan's anger (towards Marcus) began to soften / Ryan became less angry / Ryan's anger faded."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Cause: __________\nEffect: Marcus' knuckles turned white.","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Marcus held the (brass) compass very tightly.","accept":["Marcus held the compass tightly.","Marcus was holding the brass compass tightly.","Marcus was holding the compass tightly.","Marcus held the brass compass tightly.","Marcus was holding the brass compass so tightly."]},"explanation":["Find the knuckles in paragraph 6: 'Marcus was holding the brass compass **so** tightly **that** his knuckles were white.'","'so ... that' is a cause-and-effect pattern. What comes after 'so' is the cause; what comes after 'that' is the effect.","Cause: Marcus was holding the compass tightly. Include the word 'tightly'; just 'holding the compass' is not enough."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q73","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 73","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":73,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Marcus' reactions towards Ryan shifted greatly after the conversation from lines 32-37 (paragraph 7). What did Marcus do differently whenever Ryan struggled to complete an activity?","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Before the conversation","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Marcus shouted (snapped / yelled) at Ryan and embarrassed him in front of the others."},"explanation":["Look at the two times Ryan struggled before the conversation. At the rock wall (paragraph 3): Marcus 'shouted from above', 'Don't just stand there! Start climbing!' On the hike (paragraph 4): he snatched the bottle and shouted, 'You're drinking too much and stopping too often!'","Both times he scolded Ryan loudly in front of the class instead of helping.","The marker accepts: snapped / yelled at him, humiliated him, embarrassed him, reprimanded him, or felt annoyed with him."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"After the conversation","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Marcus went over to help Ryan: he taught (guided / advised) him how to do it."},"explanation":["After the conversation, Ryan struggled again, this time to light his fire (paragraph 8).","This time 'Marcus came over and crouched beside him', showed him how to stack the fuel tablets and blocked the wind for him.","The marker accepts: taught him, guided him, advised him (helped him)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-b-q75","cite":"2026 Prelim Nan Hua Paper 2 Booklet B Question 75","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nan Hua","section":"b","number":75,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"\"He nodded approvingly, thinking that sometimes, the most important lessons at camp had nothing to do with knots or rock walls.\" (lines 44-45, paragraph 9)\nWhat did Mr Lim think Marcus and Ryan learnt from their time at camp? Support your answer with evidence from the passage.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Mr Lim thought they had learnt to be kind and supportive towards each other (to empathise with others / the importance of friendship). Marcus came over to help Ryan when he was struggling to light his fire, and even used his own body to block the wind."},"explanation":["'Knots or rock walls' stand for camp skills. Mr Lim meant the boys had learnt something about how to treat people instead. (Lesson, 1 mark)","The marker accepts lessons such as: to empathise with others, to be supportive, teamwork, patience, caring, kindness, understanding, resolving conflict, being grateful, or the importance of friendship.","Evidence (1 mark): it must be something Mr Lim saw. He 'watched the interaction between the two boys' in paragraph 8: Marcus came over to help Ryan when he was struggling to light the fire, and blocked the wind with his body.","The key says the evidence must come from lines 38-43 or from an earlier incident Mr Lim witnessed (the High Elements course or the hiking expedition). The night-time talk by the benches does not count, because Mr Lim was not there."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-p1-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 1 Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"p1","number":1,"topic":"writing","subtypes":["situational-writing"],"format":"structured","marks":14,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The pictures below show Ricky and his classmate, Alice, discussing an idea during their school recess. Study the pictures carefully.\n[[img1]]\n++Your Task++\nImagine you are Ricky.\n**Write an email to your principal, Mdm Tan, asking her to consider setting up a reading corner.**\nYou are to refer to the pictures and information above for your email. The bullet point underlined below requires you to come up with your own information.\n\nIn your writing, include the following key information:\n- why you think a reading corner should be set up\n- where the reading corner can be set up\n- two ways you can contribute\n- how your schoolmates may contribute\n- ++one suggestion on how to make the reading corner more attractive to students++\nYou may reorder the points. Remember to write in complete sentences.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There is no single model answer. Check your writing against the points below."},"explanation":["Purpose: to ask (persuade) the principal to consider setting up a reading corner. Say this clearly in the opening lines and repeat the request politely at the end.","Audience: Mdm Tan, your principal. Context: you are Ricky, a pupil of the school. Use a formal, respectful tone: 'Dear Mdm Tan', no slang or short forms, and a polite close such as 'Thank you for considering my suggestion.' Sign off 'Yours sincerely, Ricky' and give your class.","Point 1, why: there are often no empty seats in the school library, so it is getting harder for avid readers to find a quiet, comfortable place to sit and read during recess.","Point 2, where: the vacant space between the library and the staircase.","Point 3, two ways you can contribute: provide an old bookshelf that is still in good condition (your parents have been thinking of giving it away), and design some posters to decorate the reading corner.","Point 4, how schoolmates may contribute: they can donate their pre-loved books for others to read.","Point 5, your own idea (the underlined point): one sensible suggestion to make the corner more attractive, for example adding cushions or beanbags, displaying a 'Book of the Week', or letting classes take turns to decorate it. The picture does not give this, so you must think of it yourself.","Format and accuracy: email layout (To, From, Subject, greeting, paragraphs, sign-off). Write as 'I' (Ricky), not 'Ricky said'. Do not copy the speech bubbles word for word; change them into your own complete sentences. Marks are for task fulfilment (all five points, purpose, audience, context) and for language."]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-p1-q1-img1","alt":"Two comic panels showing Alice and Ricky from behind, standing outside the school LIBRARY, where students are reading at a full table; a staircase is on the right and there is an empty space between the library and the staircase. Panel 1. Alice: 'Ricky, there are no empty seats in the school library again!' Ricky: 'It's getting harder for avid readers like us to find a quiet, comfortable place to sit and read during recess. Hmm... Look over there, Alice. Don't you think the vacant space between the library and the staircase would be perfect for a reading corner?' Alice: 'You're right!' Panel 2. Ricky: 'I can provide an old bookshelf which is still in good condition. My parents have been thinking of giving it away. I can also design some posters to decorate the reading corner. Students can even donate their pre-loved books for others to read.' Alice: 'Wow, those are great ideas! You should email our principal with your suggestion.' Ricky: 'Thanks, Alice! I'll do that after checking with my parents.'"}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-p1-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 1 Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"p1","number":2,"topic":"writing","subtypes":["continuous-writing"],"format":"structured","marks":36,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write a composition of ++at least 150 words++ about **making something better.**\nThe pictures are provided to help you think about this topic.\nYour composition must be based on one or more of these pictures.\n[[img1]]\nConsider the following points when you plan your composition:\n- What had to be made better?\n- How did it get better?\nYou may use the points in any order and include other relevant points as well.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There is no single model answer. Check your writing against the points below."},"explanation":["Understand the topic: 'making something better' means something was not good enough at first, and someone's effort improved it. Your story must show clearly what was wrong (before), what was done, and how it was better (after).","Picture 1, the charity bake sale: the bakes tasted bad or sales were poor at first, and you improved the recipe, the display or the teamwork so that more money was raised. It could also be about making other people's lives better through the money raised.","Picture 2, the stage with a stool and microphone: a weak speech, song or performance that got better through practice and feedback, or stage fright that you overcame. You could also make a dull school concert better.","Picture 3, the littered beach: a dirty beach made better by a clean-up that you organised or joined, or people's careless habits changed for the better.","Story arc: (1) opening that shows the problem; (2) the moment you decided something had to change; (3) what you did, including a setback or difficulty so that the improvement is not too easy; (4) the result, shown through what people saw, said and felt.","Answer both guiding points in the story: what had to be made better, and how it got better. Use at least one picture and keep the topic at the centre from start to finish, not only in the last line.","Ending: return to the topic with a reflection, for example what you learnt about effort, patience or working together to make something better.","Language: at least 150 words (aim for much more), paragraphs, past tense kept consistent, varied sentence openings, and vivid words to show feelings and actions."]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-p1-q2-img1","alt":"Three pictures, one above the other. Picture 1: a boy and a younger child at a stall with cupcakes and a cake; the sign on the stall says 'Charity Bake Sale'. Picture 2: an empty stage with a stool and a microphone on a stand. Picture 3: a beach littered with rubbish: tied rubbish bags, plastic bottles, cans and food boxes on the sand near the water."}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":1,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["tenses","subject-verb-agreement"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Sam and his groupmates __________ their group project, but they will only submit it next week.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["First find the subject: 'Sam **and** his groupmates'. Two subjects joined by 'and' are plural, so the helping verb is 'have', not 'has'. That removes options (1) and (3).","Next choose the tense. The project is already finished, because they are only waiting to submit it. A finished action with a result now takes the present perfect: have + past participle.","'have been completing' is tempting, but the present perfect continuous means the action is still going on, which does not fit a project that is ready to be submitted.","'Sam and his groupmates have completed their group project.' The answer is option (2)."]}],"options":["has completed","have completed","has been completing","have been completing"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":2,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["modals"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"You __________ take better care of your belongings or they might go missing.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["The sentence gives advice and a warning: do this, 'or they might go missing'.","Rule: use 'should' to give advice or to say what is the right thing to do.","'can' and 'could' only say that something is possible or that you are able to do it. 'would' is for imagined situations or polite requests. None of them gives advice.","'You should take better care of your belongings.' The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["can","could","would","should"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q3","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 3","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":3,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["conditionals"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"If I had known about the book sale, I __________ you there.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Look at the 'if' part: 'If I **had known**'. This is the past perfect, so the speaker is imagining a past that did not happen (he did not know about the sale).","Rule (third conditional): If + past perfect, ... would have + past participle.","'would follow' is tempting, but it goes with 'If I knew' (second conditional), which is about now or the future, not the past.","'If I had known about the book sale, I would have followed you there.' The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["will follow","would follow","will have followed","would have followed"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q4","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 4","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":4,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["prepositions","phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"We are going to be late, so I suggest we cut __________ this field to get to the bus stop.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clue: they are late, so they want the shortest way to the bus stop.","'cut across' a place means to go straight through it from one side to the other as a shortcut, instead of going round it.","'around' is the opposite of a shortcut. 'cut into' means to slice something or to interrupt, and 'cut over' is not used for taking a shortcut.","'...we cut across this field to get to the bus stop.' The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["into","over","across","around"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q5","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 5","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":5,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Having won the tournament, my teammates and I treated __________ to a feast.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Ask who did the treating and who received the treat. Both are the same people: 'my teammates and I'.","Rule: when the subject and the object are the same people, use a reflexive pronoun (a '-self' or '-selves' word).","'My teammates and I' is the same as 'we', and the reflexive pronoun for 'we' is 'ourselves'. 'themselves' goes with 'they', and 'us' is not reflexive.","'...my teammates and I treated ourselves to a feast.' The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["us","them","ourselves","themselves"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q6","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 6","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":6,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["subject-verb-agreement","tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"One-fifth of the students __________ absent from school yesterday.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'yesterday' tells us the verb must be in the past tense, so 'is' and 'are' are out.","Rule: with a fraction + 'of', the verb agrees with the noun after 'of'. 'One-fifth of the students' is a number of students, and 'students' is plural, so the verb is plural.","'was' is tempting because of the word 'One', but it would only be right with something that cannot be counted, such as 'One-fifth of the cake was eaten.'","'One-fifth of the students were absent from school yesterday.' The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["is","are","was","were"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q7","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 7","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":7,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["infinitives-and-gerunds","verb-forms"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"As I was returning home from school, I heard a dog __________ loudly.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Rule: after a verb of the senses (see, hear, watch, feel, notice) + object, use the -ing form (or the base form) of the next verb: heard + a dog + barking.","'barking' shows the dog was in the middle of barking when I heard it.","'was barking' is tempting, but 'heard' is already the main verb of this clause. 'I heard a dog was barking' would need 'that' and has a different meaning. 'barked' gives the clause two main verbs, and 'had barking' is not a real verb form.","'...I heard a dog barking loudly.' The answer is option (2)."]}],"options":["barked","barking","had barking","was barking"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q8","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 8","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":8,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["relative-pronouns"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"We visited the museum __________ the rare fossil bones are displayed.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["The blank begins a clause that tells us more about 'the museum', which is a place.","The clause after the blank is already complete: 'the rare fossil bones are displayed'. It tells us what happens **in** that place, so we need 'where' (= in which).","'which' is tempting because a museum is a thing, but then we would have to say 'in which the bones are displayed' or 'which displays the bones'. 'whom' is for people and 'whose' shows belonging.","'We visited the museum where the rare fossil bones are displayed.' The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["which","whom","where","whose"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q9","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 9","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":9,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["question-tags"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"He'll want you to accompany him, __________ he?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Rule: a positive statement takes a negative question tag, and the tag uses the same helping verb as the statement.","'He'll' is short for 'He **will**'. The helping verb is 'will', and its negative is 'won't'.","'wouldn't' is tempting, but it goes with 'He'd' (he would). 'doesn't' and 'didn't' go with statements that have no helping verb, such as 'He wants' or 'He wanted'.","'He'll want you to accompany him, won't he?' The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["won't","didn't","doesn't","wouldn't"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q10","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 10","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":10,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Please move __________ pairs of shoes here to that corner,\" my teacher said to me.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Two clues decide the word: how many, and how near.","'pairs' is plural, so we need a plural word: 'these' or 'those'. That removes 'this' and 'that'.","The shoes are 'here', near the speaker, so we use 'these'. 'those' is for things that are far away, like 'that corner'.","'Please move these pairs of shoes here to that corner.' The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["this","that","these","those"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q11","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 11","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":11,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"After weeks of __________ by the owner, the leaves of the young plant withered and it died shortly after.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Clues: the leaves 'withered' and the plant 'died', and the blank is something done 'by the owner'.","'neglect' means not giving something the care it needs. A plant that is not watered or looked after will wither and die.","'deterioration' (becoming worse) is tempting, but it happens to the plant; it is not something the owner does to it. 'disrepair' is used for buildings and machines in bad condition, and 'nourishment' (food needed to grow) would keep the plant alive.","The answer is option (1), neglect."]}],"options":["neglect","disrepair","nourishment","deterioration"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q12","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 12","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":12,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Try not to let the difficult questions __________ you and stop you from attempting the other simpler ones.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clue: the difficult questions might 'stop you from attempting' the easier ones. We need a word for making someone too scared or unsure to try.","'intimidate' means to frighten someone so that they lose confidence.","'aggravate' is tempting, but it means to make a problem worse or to annoy someone; being annoyed would not stop you from trying easier questions. 'subdue' means to bring under control by force, and 'excite' is a positive feeling.","The answer is option (3), intimidate."]}],"options":["excite","subdue","intimidate","aggravate"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q13","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 13","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":13,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"These musicians have an __________ way of making music using pots and pans.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Clue: they make music 'using pots and pans', not normal instruments. We need a word that means unusual, not done the normal way.","'unconventional' means different from what is usual or traditional.","'unassuming' means modest and not showing off; 'unbecoming' means not suitable or not proper; 'unfazed' means not worried or upset. These describe people or behaviour, not an unusual method.","The answer is option (4), unconventional."]}],"options":["unfazed","unassuming","unbecoming","unconventional"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q14","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 14","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":14,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"My father knocked __________ on my sister's door as she was going to be late for school.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Clue: she 'was going to be late', so Father kept knocking and would not stop until she responded.","'insistently' means again and again, in a way that demands attention.","'compulsively' is tempting, but it means being unable to stop doing something because of a habit you cannot control, not because of a good reason. 'laboriously' means with great effort and slowly; 'persuasively' means in a way that convinces someone with words.","The answer is option (1), insistently."]}],"options":["insistently","laboriously","persuasively","compulsively"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q15","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 15","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":15,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Bob's birthday is around the corner. I need your help to __________ some ideas so I can buy him a good gift.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clue: the speaker wants help with 'some ideas' for a gift, so the friend is being asked to suggest ideas.","'put forward' means to suggest or offer an idea for others to consider.","'take up' means to start a hobby or to accept an offer; 'count on' means to depend on someone; 'see through' means to realise that someone is trying to trick you. None of them means to suggest.","The answer is option (3), put forward."]}],"options":["take up","count on","put forward","see through"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q16","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 16","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":16,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++alleviate++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'alleviate' means to make something bad, such as pain or a problem, less severe.","Clue in the passage: the next sentence says petting a dog has an impact on 'reducing anxiety levels'. Companionship makes loneliness and stress less.","'reduce' means to make less, so it is the closest. 'intensify' is the opposite (make stronger).","'trim' and 'shorten' are tempting because they also make something smaller, but they are about cutting length or size, not about feelings. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g1","options":["trim","reduce","shorten","intensify"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q17","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 17","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":17,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++regular++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'regular' walks are walks that happen often and at steady times, such as every day.","Clue in the passage: the walks 'encourage more physical activity', so they must happen often.","'frequent' means happening often, so it is the closest.","'occasional' (only sometimes) and 'random' (with no pattern) are the opposite idea, and 'casual' means relaxed. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g1","options":["casual","random","frequent","occasional"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q18","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 18","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":18,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++anecdotes++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["An 'anecdote' is a short, interesting or funny story about something that really happened to you.","Clue in the passage: dog owners 'bond and connect' by telling one another about 'their dogs' quirky behaviours', that is, real things their dogs did.","A 'recount' is a retelling of something that happened, so it is the closest.","'fables' is tempting because fables are also short stories, but they are made-up stories with a moral. 'reports' are formal and factual, and 'testimonials' are statements praising a product or person. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g1","options":["fables","reports","recounts","testimonials"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q19","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 19","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":19,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["idioms"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the words closest in meaning to ++no walk in the park++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["The idiom 'a walk in the park' means something very easy to do. So 'no walk in the park' means not easy at all.","Clue in the passage: the paragraph starts with 'However', turning from the benefits to the hard part, and says owners must 'commit to their needs for many years'.","'not demanding' means not needing much effort, which is the opposite. 'not rewarding' is wrong because the first paragraph lists many benefits.","The answer is option (1), not easy."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g1","options":["not easy","not rewarding","not demanding","not comfortable"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q20","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 20","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":20,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++completely++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["'completely' means totally, in every way, with nothing left out.","'fully' has the same meaning: 'fully prepared' = 'completely prepared'.","'adequately' is tempting, but it means only just enough, which is weaker than completely. 'basically' means in the main or simple points only, and 'exceptionally' means unusually or extremely well.","The answer is option (1), fully."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g1","options":["fully","basically","adequately","exceptionally"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q21","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 21","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":21,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Carry Everything in Style.\" The purpose of this statement in Text 1 is to __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Break the slogan into its two halves. 'Carry Everything' says the bag holds all you need, so it is useful. 'in Style' says the bag looks good, so it is trendy.","A slogan on a poster advertises the product. Option (2) covers both halves and is about the bag.","Option (1) is tempting, but it covers only 'Carry Everything' and leaves out 'in Style'.","Options (3) and (4) are about what students should do; the slogan is selling the bag, not advising students. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g2","options":["emphasise the bag's storage capacity","highlight that the bag is useful and trendy","encourage students to be both well-prepared and stylish","remind students to look good while carrying their belongings"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q22","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 22","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":22,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"How does Ansel's review in Text 1 reinforce the information presented in the poster?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'Reinforce' means to support or repeat what the poster already says. Match Ansel's words to the poster.","Ansel: 'Everything I need for school fits inside it' matches 'Fit your entire school day into one bag' (spacious storage). 'it's extremely comfortable' matches 'Big bags never felt so comfortable!' (comfort).","(3) is wrong: he bought it for its comfort, so size is not the 'sole' (only) appeal. (1) is wrong: one boy's opinion cannot show it suits all 12-year-olds.","(4) is tempting because he says he can find any item easily, but the poster already says 'a specific space for everything', so it is not a new feature. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g2","options":["It shows that the bag is suitable for all 12-year-olds.","It highlights the bag's spacious storage and comfort.","It emphasises that the sole appeal of the bag is its size.","It mentions a specific feature of the bag not shown in the poster."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q23","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 23","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":23,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["factual","inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Based on Text 1, which of the following statements are true about the Peaks school bags?\nA: The bag is small and light.\nB: The bag comes in different colours.\nC: The bag has multiple compartments.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Check each statement against the poster.","A is false: the poster says 'Big bags never felt so comfortable!' and Ansel says 'it's bigger than other school bags'. Nothing says the bag is light.","B is true: 'Wide range of colours to choose from!'","C is true: 'Get organised with a specific space for everything!' Separate spaces for different things are compartments.","Only B and C are true. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g2","options":["A and B","A and C","B and C","A, B and C"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q24","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 24","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":24,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference","purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"What is the **main** message of Text 2?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["The main message is the idea that the whole text builds up to, not a single detail.","Paragraph 2 warns that a heavy bag 'strains your back' and tells you to 'carry a school bag that fits comfortably on your back' (choosing). Paragraph 3 tells you to pack 'only the items needed for the day' to 'protect your back' (using). Both are about comfort and protecting the back.","(2) is tempting, but lockers and desks are only an example ('For example, ...') of how to keep the bag light.","(4) is only the opening observation, which the writer then questions with 'have you considered the weight?'. (1) is wrong because the text names two helpful features, not 'more' features. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g2","options":["Students should choose school bags with more features.","Students should use lockers or desks to store their belongings.","Students should prioritise comfort when choosing and using school bags.","Students should express their personality and personal style through their choice of school bags."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-a-q25","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet A Question 25","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"a","number":25,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Texts 1 and 2 express differing views on whether __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'Differing views' means the two texts disagree. Find the point where one says yes and the other says no.","Text 1 encourages it: 'Fit your entire school day into one bag – all your books, stationery, lunch boxes and more!'","Text 2 discourages it: 'While it may seem exciting to fit everything into one bag, have you considered the weight?' and experts recommend 'packing only the items needed for the day'.","(3) is tempting, but Text 2 never says a bag **cannot** fit everything; it says you **should not** carry everything. The disagreement is about 'should'. (1) is only in Text 2, and both texts agree that bags look appealing. The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g2","options":["students can store certain items in school","school bags should look appealing to students","school bags can fit everything needed for school","students should fit everything into their school bags"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q26","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 26","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":26,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (26). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"F","accept":["f","had","G","g","have"]},"explanation":["After the blank is 'been learning', so the blank needs a helping verb that goes with 'been': have, has or had.","The visit happened 'Last Saturday' (past). The learning went on 'For months' **before** that visit. An action that continued up to a point in the past takes the past perfect continuous: had been + verb-ing.","(G) 'have' also makes a correct sentence if the members are still learning now ('have been learning'), and the key accepts both letters.","The answer is (F) had: 'the club members had been learning'. (G) have is also accepted."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q27","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 27","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":27,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (27). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"E","accept":["e","each"]},"explanation":["After the blank is the singular noun 'team', and the idea is that every one of the groups tried a different activity.","'each' is used before a singular noun to talk about every member of a group, one by one: 'each team'.","(A) 'another' also goes before a singular noun, but it means one more; it does not fit 'so that ___ team could try a **different** activity'.","The answer is (E) each."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q28","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 28","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":28,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (28). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"C","accept":["c","because"]},"explanation":["The second half gives the reason for the first half. Why loosen the soil gently? The roots were delicate.","A connector that introduces a reason is 'because'.","The answer is (C) because: 'loosen the soil gently because the roots were delicate'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q29","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 29","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":29,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (29). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Q","accept":["q","which"]},"explanation":["The words after the blank ('was safer for children to use') tell us more about 'a tool with a short handle'.","To add information about a thing, use the relative pronoun 'which'. It also acts as the subject of 'was'.","The answer is (Q) which: 'a tool with a short handle which was safer for children to use'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q30","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 30","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":30,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (30). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"H","accept":["h","must"]},"explanation":["The blank comes between 'we' and 'not pour', so it needs a modal verb (a helping verb followed by the base form 'pour').","The farmer is giving a firm instruction about something they are not allowed to do. 'must not' is used to forbid something.","(K) 'shall' is also a modal, but 'shall not' is not used for giving this kind of instruction.","The answer is (H) must: 'we must not pour too much water onto the plants'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q31","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 31","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":31,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (31). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"D","accept":["d","but"]},"explanation":["Compare the two halves: 'Too little water would dry them out' and 'too much water would drown them'. They are opposite problems.","A connector that joins two contrasting ideas is 'but'.","The answer is (D) but."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q32","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 32","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":32,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (32). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"B","accept":["b","at"]},"explanation":["'___ the end of the session' needs a preposition of time.","The fixed phrase is 'at the end of' something (at the end of the day, at the end of the lesson).","The blank starts the sentence, so it is written with a capital letter: 'At the end of the session'.","The answer is (B) at."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q33","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 33","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":33,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (33). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"L","accept":["l","than"]},"explanation":["Before the blank is the comparative adjective 'heavier'.","Rule: comparative adjective + 'than' to compare two things: heavier than, taller than.","(M) 'then' looks and sounds similar, but 'then' is about time (next, after that). Do not mix them up.","The answer is (L) than: 'heavier than the rest'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q34","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 34","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":34,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (34). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"J","accept":["j","our"]},"explanation":["The blank comes before the noun 'hands', so it needs a possessive word that says whose hands.","The farmer 'told **us** to wash ___ hands'. The hands belong to 'us', and the possessive form for we/us is 'our'.","(N) 'their' is tempting, but the writer is part of the group ('we thanked the farmer', 'told us'), so it must be 'our', not 'their'.","The answer is (J) our."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q35","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 35","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":35,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (35). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"P","accept":["p","when"]},"explanation":["The second half tells us the time or condition under which fresh produce tastes better: it is eaten soon after harvesting.","'when' joins the two clauses and means 'at the time that' or 'whenever'.","(M) 'then' cannot join two clauses like this, and (C) 'because' is already used for blank (28).","The answer is (P) when: 'tastes better when it is eaten soon after harvesting'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q36","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 36","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":36,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (36) ++have++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"had"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. The subject is 'everyone'. Words like everyone, everybody and each are singular, so the plural verb 'have' is wrong.","Next choose the tense. The sentence reports what the teacher said in the past ('reminded us that...'), and the whole passage is a past-tense recount. In reported speech the verb moves back into the past: has becomes had.","Compare the next sentence, which is also reported speech in the past: 'She also said that a panel **would** be put...'.","The correct word is 'had'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q37","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 37","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":37,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (37) ++comemoret++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"commemorate"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means to remember and show respect for an important person or event.","Say it in syllables: com-mem-o-rate. It has a double 'm' followed by a single 'm': co**mm**e**m**orate.","It ends in '-ate', like 'celebrate' and 'decorate'.","The correct word is 'commemorate'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q38","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 38","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":38,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (38) ++in++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"with"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong preposition.","The fixed phrase is 'buzzing **with** activity' (or 'buzzing with excitement'), meaning full of busy movement and noise.","Similar phrases also take 'with': filled with, bursting with, crowded with.","The correct word is 'with'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q39","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 39","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":39,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (39) ++were++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"was"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error in subject-verb agreement.","The subject is 'Every display board'. Rule: 'every' and 'each' are followed by a singular noun and a singular verb.","The sentence is in the past tense ('before the visitors arrived'), so the singular past form is 'was'.","The correct word is 'was': 'Every display board was checked very carefully'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q40","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 40","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":40,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (40) ++coherant++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"coherent"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means clear, well organised and easy to understand.","Say it in syllables: co-her-ent. The ending is '-ent', not '-ant'.","It belongs with other '-ent' words such as 'different', 'excellent' and 'confident'.","The correct word is 'coherent'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q41","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 41","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":41,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (41) ++wrote++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"written"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. Look at the helping verb in front: 'he **had** ___'.","Rule: had + past participle makes the past perfect tense. 'wrote' is the simple past form; the past participle of 'write' is 'written' (write, wrote, written).","The past perfect fits because he wrote about the place 'many years earlier', before he told the pupils.","The correct word is 'written'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q42","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 42","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":42,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (42) ++jornal++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"journal"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means a book in which you write about what happens to you, like a diary.","The first syllable is spelt 'jour', with 'ou', as in 'journey'.","j-o-u-r-n-a-l.","The correct word is 'journal'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q43","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 43","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":43,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (43) ++asppecialy++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"especially"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means particularly or very.","It is the word 'special' with 'e' in front and '-ly' at the end: e + special + ly.","So it begins with 'e' (not 'a'), has one 'p', and has a double 'l' because 'special' ends in 'l' and we add 'ly'.","The correct word is 'especially'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q44","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 44","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":44,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (44) ++Since++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Despite"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong connector.","Look at the meaning. They were tired, yet they still felt the effort was worthwhile. The two ideas contrast, so we need a word that means 'even though'. 'Since' gives a reason, which does not make sense here.","The word must also fit the grammar: it is followed by 'being tired' (an -ing form). 'Despite' is followed by a noun or an -ing form: 'Despite being tired'. 'Although' would need a full clause ('Although we were tired').","The correct word is 'Despite'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q45","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 45","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":45,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (45) ++undoubtably++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"undoubtedly"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means certainly, without any doubt.","Build it up: doubt, undoubted, undoubted + ly. The ending is '-edly', not '-ably'.","Remember the silent 'b' in 'doubt'.","The correct word is 'undoubtedly'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q46","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 46","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":46,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (46) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Instead"},"explanation":["The sentence compares the old way ('copying notes for the entire period') with the new way ('students now work in small groups').","The blank is followed by 'of' + an -ing word. The phrase 'Instead of' + verb-ing means 'in place of' doing something.","It starts the sentence, so it takes a capital letter.","The answer is 'Instead'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q47","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 47","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":47,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (47) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"groups","accept":["teams"]},"explanation":["The students work together 'to answer questions, sort cards and explain ideas to one another', so they are in small sets of people.","Look ahead for the same idea: 'They can first discuss in small **groups** before speaking to the whole class.'","The noun must be plural because there is no 'a' before 'small'. 'teams' also fits.","The answer is 'groups'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q48","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 48","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":48,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (48) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"whether","accept":["how"]},"explanation":["Students must 'think carefully about ___ their ideas make sense to others'. They are checking: do my ideas make sense, or not?","'whether' introduces a yes-or-no question inside a sentence, and it can follow a preposition such as 'about'.","'if' is tempting, but we do not use 'if' straight after a preposition ('about if' is wrong). 'how' also makes a sensible sentence.","The answer is 'whether'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q49","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 49","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":49,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms","contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (49) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"sharing","accept":["discussing"]},"explanation":["After the small-group talk, the students tell their answers ('them') to 'the class'.","The verb must go with 'with': we **share** something **with** someone.","After the preposition 'before', the verb takes the -ing form: 'before sharing them'.","The answer is 'sharing'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q50","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 50","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":50,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (50) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"This","accept":["that"]},"explanation":["The blank is the subject of 'gives them time to build their confidence'. What gives them time? The whole idea in the sentence before: discussing in small groups first.","To point back to a whole idea that has just been mentioned, use 'This'.","The verb 'gives' is singular, so a plural word like 'These' or 'They' is wrong.","The answer is 'This'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q51","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 51","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":51,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (51) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"order","accept":["sequence"]},"explanation":["Clue cards are arranged 'to show how a process works'. A process has steps that happen one after another.","The phrase is 'in the correct order', meaning each step is in its right place. 'sequence' means the same.","The answer is 'order'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q52","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 52","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":52,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms","contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (52) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"explaining","accept":["stating","justifying"]},"explanation":["Students earn points by doing something about 'why one answer is wrong while another is correct'. Giving the reason why is explaining.","Look back for the same idea: students 'explain ideas to one another' in the first paragraph.","After the preposition 'by', the verb takes the -ing form: 'by explaining'.","The answer is 'explaining'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q53","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 53","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":53,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (53) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"more"},"explanation":["The sentence tells us a change teachers have noticed 'after taking part in such activities', so it compares students now with students before.","The whole passage is positive about the games (quieter students join in and build confidence), so the students are 'more willing' to ask questions, not less.","'willing' is a long adjective, so its comparative is formed with 'more'.","The answer is 'more'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q54","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 54","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":54,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (54) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"make"},"explanation":["The pattern after the blank is noun + base verb: '___ learning feel less stressful'. The verb that fits this pattern is 'make': make + something + do something.","The same pattern appears in the last sentence: 'revision games can **make** learning enjoyable'.","The subject 'the games' is plural and the paragraph is in the present tense, so we write 'make', not 'makes' or 'made'.","The answer is 'make'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q55","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 55","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":55,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (55) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"if","accept":["when"]},"explanation":["'Of course' introduces a condition: the games help **only** on the condition that students still revise and do their homework.","'only if' introduces the one condition that must be met.","The answer is 'if': 'beneficial only if students continue to revise their notes'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q56","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 56","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":56,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (56) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"alike"},"explanation":["The blank comes after two groups, 'teachers and students', and before the verb 'should understand'.","'A and B alike' means both A and B equally. It is placed after the two nouns: 'teachers and students alike'.","'both' is tempting, but it normally comes before the pair ('both teachers and students'), not in this position.","The answer is 'alike'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q57","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 57","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":57,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (57) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"is"},"explanation":["The clause needs a verb: 'the use of such activities in lessons ___ just one part of the learning process'.","Find the true subject. It is 'the use', not 'activities' or 'lessons', which only belong to the phrases after 'of' and 'in'. 'the use' is singular.","The paragraph is in the present tense, so the singular present verb is 'is'. 'are' is the trap.","The answer is 'is'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q58","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 58","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":58,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (58) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"aside","accept":["apart"]},"explanation":["'time set ___ for reading, practice and discussion' is about keeping some time for a special purpose.","The phrasal verb 'set aside' means to keep or save something, especially time or money, for a purpose.","The answer is 'aside': 'with time set aside for reading, practice and discussion'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q59","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 59","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":59,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (59) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"not"},"explanation":["'more often than ___' is a fixed phrase.","'more often than not' means usually, or most of the time.","So a balanced routine is usually the most effective way to prepare for an examination.","The answer is 'not'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q60","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 60","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":60,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (60) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"on"},"explanation":["'stay ___ task' is a fixed phrase.","To 'stay on task' means to keep your attention on the work you are supposed to be doing.","It fits the meaning: students who enjoy learning are more likely to keep working.","The answer is 'on'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q61","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 61","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":61,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["no-sooner-than"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The rain stopped. The players continued with the match immediately.\nNo sooner ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"No sooner had the rain stopped than the players continued with the match.","accept":["No sooner had the rain stopped than the players continued with the match immediately."]},"explanation":["'No sooner ... than ...' shows that the second action happened straight after the first.","When a sentence begins with 'No sooner', the helping verb comes before the subject (inversion), and the first action is in the past perfect: No sooner **had** the rain **stopped** ...","The second action follows 'than' (not 'when' or 'then') and stays in the simple past: ... than the players continued with the match.","Drop 'immediately', because 'No sooner' already carries that meaning. Answer: No sooner had the rain stopped than the players continued with the match."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q62","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 62","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":62,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["relative-clause"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Mr Bala invited the artist to speak at the assembly. Her painting had won first prize.\n____________________ whose ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Mr Bala invited the artist whose painting had won first prize to speak at the assembly."},"explanation":["'whose' is the relative pronoun that shows belonging. It replaces 'Her' in the second sentence: 'Her painting' becomes 'whose painting'.","The 'whose' clause must come straight after the noun it describes, 'the artist': ... the artist whose painting had won first prize ...","Then finish the first sentence: ... to speak at the assembly. Do not put the clause at the end ('...at the assembly whose painting...'), or it will seem to describe the assembly.","Keep the tenses: 'invited' and 'had won'. Answer: Mr Bala invited the artist whose painting had won first prize to speak at the assembly."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q63","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 63","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":63,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["passive-voice"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Tommy is writing several articles for the school newsletter.\nSeveral articles ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Several articles are being written by Tommy for the school newsletter.","accept":["Several articles for the school newsletter are being written by Tommy."]},"explanation":["The sentence now starts with the object, 'Several articles', so it must change from active to passive voice.","Keep the same tense. 'is writing' is the present continuous; its passive form is am/is/are + being + past participle.","'Several articles' is plural, so use 'are': are being written. The past participle of 'write' is 'written'.","Add the doer with 'by': by Tommy. Answer: Several articles are being written by Tommy for the school newsletter."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q64","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 64","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":64,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["reported-speech"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Lisa asked Ben if he had managed to complete all his homework the previous week.\nLisa asked Ben, \"____________________?\"\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Lisa asked Ben, \"Did you manage to complete all your homework last week?\"","accept":["Did you manage to complete all your homework last week?","Did you manage to complete all your homework last week"]},"explanation":["This changes reported speech back into direct speech, so undo each change that reporting makes.","Tense: move one step forward. The past perfect 'had managed' becomes the simple past. In a question that is 'Did ... manage', and 'if' is dropped because a direct yes/no question starts with the helping verb.","Pronouns: Lisa is speaking to Ben, so 'he' becomes 'you' and 'his' becomes 'your'.","Time: 'the previous week' becomes 'last week'. End with a question mark inside the quotation marks. Answer: Lisa asked Ben, \"Did you manage to complete all your homework last week?\""]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q65","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 65","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":65,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["inversion","noun-form"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Mrs Lee had such confidence in her team that she did not supervise them at all.\nSo ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"So confident was Mrs Lee in her team that she did not supervise them at all.","accept":["So much confidence did Mrs Lee have in her team that she did not supervise them at all."]},"explanation":["'such' is followed by a noun (such confidence), but 'so' is followed by an adjective. Change the noun 'confidence' to the adjective 'confident'.","When a sentence begins with 'So + adjective', the verb comes before the subject (inversion): So confident **was Mrs Lee** ... (not 'So confident Mrs Lee was').","Because the noun has become an adjective, 'had' changes to the verb 'to be' in the same tense: was.","Keep the rest: ... in her team that she did not supervise them at all. Answer: So confident was Mrs Lee in her team that she did not supervise them at all."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q66","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 66","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":66,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["evidence-phrase","character-and-feelings"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on paragraph one, which **three-word phrase** shows how the narrator felt after the teacher's comment?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"a quiet heaviness"},"explanation":["Find the sentence in paragraph 1 that tells us how she felt afterwards: 'For the rest of the day, I carried a quiet heaviness in me'.","'heaviness' here is a feeling, not a weight: she felt sad and weighed down by the teacher's words. 'quiet' shows she kept the hurt to herself.","Count exactly three words and copy them without changing anything: a (1) quiet (2) heaviness (3). Writing more or fewer words loses the mark.","The answer is 'a quiet heaviness'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q67","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 67","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":67,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","vocabulary-in-context"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"What does the phrase \"turn themselves into the shape the school wanted\" in lines 6-7 (paragraph 2, from \"Everyone around me\" to \"school wanted\") suggest about the students? Tick the correct answer.\ncompliant / empathetic / flexible\n(Type compliant, empathetic or flexible.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"compliant"},"explanation":["Read the sentence before the phrase: pupils were 'supposed to line up neatly, socks pulled up, hair tied properly, badges pinned straight'. These are the school's rules.","'The shape the school wanted' means the way the school expected pupils to look and behave. The others knew how to fit that shape, so they followed the rules. The writer, 'unlike the others', did not.","'compliant' means willing to obey rules and do what you are told, so it matches.","'flexible' is tempting because of the word 'shape', but it means able to change easily to suit new situations, which is not the same as obeying. 'empathetic' means able to understand other people's feelings. The answer is 'compliant'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q68","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 68","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":68,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on paragraph 2, state **two** ways in which the writer's attire was different from the other students.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"First way:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Her shirt was tucked out. (Any two of these three: her shirt was tucked out; her shoes were dirty; her shoelaces were two different shades of blue.)","accept":["Her shirt was tucked out.","She had her shirt tucked out."]},"explanation":["'Attire' means what a person wears: clothes and shoes.","Find the sentence that begins 'I, unlike the others, ...'. The words 'unlike the others' tell you this is where she was different: 'had my shirt tucked out and dirty shoes. My shoelaces were two different shades of blue'.","That gives three possible answers: shirt tucked out, dirty shoes, shoelaces of two different shades of blue. Write one here and a different one in (b).","Change 'my' to 'her': Her shirt was tucked out."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Second way:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Her shoes were dirty. (Or: her shoelaces were two different shades of blue. It must be a different point from your first answer.)","accept":["Her shoes were dirty.","She had dirty shoes.","Her shoelaces were two different shades of blue."]},"explanation":["Give a second, different point from the same sentences: 'dirty shoes' or 'My shoelaces were two different shades of blue'.","Do not write about the drawing on the newsletter. That is behaviour, not attire.","Do not copy the rules ('socks pulled up, hair tied properly'); those describe the other students, not the writer.","Answer: Her shoes were dirty (or: Her shoelaces were two different shades of blue)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q69","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 69","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":69,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual","cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Why did the writer feel that things were not easy with her mother?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Her mother often scolded her (for not doing what she was told) and compared her with her cousin, who knew how to behave."},"explanation":["The question words 'not easy' match the start of paragraph 4: 'At home, things were not much easier.' The reasons follow straight after.","Point 1 (1 mark): '\"Can you do what you are told?\" Mother often scolded.' Her mother often scolded her.","Point 2 (1 mark): '\"Why can't you be more like your cousin? She knows how to behave.\"' Her mother compared her with her cousin.","The question is worth 2 marks, so give both points in your own sentence instead of copying the speech: Her mother often scolded her and compared her with her cousin."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q70","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 70","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":70,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["true-false-with-reason"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on paragraphs 2 to 7, state whether each statement in the table below is true or false, then give one reason why you think so.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"The teacher approved of the writer's drawing on the newsletter.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. The teacher questioned her sharply about the drawing (and said that it showed what her problem was)."},"explanation":["Find the drawing in paragraph 2: the writer 'had been **caught** drawing a strange shape in the margin of the school newsletter instead of reading it'.","The teacher's reaction: '\"What is that?\" my form teacher had asked **sharply**', and then 'That is your problem.' To approve of something is to think it is good; a sharp question and a criticism show the opposite.","So the statement is False. The reason must be evidence from the passage: the teacher asked about it sharply."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"The writer had planned to contribute to the script-writing from the beginning.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. The writer had never imagined herself taking over the script; the words slipped out before she could stop herself."},"explanation":["Find the script-writing in paragraph 5. 'I **suddenly** felt something stir inside me. **Before I could stop myself**, the words slipped out.' This was sudden, not planned.","The clearest evidence: 'This was something I never imagined myself doing.' You cannot plan something you never imagined doing.","So the statement is False. Reason: she had never imagined herself writing (taking over) the script."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"The audience were entertained by the performance.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. The audience laughed at the right moments."},"explanation":["Find the performance in paragraph 6: 'During the performance, the audience laughed at the right moments.'","The writer had made the dialogue 'sharper and funnier', so laughing at the right moments shows the audience enjoyed it. 'Entertained' means amused and interested.","So the statement is True. Reason: the audience laughed at the right moments."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q71","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 71","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":71,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Look at the amoeba traits in the table below. Complete the table to compare what the teacher thought of the writer at the beginning of the story with what the writer thought of herself at the end.\n[[table1]]","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"No proper shape: the writer's view of herself (at the end of the story)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Adaptable (she could adapt and change; she was still forming, not broken).","accept":["adaptable","Adaptable","able to adapt","could adapt","still forming","She was still forming.","She could adapt."]},"explanation":["The end of the story is paragraph 8, where she draws the amoeba again: 'this time, I saw a creature that **could adapt** and find its own way forward.'","'Find its own way forward' is already used in the table ('Finding her own path') for 'Float around'. So 'could adapt' is the one that matches 'No proper shape': something with no fixed shape can change to suit what is around it.","She adds: 'not fitting in did not mean I was broken; it simply meant I was still forming.' She now sees having no fixed shape as a strength.","The answer is adaptable (able to adapt)."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Float around: the teacher's view of the writer (at the start of the story)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Aimless (she had no aim, direction or purpose).","accept":["aimless","Aimless","She was aimless.","no direction","had no direction","no purpose","no aim"]},"explanation":["The teacher's view is in paragraph 2: 'A single-celled organism with no proper shape. Just floating about **aimlessly**. That is your problem.'","The teacher linked 'floating about' with 'aimlessly', which means without any aim, purpose or direction.","The table needs a describing word for the writer, so change the adverb 'aimlessly' to the adjective 'aimless'.","The answer is aimless."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g6","tables":[{"id":"table1","header":["Characteristic of amoeba","Teacher's view of the writer (at the start of the story)","Writer's view of herself (at the end of the story)"],"rows":[["No proper shape","Did not follow expectations","(a) ______"],["Float around","(b) ______","Finding her own path"]]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q72","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 72","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":72,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["character-and-feelings","factual"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on the information from lines 35 to 44 (paragraphs 6 and 7), fill in the blanks in the following table.\n[[table1]]\n[[table2]]","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"How the writer felt when the English teacher approached her and asked if she had written most of the performance:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Nervous (she was expecting to be criticised).","accept":["nervous","Nervous","anxious","worried","apprehensive","afraid","fearful","scared","uneasy"]},"explanation":["Find the event in paragraph 6: '\"You wrote most of that, didn't you?\" she asked. I nodded, **expecting criticism**.'","The table wants a feeling, not a quotation. If you expect to be criticised, you feel nervous or worried about what is coming.","Remember her past: teachers had only ever singled her out for being 'wrong', so she expected the same again.","The answer is nervous (anxious, worried and apprehensive mean the same)."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"What the loudest girl did when she realised the writer was not a joke, but an expert at directing plays:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"She asked the writer to help her with her drama club audition (the following week).","accept":["She asked the writer to help her with her drama club audition.","She asked the writer for help with her drama club audition."]},"explanation":["Find the loudest girl in paragraph 7: 'One of them, the loudest in the group, smiled awkwardly and **asked if I would help her with her drama club audition** the following week.'","The table says she felt 'impressed', and the passage says she now looked at the writer 'not as a joke, but as an expert'. Asking for help is the action that shows this.","Change 'I' to 'the writer'. 'Smiled awkwardly' alone is not enough; the important action is asking for help.","The answer is: She asked the writer to help her with her drama club audition."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g6","tables":[{"id":"table1","header":["Event","How the writer felt","What the writer did"],"rows":[["The English teacher approached the writer and asked if she had written most of the performance.","(a) ______","She nodded."]]},{"id":"table2","header":["Event","How the loudest girl felt","What the loudest girl did"],"rows":[["The loudest girl realised the writer was not a joke, but an expert at directing plays.","impressed","(b) ______"]]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q73","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 73","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":73,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["referencing"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Look at the table below. What do the word(s) in the left column refer to in the passage? Write your answers in the column on the right.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"my paper (line 10, paragraph 2)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The school newsletter (in whose margin the writer had drawn the amoeba).","accept":["the school newsletter","The school newsletter","school newsletter","the writer's school newsletter","her school newsletter","the newsletter"]},"explanation":["Read the sentence just before: 'I had been caught drawing a strange shape in the margin of **the school newsletter** instead of reading it.'","Then: '\"What is that?\" my form teacher had asked sharply, holding up **my paper**.' The teacher held up the thing the writer had been drawing on.","Test by replacing the words: 'holding up the school newsletter' makes sense.","The answer is the school newsletter."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"the task (line 33, paragraph 5)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Rewriting the script (the whole scene) for the class performance.","accept":["rewriting the script","Rewriting the script","rewriting the whole scene","Rewriting the whole scene","rewriting the whole script"]},"explanation":["Read the sentence just before: 'I began **rewriting the whole scene**. In half an hour, I completed **the task**.'","The task she completed is the thing she had just begun: rewriting the scene, that is, the script she had taken over.","'Creating the short performance' is tempting, but that was the whole class's job and it was not finished in half an hour. 'The task' is her own job of rewriting.","The answer is rewriting the script (the whole scene)."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"a gift (line 39, paragraph 6)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The writer's ability to notice things that other people miss.","accept":["the writer's ability to notice things that other people miss","the writer's ability to notice things other people miss","her ability to notice things that other people miss","her ability to notice things other people miss","noticing things other people miss"]},"explanation":["Read what the English teacher said: 'You notice things other people miss. **That** is **a gift**.'","'That' points back to the sentence before it, so the gift is noticing things other people miss. 'Gift' here means a natural talent, not a present.","Change 'You' to 'the writer' and name it as an ability.","The answer is the writer's ability to notice things that other people miss."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q74","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 74","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":74,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Explain clearly why the writer's classmates \"groaned and did the bare minimum\" (line 29, paragraph 5) for the short performance?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"They did not want to do the performance (their fun of playing board games, gossiping and laughing had been interrupted), and they only wanted to get it over with."},"explanation":["Split the phrase in two, because there are 2 marks. Why did they groan? Why did they do only the bare minimum?","'groaned' (1 mark): a groan shows you are unhappy about something. They were 'engrossed in playing board games, gossiping and laughing' when this 'was interrupted' by the teacher's instructions. They did not want to do the performance.","'did the bare minimum' (1 mark): this means doing as little as possible. The next sentence explains why: 'they argued lazily over the script, **hoping to get it over with**'. They just wanted to finish it quickly.","Answer: They did not want to do the performance and wanted to get it over with."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-b-q75","cite":"2026 Prelim Nanyang Paper 2 Booklet B Question 75","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Nanyang","section":"b","number":75,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Explain clearly what the writer hoped to achieve by writing and directing plays.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"She hoped that her plays would reach someone like her who did not fit in, and let that person know that she was not alone."},"explanation":["The answer is in the last two sentences of paragraph 8: 'I began to imagine myself writing and directing plays. Maybe one day, these would reach a fellow amoeba who needed to know that she was not alone.'","Point 1 (1 mark): to reach 'a fellow amoeba'. This is not a real amoeba. In this story 'amoeba' stands for a person who does not fit in, so 'a fellow amoeba' is someone like the writer. You must explain this instead of copying the words.","Point 2 (1 mark): to let that person know that she is not alone.","Answer: She hoped to reach someone like her who did not fit in, and let that person know that she was not alone."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":1,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Billy turned weak __________ the thought of speaking in front of his classmates for his 'Show and Tell'.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["'at the thought of' is a fixed phrase. It means 'when thinking about' something.","We use 'at' to show what causes a sudden feeling or reaction: shocked at the news, trembled at the sight of the dog, turned weak at the thought of speaking.","'with' is tempting because we say 'weak with fear' or 'weak with hunger', but there the word after 'with' is the feeling itself, not 'the thought of'.","The answer is option (1), at."]}],"options":["at","on","with","over"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":2,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["quantifiers"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"You may help yourself to all the food since there isn't __________ left on the table anyway,\" Mother told Sarah.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'food' cannot be counted, and the verb is the singular 'isn't', so 'many' (for things we can count: many apples) is out.","In negative sentences we use 'much' with uncountable nouns: 'there isn't much left' means only a small amount is left. That is why Sarah may finish all of it.","'any' is tempting, but 'there isn't any left' means there is nothing at all on the table, so there would be no food for Sarah to help herself to.","'little' is not used after 'isn't' in this way (we say 'there is little left'). The answer is option (3), much."]}],"options":["any","little","much","many"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q3","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 3","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":3,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Patrick decided to go ahead with his trip __________ the weather forecast was unfavourable.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Look at how the two halves are related. A bad weather forecast would normally make a person cancel a trip, but Patrick still went. The second half is a surprise, a contrast.","'although' joins two ideas that contrast: he went ahead although the forecast was bad.","'as' and 'because' give a reason, but a bad forecast is not a reason to go ahead. 'until' is about time.","The answer is option (4), although."]}],"options":["as","until","because","although"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q4","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 4","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":4,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["conditionals","tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"If I __________ that you were coming over, I would have rescheduled my appointment.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["The main clause has 'would have rescheduled'. 'would have + past participle' talks about a past situation that did not happen.","Rule (third conditional): If + past perfect (had + past participle), ... would have + past participle.","So the 'if' part needs 'had known'. In real life the speaker did not know, and did not reschedule.","'knew' is tempting, but 'If I knew' pairs with 'I would reschedule' (an imagined present). The answer is option (3), had known."]}],"options":["know","knew","had known","have known"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q5","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 5","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":5,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Did you lock __________ out of the house again?\" Mrs Tan questioned her sons.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["When the doer and the receiver of an action are the same, we use a reflexive pronoun (-self or -selves) that matches the subject.","The subject is 'you'. Mrs Tan is speaking to 'her sons', which is more than one person, so 'you' is plural here.","The plural reflexive pronoun for 'you' is 'yourselves'. 'yourself' is for one person only.","'themselves' is tempting because of 'sons', but she is talking to them, not about them. The answer is option (3), yourselves."]}],"options":["himself","yourself","yourselves","themselves"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q6","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 6","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":6,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["relative-pronouns"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The professor __________ the principal introduced at the conference has won an international award.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["The blank starts a clause that tells us which professor. Inside that clause, 'the principal' is the subject (the doer) and the professor is the object: the principal introduced him.","Rule: use 'whom' when the person is the object of the clause, and 'who' when the person is the subject (the professor who spoke).","Test: the principal introduced 'him' (not 'he'), so we need 'whom'.","'which' is for things and 'whose' shows belonging. The answer is option (3), whom."]}],"options":["who","which","whom","whose"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q7","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 7","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":7,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["modals"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Everyone has submitted the project except Ryan. He __________ have forgotten the deadline.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["The speaker does not know why Ryan has not submitted his project. He is making a guess about the past.","Rule: could / may / might + have + past participle shows that something possibly happened: 'He could have forgotten' means 'maybe he forgot'.","'should have forgotten' is tempting, but 'should have' means it was the right thing to do, and forgetting a deadline is not something a person ought to do.","'can have' and 'shall have' are not used to guess about the past. The answer is option (3), could."]}],"options":["can","shall","could","should"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q8","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 8","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":8,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["question-tags"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"They've left for Norway this morning, __________ they?\" Mariam asked.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Rule: a positive statement takes a negative question tag, and the tag repeats the helping verb of the statement.","\"They've left\" is short for 'They have left' (present perfect). The helping verb is 'have'.","So the tag is 'haven't they?'.","'hadn't' is tempting, but that would match \"They'd left\" (they had left). The answer is option (4), haven't."]}],"options":["aren't","hadn't","weren't","haven't"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q9","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 9","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":9,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["infinitives-and-gerunds"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Dylan saw the Head Prefect __________ the latecomer's name into her notebook.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Rule: after verbs of the senses (see, hear, watch, feel, notice) + a person, use the base form of the verb (or the -ing form): 'I saw him run', 'She heard the baby cry'.","The first verb 'saw' already shows the past tense, so the second verb does not change.","'wrote' is tempting because the sentence is in the past, but only the first verb carries the tense.","'Dylan saw the Head Prefect write the latecomer's name.' The answer is option (1), write."]}],"options":["write","wrote","writes","written"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q10","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 10","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":10,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["subject-verb-agreement","tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The group of researchers, together with their assistants, __________ several experiments since the beginning of the year.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Tense: 'since the beginning of the year' tells us the action started in the past and continues up to now. That needs the present perfect (has/have + past participle), so 'conduct' and 'conducts' are out.","Agreement: find the real subject. It is 'The group', which is one group, so it is singular. 'of researchers' only describes the group.","The phrase between the commas, 'together with their assistants', is extra information and does not change the subject.","A singular subject takes 'has'. 'have conducted' is tempting because of the plural words 'researchers' and 'assistants'. The answer is option (3), has conducted."]}],"options":["conduct","conducts","has conducted","have conducted"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q11","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 11","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":11,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning","collocation"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Mei Ling's interest in ballet began to __________ ever since her cousin introduced her to inline skating.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Clue: 'began to' tells us the change was slow and gradual. She found a new hobby, so her interest in ballet slowly became weaker.","'wane' means to become gradually weaker or smaller. It is the usual word for interest, enthusiasm or popularity: 'her interest began to wane'.","'plunge' and 'plummet' mean to fall suddenly and steeply, and are used for prices, numbers or temperatures. They do not fit with a slow change.","'drop' is tempting, but it is used for amounts and levels rather than for a person's interest fading. The answer is option (2), wane."]}],"options":["drop","wane","plunge","plummet"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q12","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 12","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":12,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The __________ determination of the athletes had earned them great respect from the spectators.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clue: the determination 'earned them great respect', so we need a word that praises how strong it was.","'unwavering' means steady and never weakening. To waver is to become unsure, so 'unwavering determination' never gives way.","'unassuming' is tempting because it is also praise, but it means modest and not showing off, and describes a person, not determination.","'unthinking' means careless, and 'underlying' means hidden beneath the surface. The answer is option (3), unwavering."]}],"options":["unthinking","underlying","unwavering","unassuming"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q13","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 13","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":13,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The progress of the rescue mission at the site of the earthquake was __________ by the heavy downpour.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Clue: heavy rain makes rescue work harder, so the progress was slowed down or blocked.","'impede' means to slow down or get in the way of movement or progress. 'Progress was impeded' is a common pairing.","'impaired' is tempting, but it means weakened or damaged, and is used for abilities such as eyesight, hearing or judgement, not for progress.","'inflicted' is used for causing pain or damage to someone (inflict harm on), and 'influenced' is too weak. The answer is option (2), impeded."]}],"options":["inflicted","impeded","impaired","influenced"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q14","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 14","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":14,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Chantal had the __________ to meet the members of the boy band as she had won the backstage pass in a lucky draw.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Clue: she got to meet the band only because she was lucky enough to win a backstage pass. Most fans do not get this chance.","A 'privilege' is a special chance or benefit that only a few people get.","'right' is tempting, but a right is something everyone is allowed to have, such as the right to go to school. 'entitlement' is also something a person is owed.","An 'advantage' is something that helps you do better than others, as in a race. The answer is option (2), privilege."]}],"options":["right","privilege","advantage","entitlement"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q15","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 15","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":15,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["idioms"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"I suggest you bury the __________ and be friends again with Rani since you value her friendship.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'bury the hatchet' is an idiom. It means to end a quarrel and make peace.","Clue: 'be friends again with Rani' shows the two had quarrelled and should now make up.","A hatchet is a small axe. Long ago, some Native American tribes buried their weapons in the ground to show that the fighting was over.","The other tools are not part of any such idiom. The answer is option (3), hatchet."]}],"options":["mallet","dagger","hatchet","hammer"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q16","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 16","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":16,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++quandary++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["A 'quandary' is a difficult situation in which you do not know what to do.","Clues in the passage: 'no sight of land' and 'food supplies running low'. The captain had a hard problem with no easy choice.","'dilemma' means a situation where a difficult choice has to be made, which matches.","'confusion' is tempting, but it is about not understanding something. The captain understood the problem well; what he lacked was a good way out. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g1","options":["state","mess","dilemma","confusion"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q17","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 17","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":17,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++floundered++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["To 'flounder' in water is to move about helplessly, struggling to stay afloat.","Clues: the man 'fell overboard' and 'Thankfully, he was rescued', so he was in danger and could not save himself.","'struggled' means tried very hard with great difficulty, which matches.","'splashed' is tempting because a struggling person splashes, but splashing can also be playful and does not show difficulty. 'waded' means walked through shallow water. The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g1","options":["stirred","waded","splashed","struggled"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q18","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 18","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":18,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++murky++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'murky' water is dark and dirty, so that it is hard to see through.","Clue: 'with poor visibility in the night'. They could not see clearly.","'cloudy' can describe a liquid that is not clear, so it is the closest.","'mouldy' is tempting because it also sounds dirty, but it describes old food or damp things with mould growing on them. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g1","options":["sandy","cloudy","mouldy","shabby"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q19","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 19","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":19,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++alarming++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'alarming' means causing sudden worry or fear.","The word describes how the effect made Colt feel when he saw his crew fall ill with high fevers. It was frightening enough for him to decide to leave the island.","'shocking' means causing a strong feeling of surprise and upset, which is the closest.","'damaging' is tempting because the disease did harm the crew, but 'alarming' is about the worry it causes, not the harm itself. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g1","options":["hurting","shocking","wounding","damaging"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q20","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 20","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":20,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++revived++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["To 'revive' something is to bring it back to life or make it strong again after it had faded.","The sick crew had been losing hope. Colt's decision to leave the island gave them hope again.","'renewed' means made fresh and strong again, which matches.","'reinforced' is tempting, but it means made something that is already there even stronger. Their hopes had been fading, so they needed to be brought back, not just strengthened. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g1","options":["replaced","renewed","reinstated","reinforced"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q21","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 21","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":21,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is true about the launch?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Check each statement against the poster (Text 1).","The poster says 'Only 100 pairs!' A fixed, small number means the supply is limited, so statement (2) is true.","(1) is false: 'No reservations for customers or online purchases.' (3) is false: the launch is 'Exclusively for Sports House members'. (4) is false: it is only 'at the Vista City Outlet'.","The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g2","options":["Reservations for the shoes can be made online.","Only a limited number of shoes will be available.","Anyone can purchase the shoes on 12 December.","Falcon shoes will be sold at all Sports House outlets."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q22","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 22","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":22,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference","purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Why is the shoe shown in this manner in the poster?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Look at the picture: the shoe is tilted as if lifting off the ground, with feather-like wing lines rising from the heel.","Link it to the words around it: the shoe is called the FALCON (a fast bird) and the slogan is 'DON'T RUN, FLY!'","Something that can fly must be very light. The picture matches feature A, Lightweight: 'Makes running easier and faster'.","Nothing in the picture is about the sole lasting long, and the poster does not say the design or shape is new. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g2","options":["to highlight a new design","to emphasise how light it is","to draw attention to its shape","to show how durable the sole is"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q23","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 23","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":23,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Linda often experiences pain in her ankle and prefers to run in the evenings. Based on the information in Text 1, which features of the shoes would be most important for her?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Pick out Linda's two needs: she has ankle pain, and she runs in the evenings.","Running in the evenings means low light. Feature D, Visibility: 'Reflective materials keep you visible in low light'.","Ankle pain means she must avoid hurting herself further. Feature F, Support: 'Provides stability for injury-free running'.","B (Cushioned Comfort) is tempting, but the poster says it keeps the feet free from blisters, not that it protects the ankle. The answer is option (3), D and F."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g2","options":["A and B","A and E","D and F","E and F"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q24","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 24","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":24,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"What kind of poster is shown in Text 1?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Ask what the poster wants the reader to do. It invites people to 'Join us for the sales launch' and gives a price, a date and a place to buy the shoes.","A poster that tries to get people to buy a product or attend a sale is promotional.","'informational' is tempting because the poster does give information, but the information is there to sell the shoes, with a catchy slogan and 'Only 100 pairs!' to create excitement.","It does not teach a topic (educational) or encourage people to keep going (motivational). The answer is option (2), promotional."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g2","options":["educational","promotional","motivational","informational"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-a-q25","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet A Question 25","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"a","number":25,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience","inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Both the descriptions for the shoe in Text 1 and the content of Text 2 __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["The answer must be true of both texts, so test each option against Text 1 and Text 2.","Text 1's feature boxes say what a good shoe does for the runner: makes running easier, protects the feet, gives 'stability for injury-free running'. Text 2 says good shoes 'play a key role in helping runners perform well and in preventing injuries'. Both show why good shoes matter.","(1) fits only Text 1; Text 2 is an article and does not advertise any shoe. (2) is tempting, but neither text tells the reader how to choose a pair. (3) is wrong because the features are listed, not compared.","The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g2","options":["advertise the latest running shoes","offer tips on how to select running shoes","compare different features of running shoes","highlight the importance of quality running shoes"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q26","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 26","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":26,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns","articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (26). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"M","accept":["m","this"]},"explanation":["The blank is the subject of 'is true'. It must point back to the idea in the question before it: a home feeling warmer than the weather outside.","'this' is used to point back to a whole idea that has just been mentioned.","'it' is tempting, but 'it' is needed for blank (29), and each word can be used only once. 'this' is also the better word for referring to a whole situation.","The answer is (M) this: 'A recent study has found that this is true for many HDB flats'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q27","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 27","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":27,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (27). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"K","accept":["k","than"]},"explanation":["'hotter' is a comparative adjective (hot, hotter).","Rule: a comparative is followed by 'than' when two things are compared: hotter than, taller than.","Here the homes are compared with the outdoor environment. The first sentence has the same pattern: 'warmer than the weather outside'.","The answer is (K) than."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q28","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 28","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":28,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (28). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"H","accept":["h","of"]},"explanation":["'pieces ___ furniture': 'furniture' cannot be counted, so we count it in pieces.","Rule: a piece of + uncountable noun: a piece of furniture, a piece of advice, large pieces of furniture.","The answer is (H) of."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q29","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 29","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":29,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (29). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"G","accept":["g","it"]},"explanation":["'absorb heat during the day and slowly release ___ at night': the blank is the object of 'release'. What is released? The heat.","'heat' is uncountable and singular, so the pronoun is 'it'.","'them' is tempting because 'walls and floors' are plural, but the walls and floors are doing the releasing; they are not the thing released.","The answer is (G) it."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q30","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 30","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":30,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (30). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"D","accept":["d","after"]},"explanation":["'making homes feel warm even ___ the sun has set': we need a time word that joins the two clauses.","The walls release heat at night, which is later than sunset. The homes stay warm 'even after' the sun has gone down.","'before' would not make sense: it is no surprise that a home is warm before the sun sets, so 'even' would have no meaning.","The answer is (D) after."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q31","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 31","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":31,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (31). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"B","accept":["b","an"]},"explanation":["'can be ___ uncomfortable experience': 'experience' is a singular countable noun, so it needs an article.","Rule: use 'an' before a vowel sound and 'a' before a consonant sound. The next word, 'uncomfortable', begins with the vowel sound 'uh'.","So we need 'an', not 'a'.","The answer is (B) an."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q32","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 32","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":32,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (32). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"P","accept":["p","who"]},"explanation":["'The elderly and families ___ do not use air-conditioning often find it challenging': the blank starts a clause that tells us which people.","Rule: use the relative pronoun 'who' for people when they are the subject of the clause (they do not use air-conditioning).","'which' is for things and animals, so it does not fit 'the elderly and families'.","The answer is (P) who."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q33","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 33","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":33,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (33). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"F","accept":["f","however"]},"explanation":["The blank starts a sentence and is followed by a comma, so it is a linking word.","The sentence before is about a problem (it is hard to cope with the heat). This sentence gives good news (there are several ways to cool homes). The ideas contrast.","'However,' shows a contrast with the sentence before.","The answer is (F) however."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q34","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 34","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":34,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (34). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"C","accept":["c","also"]},"explanation":["'Using fans wisely can ___ help circulate cooler air': the sentence is complete without the blank, so we need an adverb that sits between 'can' and 'help'.","The sentence before gives one way to cool the home (open windows and remove clutter). This sentence adds another way.","'also' adds a point and goes after the helping verb: can also help.","The answer is (C) also."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q35","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 35","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":35,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (35). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Q","accept":["q","will"]},"explanation":["'keeping homes cool ___ become increasingly important': the verb after the blank is the base form 'become', not 'becomes'.","A base form follows a helping verb such as will, can or must.","'As Singapore's weather becomes warmer' looks ahead to the future, so we use 'will': keeping homes cool will become increasingly important.","The answer is (Q) will."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q36","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 36","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":36,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (36) ++receeved++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"received"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means 'got' or 'was given'.","Rule: 'i before e, except after c'. After the letter c, the long 'ee' sound is spelt 'ei': receive, ceiling, deceive.","re-cei-ved: 'cei', not 'cee'.","The correct word is 'received'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q37","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 37","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":37,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (37) ++on++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"in"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong preposition.","Rule: use 'in' for countries, cities and other large places (in Poland, in Singapore). Use 'on' for days and dates (on November 7).","The sentence has both: 'born in Poland on November 7, 1867'.","The correct word is 'in'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q38","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 38","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":38,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (38) ++aloudd++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"allowed"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The sentence means girls were not permitted to study at the university.","The word is 'allowed' (allow + ed), with double 'l' and 'ow'.","Do not mix it up with 'aloud', which sounds the same but means 'in a voice that can be heard' (read aloud).","The correct word is 'allowed'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q39","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 39","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":39,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (39) ++other++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"another"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. 'country' is a singular countable noun, so it needs a determiner such as 'a' or 'another' in front of it.","Rule: another + singular noun (another country); other + plural noun (other countries).","'another' means one more, or a different one.","The correct word is 'another'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q40","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 40","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":40,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (40) ++persoo++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"pursue"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means to work hard to achieve something: to pursue a dream.","It is spelt p-u-r-s-u-e: 'pur', not 'per', and it ends in 'sue'.","It comes after 'to continue studying and ...', so it stays in the base form, like 'continue'.","The correct word is 'pursue'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q41","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 41","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":41,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (41) ++deturminasion++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"determination"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means the quality of not giving up.","Start from the verb 'determine' and add '-ation': de-ter-mi-na-tion.","It has 'ter', not 'tur', and the 'shun' sound at the end is spelt '-tion'.","The correct word is 'determination'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q42","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 42","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":42,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (42) ++accurately++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"accurate"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong word form.","The word describes 'pictures', which is a noun. A word that describes a noun must be an adjective.","'accurately' is an adverb (it describes a verb: measure accurately). The adjective is 'accurate'.","'more accurate pictures'. The correct word is 'accurate'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q43","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 43","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":43,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (43) ++In++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"To"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong preposition in a fixed phrase.","'To this day' means 'even now, after all this time'.","'In this day and age' is a different phrase that means 'in modern times', and it does not fit here.","The correct word is 'To'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q44","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 44","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":44,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (44) ++xtraordinery++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"extraordinary"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means very unusual and impressive.","It is made of two words joined together: extra + ordinary (beyond the ordinary).","So it starts with 'e' and ends in '-ary', like 'ordinary'.","The correct word is 'extraordinary'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q45","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 45","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":45,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (45) ++successes++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"success"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. Look at the verb that follows: 'does not come'.","'does' is a singular verb, so the subject must be singular.","When we speak about success in general, it is an uncountable noun and has no plural ending.","'success does not come easily'. The correct word is 'success'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q46","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 46","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":46,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (46) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"gather","accept":["sit","crowd"]},"explanation":["The blank is the verb for 'children and adults'. They do something 'around chessboards' in order to play each other.","'gather around' means to come together around something. 'sit around' and 'crowd around' also fit.","The subject is plural and the paragraph is in the present tense ('Some play for fun'), so use the base form: gather, not gathers or gathered.","The answer is 'gather'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q47","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 47","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":47,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (47) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"while","accept":["whereas"]},"explanation":["Two groups of players are compared in one sentence: 'Some play for fun' and 'others train hard for competitions'.","We need a connector that joins two clauses and shows a contrast between them.","'while' does this: Some play for fun, while others train hard.","The answer is 'while'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q48","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 48","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":48,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (48) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"is","accept":["seems","feels"]},"explanation":["'every game ___ different': the clause has no verb, so the blank is a linking verb.","'every game' is singular, and the sentence is in the present tense ('the rules ... are simple').","So we need 'is'. 'seems' and 'feels' are also accepted.","The answer is 'is'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q49","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 49","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":49,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (49) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"games"},"explanation":["'one of the world's oldest ___': after 'one of the' we always use a plural noun (one of the tallest buildings).","What is chess? The passage calls it a 'game' several times.","So chess is one of the world's oldest games.","The answer is 'games' (plural)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q50","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 50","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":50,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (50) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"where","accept":["how"]},"explanation":["'no one knows exactly when or ___ it began': the blank is a question word paired with 'when'.","The next sentence answers both: 'chess started in northwestern India (where) around 600 A.D. (when)'.","So the pair is 'when or where'.","The answer is 'where'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q51","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 51","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":51,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (51) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"word","accept":["term"]},"explanation":["'chaturanga', a Sanskrit ___ that refers to the four parts of the ancient Indian army': the blank says what 'chaturanga' is.","Sanskrit is an ancient Indian language, and 'chaturanga' is in quotation marks because it is a word from that language.","A word 'refers to' something, so 'a Sanskrit word' fits. 'term' means the same.","The answer is 'word'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q52","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 52","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":52,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (52) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"These","accept":["they"]},"explanation":["The blank is the subject of 'later inspired the pieces used in modern chess'.","What inspired the chess pieces? The four parts of the army just listed: soldiers, chariots, horsemen and elephants.","We need a plural word that points back to them: 'These' or 'They'.","The answer is 'These'. Begin with a capital letter because it starts the sentence."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q53","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 53","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":53,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (53) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"spread","accept":["travelled","traveled","moved"]},"explanation":["'As the game ___ to different parts of the world': the game started in India and reached other countries.","'spread to' means to reach more and more places.","The rest of the sentence is in the past tense ('its rules gradually changed'). The past tense of 'spread' is also 'spread'. 'travelled' is accepted too.","The answer is 'spread'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q54","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 54","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":54,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (54) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"into"},"explanation":["'its rules gradually changed and developed ___ the modern chess we know today'.","'develop into' means to slowly change and become something else: A caterpillar develops into a butterfly.","The old game became modern chess.","The answer is 'into'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q55","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 55","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":55,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (55) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"meticulous","accept":["careful","thorough"]},"explanation":["'Every move requires ___ planning because one wrong decision can change the outcome.'","If one mistake can change the result, the planning must be done with great care. We need an adjective that describes such planning.","'meticulous' means very careful and paying attention to every detail. 'careful' has the same meaning and also fits.","The answer is 'meticulous'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q56","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 56","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":56,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (56) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"remain","accept":["stay","keep"]},"explanation":["'it teaches them to solve problems and ___ calm under pressure': the blank is a verb joined by 'and' to 'solve', so it is in the base form too.","The verb is followed by the adjective 'calm'. 'remain calm' and 'stay calm' mean to continue to be calm.","Do not write 'be' if you can think of a more exact word; 'remain' shows that the player keeps calm even when things get tense.","The answer is 'remain'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q57","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 57","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":57,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (57) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"children","accept":["youths","kids"]},"explanation":["Look at the next sentence: 'For adults, it keeps the mind active'. The two sentences are a pair: 'For ___, chess helps ...' and 'For adults, ...'.","The first paragraph names the two groups of players: 'children and adults'.","So the blank is the other group: children.","The answer is 'children'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q58","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 58","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":58,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (58) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"only","accept":["just"]},"explanation":["Look for the partner words later in the sentence: 'not ___ on the chessboard, but also in everyday life'.","'not only ... but also ...' is a fixed pair used to add a second point.","'not just ... but also' is accepted too.","The answer is 'only'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q59","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 59","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":59,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (59) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"together","accept":["closer"]},"explanation":["'chess has a unique way of bringing people ___.'","The rest of the paragraph explains: children play with parents and grandparents, and 'families can bond'.","'bring people together' is a set phrase that means to help people become close.","The answer is 'together'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q60","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 60","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":60,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (60) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"their"},"explanation":["'Children can enjoy a game of chess with both ___ parents and grandparents.'","The blank comes before the nouns 'parents and grandparents' and shows whose they are. They belong to the children.","'Children' is plural, so the possessive word is 'their'.","The answer is 'their'. Spell it t-h-e-i-r (not 'there')."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q61","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 61","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":61,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["passive-voice"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The house had been cleaned by Tom before the guests arrived.\nTom ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Tom had cleaned the house before the guests arrived."},"explanation":["The given sentence is in the passive voice: the house (receiver) + had been cleaned + by Tom (doer). Starting with 'Tom' changes it to the active voice.","Active pattern: doer + verb + receiver. Tom + had cleaned + the house.","Keep the same tense. 'had been cleaned' is past perfect passive, so the active verb is 'had cleaned' (not 'cleaned' or 'has cleaned').","Keep the rest unchanged. Answer: Tom had cleaned the house before the guests arrived."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q62","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 62","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":62,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["reported-speech"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"\"What time did you take the bus yesterday?\" Mother asked me.\nMother asked me ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Mother asked me what time I had taken the bus the previous day.","accept":["Mother asked me what time I had taken the bus the day before."]},"explanation":["This changes a direct question into reported speech. Four things change.","Word order: a reported question is no longer a question. Drop 'did' and use statement order (subject, then verb): 'what time I ...', with a full stop and no question mark.","Pronoun and tense: 'you' becomes 'I' because Mother was speaking to me. The past simple 'did ... take' moves one step back to the past perfect 'had taken'.","Time word: 'yesterday' becomes 'the previous day' or 'the day before'.","Answer: Mother asked me what time I had taken the bus the previous day."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q63","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 63","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":63,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["noun-form"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Upon seeing the cat chase the toy mouse, the child laughed.\nThe child laughed at ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The child laughed at the sight of the cat chasing the toy mouse.","accept":["The child laughed at the sight of the toy mouse being chased by the cat."]},"explanation":["'at' is a preposition, so it must be followed by a noun. Change the verb 'seeing' into its noun: see → sight.","'Upon seeing' becomes 'at the sight of'.","'of' is also a preposition, so the verb after the cat takes the -ing form: 'the cat chasing the toy mouse' (not 'chase').","Answer: The child laughed at the sight of the cat chasing the toy mouse."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q64","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 64","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":64,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["noun-form"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"He was curious about planets and galaxies. He read up on them.\n____________________ due to his ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"He read up on planets and galaxies due to his curiosity.","accept":["He read up on planets and galaxies due to his curiosity about them."]},"explanation":["'due to' means 'because of'. It is followed by a noun, so the result goes first and the cause goes after 'due to his'.","Result: He read up on them. Cause: He was curious.","Change the adjective 'curious' into its noun: curiosity. Watch the spelling: the 'u' is dropped (curious → curiosity, not 'curiousity').","Replace 'them' with 'planets and galaxies' so the reader knows what he read up on.","Answer: He read up on planets and galaxies due to his curiosity."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q65","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 65","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":65,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["noun-form"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"She could not believe that she won the art competition.\nMuch to ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Much to her disbelief, she won the art competition.","accept":["Much to her disbelief, she had won the art competition."]},"explanation":["'Much to her ...' is followed by a noun that names a feeling: much to her surprise, much to her delight.","Change 'could not believe' into a noun: believe → belief, and 'not' is carried by the prefix 'dis-': disbelief.","Use 'her' because the sentence is about 'she'. Put a comma after the opening phrase, then say what happened: she won the art competition.","Answer: Much to her disbelief, she won the art competition."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q66","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 66","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":66,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["evidence-phrase"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Which 4-word phrase from the fourth paragraph indicates that the wound was not serious?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"no stitches were required"},"explanation":["Paragraph 4 begins 'The cut was deep and bled profusely', which sounds serious, so read on for the part that shows it was not.","'After stopping the bleeding, we decided no stitches were required.' A serious cut has to be stitched up by a doctor. This one did not need that.","Count exactly four words and copy them without changing anything: no (1) stitches (2) were (3) required (4).","The answer is 'no stitches were required'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q67","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 67","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":67,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","referencing"],"format":"structured","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"\"I could not be certain of **whose guilt** I was reading.\" (line 12, paragraph 5)\nWho are the two individuals that the writer is referring to?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Gato and the writer.","accept":["Gato and the writer","The writer and Gato","the writer and Gato","Gato and herself","The writer herself and Gato"]},"explanation":["'whose guilt' means the writer could sense guilt in the house but was not sure who was feeling it. Ask who had done something to feel guilty about.","Gato had ripped the flesh on the writer's arm with his claws. The writer had 'shoved Gato with all my strength' so that he 'landed with a thud'. Each had hurt the other.","Jane only turned on the hairdryer and then helped to dress the wound, so she is not one of the two.","The marker looks for both names: Gato and the writer. Write 'the writer', not 'I'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q68","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 68","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":68,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["vocabulary-in-context","character-and-feelings"],"format":"short","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Pick out **two** words from lines 14-16 (paragraph 6) which show how Gato's mood had changed after the writer found him. Each word is found in a separate sentence.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Before the writer found Gato","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"text","value":"racing"},"explanation":["Look in the first sentence of paragraph 6: 'As I held Gato close, I felt his heart racing.'","A racing heart beats very fast. It shows Gato was still frightened and tense from what had happened before the writer found him.","Give one word only, copied exactly: 'racing'."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"After the writer found Gato","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"text","value":"relaxed"},"explanation":["Look in the last sentence of paragraph 6: 'I sat on the stairs holding and stroking him in my lap until he relaxed.'","'relaxed' shows that he had calmed down after the writer comforted him.","The two words come from separate sentences, as the question says. The answer is 'relaxed'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q69","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 69","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":69,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["referencing"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Look at the table below. What do the word(s) in the left column refer to in the passage? Write your answers in the column on the right.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"the mark (line 26, paragraph 11)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The prominent white curved scar (on the writer's arm).","accept":["the scar","The scar","the prominent white curved scar","the scar on the writer's arm","the prominent white curved scar on the writer's arm","the writer's scar"]},"explanation":["A phrase like 'the mark' points back to something already mentioned. Read the sentences just before it.","'in its place was a prominent white curved scar — almost three inches in length. While many would wish for it to fade, I liked **the mark**.'","Test by replacing the words: 'I liked the scar.' It makes sense.","The answer is the prominent white curved scar. Do not write 'the wound': the wound had healed, and the mark is what was left behind."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"it (line 41, paragraph 16)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The true reason behind Jane's outburst (what was troubling or frustrating Jane)."},"explanation":["'\"It's alright,\" I said softly. \"Do you want to talk about **it**?\"' Here 'it' is not a noun in the sentence before; it is something the writer has just noticed.","She 'saw not anger but exhaustion behind her eyes'. She understood that something else was making Jane lash out.","So 'it' is whatever was really troubling Jane and had made her snap. We find out later: the piano lesson had gone wrong and she was stressed about the competition.","The marker looks for: the real reason for Jane's outburst, or what was troubling her. 'The book' or 'the folded pages' is not accepted."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q70","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 70","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":70,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["sequencing"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write 1, 2 and 3 in the blanks below to indicate the order in which the events occurred in the story.\n___ Jane snapped at the writer.\n___ The writer's wound healed.\n___ The writer invited Jane to share her feelings.\n(Type the three numbers in the order of the statements above, for example 3, 2, 1.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"2, 1, 3","accept":["2,1,3","2 1 3","213"]},"explanation":["Find each event in the passage and note where it happens.","'Several weeks later, the wound was completely healed' (paragraph 11) comes first, so 'The writer's wound healed' is 1.","Then Jane came home and scolded the writer about the folded pages (paragraphs 12 and 13), so 'Jane snapped at the writer' is 2.","Last, the writer asked, 'Do you want to talk about it?' (paragraph 16), so 'The writer invited Jane to share her feelings' is 3.","Written in the order of the statements, the answer is 2, 1, 3."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q71","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 71","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":71,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["true-false-with-reason"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on the passage, state whether each statement in the table below is true or false. Then give one reason to support your answer.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"The writer agreed with the neighbour's response.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. After the writer heard the neighbour's response about a dead cat, she quickly left the store with a sour taste in her mouth."},"explanation":["The neighbour said, 'If that had happened to me, it would be one dead cat! Accident or not!' He would have punished the cat.","The writer's reaction: 'I quickly left the store after paying, a sour taste in my mouth.' A sour taste in the mouth means an unpleasant feeling. She disliked what he said.","She had also already forgiven Gato: 'It was just an accident. I know you didn't mean to hurt me.'","So the statement is False. The reason must be evidence from the passage: she left with a sour taste in her mouth."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Jane was upset by her piano lesson.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. Jane said that everything had gone wrong during her piano lesson (and she had been feeling very stressed about the upcoming competition)."},"explanation":["Paragraph 17: 'Everything had gone wrong during the piano lesson today and I've been feeling very stressed about the upcoming competition.'","There is an earlier hint too: she came home 'throwing her bag down without a word'.","So the statement is True, and the reason is Jane's own words about the piano lesson going wrong."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"Jane was not remorseful after lashing out at the writer.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"False. Jane said that she did not mean what she had said (and her eyes watered)."},"explanation":["'remorseful' means feeling sorry for something you have done. Watch the 'not' in the statement.","Paragraph 17: '\"I didn't mean what I said before.\"' and 'Her eyes watered.' Then she took both of the writer's hands in hers. These show she was sorry.","So Jane was remorseful, and the statement is False.","Reason: she said she did not mean what she had said."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q72","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 72","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":72,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["cause-and-effect","inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"What were the two reasons why the writer says that Gato lashing out at her was \"a reaction and not a malicious intent\" in line 37 (paragraph 15)?\n**Gato lashed out at the writer because…**","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"First reason:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"He was frightened of the hairdryer's noise."},"explanation":["'a reaction' means Gato was responding to something. Go back to paragraph 3 to see what set him off.","'When Jane turned on the hairdryer, Gato bolted in panic.' The sudden noise scared him.","So the first reason is that he was frightened (in a panic) because of the hairdryer. He scratched out of fear, not out of anger at the writer."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Second reason:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The writer was the nearest thing at hand."},"explanation":["'not a malicious intent' means he did not mean to harm her. The same sentence goes on: 'there is a difference between being the target and being the nearest thing at hand.'","The writer was not the target. Gato was in her arms, so she was simply the nearest thing when he rushed to escape: 'In his hurry to escape, Gato's claws ripped the soft flesh'.","So the second reason is that the writer happened to be the nearest thing at hand (she was holding him)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q73","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 73","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":73,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":4,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on information from lines 33 to 48 (paragraphs 14 to 18), complete the following table.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"The writer: What was she trying to tell herself when she \"traced the raised skin of the scar\" (line 35, paragraph 14)?","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"She was trying to tell herself that Jane lashing out was a reaction and not a malicious intent (just like Gato's scratch)."},"explanation":["Just before this, the writer 'could feel the cutting words rising in my throat'. She was about to snap back at Jane. Then Gato appeared 'as if reminding me to remember', and she touched the scar.","The next paragraph says what the scar stood for: 'His lashing out was a reaction and not a malicious intent' and 'People are not so different from animals in this way.'","So she was reminding herself that Jane, like Gato, was only reacting to something and did not mean to hurt her.","The marker looks for: Jane's lashing out was a reaction, not meant to hurt (not a malicious intent)."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"The writer: When she traced her scar, what does her action tell us about how she felt?","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"She felt reflective (thoughtful / pensive / contemplative).","accept":["reflective","thoughtful","pensive","contemplative","She felt reflective","She felt thoughtful","She was reflective","She was thoughtful"]},"explanation":["Tracing a scar slowly with a finger is what a person does when thinking back on what happened.","She stopped herself from answering at once and thought about the lesson Gato had taught her.","The feeling word must fit someone who is thinking deeply: reflective, thoughtful, pensive or contemplative. 'Angry' or 'hurt' does not fit, because she chose not to let the cutting words out."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"Jane: What was she trying to tell the writer when she held the writer's hands and said nothing in lines 46 to 47 (paragraph 18)?","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"She was trying to apologise to the writer (to say that she was sorry)."},"explanation":["Look at what happened just before: Jane said, 'I didn't mean what I said before', and 'Her eyes watered'.","Then she 'took both my hands in hers' and 'simply held them and said nothing'. She could not find the words, so she used the action instead.","Holding someone's hands after hurting them is a way of saying sorry.","The marker looks for: she was apologising for lashing out."]},{"label":"d","prompt":"Jane: What does her action tell us about how she felt?","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"She felt guilty (remorseful / sorry).","accept":["guilty","remorseful","sorry","She felt guilty","She felt remorseful","She felt sorry","She was guilty","She was remorseful","apologetic","She felt apologetic"]},"explanation":["Jane had said hurtful things ('So much for being my sister') that she did not mean.","Her watering eyes and the long, silent holding of hands show she felt bad about what she had done.","The feeling word is guilty, remorseful or sorry."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q74","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 74","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":74,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Why did the writer \"really look\" at her sister in line 40 (paragraph 16)?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The writer knew that Jane's outburst was not intentional, and she wanted to find out the real reason for her lashing out."},"explanation":["A 2-mark 'why' question needs two ideas.","Idea 1 (what she knew): the incident with Gato had taught her that 'wounds seldom tell the whole story' and that lashing out can be 'a reaction and not a malicious intent'. So she believed Jane did not really mean to hurt her.","Idea 2 (what she wanted): to 'really look' is to look carefully, beyond the angry words, to find the true cause. It worked: she 'saw not anger but exhaustion behind her eyes'.","The marker looks for: Jane's outburst was not intentional, and the writer wanted to find the real reason behind it."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-b-q75","cite":"2026 Prelim PLMGS Paper 2 Booklet B Question 75","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"PLMGS","section":"b","number":75,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["character-and-feelings","inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"What is a character trait that best describes the writer? Support your answer with evidence from lines 36 to 48 (paragraphs 15 to 18).","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The writer is understanding. Even after Jane lashed out at her, she did not get angry. Instead, she told Jane softly that it was alright and invited her to share her feelings. This shows that she understood that Jane was feeling stressed."},"explanation":["This needs a trait (1 mark) and evidence from the lines given (1 mark).","Trait: a describing word for the kind of person she is, such as understanding, empathetic or forgiving. Do not give a passing feeling such as 'sad'.","Evidence: instead of snapping back, she took a slow breath, said softly, 'It's alright. Do you want to talk about it?', and at the end 'knew I would never hold this incident against her'.","Link the two: say what she did and what it shows about her. Evidence from outside lines 36 to 48 (such as forgiving Gato) does not count."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-p1-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 1 Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"p1","number":2,"topic":"writing","subtypes":["continuous-writing"],"format":"structured","marks":36,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Part 2: Continuous Writing (36 marks)\nWrite a composition of ++at least 150 words++ about **making a difference**.\nThe pictures are provided to help you think about the topic.\nYour composition should be based on one or more of these pictures.\n[[img1]]\nConsider the following points when you plan your composition:\n• Why was the act needed?\n• What changed after the act?\nYou may use the points in any order and include other relevant points as well.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There is no single model answer. Check your writing against the points below."},"explanation":["Understand the topic: 'making a difference' means doing something that changes a person's (or an animal's) situation for the better. The act can be small, but the story must show clearly what was different afterwards.","Picture 1 (helping an elderly person): you or a character could help an elderly neighbour who struggles to walk, shop or cross the road, and that help changes how lonely or unsafe the person feels.","Picture 2 (an empty box): the box could be filled, for example a donation drive of food, books or toys for a family or a home in need; or it could be a box in which something was found or delivered.","Picture 3 (a cat with a bandaged leg): a character could rescue an injured stray, take it to a vet and nurse it back to health.","Use at least one picture and make it important to the story, not just mentioned in passing.","Answer both guiding points. Why was the act needed? Show the problem first (what the person or animal was going through). What changed after the act? Show the result for the one helped and also for the one who helped.","Story arc: opening that sets the scene, the problem noticed, a decision to act (include a moment of hesitation or difficulty), the act itself in detail as the climax, then the outcome.","Ending: return to the topic. Reflect on how one act made a difference and what the main character learnt, so that the last paragraph links back to 'making a difference'.","Language: show feelings through actions and speech, vary your sentence openings, keep to the past tense, and write well over 150 words."]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-p1-q2-img1","alt":"Three pictures in a column. Picture 1: a silhouette of a younger person supporting an elderly person who walks with a stick. Picture 2: an open, empty cardboard box. Picture 3: a smiling cat sitting with a bandage wrapped around one front leg."}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":1,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["question-tags"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Father's eaten his lunch, __________?\" Mary asked.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Rule: a question tag repeats the helping verb of the statement, and a positive statement takes a negative tag.","'Father's eaten' is short for 'Father **has** eaten', because 'eaten' is a past participle and it follows 'has', not 'is'.","The helping verb is 'has', so the tag is 'hasn't he'. 'isn't he' is the trap: 's can be 'is' or 'has', and here it is 'has'.","The answer is option (2), hasn't he."]}],"options":["isn't he","hasn't he","wasn't he","doesn't he"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":2,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Rani is well-known __________ her expertise in growing orchids.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Some adjectives always go with a fixed preposition. We say a person is 'well-known **for**' or 'famous **for**' the thing that makes them known.","Rani's expertise in growing orchids is the reason people know her, so the word is 'for'.","'well-known by' would be followed by the people who know her (well-known by gardeners), not by the reason.","The answer is option (2), for."]}],"options":["by","for","from","about"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q3","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 3","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":3,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Won't Alice be excited when she __________ that she's representing the class for the speech competition?\" Ben asked.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Rule: after time words such as 'when', 'after' and 'as soon as', use the simple present tense to talk about the future. Do not use 'will' in that part of the sentence.","'Won't Alice be excited' is about the future (will not), so the 'when' part takes the simple present: 'when she hears'.","'heard' is past tense, but Alice has not been told yet. 'has heard' is possible in some sentences, but here the excitement happens at the moment she hears, so the simple present fits best.","The answer is option (1), hears."]}],"options":["hears","heard","has heard","has been hearing"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q4","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 4","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":4,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["infinitives-and-gerunds","prepositions"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Mother objected to __________ the bag for Sarah as it was too expensive.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["In 'object to', the word 'to' is a preposition, not part of a to-infinitive.","Rule: a verb that comes after a preposition takes the -ing form (a gerund): object to buying, look forward to seeing.","'buy' is the trap because 'to buy' looks right, but 'objected to buy' is wrong. 'having bought' would mean the bag was already bought, which does not fit an objection.","The answer is option (2), buying."]}],"options":["buy","buying","be buying","having bought"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q5","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 5","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":5,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["quantifiers"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Tina made __________ progress in her studies because she had been revising diligently.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'progress' is an uncountable noun (we cannot say 'two progresses'), so 'few' and 'many', which go with countable nouns, are out.","That leaves 'little' and 'much'. 'little progress' means hardly any; 'much progress' means a lot.","The reason given is that she 'had been revising diligently'. Hard work leads to a lot of progress, so 'much' fits the meaning.","The answer is option (3), much."]}],"options":["few","little","much","many"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q6","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 6","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":6,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["subject-verb-agreement","passive-voice"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"All the information about the upcoming school workshops __________ to the students last week.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Tense: 'last week' shows the past, so 'is given' and 'are given' are out.","Agreement: find the real subject. It is 'information', not 'workshops', which only sits in the phrase 'about the upcoming school workshops'.","'information' is uncountable and always takes a singular verb, even with 'all the' in front. So the verb is 'was given', not 'were given'.","The answer is option (3), was given."]}],"options":["is given","are given","was given","were given"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q7","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 7","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":7,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["subject-verb-agreement","tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The commitment displayed by the students __________ the reason why they did well in the project.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Agreement: the subject is 'The commitment' (singular). 'displayed by the students' only describes it, so do not let the plural 'students' trick you. 'are' and 'were' are out.","Tense: the rest of the sentence is in the past ('they **did** well in the project'), so the verb should be past too.","Singular and past gives 'was'. 'is' is tempting, but the project is over and the sentence is told in the past.","The answer is option (3), was."]}],"options":["is","are","was","were"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q8","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 8","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":8,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["infinitives-and-gerunds"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The young boy helped the old lady __________ the busy road.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Rule: after help + a person, use the base form of the verb (with or without 'to'): helped her cross, helped her to cross.","The verb 'helped' already carries the past tense, so the second verb does not change: it stays 'cross'.","'crossed' is the trap because the sentence is in the past, but only the main verb 'helped' shows the tense.","The answer is option (1), cross."]}],"options":["cross","crosses","crossed","was crossing"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q9","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 9","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":9,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["conditionals","tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"If John __________ early last night, he would not have missed his school bus this morning.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["This is a conditional about something in the past that did not happen: John did not sleep early, and he did miss the bus.","Rule (third conditional): If + had + past participle, ... would have + past participle. The second half has 'would not have missed', so the 'if' half needs 'had slept'.","'had been sleeping' describes an action in progress, not the completed act of going to sleep early. 'sleeps' and 'has slept' are present forms and do not match 'last night'.","The answer is option (3), had slept."]}],"options":["sleeps","has slept","had slept","had been sleeping"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q10","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 10","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":10,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"__________ his injury, the participant was unable to complete the race.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Work out how the two ideas are linked. The injury is the **cause**; being unable to finish is the **result**. We need a connector that shows a reason.","'Due to' means 'because of' and is followed by a noun phrase ('his injury').","'Despite' and 'Regardless of' show contrast: they would fit if he had finished the race even though he was hurt. 'Although' must be followed by a clause with a verb (Although he was injured), not just a noun phrase.","The answer is option (1), Due to."]}],"options":["Due to","Despite","Although","Regardless of"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q11","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 11","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":11,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The children were thrilled at the idea of going for a class learning journey. They felt __________ the entire day.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clue: 'thrilled' in the first sentence. The second sentence repeats the same feeling, so we need a word for very happy and excited.","'exhilarated' means extremely happy and full of excitement.","'daunted' and 'apprehensive' both mean worried or afraid, which is the opposite. 'moved' means touched with emotion, such as by a sad story.","The answer is option (3), exhilarated."]}],"options":["moved","daunted","exhilarated","apprehensive"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q12","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 12","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":12,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"To prevent people from getting too close to the turtle's nest on the beach, the Nature Lovers' Society __________ the area.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Clue: the aim is 'to prevent people from getting too close'. We need a phrasal verb meaning to block off a place so that people cannot enter.","'cordon off' means to put a rope, tape or barrier around an area to keep people out.","'fend off' means to defend yourself against an attack. 'round off' means to finish something nicely or to round a number. 'mark off' only shows a boundary; it does not carry the idea of keeping people out as strongly.","The answer is option (4), cordoned off."]}],"options":["fended off","marked off","rounded off","cordoned off"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q13","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 13","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":13,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning","collocation"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The top student of the school __________ her success to her parents and teachers who had nurtured her in the formative years of her life.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Look at the pattern: ___ her success **to** her parents and teachers. The word must fit 'something to somebody' and mean 'said it was because of'.","'attribute something to someone' means to say that someone is the cause of it. She said her success was thanks to her parents and teachers.","'acknowledged' is tempting because it is about giving credit, but we acknowledge a person or a fact (she acknowledged her teachers' help); it does not take 'her success to'. 'associated' goes with 'with', and 'assigned' means gave out a task.","The answer is option (2), attributed."]}],"options":["assigned","attributed","associated","acknowledged"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q14","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 14","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":14,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The fight between Peter and Ming was __________ only because their teacher intervened.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Clue: 'only because their teacher intervened' (stepped in). The teacher stopped the fight from happening.","'averted' means prevented something bad from happening.","The other words look alike but mean different things: 'inverted' is turned upside down, 'diverted' is sent in another direction (diverted traffic), and 'reverted' is went back to an earlier state.","The answer is option (1), averted."]}],"options":["averted","inverted","diverted","reverted"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q15","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 15","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":15,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Due to time constraints, the debate team members ensured that their argument was brief and to the point. They were very __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clue: 'brief and to the point'. We need the word that means saying what is needed in few words.","'concise' means short and clear, with no unnecessary words.","'precise' and 'accurate' mean exact and correct, and 'specific' means detailed and clear about one thing. A speech can be precise and still be long, so they do not match 'brief'.","The answer is option (3), concise."]}],"options":["precise","specific","concise","accurate"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q16","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 16","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":16,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++deepen++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["To 'deepen' her understanding is to make an understanding she already has stronger and fuller.","'enhance' means to improve something or make it greater, which matches.","'create' means to make something new, but she already has some understanding. 'foster' and 'cultivate' mean to help something start and grow over time, which is close but is about developing it, not about making it deeper.","The answer is option (4), enhance."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g1","options":["foster","create","cultivate","enhance"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q17","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 17","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":17,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++fascination++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["'fascination' is the feeling of being strongly attracted to and amazed by something interesting.","Clue: 'each corner revealing a new surprise'. She was amazed by what she saw, which is 'wonder'.","'curiosity' is tempting, but it is the wish to find out; fascination is the amazed feeling when you do see it. 'delight' and 'ecstasy' are about happiness, not amazement.","The answer is option (2), wonder."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g1","options":["delight","wonder","ecstasy","curiosity"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q18","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 18","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":18,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++illuminate++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["To 'illuminate' is to light something up.","The sunset lit up the sky 'with brilliant shades of orange and pink', so the closest word is 'brighten'.","'adorn' means to decorate. 'highlight' and 'accentuate' mean to make one part stand out, not to fill something with light.","The answer is option (2), brighten."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g1","options":["adorn","brighten","highlight","accentuate"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q19","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 19","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":19,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++frail++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["'frail' means physically weak and delicate, often because of old age. The man is 'elderly'.","'feeble' also means very weak.","'weary' means tired, which passes after a rest. 'slender' means thin in an attractive way, and 'gentle' describes how a person behaves.","The answer is option (1), feeble."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g1","options":["feeble","weary","gentle","slender"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q20","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 20","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":20,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++enlighten++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["To 'enlighten' someone is to give them knowledge and understanding.","Clue: the stories would enlighten her 'in ways books never could'. Books teach us, so the comparison is about learning: 'educate'.","'inspire' is tempting because the stories are 'inspirational', but inspire and motivate mean to make someone want to do something, not to give understanding.","The answer is option (2), educate."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g1","options":["inspire","educate","motivate","stimulate"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q21","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 21","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":21,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Which of the following is true about the launch?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Check each statement against the poster (Text 1).","The poster says 'Only 100 pairs!' A fixed, small number means the supply is limited, so statement (2) is true.","(1) is false: 'No reservations for customers or online purchases.' (3) is false: the launch is 'Exclusively for Sports House members'. (4) is false: it is only 'at the Vista City Outlet'.","The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g2","options":["Reservations for the shoes can be made online.","Only a limited number of shoes will be available.","Anyone can purchase the shoes on 12 December.","Falcon shoes will be sold at all Sports House outlets."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q22","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 22","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":22,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"What kind of poster is shown in Text 1?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Ask: what does the poster want the reader to do?","It announces a 'sales launch', gives a price ($129.90), has a catchy slogan ('DON'T RUN, FLY!') and lists the shoe's selling points. It wants people to come and buy the Falcon.","A poster that advertises a product so that people will buy it is promotional.","'informational' is tempting because the poster gives a date and place, but those details serve the main aim, which is to sell. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g2","options":["educational","promotional","motivational","informational"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q23","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 23","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":23,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Linda often experiences pain in her feet and prefers to run at night. Based on the information in Text 1, which features of the shoes would be most important for her?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Linda has two needs. Match each one to a feature box on the poster.","She runs at night: D) Visibility, 'Reflective materials keep you visible in low light'. That leaves options (3) and (4).","She often has pain in her feet: F) Support, 'Provides stability for injury-free running'. E) Durability is only about how long the shoes last, which does not help with pain.","The answer is option (3), D and F."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g2","options":["A and B","C and F","D and F","D and E"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q24","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 24","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":24,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"What is the **main message** of Text 2?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["The main message is the idea the whole text supports. Look at the first and last paragraphs.","First sentence: 'Wearing the right shoes is important for every runner'. Conclusion: choosing the right pair 'is an important one'. The middle paragraphs give the reasons (support, cushioning, balance).","(3) is only a detail and is not even stated: the text says runners 'perform well' and run 'more efficiently', not simply faster. (1) is the opposite of the point that the decision matters.","The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g2","options":["It is easy to choose a pair of running shoes.","Running shoes are for the young and old.","Proper running shoes makes one run faster.","Wearing the right pair of running shoes is important."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-a-q25","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet A Question 25","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"a","number":25,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience","inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Both the descriptions for the shoes in Text 1 and the content of Text 2 __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["The word 'Both' is the key: the answer must be true of Text 1's feature descriptions **and** of Text 2.","Text 1's boxes tell what good shoes do for a runner (comfort, grip, support, injury-free running). Text 2 explains that good shoes help runners perform well and prevent injuries. Both show why good-quality running shoes matter.","(1) fits only Text 1; Text 2 does not advertise any shoe. Neither text compares features with one another or gives step-by-step tips for choosing.","The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g2","options":["advertise the latest running shoes","offer tips on how to select running shoes","compare different features of running shoes","highlight the importance of quality running shoes"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q26","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 26","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":26,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (26). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"P","accept":["p","when"]},"explanation":["The blank joins two clauses: 'They occur' and 'the diaphragm ... suddenly contracts'. We need a connector.","The second clause tells us the time at which hiccups happen, so the connector is 'when'.","'although' and 'unless' are also connectors, but they show contrast and condition, which do not fit: hiccups happen at the moment the diaphragm contracts.","The answer is (P) when: 'They occur when the diaphragm ... suddenly contracts.'"]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q27","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 27","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":27,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["relative-pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (27). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Q","accept":["q","which"]},"explanation":["'a muscle under the lungs ___ helps you breathe' sits between commas and describes the diaphragm.","The blank must stand for 'a muscle' and be the subject of 'helps'. That is the job of a relative pronoun.","A muscle is a thing, so we use 'which' (for people we would use 'who').","The answer is (Q) which."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q28","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 28","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":28,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions","phrasal-verbs"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (28). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"D","accept":["d","in"]},"explanation":["'breathe ___ air' needs a small word that completes the verb.","'breathe in' means to take air into the lungs. When the diaphragm contracts, air rushes into the body.","The answer is (D) in: 'makes you breathe in air very fast'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q29","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 29","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":29,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (29). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"J","accept":["j","the"]},"explanation":["'causing ___ familiar \"hic\" sound' needs an article before the noun phrase.","Everyone knows this particular sound, and the word 'familiar' tells us it is a known, specific one. For something specific and known we use 'the'.","'an' is wrong because 'familiar' starts with a consonant sound.","The answer is (J) the."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q30","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 30","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":30,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (30). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"F","accept":["f","many"]},"explanation":["'There are ___ reasons' needs a quantity word before a plural countable noun.","'many' goes with countable nouns (many reasons); 'much' goes with uncountable nouns (much air).","The next sentences list several reasons (eating too quickly, swallowing air, laughing, feeling nervous), which confirms 'many'.","The answer is (F) many."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q31","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 31","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":31,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (31). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"K","accept":["k","their"]},"explanation":["'on ___ own' is a fixed phrase meaning 'by itself or themselves, without help'. The blank is a possessive word.","The word must match the noun it points back to: 'hiccups', which is plural. So we use 'their', not 'its'.","The answer is (K) their: 'hiccups ... stop on their own'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q32","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 32","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":32,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (32). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"H","accept":["h","of"]},"explanation":["'get rid of' is a fixed phrase meaning to remove or be free from something.","The verb phrase always takes 'of': get rid of hiccups, get rid of rubbish.","The answer is (H) of."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q33","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 33","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":33,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners","pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (33). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"L","accept":["l","these"]},"explanation":["The blank starts a sentence and comes before the plural noun 'methods'.","The methods were just listed in the sentence before (holding their breath, drinking water slowly, breathing into a paper bag). To point back to plural things just mentioned, use 'these'.","The answer is (L) these: 'These methods help the diaphragm relax'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q34","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 34","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":34,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (34). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"M","accept":["m","to"]},"explanation":["'goes back ___ normal' needs a preposition that shows returning to a state.","We say 'go back to' or 'return to' something: back to school, back to normal.","The answer is (M) to."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q35","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 35","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":35,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (35). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"A","accept":["a","although"]},"explanation":["The blank joins two clauses with opposite ideas: hiccups are 'harmless' (good) but 'they can be annoying' (not so good).","A connector of contrast is needed. 'although' means 'even though'.","'unless' means 'if not' and would make no sense here.","The answer is (A) although: 'Hiccups are harmless, although they can be annoying.'"]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q36","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 36","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":36,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (36) ++occassion++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"occasion"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error.","'occasion' has a double 'c' but only one 's': oc-ca-sion.","A way to remember: two c's, one s, like the word 'accuse'.","The correct word is 'occasion'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q37","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 37","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":37,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (37) ++appearence++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"appearance"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error.","The noun comes from 'appear' + '-ance', not '-ence': appear-ance.","Words in the same family: appear, disappear, appearance, disappearance.","The correct word is 'appearance'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q38","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 38","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":38,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (38) ++embrassed++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"embarrassed"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error: letters are missing.","'embarrassed' has a double 'r' and a double 's': em-bar-rass-ed.","The underlined word left out 'ar' in the middle.","The correct word is 'embarrassed'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q39","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 39","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":39,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (39) ++wore++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"worn"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. Look at the helping verb in front: 'she **had** accidentally ___'.","Rule: had + past participle makes the past perfect tense. 'wore' is the simple past form, not the past participle.","wear, wore, worn: the past participle is 'worn'.","The correct word is 'worn'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q40","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 40","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":40,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (40) ++on++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"in"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong preposition.","The fixed phrase is 'in a rush' or 'in her rush to do something'.","'in her frantic rush to leave home' means while she was hurrying to leave.","The correct word is 'in'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q41","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 41","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":41,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (41) ++soft++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"softly"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong word class.","The word describes **how** everyone chuckled. A word that describes a verb is an adverb.","'soft' is an adjective (a soft pillow). Add -ly to make the adverb: softly.","The correct word is 'softly'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q42","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 42","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":42,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (42) ++comfort++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"comforting"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong word class.","The blank is in a list of describing words before the noun 'hug': 'a warm, ___ hug'. It must be an adjective, like 'warm'.","'comfort' is a noun or a verb. The adjective for something that gives comfort is 'comforting'.","The correct word is 'comforting'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q43","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 43","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":43,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (43) ++receives++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"received"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong tense.","The whole passage tells what happened 'Last Saturday', and the other verbs are in the past: gathered, arrived, gave.","'receives' is present tense. Change it to the simple past.","The correct word is 'received' (remember: i before e except after c)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q44","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 44","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":44,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (44) ++elegeant++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"elegant"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error: there is an extra letter.","'elegant' is spelt e-l-e-g-a-n-t. There is no 'e' after the 'g'.","Say it in three beats: el-e-gant.","The correct word is 'elegant'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q45","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 45","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":45,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (45) ++convinient++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"convenient"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error.","'convenient' is spelt con-ve-ni-ent: 'e' after the 'v', then 'ni'.","The underlined word used 'i' instead of 'e' in the second syllable.","The correct word is 'convenient'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q46","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 46","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":46,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (46) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"through","accept":["with","using","via"]},"explanation":["The sentence says courtesy is shown ___ simple greetings. The greetings are the **way** or the means of showing courtesy.","The preposition that means 'by means of' is 'through'.","'with' and 'using' give the same meaning and also fit.","The answer is 'through'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q47","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 47","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":47,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (47) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"everyone","accept":["everybody"]},"explanation":["'where ___ feels valued' needs a subject for the verb 'feels'.","'feels' is singular, and a positive, friendly environment is good for all the people in it. The singular pronoun that means all people is 'everyone'.","'all' would need the plural verb 'feel', so it does not fit.","The answer is 'everyone' ('everybody' means the same)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q48","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 48","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":48,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (48) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"ensure","accept":["maintain","promote","guarantee"]},"explanation":["'Rules are designed to ___ safety, fairness, and order for all.' After 'to' we need a base verb that says what rules do for safety.","Rules make sure that these things are there. 'ensure' means to make certain that something happens.","'maintain' (keep up) and 'promote' (encourage) also make sense with safety, fairness and order.","The answer is 'ensure'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q49","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 49","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":49,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (49) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"waiting","accept":["queueing","queuing"]},"explanation":["Look at the list: '___ patiently for one's turn, **keeping** noise levels low ... and **following** instructions'. All items in a list take the same form, so the blank is an -ing word.","What do we do patiently 'for one's turn'? We wait. The phrase is 'wait for one's turn'.","The answer is 'waiting'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q50","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 50","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":50,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (50) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"respect","accept":["consideration","concern","care"]},"explanation":["'ways to demonstrate self-discipline and ___ for others' needs a noun that goes with 'for others'.","The paragraph begins 'Respecting school rules'. Waiting your turn and keeping quiet in the library show that you respect other people.","'consideration for others' has a similar meaning and also fits.","The answer is 'respect'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q51","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 51","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":51,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (51) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"may","accept":["can","could","might"]},"explanation":["'how your actions ___ possibly affect other people' has the base verb 'affect' after the blank, so the blank is a modal (helping) verb.","The word 'possibly' shows that it is about possibility, not certainty. Modals of possibility are may, might, can and could.","The answer is 'may'. 'can', 'could' and 'might' fit in the same way."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q52","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 52","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":52,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","collocation"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (52) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"discussions","accept":["activities","work","projects","tasks"]},"explanation":["'including quieter students in group ___' needs a noun that goes with 'group' and that students do together.","Quieter students tend to be left out when a group is talking, so the best fit is 'group discussions'. The verb after the blank is 'are', but its subject is the whole list (Helping ..., offering ..., or including ...), so the blank can be plural or uncountable.","'group activities', 'group work' and 'group projects' also make sense.","The answer is 'discussions'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q53","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 53","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":53,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (53) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"personal","accept":["private","individual"]},"explanation":["'both ___ and shared resources': the word 'both ... and' pairs two kinds of resources, and one of them is 'shared'.","We need an adjective that contrasts with 'shared': things that belong to one person. That is 'personal'.","The next sentence gives examples of shared resources (borrowed books, the canteen, school property), which confirms the contrast.","The answer is 'personal'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q54","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 54","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":54,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (54) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"with"},"explanation":["'using school property ___ care' describes **how** the property is used.","The fixed phrase is 'with care', which means carefully: handle with care.","The answer is 'with'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q55","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 55","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":55,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (55) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"and"},"explanation":["'help to strengthen existing bonds ___ maintain good relationships' joins two base verbs that follow 'help to': strengthen and maintain.","Both are good results that happen together, so they are joined with 'and'.","The answer is 'and'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q56","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 56","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":56,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (56) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"when","accept":["whenever","as"]},"explanation":["'Handling disagreements ___ they arise' joins 'handling disagreements' to the clause 'they arise' (they come up).","The clause tells the time: we handle disagreements at the time they come up. The connector is 'when'.","'as they arise' and 'whenever they arise' mean the same.","The answer is 'when'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q57","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 57","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":57,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","collocation"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (57) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"doubt"},"explanation":["'when you are in ___ about a difficult concept' needs a noun that completes the phrase 'in ___ about'.","The fixed phrase is 'in doubt', which means not sure. You ask questions when you are not sure about something.","The answer is 'doubt'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q58","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 58","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":58,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (58) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"being"},"explanation":["Look at the list after 'by': 'by **participating** in community activities, ___ kind towards newcomers, and **helping** others'. Every item is an -ing word.","The blank is followed by the adjective 'kind'. The verb that links to an adjective is 'be': be kind.","Put 'be' into the -ing form to match the list: 'being kind towards newcomers'.","The answer is 'being'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q59","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 59","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":59,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (59) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"values","accept":["attitudes","traits","qualities","virtues"]},"explanation":["'positive ___ such as graciousness and gratitude': the blank is a plural noun, and 'such as' gives two examples of it.","Graciousness and gratitude are good qualities that guide how we behave. Such qualities are called 'values'.","'qualities', 'traits' and 'attitudes' also describe them.","The answer is 'values'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q60","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 60","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":60,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","collocation"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (60) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"contribute"},"explanation":["'students ___ to a better school experience' needs a verb that is followed by 'to' and agrees with the plural subject 'students'.","'contribute to' means to help to bring something about. Students who show good values help to make school better for everyone.","The answer is 'contribute'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q61","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 61","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":61,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["noun-form"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"I was impressed by how deep her knowledge was.\n____________________ of her knowledge.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"I was impressed by the depth of her knowledge."},"explanation":["The ending 'of her knowledge' needs a noun in front of it: the ___ of her knowledge.","Change the adjective 'deep' into its noun form: deep becomes 'depth' (like wide, width and long, length).","'how deep her knowledge was' becomes 'the depth of her knowledge'. Drop 'how' and 'was'.","Answer: I was impressed by the depth of her knowledge. Spell 'depth' correctly and keep 'was impressed' in the past tense."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q62","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 62","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":62,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["as-soon-as","connectors"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"We left immediately when the movie ended.\nAs soon as ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"As soon as the movie ended, we left.","accept":["As soon as the movie ended we left"]},"explanation":["'As soon as' means 'immediately when', so it replaces both words. Do not write 'immediately' again.","The event that happened first follows 'As soon as': the movie ended. Then comes what happened next: we left.","Keep both verbs in the simple past: ended, left.","Answer: As soon as the movie ended, we left."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q63","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 63","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":63,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["would-rather","preference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"David does not like to play computer games. He likes to read.\n____________________ would rather ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"David would rather read than play computer games."},"explanation":["'would rather A than B' shows preference: the thing the person likes comes first, the thing he does not like comes after 'than'.","Rule: after 'would rather' and after 'than', use the base form of the verb with no 'to': read, play.","David likes reading, so 'read' goes first; 'play computer games' goes after 'than'.","Answer: David would rather read than play computer games."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q64","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 64","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":64,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["reported-speech"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"\"Did you sit for the Science test last week?\" Mother asked Sarah.\nMother asked Sarah ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Mother asked Sarah if she had sat for the Science test the previous week.","accept":["Mother asked Sarah whether she had sat for the Science test the previous week.","Mother asked Sarah if she had sat for the Science test the week before.","Mother asked Sarah whether she had sat for the Science test the week before."]},"explanation":["This is a yes/no question in reported speech. Join it with 'if' or 'whether', and use statement word order (subject before verb) with no question mark.","Pronoun: 'you' is Sarah, so it becomes 'she'.","Tense moves one step back: 'Did you sit' (simple past) becomes 'had sat' (past perfect).","Time word: 'last week' becomes 'the previous week' or 'the week before'.","Answer: Mother asked Sarah if she had sat for the Science test the previous week."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q65","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 65","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":65,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["adjective-of-infinitive"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"She is such a nice person. She collected my worksheets for me when I was absent.\nIt was so ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"It was so nice of her to collect my worksheets for me when I was absent.","accept":["It was so nice of her to have collected my worksheets for me when I was absent."]},"explanation":["The pattern is: It was + so + adjective + of + person + to-infinitive. It praises a person for something he or she did.","'such a nice person' becomes 'so nice'. After 'of', the pronoun changes to the object form: 'she' becomes 'her'.","The action follows as a to-infinitive: 'collected' becomes 'to collect'.","Answer: It was so nice of her to collect my worksheets for me when I was absent."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q66","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 66","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":66,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"From paragraph 1, state three **physical** signs that tell us how terribly parched Brian had been.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"First physical sign:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Brian's mouth was dry and tasted foul and sticky."},"explanation":["'parched' means extremely thirsty and dried out. A **physical** sign is something that can be seen or felt on his body.","Paragraph 1, third sentence: 'His mouth was dry and tasted foul and sticky.'","The marker looks for the mouth being dry (and tasting foul and sticky). 'He was viciously thirsty' is a feeling, not a physical sign, so it does not score."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Second physical sign:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"His lips were cracked and seemed as if they were bleeding."},"explanation":["Paragraph 1, fourth sentence: 'His lips were cracked and seemed as if they were bleeding.'","Cracked lips are a sign of a body that has dried out.","The marker looks for: lips cracked (and seemed to be bleeding)."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"Third physical sign:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"His skin was all withered up."},"explanation":["Paragraph 1, fifth sentence: 'His skin was all withered up.' 'withered' means dried up and shrivelled, like a plant without water.","Do not give the burn on his face or the blistering: those are signs of sunburn, caused by the sun while he slept, not signs of thirst.","The marker looks for: skin all withered up. The three signs may be written in any order."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q67","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 67","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":67,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["factual","cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":4,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Brian struggled hard to decide if he wanted to try drinking water from the lake. Using information from lines 6 to 17 (paragraphs 2 and 3), complete the following tables.\n[[table1]]\n[[table2]]","parts":[{"label":"ai","prompt":"Table (a), reason (i): Brian thought he ++should drink++ water from the lake because","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The lake was blue and looked clean."},"explanation":["Table (a) lists Brian's reasons **for** drinking. The reasons follow the order of the passage, and reason (ii), 'his mouth and throat raged with thirst', is given.","Read the words just before it in paragraph 2: 'but the lake was blue, looked clean and his mouth and throat raged with thirst'.","So reason (i) is that the lake was blue and looked clean."]},{"label":"aiii","prompt":"Table (a), reason (iii): Brian thought he ++should drink++ water from the lake because","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"There might not be another water source he could drink from."},"explanation":["Reason (iii) lies between (ii) 'raged with thirst' and (iv) 'he had swallowed a lot of the water'.","Between them is the question in Brian's mind: 'Would there be another water source he could drink from?'","Turn the question into a reason: he did not know if there was any other water source, so the lake might be his only water."]},{"label":"bii","prompt":"Table (b), reason (ii): Brian thought he ++should not drink++ water from the lake because","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"There were thick, prickly grasses in the way."},"explanation":["Table (b) lists the reasons **against** drinking. Reason (iii), 'the water looked a little murky', is given, and the reasons are in passage order.","Read the words just before it in paragraph 3: 'Along the edge, there were thick prickly grasses in the way and the water looked a little murky.'","So reason (ii) is the thick prickly grasses in the way, which made the water hard to get to."]},{"label":"biv","prompt":"Table (b), reason (iv): Brian thought he ++should not drink++ water from the lake because","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"There were small bugs swimming in the water."},"explanation":["Reason (iv) comes after 'the water looked a little murky'.","The next sentence: 'He scowled in disgust at the small bugs swimming in the water'. 'scowled in disgust' shows how much this put him off.","So reason (iv) is that there were small bugs swimming in the water."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g6","tables":[{"id":"table1","header":["What Brian thought:","Reason why Brian thought that way:"],"rows":[[{"t":"(a) Brian thought he ++should drink++ water from the lake because","rowspan":4},"(i) ____________________"],["(ii) his mouth and throat raged with thirst."],["(iii) ____________________"],["(iv) he had swallowed a lot of the water in the lake and was fine."]]},{"id":"table2","header":["What Brian thought:","Reason why Brian thought that way:"],"rows":[[{"t":"(b) Brian thought he ++should not drink++ water from the lake because","rowspan":4},"(i) he felt the idea of drinking water from a lake was awful."],["(ii) ____________________"],["(iii) the water looked a little murky."],["(iv) ____________________"]]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q68","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 68","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":68,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["true-false-with-reason"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on information from lines 12 to 17 (paragraph 3), state whether each statement in the table below is true or false, then give one reason why you think so.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Brian had injured himself in the plane wreck.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. Brian had a badly bruised forehead and an aching body."},"explanation":["Paragraph 3, first sentence: his situation consisted of 'a plane wreck, a badly bruised forehead, an aching body and a thirst'.","A badly bruised forehead and an aching body are injuries, and they are listed right after the plane wreck. So the statement is True.","The reason must be evidence from the passage (bruised forehead, aching body), not the statement repeated."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Brian could not think too well because he was dehydrated.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. The thirst tore at Brian until he could not think."},"explanation":["'dehydrated' means the body does not have enough water, which is why he was so thirsty.","Paragraph 3, first sentence: 'a thirst that tore at him until he could not think'. The thirst was so strong that it stopped him from thinking.","So the statement is True, and the reason is that his thirst tore at him until he could not think."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q69","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 69","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":69,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["vocabulary-in-context"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Which word from lines 18 to 23 (paragraph 4) tells us that Brian was moving unsteadily on the log?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"teetered"},"explanation":["The question asks for one word about **how he moved**, so look for a verb of movement in paragraph 4.","'He balanced on the log, holding himself up with the limbs, and **teetered** out past the weeds and muddy water.'","To 'teeter' is to move in an unsteady way, as if about to fall. 'balanced' is the trap: it means he kept steady, which is the opposite. ('staggered' also means unsteady, but it is in paragraph 5 and he was no longer on the log.)","The answer is 'teetered'. Copy one word only, spelt exactly."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q70","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 70","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":70,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Brian spent quite some time at the log on the lake. Based on information from lines 18 to 28 (paragraphs 4 and 5), fill in the following table.\n[[table1]]","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"How Brian felt about the water in the lake in (a)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Brian felt undecided (unsure whether the water in the lake was good and safe enough to drink)."},"explanation":["Look at what Brian thought: he 'would **just try a sip**, still **pondering** over the lake water'.","'pondering' means still thinking it over, and taking only a sip shows he was being careful. He had not made up his mind whether the water was safe.","The given answer for (b) helps: by then he 'was past caring whether or not the water ... was good and safe enough to drink'. So in (a) he still cared and was unsure.","The marker looks for a feeling such as undecided, unsure, doubtful or hesitant about the water."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"What Brian did","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Brian could not stop drinking the lake water (he stooped, put his mouth to the lake and drank and drank in great gulps) as he was too thirsty."},"explanation":["The feeling given for (b) is that he 'was past caring' whether the water was safe. Find the action that shows this.","End of paragraph 4: once the cold water touched his lips, 'he could not stop'. Paragraph 5: 'He stooped and put his mouth to the lake and drank and drank ... swallowing great gulps of it. He drank until his stomach was swollen'.","The marker looks for an action: he could not stop drinking, or he drank and drank great gulps of the lake water. Write what he **did**, not how he felt."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g6","tables":[{"id":"table1","rows":[["**(a) What Brian thought**","**How Brian felt about the water in the lake in (a)**"],["Brian thought he would just try a sip, still pondering over the lake water.","____________________"],["**(b) What Brian did**","**How Brian felt about the water in the lake in (b)**"],["____________________","Brian felt that he was past caring whether or not the water in the lake was good and safe enough to drink."]]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q71","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 71","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":71,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"structured","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"When Brian 'felt the cold lake water trickle past his cracked lips and over his tongue, he could not stop' (lines 22–23, paragraph 4, from \"mouth and felt\" to \"could not stop\"). What was it he could not stop doing?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Brian could not stop drinking the lake water.","accept":["He could not stop drinking the lake water.","Drinking the lake water","He could not stop drinking the water from the lake.","Drinking the water from the lake"]},"explanation":["The sentence does not say what he could not stop, so read on to see what he did next.","Paragraph 5: 'He stooped and put his mouth to the lake and **drank and drank**'. Before that, he had only meant to 'try a sip'.","So what he could not stop doing was drinking the water from the lake.","The marker looks for: drinking (the lake water)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q72","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 72","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":72,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["cause-and-effect","inference"],"format":"structured","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Why did Brian throw up most of the water he had drunk?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Brian had drunk too much water, until his stomach was swollen, and he felt sick, so he threw up most of the water."},"explanation":["Find 'threw up' at the end of paragraph 5 and read the cause just before it.","'He drank until his stomach was swollen, until he nearly fell off the log with it ... he was immediately sick and threw up most of the water.' He had also been 'swallowing great gulps'.","He took in far more water than his stomach could hold, and that made him sick.","The marker looks for: he drank too much water (until his stomach was swollen)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q73","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 73","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":73,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["referencing"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Look at the table below. What do the words in the left column refer to in the passage? Write your answers in the column on the right.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"It (line 5, paragraph 1)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Brian's face","accept":["his face","Brian's face","the face","face","Brians face"]},"explanation":["A pronoun like 'It' points back to something just mentioned. Read the sentence before it.","'His face felt like it was on fire. **It** was going to blister and peel.' What was going to blister and peel after being burnt by the sun? His face.","Test by replacing the word: 'His face was going to blister and peel.' It makes sense.","The answer is Brian's face."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"it (line 27, paragraph 5)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Brian's swollen stomach","accept":["his swollen stomach","his stomach","Brian's stomach","the swollen stomach","swollen stomach"]},"explanation":["'He drank until his stomach was swollen, until he nearly fell off the log with **it**'.","What made him so heavy that he nearly fell off the log? The stomach that was swollen with water, mentioned just before.","Earlier in the paragraph, 'it' ('pulling it deep', 'great gulps of it') means the water, but this 'it' follows 'his stomach was swollen', so it points to the swollen stomach.","The answer is Brian's swollen stomach."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"it (line 31, paragraph 6)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"the tall tree without branches","accept":["the tall tree","the tree","tall tree without branches","the tree without branches"]},"explanation":["'He pulled himself once more up the bank to the tall tree without branches and sat again with his back against **it**.'","What did he lean his back against? The nearest noun that fits is the tall tree. ('the bank' does not fit: he climbed up the bank to reach the tree.)","Test by replacing the word: 'sat again with his back against the tall tree'. It makes sense.","The answer is the tall tree without branches."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q74","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 74","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":74,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["sequencing"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write 1, 2 and 3 in the blanks below to indicate the order in which the events occurred in the story.\n_____ Brian fell asleep under the sun.\n_____ Brian's plane crashed.\n_____ Brian drank water from the lake.\n(Type the three numbers in the order of the blanks from top to bottom, for example 3, 2, 1.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"2, 1, 3","accept":["2 1 3","213","2,1,3","2, 1, 3"]},"explanation":["Be careful: the order in which the passage **tells** things is not the order in which they **happened**.","The plane crash came first. It is only mentioned later as something already over: he swallowed water 'while he was swimming out of the plane' and his situation included 'a plane wreck'.","Next he fell asleep: when the passage opens he has just woken up, and 'The sun had come over him and cooked him while he slept.'","Last, after waking, he walked down to the lake and drank (paragraphs 4 and 5).","So: Brian fell asleep under the sun is 2, Brian's plane crashed is 1, Brian drank water from the lake is 3. The answer is 2, 1, 3."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-b-q75","cite":"2026 Prelim Raffles Girls Paper 2 Booklet B Question 75","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Raffles Girls","section":"b","number":75,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["character-and-feelings","inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Using information from lines 29 to 33 (paragraph 6), answer the following questions.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Which **ONE** of the following words best describes how Brian felt in the last paragraph about what was happening? Put a tick in the box beside your answer.\nDO NOT TICK MORE THAN ONE BOX.\n• determined\n• disappointed\n• puzzled\n• affirmed\n(Type the word you would tick.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"text","value":"determined"},"explanation":["Look at how the last paragraph ends: 'but there were things he knew he had to do.'","Brian was feeling better (his thirst was gone and the pain in his head was less), and instead of just resting he was already thinking about what he must do next. A person who has made up his mind to act is 'determined'.","He was not 'disappointed' (things had improved) or 'puzzled' (he **knew** what he had to do). 'affirmed' means confirmed or declared to be true; it is not a feeling about what to do next.","The answer is 'determined'."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Why did Brian feel that way?","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Brian's thirst was gone and the water seemed to reduce the pain in his head, and he knew there were things he had to do."},"explanation":["The reason must come from paragraph 6 and must explain the feeling chosen in (a), determined.","First, he was now in a better state: 'his thirst was gone by then and the water seemed to reduce the pain in his head as well'.","Second, he had a purpose: 'there were things he knew he had to do'.","The marker looks for: he knew there were things he had to do (now that his thirst was gone and his pain was less)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":1,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["infinitives-and-gerunds"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"My grandfather had trouble __________ an attachment to his email until I taught him.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Rule: after the phrase 'have trouble' (and 'have difficulty', 'have a hard time'), the next verb takes the -ing form.","We say 'had trouble adding', just like 'had trouble sleeping' or 'had difficulty walking'.","'to add' is tempting because many verbs are followed by 'to', but 'trouble' is not one of them. 'having added' would mean the adding was already finished, which does not fit.","'My grandfather had trouble adding an attachment...' The answer is option (2)."]}],"options":["to add","adding","having added","to have added"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":2,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Minah apologised __________ arriving late for the family dinner of her best friend.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Rule: some verbs always go with the same preposition. We apologise **to** a person **for** something we did.","The thing Minah did wrong was 'arriving late', so the preposition is 'for': 'apologised for arriving late'.","'about' is tempting because we say 'sorry about', but with the verb 'apologise' the correct word is 'for'.","The answer is option (3), for."]}],"options":["about","over","for","at"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q3","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 3","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":3,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The lifeguards continued searching for the missing swimmer in the sea __________ night had already fallen.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Work out how the two halves are linked. Night falling makes a search harder, yet the lifeguards carried on. The second half is a surprise, a contrast.","'though' (like 'although') joins two ideas that contrast: they continued **though** night had fallen.","'since' and 'because' give a reason, but darkness is not a reason to keep searching. 'unless' gives a condition (if not), which does not fit a past event that had already happened.","The answer is option (3), though."]}],"options":["since","unless","though","because"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q4","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 4","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":4,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["quantifiers"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"There was very __________ evidence to support the claim, so the police continued their investigations.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Rule: 'evidence' is an uncountable noun (we cannot say 'two evidences'). Uncountable nouns take 'little' or 'much'. Countable nouns take 'few' or 'several'.","That rules out 'few' and 'several'. The verb 'was' (singular) is another clue that the noun is uncountable.","Now use the meaning. The police **continued** investigating, so they did not have enough evidence yet. 'very little evidence' means almost none. 'very much evidence' would mean they had plenty, and it is also not natural English in a positive sentence.","The answer is option (2), little."]}],"options":["few","little","much","several"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q5","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 5","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":5,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["question-tags"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Everyone on the debate team has submitted the speech outline, __________?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Rule: a positive statement takes a negative tag, and the tag repeats the helping verb of the statement.","The helping verb is 'has' in 'has submitted' (present perfect), so the tag must use have/has. That rules out 'don't', 'didn't' and 'hadn't' (past perfect).","Special rule: 'everyone' takes a singular verb in the statement ('has'), but in the tag we use the pronoun 'they', and 'they' needs 'have'. So the tag is 'haven't they'.","'Everyone ... has submitted the speech outline, haven't they?' The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["don't they","didn't they","hadn't they","haven't they"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q6","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 6","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":6,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["conditionals"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"If Ahmad had checked the noticeboard, he __________ where to go for his Art class.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["The 'if' part uses 'had checked' (past perfect). This is about something in the past that did **not** happen: Ahmad did not check the noticeboard.","Rule (third conditional): If + had + past participle, ... would have + past participle.","So the result part is 'would have known'. 'would know' is tempting, but it belongs with 'If Ahmad checked...' (second conditional).","'If Ahmad had checked the noticeboard, he would have known where to go.' The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["knew","had known","would know","would have known"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q7","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 7","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":7,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The description of the suspect by the witness was consistent __________ the evidence collected by the police.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Rule: some adjectives always go with the same preposition. 'consistent' goes with 'with'.","'consistent with' means agreeing with or matching. The witness's description matched the evidence.","'to' is tempting because we say 'similar to', but we say 'consistent with', just like 'agree with'.","The answer is option (3), with."]}],"options":["to","by","with","from"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q8","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 8","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":8,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["subject-verb-agreement"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Currently, neither the twins nor their younger brother __________ willing to participate in the competition.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Rule: with 'neither ... nor ...', the verb agrees with the subject nearest to it.","The nearest subject is 'their younger brother', which is one person, so the verb is singular. That rules out 'are' and 'were'.","'Currently' means now, so we need the present tense: 'is', not 'was'.","'are' is tempting because of 'the twins', but the twins are the subject further from the verb. The answer is option (1), is."]}],"options":["is","are","was","were"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q9","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 9","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":9,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The workers __________ the beach since morning, so there is hardly any litter on the ground.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Clues: 'since morning' tells us the action started in the past and has gone on up to now. 'there **is** hardly any litter' shows the result is in the present.","Rule: for an action that began in the past and has continued over a period up to now, use the present perfect continuous: have/has + been + verb-ing.","'had been cleaning' is the past perfect continuous; it needs another past event to come before, but the result here is in the present ('is'). 'cleaned' (simple past) cannot be used with 'since'.","'have cleaned' is tempting, but with 'since morning' the stress is on the activity going on all that time, so 'have been cleaning' is the best answer. The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["cleaned","have cleaned","had been cleaning","have been cleaning"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q10","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 10","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":10,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["relative-pronouns"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The restaurant __________ chefs create award-winning desserts is often very crowded.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Look at the word after the blank: 'chefs'. The chefs belong to the restaurant (the restaurant's chefs).","Rule: 'whose' shows belonging, and it is followed by a noun. It can be used for things as well as people.","'which' is tempting because a restaurant is a thing, but 'which chefs create' does not make sense: 'which' would have to be the subject or object of 'create', and 'chefs' already is the subject.","'The restaurant whose chefs create award-winning desserts...' The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["who","which","whom","whose"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q11","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 11","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":11,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning","collocation"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Despite the heavy downpour, the volunteers remained __________ in their efforts to distribute supplies to the needy.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Clue: 'Despite the heavy downpour' tells us the volunteers did not give up. We need a word for people who stay firm and determined.","'steadfast' means firm and not changing in what you do or believe. 'remained steadfast in their efforts' is a common phrase.","'unfaltering' is tempting because it has almost the same meaning, but it normally describes a thing (unfaltering support, unfaltering courage), not a person 'in' their efforts. 'immovable' is for something that cannot be physically moved or a person who refuses to change their opinion. 'hesitant' is the opposite idea.","The answer is option (2), steadfast."]}],"options":["hesitant","steadfast","unfaltering","immovable"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q12","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 12","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":12,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Jane smiled warmly and nodded __________ as her new classmate shared on her difficulty in making friends in class.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Clues: Jane 'smiled warmly', and her classmate was sharing a problem. Jane was being kind.","'reassuringly' means in a way that makes someone feel less worried. A warm smile and a reassuring nod go together.","'casually' means in a relaxed way without much care, which does not show kindness. 'reluctantly' (unwillingly) and 'impatiently' do not match a warm smile.","The answer is option (4), reassuringly."]}],"options":["casually","reluctantly","impatiently","reassuringly"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q13","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 13","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":13,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Although his teammates encouraged him, Ryan continued to __________ his own abilities before the competition.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Clue: 'Although' signals a contrast. His teammates encouraged him, yet Ryan still did not feel confident.","To 'question' your abilities means to doubt them, to be unsure whether you are good enough.","'dismiss' is tempting, but to dismiss something is to decide it is not worth thinking about; we do not usually 'dismiss our abilities'. 'prove' and 'accept' are positive, so they do not contrast with being encouraged.","The answer is option (4), question."]}],"options":["prove","accept","dismiss","question"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q14","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 14","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":14,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Clear instructions on medicine labels ensure correct __________ of medicine.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["The instructions on a medicine label tell you how much to take and when. We need a word for the giving or taking of medicine.","'administration' of medicine means the act of giving medicine to someone in the right amount and way. (To 'administer' medicine is to give it.)","'supervision' means watching over someone, and 'organisation' means arranging things neatly; labels are not about those. 'digestion' is how the body breaks down food.","The answer is option (4), administration."]}],"options":["digestion","supervision","organisation","administration"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q15","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 15","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":15,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Even though the task was not easy, Lily did not __________ from the challenge.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Look at the word after the blank: 'from'. The phrasal verb must fit with 'from the challenge'.","'back down from' something means to give in or withdraw because it is too hard or frightening. Lily did **not** back down, so she faced the challenge.","'give up' is tempting because the meaning is close, but we say 'give up the challenge' or 'give up on', not 'give up from'. 'pull back' is usually about moving away or troops retreating, and 'turn off' means to switch off.","The answer is option (1), back down."]}],"options":["back down","pull back","turn off","give up"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q16","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 16","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":16,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word(s) closest in meaning to ++a deep sense of embarrassment++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Amir 'felt a deep sense of embarrassment' because he had missed the winning penalty in front of his teammates.","To feel deeply embarrassed about something you did is to feel 'ashamed'.","'awkward' is tempting, but it is a much milder feeling of being uncomfortable; it does not match 'deep'. 'frustrated' means annoyed at not being able to do something, and 'disappointed' means sad that things did not turn out well. Neither is about embarrassment.","The answer is option (1), ashamed."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g1","options":["ashamed","awkward","frustrated","disappointed"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q17","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 17","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":17,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning","idioms"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word(s) closest in meaning to ++catch him off guard++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["To 'catch someone off guard' is to surprise them when they are not ready.","Clue: Amir 'was deep in thought', so someone appearing suddenly would give him a shock. That is why Kai 'walked over quietly' and 'spoke in a calm tone'.","'startle' means to give someone a sudden surprise or shock, which matches. 'distract him' is tempting because Amir was thinking, but being caught off guard is about surprise, not about losing attention.","The answer is option (2), startle him."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g1","options":["worry him","startle him","distract him","confuse him"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q18","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 18","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":18,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word(s) closest in meaning to ++allay++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["To 'allay' worries or fears is to make them less strong.","Kai spoke calmly and told Amir nobody blamed him. He was trying to make Amir's worries smaller.","'ease' means to make something less painful or serious, and we say 'ease someone's worries'. 'pacify' and 'soothe' are tempting, but we pacify or soothe a **person** (or a baby), not worries. 'resolve' means to solve completely, which is more than 'allay'.","The answer is option (1), ease."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g1","options":["ease","pacify","soothe","resolve"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q19","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 19","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":19,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word(s) closest in meaning to ++deeply++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'deeply touched' means Amir's feelings were moved in a strong and sincere way by Kai's kindness.","'genuinely' means truly and sincerely. 'genuinely touched' keeps the same meaning.","'seriously' is tempting because it can mean 'very', but it is used for bad or grave things (seriously hurt, seriously ill), not for warm feelings. 'fairly' means only quite, which is too weak.","The answer is option (4), genuinely."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g1","options":["fairly","easily","seriously","genuinely"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q20","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 20","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":20,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning","phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word(s) closest in meaning to ++stand by++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["To 'stand by' someone is to stay loyal to them and help them when things are hard.","Clue: 'true friends stand by one another during difficult times'. Kai stayed with Amir and comforted him.","'support' means to help and encourage someone, which matches. 'defend' and 'protect' are tempting, but they mean keeping someone safe from an attack, and nobody was attacking Amir. 'motivate' means to make someone want to do something.","The answer is option (3), support."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g1","options":["defend","protect","support","motivate"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q21","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 21","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":21,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The slogan \"Carry Everything in Style\" suggests that the Peaks school bag is both __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Break the slogan into two halves and match each half to one word.","'Carry Everything' tells us the bag does its job well: it holds all you need. That is 'useful'.","'in Style' tells us the bag looks good. That is 'fashionable'.","'large and expensive' is tempting because a bag that carries everything is large, but nothing in the slogan is about price. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g2","options":["large and expensive","useful and fashionable","colourful and comfortable","strong and easy to clean"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q22","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 22","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":22,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Why was Ansel featured in Text 1?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Ansel's words are a testimonial: a real user saying good things about a product. Advertisers use testimonials to make their claims more believable.","Compare his words with the poster. Poster: 'Fit your entire school day into one bag'. Ansel: 'Everything I need for school fits comfortably into it.' Poster: 'a specific space for everything'. Ansel: 'I can also find and take out any item I need easily!'","His comments repeat and back up what the poster claims. Option (2) is tempting, but the poster says the bag is 'Suitable for students of all ages', so his age is not the reason.","The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g2","options":["He appears to be a good student.","Most school bag users are around his age.","His comments support the claims of the poster.","His review was short enough to fit into the poster."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q23","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 23","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":23,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Based on Text 1, which of the following statements are true about the Peaks school bag?\nA: The bag comes in different colours.\nB: The bag helps students organise their belongings\nC: The bag is designed to reduce pressure on students' backs.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Check each statement against the poster (Text 1) only.","A is true: 'Wide range of colours to choose from!'","B is true: 'Get organised with a specific space for everything! No more mess or things left behind!'","C is not in Text 1. Reducing pressure on the back is mentioned only in Text 2, and it is about school bags in general. The question says 'Based on Text 1'. The answer is option (1), A and B."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g2","options":["A and B","A and C","B and C","A, B and C"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q24","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 24","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":24,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"What is the main focus of Text 2?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["The main focus is the idea that most of the text builds up to, not a small detail.","Paragraph 2 warns that 'Carrying too many items strains your back.' The last paragraph begins with 'Ultimately' (the most important point): 'health experts recommend packing only the items needed for the day' and 'leaving out unnecessary items'.","Option (4) is tempting, but wide straps and padded backs are only one way to 'cushion the effect'. Option (3) says 'all' materials, but the text says 'certain items' and 'some materials'.","The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g2","options":["Choose a school bag that looks attractive.","Avoid carrying too many unnecessary items.","Keep all school materials in lockers or under desks.","Buy a school bag with wide straps and padded backs."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-a-q25","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet A Question 25","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"a","number":25,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Text 1 and Text 2 express differing views on whether __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Look for a point where the two texts say opposite things.","Text 1 encourages it: 'Fit your entire school day into one bag, all your books, stationery, lunch boxes and more!'","Text 2 warns against it: 'While it may seem exciting to fit everything into one bag, have you considered the weight?' and recommends 'packing only the items needed for the day'.","Option (1) is not a disagreement: both texts accept that bags look good. Option (2) is about whether bags **can** fit the books, which Text 2 does not deny. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g2","options":["school bags should look appealing to students","school bags can fit all books needed for school","students should fit everything into their school bags","students can keep their belongings in school lockers"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q26","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 26","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":26,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (26). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"F","accept":["f","have"]},"explanation":["After the blank is 'introduced', a past participle. We need a helping verb in front of it.","The subject 'More neighbourhoods' is plural, and the first sentence is in the present ('are becoming'), so we use the present perfect: have + introduced.","'had' (E) is tempting, but the past perfect is for something that happened before another past event, and there is no other past event here.","The answer is (F) have."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q27","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 27","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":27,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (27). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Q","accept":["q","unlike"]},"explanation":["The sentence compares book-sharing corners with traditional libraries and shows a difference: the corners are 'less structured and have fewer rules'.","'Unlike' means 'different from' and is followed by a noun: Unlike traditional libraries, these corners ...","The answer is (Q) unlike."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q28","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 28","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":28,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (28). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"J","accept":["j","or"]},"explanation":["The sentence lists things the corners do **not** do: 'They do not need membership cards and borrowing records, ___ have fixed return dates'.","Rule: after a negative ('do not'), we join another thing that is also not true with 'or', not 'and'.","'They do not need membership cards ..., or have fixed return dates' means they do not have fixed return dates either.","The answer is (J) or."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q29","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 29","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":29,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (29). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"D","accept":["d","each"]},"explanation":["Look at the words after the blank: 'corner has its own selection'. 'corner' is singular and the verb 'has' is singular.","'each' is used before a singular noun to talk about every one separately, and it goes well with 'its own'.","'all' and 'both' need a plural noun (all corners have). 'one' (H) is tempting, but the sentence is about every corner, not just one of them.","The answer is (D) each."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q30","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 30","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":30,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (30). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"H","accept":["h","one"]},"explanation":["'Readers can take a book home or leave ___ behind for another reader.' The blank stands for 'a book'.","The pronoun 'one' replaces 'a' + singular noun, so we do not have to repeat 'a book'.","The answer is (H) one."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q31","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 31","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":31,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (31). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"B","accept":["b","at"]},"explanation":["'___ their own pace' is a fixed phrase.","We say 'at your own pace', meaning as fast or as slowly as you like. 'at' is used with speeds and rates (at high speed, at a slow pace).","The answer is (B) at."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q32","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 32","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":32,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (32). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"K","accept":["k","over"]},"explanation":["'keeping the collection varied ___ time'. We need a word that goes with 'time' to mean 'as time passes'.","'over time' is a fixed phrase meaning gradually, during a long period.","'through' (P) is tempting, but 'through time' is not the usual phrase, and 'through' is needed for blank (35).","The answer is (K) over."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q33","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 33","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":33,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (33). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"L","accept":["l","than"]},"explanation":["Look at the word before the blank: 'more'. A comparison with 'more' is completed by 'than'.","'more than just a way to reduce waste' means it is that and something extra as well.","The answer is (L) than."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q34","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 34","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":34,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (34). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"M","accept":["m","that"]},"explanation":["'It is a simple idea ___ brings neighbours together'. The blank joins 'a simple idea' to more information about it and is the subject of 'brings'.","For a thing, the relative pronoun is 'that' (or 'which'). Only 'that' is in the list.","The answer is (M) that."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q35","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 35","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":35,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (35). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"P","accept":["p","through"]},"explanation":["'___ talking about the stories they have enjoyed, they get to know one another'. The first part tells us **how** they get to know one another.","'Through' + verb-ing means 'by means of': Through talking about the stories, they get to know one another.","'then' (N) is tempting at the start of a sentence, but 'Then talking about the stories, they get to know...' is not a proper sentence.","The answer is (P) through."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q36","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 36","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":36,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (36) ++steemulating++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"stimulating"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means making you interested and excited.","Say it in syllables: stim-u-lat-ing. The first sound is a short 'i' (as in 'still'), spelt with 'i', not 'ee'.","It comes from 'stimulate': drop the 'e' and add '-ing'.","The correct word is 'stimulating'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q37","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 37","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":37,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (37) ++and++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"but"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. Look earlier in the sentence: 'activities that **not only** encouraged students to interact ...'.","Rule: 'not only' is always paired with 'but also': not only A but also B.","The word 'also' is already there, so 'and' must be changed to 'but'.","The correct word is 'but'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q38","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 38","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":38,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (38) ++away++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"aside"},"explanation":["This is a grammar (wrong word) error in a phrasal verb.","To 'set aside' time means to keep some time for a special purpose. Here, time during recess was kept for playing or talking with friends.","'set away' is not an English phrasal verb. Do not confuse it with 'put away' (to tidy something into its place).","The correct word is 'aside'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q39","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 39","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":39,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (39) ++fermilier++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"familiar"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means used to something, knowing it well.","Say it in syllables: fa-mil-iar. It is related to 'family', so it starts with 'fam'.","The ending is '-iar' (as in 'peculiar'), not '-ier'.","The correct word is 'familiar'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q40","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 40","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":40,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (40) ++relyant++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"reliant"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. 'reliant on' means depending on.","It comes from 'rely'. Rule: when a word ends in a consonant + 'y', change the 'y' to 'i' before adding an ending: rely → reliant, reliable, relies.","The correct word is 'reliant'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q41","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 41","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":41,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (41) ++by++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"from","accept":["through"]},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong preposition.","We **gain** something **from** an experience: 'gained confidence from the activities and discussions'. 'through' (by means of) is also correct.","'by' is used for the doer in a passive sentence (was written by him), which is not the case here.","The correct word is 'from' ('through' is also accepted)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q42","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 42","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":42,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (42) ++learnt++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"learning"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. Read to the end of the sentence: '..., ___ to identify new ways ... **and developing** greater curiosity'.","'and' joins two matching forms. 'developing' is not underlined, so it is correct, and the word before 'and' must match it: an -ing word.","The two -ing phrases describe how the students gained confidence: 'learning to identify new ways ... and developing greater curiosity'.","The correct word is 'learning'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q43","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 43","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":43,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (43) ++curious++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"curiosity"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong word form.","'greater' is an adjective, so it must be followed by a noun: 'developing greater ___ about trying new pastimes'. 'curious' is an adjective.","The noun is 'curiosity'. Watch the spelling: the 'u' in 'curious' is dropped (curi-os-ity).","The correct word is 'curiosity'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q44","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 44","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":44,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (44) ++akomplishment++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"accomplishment"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means something good that has been achieved with effort.","Say it in syllables: ac-com-plish-ment. The 'k' sound is spelt with a double 'c', as in 'accompany' and 'account'.","The correct word is 'accomplishment'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q45","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 45","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":45,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (45) ++ristraynt++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"restraint"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. 'restraint' means self-control, holding yourself back.","It comes from the verb 'restrain' (re + strain). It begins with 're', and the long 'a' sound is spelt 'ai'.","Add 't' to make the noun: restrain → restraint.","The correct word is 'restraint'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q46","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 46","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":46,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (46) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"aim","accept":["purpose","objective","goal","target"]},"explanation":["'With the ___ of planting one million more trees across the island by 2030'. The blank names what the movement wants to achieve.","'with the aim of' + verb-ing is a set phrase meaning 'in order to'.","'goal', 'purpose', 'objective' and 'target' carry the same meaning and are accepted.","The answer is 'aim'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q47","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 47","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":47,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","collocation"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (47) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"commitment"},"explanation":["Look at the words around the blank: 'Singapore's long-term ___ **to becoming** a City in Nature'.","We need a noun that is followed by 'to' + verb-ing and means a firm promise to keep working at something. That is 'commitment to'.","'long-term commitment' is a common word pair.","The answer is 'commitment'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q48","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 48","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":48,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (48) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"places","accept":["locations","spaces","areas","sites"]},"explanation":["'Parks, nature reserves and park connectors are common ___ where trees are planted'.","'where' tells us the blank is a word for a place. It must be plural to match 'are' and the three examples.","'places', 'locations', 'spaces', 'areas' and 'sites' all fit.","The answer is 'places'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q49","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 49","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":49,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (49) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"more"},"explanation":["Look back at the first paragraph: the aim is 'planting one million **more** trees'.","'By planting ___ trees, Singapore hopes to create a greener ... environment'. Greener means adding to the trees already there, so 'more trees'.","The answer is 'more'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q50","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 50","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":50,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","collocation"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (50) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"future"},"explanation":["'a greener and more sustainable environment for ___ generations'.","'Sustainable' means able to last a long time, so the environment is being protected for people who will live later: 'future generations' is the set phrase.","The answer is 'future'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q51","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 51","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":51,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","collocation"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (51) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"quality"},"explanation":["'They absorb carbon dioxide which helps to regulate temperatures and improve air ___.'","When trees take in carbon dioxide, the air becomes cleaner. How clean the air is, is called 'air quality'. We 'improve' quality.","'pollution' does not fit because we reduce pollution, not improve it.","The answer is 'quality'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q52","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 52","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":52,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (52) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"provide","accept":["offer"]},"explanation":["'Their branches and leaves also ___ food and shelter for wildlife.' We need a verb meaning 'give'.","The subject 'branches and leaves' is plural and the paragraph is in the present tense, so the verb has no 's': 'provide'.","'provide ... for' is a common pattern: provide food and shelter for wildlife. 'offer' also fits.","The answer is 'provide'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q53","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 53","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":53,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (53) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"move","accept":["wander","travel","roam"]},"explanation":["'trees allow animals to ___ freely between parks and nature reserves'.","After 'allow animals to' we need the base form of a verb. 'between parks and nature reserves' shows the animals go from one place to another.","'move' fits. 'wander', 'travel' and 'roam' mean the same here.","The answer is 'move'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q54","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 54","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":54,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (54) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"humans","accept":["people","residents"]},"explanation":["'As a result, **both** wildlife **and** ___ benefit from a healthier environment.'","'both ... and ...' joins two groups. The paragraph has talked about animals; cleaner air and cooler temperatures also help people.","The verb 'benefit' has no 's', so the noun is plural: 'humans' or 'people'.","The answer is 'humans'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q55","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 55","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":55,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","collocation"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (55) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"public","accept":["community"]},"explanation":["'Members of the ___ can contribute by taking part in educational programmes'.","The sentence before talks about 'community participation'. Ordinary people are called 'members of the public'. 'members of the community' also fits.","The answer is 'public'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q56","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 56","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":56,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (56) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"plants","accept":["trees","saplings","seedlings"]},"explanation":["'small nurseries where young ___ are carefully grown before being planted in gardens'.","A nursery is a place where young plants are grown. The verb 'are' shows the noun is plural.","'plants' fits. 'trees', 'saplings' (young trees) and 'seedlings' also fit.","The answer is 'plants'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q57","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 57","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":57,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","collocation"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (57) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"experience","accept":["skills","knowledge"]},"explanation":["'Through these hands-on activities, students gain practical ___ in caring for plants'.","'hands-on' means learning by doing. What you get from doing something yourself is 'practical experience'. We 'gain experience in' doing something.","'skills' and 'knowledge' also go with 'gain practical ... in'.","The answer is 'experience'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q58","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 58","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":58,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (58) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"grow","accept":["mature","flourish","thrive"]},"explanation":["'As they watch the trees ___ and attract different kinds of wildlife'.","Rule: after 'watch' + object, use the base form of the verb (watch the trees grow), and it must match 'attract'.","The trees were planted young, so over time they 'grow'.","The answer is 'grow'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q59","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 59","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":59,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary","collocation"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (59) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"deeper","accept":["greater","deep","stronger"]},"explanation":["'they develop a ___ appreciation of nature'. We need an adjective that goes with 'appreciation'.","After watching the trees grow, the students value nature more than before, so a comparative fits: 'a deeper appreciation' or 'a greater appreciation'.","The answer is 'deeper'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q60","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 60","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":60,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (60) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"important","accept":["necessary","crucial","essential","vital"]},"explanation":["'... and understand why conserving green spaces is ___.' We need an adjective after 'is'.","The whole passage shows how useful trees are, so the students see that looking after green spaces matters.","'important' fits. 'necessary', 'crucial', 'essential' and 'vital' mean the same.","The answer is 'important'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q61","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 61","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":61,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["unless"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"If you charge the battery fully, your phone will not run out of power.\n____________________ unless ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Your phone will run out of power unless you charge the battery fully.","accept":["Your phone will run out of power unless you charge the battery fully"]},"explanation":["'unless' means 'if ... not'. It already carries a 'not', so one of the two halves must switch between positive and negative.","The given sentence says: charge fully → will **not** run out. With 'unless' in the middle we say what happens if you do not charge it: the phone **will** run out of power.","Keep the 'unless' part positive: 'unless you charge the battery fully'. Do not write 'unless you do not charge'.","Answer: Your phone will run out of power unless you charge the battery fully."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q62","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 62","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":62,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["noun-form"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The match was cancelled as the weather was bad.\nOwing to ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Owing to the bad weather, the match was cancelled.","accept":["Owing to the bad weather the match was cancelled.","Owing to bad weather, the match was cancelled."]},"explanation":["'Owing to' means 'because of'. It is a preposition, so it must be followed by a noun phrase, not a clause with a verb.","Change the clause 'the weather was bad' into a noun phrase: 'the bad weather'.","Put a comma after the opening phrase, then write the result in full: 'the match was cancelled'.","Answer: Owing to the bad weather, the match was cancelled."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q63","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 63","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":63,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["despite-in-spite-of"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"He spoke to her politely although he was upset.\n____________________ in spite of ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"He spoke to her politely in spite of being upset.","accept":["He spoke to her politely in spite of the fact that he was upset.","He spoke to her politely in spite of his being upset.","He spoke to her politely in spite of feeling upset."]},"explanation":["'in spite of' shows contrast like 'although', but it is a preposition. It must be followed by a noun or a verb-ing, not by a subject and verb.","Change 'he was upset' to the -ing form: 'being upset'. Drop 'he was'.","Keep the order of the given sentence because 'in spite of' is in the middle: He spoke to her politely in spite of ...","Another correct way is to add 'the fact that' and keep the clause: 'in spite of the fact that he was upset'. Answer: He spoke to her politely in spite of being upset."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q64","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 64","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":64,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["reported-speech"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"\"How do you plan to get home after the party?\" Uncle Joe asked his daughter.\nUncle Joe asked his daughter how ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Uncle Joe asked his daughter how she planned to get home after the party.","accept":["Uncle Joe asked his daughter how she planned to get home after the party"]},"explanation":["In reported speech a question becomes a statement: the subject comes before the verb, 'do' is dropped, and there is no question mark.","Change the pronoun: 'you' is his daughter, so it becomes 'she'.","Move the tense one step back because the reporting verb 'asked' is in the past: 'do you plan' becomes 'she planned'.","Answer: Uncle Joe asked his daughter how she planned to get home after the party."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q65","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 65","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":65,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["noun-form"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Tim was able to complete the task because his sister encouraged him.\nWith ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"With his sister's encouragement, Tim was able to complete the task.","accept":["With the encouragement of his sister, Tim was able to complete the task.","With his sister's encouragement Tim was able to complete the task.","With encouragement from his sister, Tim was able to complete the task."]},"explanation":["'With' is a preposition, so it must be followed by a noun phrase, not a clause.","Change the verb 'encouraged' into its noun form, 'encouragement', and show whose it is: 'his sister's encouragement' (remember the apostrophe).","Put a comma after the opening phrase. Then name the person in the main clause: 'Tim', not 'he', because Tim has not been mentioned yet.","Answer: With his sister's encouragement, Tim was able to complete the task."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q66","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 66","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":66,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"structured","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"In paragraph one, why did Farid and Lina hurry to their favourite play corner with lightning speed?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"They were excited to play at their favourite play corner."},"explanation":["Paragraph 1 opens with the mood in the room: 'The kindergarten classroom hummed with excitement as children streamed towards the various play corners.'","Farid and Lina were part of that excitement, and the corner was their 'favourite', so they could not wait to play there.","The marker looks for the feeling (excited / eager) and what it was about (playing at their favourite play corner)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q67","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 67","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":67,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"structured","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Why did Farid take a bow after Lina handed him the apron?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"He wanted to thank Lina for handing him the apron."},"explanation":["Find the sentence in paragraph 2: 'As usual, Lina handed Farid a tiny apron. With a grin, he took a bow and put on the apron.'","The bow comes straight after he receives the apron, and he is grinning, so it is a friendly way of saying 'thank you'.","He used an action instead of words because Lina had hearing difficulties (stated later in the paragraph).","The marker looks for: to thank Lina / to show his appreciation for the apron."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q68","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 68","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":68,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Why did Farid pretend to put on a chef's top hat instead of telling Lina he was the chef?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Farid knew that Lina had hearing difficulties, so he gestured to help her understand that he was pretending to be the chef."},"explanation":["The reason is given a little later in paragraph 2: 'Not many children wanted to play with someone who had hearing difficulties.' The 'someone' is Lina.","Point 1 (1 mark): Lina had hearing difficulties, so she might not hear him if he spoke.","Point 2 (1 mark): by acting it out ('raising his hands as if he were putting on a tall chef's hat'), he helped her understand that he was the chef.","A 2-mark 'why' question needs both the cause (her hearing difficulties) and what the action achieved (she could understand him)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q69","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 69","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":69,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["evidence-phrase","character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on lines 11 to 14 (paragraph 3), provide two pieces of evidence that suggest that Lina was upset when the food tumbled onto the floor.","parts":[{"label":"i","prompt":"First piece of evidence:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Lina teared up."},"explanation":["Look at the last sentence of paragraph 3: Farid realised 'that Lina was tearing up and her shoulders were stiffening'.","'tearing up' means her eyes were filling with tears, which shows she was upset.","Write it as a full sentence about Lina: Lina teared up / Lina was tearing up. The two pieces of evidence can be given in either order."]},{"label":"ii","prompt":"Second piece of evidence:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Lina's shoulders stiffened."},"explanation":["The same sentence gives a second sign: 'her shoulders were stiffening'.","When a person is upset or tense, the body goes stiff.","Do not use 'Farid let out a short laugh'; that is about Farid, not evidence of Lina's feelings."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q70","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 70","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":70,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["character-and-feelings","evidence-phrase"],"format":"structured","marks":4,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on the given information, fill in the following table.\n(For each situation, write Lina's feeling, then the three-word phrase from the passage that shows it.)","parts":[{"label":"ai","prompt":"Situation (a): When Lina realised that she had dropped the toy food on the floor (paragraph 4).\nLina's feeling:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Shocked"},"explanation":["In paragraph 4, Farid tapped Lina's shoulder and made her turn around. That was the moment she saw the toy food on the floor.","The passage says 'she gasped softly'. A gasp is a sudden breath we take when we are shocked or surprised.","Accepted feelings: shocked / surprised / startled. 'Relieved' is wrong here; her expression softened only afterwards, when Farid showed her how to scoop the food up."]},{"label":"aii","prompt":"Situation (a): When Lina realised that she had dropped the toy food on the floor (paragraph 4).\nThree-word phrase from the passage:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"text","value":"she gasped softly","accept":["as she gasped softly"]},"explanation":["Find the words in paragraph 4 that show shock: 'As she gasped softly, he made a deliberate scooping motion with his hands.'","Count exactly three words and copy them without changing anything: she (1) gasped (2) softly (3).","The answer is 'she gasped softly'."]},{"label":"bi","prompt":"Situation (b): When Lina saw everyone moving quickly (paragraph 7).\nLina's feeling:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Confused"},"explanation":["In paragraph 7, Lina 'looked across the room' and the writer gives her thought: 'Why was everyone moving so quickly?'","A question like that shows she did not understand what was going on, so she was confused (puzzled).","The feeling must match the phrase you choose in the next blank."]},{"label":"bii","prompt":"Situation (b): When Lina saw everyone moving quickly (paragraph 7).\nThree-word phrase from the passage:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"text","value":"her brows furrowing"},"explanation":["Find the words in paragraph 7 that show confusion: 'Lina glanced about uneasily, **her brows furrowing** as she looked across the room.'","To furrow your brows is to frown so that lines appear on your forehead, which people do when they are puzzled.","Count exactly three words: her (1) brows (2) furrowing (3). The answer is 'her brows furrowing'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q71","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 71","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":71,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["referencing"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Look at the table below. What do the words or phrases in the left column refer to in the passage? Write your answers in the column on the right.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"movement (line 17, paragraph 4)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Farid's scooping motion (with his hands)"},"explanation":["For a 'refers to' question, read the sentence just before the word.","Sentence before: 'he made a deliberate scooping motion with his hands.' Then: 'Lina's gaze followed **his movement**'.","'his movement' is that scooping motion. Say whose it was: Farid's scooping motion with his hands."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"the sudden disruption (line 24, paragraph 6)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The children tidying up / hurrying to return the toys to their shelves"},"explanation":["Read the sentence just before the phrase: 'Chairs scraped against the floor as children hurried to return toys to their shelves.'","That noisy rush of activity is 'the sudden disruption behind her' which Lina did not notice.","The marker looks for: the children tidying up / returning the toys to their shelves."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q72","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 72","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":72,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["true-false-with-reason","inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Read the statement below.\n**Lina wanted to finish feeding the cartoon character before tidying up the toys.**\nDo you agree with the statement? Yes / No (Tick one box only)\nSupport your answer with evidence from the passage, excluding Lina's hearing disability.\n(Write Yes or No, then give two pieces of evidence, (i) and (ii).)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"No.\n(i) Lina could not see what the other children were doing because her back was turned.\n(ii) Lina did not realise that tidy-up time had begun."},"explanation":["The statement says Lina **chose** to carry on playing. Check whether she even knew it was time to tidy up.","Evidence (i): the tidying was happening 'behind her', and she was facing 'the wall poster in front of her', so she could not see the other children returning the toys.","Evidence (ii): she was 'oblivious to the sudden disruption' and later wondered, 'Why was everyone moving so quickly?' She did not realise that tidy-up time had begun.","So the answer is No: she did not carry on because she wanted to finish first; she simply did not know. Do not use her hearing difficulty as evidence, because the question excludes it."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q73","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 73","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":73,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","vocabulary-in-context"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"What does the phrase **\"Realisation flickered across her features\"** (line 34, paragraph 9) tell you about Lina's understanding at that moment?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Lina suddenly understood what Farid was trying to tell her and realised that it was time to tidy up."},"explanation":["'Realisation' means suddenly understanding something. 'flickered across her features' means it showed quickly on her face, like a light coming on.","Point 1 (1 mark): at that moment Lina suddenly understood what Farid was telling her with his hands. Just before this, he 'swept his hands together and pointed towards the toy basket', and she 'studied his face and hands'.","Point 2 (1 mark): say what she understood: it was time to tidy up. Evidence that follows: 'her tense shoulders relaxed' and she 'followed suit'.","Answer with both: she suddenly understood Farid's gestures and realised it was time to tidy up."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q74","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 74","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":74,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"At the end of the passage, Lina pressed her hand against her chest before extending it towards Farid. What did this gesture show? Why?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The gesture showed that Lina was thankful to Farid. She wanted to thank him for helping her understand that it was time to tidy up."},"explanation":["There are two questions, so give two parts: what the gesture showed (1 mark) and why (1 mark).","What: a hand on the heart, then held out to someone, is a way of saying 'thank you' without words. The passage calls it 'Lina's grateful smile', and earlier 'A grateful smile spread across Lina's face.' So it showed she was thankful (grateful) to Farid.","Why: Farid had noticed what she had missed and used gestures to help her understand that it was time to tidy up (he also called classmates over to help).","Answer: it showed Lina was thankful to Farid, because he had helped her understand that it was time to tidy up."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-b-q75","cite":"2026 Prelim Red Swastika Paper 2 Booklet B Question 75","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Red Swastika","section":"b","number":75,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"What does the writer mean by \"Lina's grateful smile and Farid's quiet nod said more than words ever could\" (lines 40 – 41, paragraph 10)?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Lina and Farid understood each other without needing to speak."},"explanation":["The sentence before says 'Neither of them spoke.' So the smile and the nod were doing the talking.","Point 1: they did not need to speak. Lina's smile told Farid she was thankful, and his nod told her he understood.","Point 2: 'more than words ever could' means these actions showed their feelings and their friendship more strongly than talking would have.","Answer: Lina and Farid understood each other (her thanks and his acceptance) without needing to speak."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":1,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["tenses"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"I __________ this movie several times but I still find it enjoyable each time I do.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clue: 'several times'. The watching happened a number of times at some point before now, and it still matters now.","Rule: use the present perfect (have + past participle) for actions completed before now when the exact time is not given, especially with 'several times', 'twice', 'before'.","'have been watching' is tempting, but the continuous form is for one activity that is still going on, not for a counted number of finished times.","'I have watched this movie several times...' The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["watch","am watching","have watched","have been watching"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":2,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"__________ among the passengers witnessed the accident and reported it?\" the reporter asked the police.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["The blank is the subject of the verbs 'witnessed' and 'reported', and it stands for a person (one of the passengers).","Rule: use 'Who' to ask about a person who does the action. 'Whom' is for the person who receives the action (Whom did you see?).","'Which' is tempting because there is a group to choose from, but it normally comes with a noun or 'of' (Which passenger...? Which of the passengers...?). 'What' is for things.","'Who among the passengers witnessed the accident...?' The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["Who","What","Which","Whom"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q3","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 3","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":3,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["subject-verb-agreement"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Neither of the twins __________ sleepy. They are still playing in their room.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Rule: 'Neither of' + plural noun takes a singular verb, because it means 'not one and not the other'.","That rules out 'are' and 'were'.","Tense clue: the next sentence is in the present ('They are still playing'), so we need the present 'is', not 'was'.","'Neither of the twins is sleepy.' The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["is","are","was","were"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q4","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 4","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":4,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The coach attributed the team's success __________ their consistent effort and dedication.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Some verbs go with a fixed preposition. We attribute something **to** its cause.","'attribute A to B' means to say that A happened because of B. Example: 'She attributed her good results to hard work.'","'by' is tempting because we think 'caused by', but 'attributed by' is wrong.","'...attributed the team's success to their consistent effort...' The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["to","by","on","with"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q5","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 5","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":5,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["question-tags"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Hardly anyone in class knew the answer to this question, __________?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Rule: a negative statement takes a positive question tag.","'Hardly' means 'almost no', so 'Hardly anyone ... knew' counts as a negative statement, even though there is no 'not'. The tag must be positive, which rules out 'didn't they' and 'hadn't they'.","'knew' is the simple past, so the helping verb in the tag is 'did', not 'had'. 'anyone' becomes 'they' in a tag.","'Hardly anyone in class knew the answer..., did they?' The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["did they","had they","didn't they","hadn't they"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q6","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 6","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":6,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"__________ the ongoing construction work at the school field, the football match was postponed.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Work out how the two halves are linked: the construction work is the reason, and postponing the match is the action taken because of it.","'In response to' means 'as a reaction to'. The match was postponed as a reaction to the construction work.","'In spite of' shows contrast and would fit only if the match had gone on anyway. 'In the event of' means 'if it happens' and is for something that may happen later, but the work is already 'ongoing'. 'In addition to' adds one more thing.","The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["In spite of","In addition to","In the event of","In response to"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q7","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 7","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":7,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["comparatives"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The group's success depends __________ on effective collaboration than individual ability.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clue: 'than'. It tells us two things are being compared, so we need a comparative form (more or less), not a superlative (most or least).","Now think about meaning. It is a **group's** success, and collaboration means working together well. A group succeeds mainly through working together.","So the success depends 'more on effective collaboration than individual ability'. 'less' is grammatical but gives the opposite, unlikely meaning.","The answer is option (3)."]}],"options":["less","least","more","most"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q8","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 8","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":8,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["infinitives-and-gerunds"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The teacher made the class __________ the furniture for the oral examination the next day.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Rule: make + person + base form of the verb (no 'to', no '-s', no '-ed'). It means to cause or force someone to do something.","Example: 'Mother made me tidy my room.'","'arranged' is tempting because the sentence is in the past, but 'made' already carries the past tense. The second verb stays in its base form.","'The teacher made the class arrange the furniture...' The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["arrange","arranges","arranged","arranging"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q9","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 9","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":9,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["pronouns","subject-verb-agreement"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Anna, as well as her classmate, Tom, completed the marble run project by __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Find the real subject. The words between the commas, 'as well as her classmate, Tom,' are only extra information. They do not become part of the subject.","Rule: with 'as well as', 'together with' and 'along with', the subject is the noun before the phrase. Here the subject is 'Anna' alone.","A reflexive pronoun must match the subject. Anna is one girl, so the pronoun is 'herself'.","'themselves' is tempting because two people are named, but it would be right only with 'Anna **and** Tom'. The answer is option (1)."]}],"options":["herself","himself","ourselves","themselves"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q10","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 10","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":10,"topic":"grammar","subtypes":["quantifiers"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Without __________ equipment, the team still managed to complete the project successfully.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'Equipment' is an uncountable noun (we cannot say 'an equipment' or 'equipments'). 'few' and 'many' are for countable plural nouns, so they are out.","That leaves 'little' and 'much'. 'Without much equipment' means 'not having a lot of equipment'.","'little' already means 'almost none', so 'without little' does not make sense.","'still managed' shows they succeeded even though they did not have much. The answer is option (4)."]}],"options":["few","little","many","much"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q11","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 11","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":11,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Hui Ling had to deal with the __________ of repeatedly submitting homework after the deadline.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Clue: she handed in homework late again and again, and then 'had to deal with' what followed. We need a word for an unpleasant result of an action.","'repercussion' means an unwelcome consequence of something you did.","'retaliation' is hurting someone back in revenge, and 'deterrence' is stopping people from doing something by making them fear the result. 'damage' is physical harm.","The answer is option (4), repercussion."]}],"options":["damage","retaliation","deterrence","repercussion"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q12","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 12","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":12,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Artificial intelligence will be introduced to schools __________ to give students time to adapt to it.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Clue: 'to give students time to adapt'. The change must come slowly, step by step.","'progressively' means gradually, in stages.","'intermittently' is tempting, but it means stopping and starting at irregular times, not moving steadily forward. 'incessantly' means without stopping, and 'simultaneously' means at the same time.","The answer is option (3), progressively."]}],"options":["incessantly","intermittently","progressively","simultaneously"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q13","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 13","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":13,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning","collocation"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The volcanic eruption __________ the flight schedules in the region due to poor visibility.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["We need a word for what the eruption did to 'flight schedules', which are plans and timetables.","'disrupted' means stopped something from carrying on in its normal way. Schedules, services and lessons are disrupted.","'diverted' is tempting because flights can be diverted (sent to another airport), but a schedule cannot be sent somewhere else. 'disturbed' is used for people, sleep or peace, and 'disabled' for machines.","The answer is option (3), disrupted."]}],"options":["diverted","disabled","disrupted","disturbed"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q14","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 14","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":14,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["phrasal-verbs"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"The doctor advised him not to __________ his persistent headaches as they might have a detrimental effect on his health.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Clue: the headaches 'might have a detrimental (harmful) effect', so the doctor wants him to take them seriously.","'brush off' means to refuse to pay attention to something, treating it as unimportant. The doctor advised him not to do that.","'wear off' is tempting because pain can wear off (slowly go away), but a person cannot 'wear off' a headache. 'call off' means cancel and 'hold off' means delay.","The answer is option (4), brush off."]}],"options":["call off","hold off","wear off","brush off"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q15","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 15","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":15,"topic":"vocabulary","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Ever so __________, Mohan presented complex ideas in a way that was easy to understand.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Clue: his ideas were complex, yet 'easy to understand'. We need a word for someone who expresses ideas clearly. ('Ever so' means 'very'.)","'coherent' means clear, logical and easy to follow.","'consistent' is tempting because it looks similar, but it means always behaving in the same way. 'objective' means not influenced by personal feelings, and 'dexterous' means skilful with the hands.","The answer is option (1), coherent."]}],"options":["coherent","objective","dexterous","consistent"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q16","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 16","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":16,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++scurried++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["To 'scurry' is to move quickly with short, hurried steps.","Clue: the boys were 'bubbling with excitement' and busy preparing, so they were moving about quickly.","'scampered' also means ran with quick, light steps, so it is the closest.","'wandered' means walked slowly with no purpose, and 'prowled' means moved quietly as if hunting. 'fussed' is about worrying over small things, not about moving. The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g1","options":["fussed","prowled","wandered","scampered"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q17","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 17","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":17,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++juicy++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["'juicy' means full of juice.","'succulent' means juicy and tender, so it is the closest.","'delectable' and 'palatable' are tempting because the watermelon would be nice to eat, but they are about a pleasant taste, not about being full of juice.","'savoury' means salty or spicy rather than sweet. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g1","options":["savoury","palatable","succulent","delectable"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q18","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 18","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":18,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++sturdy++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["'sturdy' means strong and not easily broken. The basket had to hold sandwiches, watermelon, a flask and a first aid kit.","'robust' means strong and solid, so it is the closest.","'malleable' is nearly the opposite: easy to bend or press into a new shape. 'intricate' means having many small details, and 'versatile' means having many uses.","The answer is option (1)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g1","options":["robust","intricate","versatile","malleable"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q19","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 19","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":19,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["idioms"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the words closest in meaning to ++It is good to be prepared++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Aziz said this as he packed a first aid kit, in case someone got hurt at the picnic. He was taking care in advance.","'Better safe than sorry' means it is wiser to be careful and take precautions than to regret it later. That matches.","'Save for a rainy day' is tempting, but it is about keeping money for a time of need. 'Look before you leap' means think about the results before you act.","'Think on your feet' means to react quickly without preparing. The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g1","options":["Think on your feet","Save for a rainy day","Look before you leap","Better safe than sorry"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q20","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 20","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":20,"topic":"vocabulary-cloze","subtypes":["word-meaning"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Choose the word closest in meaning to ++thoroughly++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["'thoroughly' means completely and with great attention to detail. Clue: 'they ensured that everything was perfect'.","'painstakingly' means with great care and effort, checking every detail, so it is the closest.","'extensively' is tempting, but it means over a wide area or range (he travelled extensively), not carefully. 'deliberately' means on purpose or slowly.","'gingerly' means in a careful, nervous way so as not to break or hurt something. The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g1","options":["gingerly","extensively","deliberately","painstakingly"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q21","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 21","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":21,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"\"Ready to run, bond and build lasting memories?\" The purpose of this question in Text 1 is to __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":3},"explanation":["Look at the words used: 'run, bond and build lasting memories'. They are all fun, happy things to look forward to. A question like this, put to the reader in large bold print, is there to stir up feelings.","So its purpose is to make students excited before the event.","(1) is tempting, but the students are not being invited. The run is for all 'Primary 3 to 6 students', who 'assemble at the hall for attendance-taking', so they are expected to take part.","(2) is the job of the details below it (date, time, route). (4) does not fit because nothing is kept secret. The answer is option (3)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g2","options":["invite students to join the run","familiarise students with the run","excite students ahead of the run","make students curious about the run"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q22","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 22","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":22,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["factual"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"According to the event information in Text 1, which of the following are true of the run?\nA. The reporting venue is Crest Park.\nB. All students will have the same end point.\nC. There is only one spot to get drinking water.\nD. Students can attend the Sports Carnival after the run.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Check each statement against Text 1.","A is false: students 'Assemble at the hall for attendance-taking'. Crest Park is where the route is, not where they report.","B is true: there are two start lines but only one FINISHING LINE on the map. C is true: the note says 'A water point is available at Checkpoint 2', and no other water point is mentioned.","D is not supported: the Sports Carnival is for Primary 1 and 2, who 'remain in school'. So B and C are true. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g2","options":["A and C","B and C","B and D","B, C and D"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q23","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 23","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":23,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["purpose-and-audience"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Text 1 is most probably __________.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":1},"explanation":["Look at the layout: a big heading, a catchy line, a starburst, short points for date and time, a map and a note. That is how a poster about an event looks.","It is not a letter: there is no greeting, no paragraphs and no sign-off.","It is not a report: a report is written after an event, but this run is on a coming date.","'a programme itinerary' is tempting, but an itinerary lists activities with their times in order, and here only the reporting time is given. The answer is option (1)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g2","options":["an event poster","an invitation letter","a sports event report","a programme itinerary"]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q24","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 24","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":24,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"What is the **main** message of Text 2?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":2},"explanation":["Both halves of an option must be right. Look for the ideas the writer repeats.","Resilience (not giving up): 'Even when it is tough, we keep going', 'We show determination', 'growing stronger, both physically and mentally'.","Relationships: 'we learn to support one another', 'our friends are there to cheer us on', 'we are a part of one big family'. So (2) matches.","(3) is tempting because of 'stay active' and 'keep fit', but 'It is not about winning' rules out a competitive spirit. (1) fails because the run is for talented runners and beginners alike. The answer is option (2)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g2","options":["The school park run showcases talents and fosters empathy.","The school park run builds resilience and deepens relationship.","The school park run promotes active living and a competitive spirit.","The school park run encourages students to be driven and adventurous."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-a-q25","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet A Question 25","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"a","number":25,"topic":"visual-text","subtypes":["inference"],"format":"mcq","marks":1,"difficulty":1,"stem":"Which idea is expressed in Text 2 but not Text 1?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"mcq","value":4},"explanation":["Cross out every idea that Text 1 also has.","(1) is in Text 1: 'To promote physical well-being'. (2) is in Text 1: 'To foster school community spirit'. (3) is in Text 1: 'build lasting memories'.","Only Text 2 talks about the mind: 'growing stronger, both physically and **mentally**'. Text 1 mentions only physical well-being.","The answer is option (4)."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g2","options":["The run promotes a healthy lifestyle.","The run brings the school community together.","The run helps students to create lifelong memories.","The run prepares students to become mentally stronger."]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q26","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 26","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":26,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (26). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"J","accept":["j","of"]},"explanation":["The blank joins two nouns: 'the inclusivity ___ individuals with special needs'.","'of' links a noun to the people it is about, like 'the safety of children' or 'the inclusion of everyone'.","The answer is (J) of."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q27","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 27","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":27,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (27). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"Q","accept":["q","will"]},"explanation":["After the blank comes 'be raised'. Only a helping verb such as will, can or may can stand before 'be'.","'it is hoped that' looks ahead to something people want to happen in the future, so we use 'will'.","'...awareness of the needs of these people will be raised.' The answer is (Q) will."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q28","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 28","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":28,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (28). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"C","accept":["c","always"]},"explanation":["'it has ___ been considered' is already complete (has + been + considered), so the blank needs an adverb that sits between 'has' and 'been'.","'always' is the only adverb in the list. It tells us purple has been linked with royalty for all of history.","The answer is (C) always."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q29","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 29","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":29,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (29). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"B","accept":["b","all"]},"explanation":["'not ___ could wear purple!' The next sentence explains: 'It was reserved for the upper class'. So some people could wear it, but not everyone.","'not all' means 'not everyone'.","'any' is tempting, but 'not any could wear' is not correct English, and it would mean nobody could wear purple.","The answer is (B) all."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q30","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 30","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":30,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (30). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"K","accept":["k","their"]},"explanation":["The blank comes before the noun 'people', so we need a word that shows whose people they were.","The people belonged to 'Some Roman emperors' (plural), so the word is 'their'.","'these' and 'those' are tempting because they also go before plural nouns, but no people have been pointed out yet. The emperors forbade their own people.","The answer is (K) their."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q31","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 31","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":31,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (31). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"M","accept":["m","those"]},"explanation":["'in ___ days' is a set phrase about time.","'in those days' means at that time in the far past. 'those' points to things far away in time or place.","'these days' means nowadays, but the verb 'came' is in the past and the passage is describing ancient times.","The answer is (M) those."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q32","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 32","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":32,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["pronouns"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (32). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"G","accept":["g","it"]},"explanation":["The blank is the subject of 'was therefore extremely expensive', and it starts a new sentence.","What was expensive? The purple dye from the sentence before. One thing is replaced by the pronoun 'It'.","'its' is tempting, but 'its' shows belonging and must be followed by a noun (its colour).","The answer is (G) it."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q33","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 33","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":33,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["connectors"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (33). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"E","accept":["e","but"]},"explanation":["Look for the partner words: 'not only to show wealth ___ also to signal authority'.","The pair is 'not only ... but also'. It joins two reasons of the same kind.","The answer is (E) but."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q34","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 34","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":34,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["articles-and-determiners"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (34). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"A","accept":["a","a"]},"explanation":["'wearing ___ purple robe': 'robe' is a singular countable noun, so it needs an article or a similar word in front.","It is one robe, mentioned for the first time, and 'purple' begins with a consonant sound, so we use 'a'.","'its' does not fit because Cyrus is a person (it would have to be 'his').","The answer is (A) a."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q35","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 35","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":35,"topic":"grammar-cloze","subtypes":["verb-forms"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Choose the most suitable word for blank (35). (Type its letter, A to Q.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"N","accept":["n","was"]},"explanation":["The sentence has no verb yet: 'Purple, ..., ___ everywhere in royal life back then.'","Find the subject by leaving out the words between the commas. The subject is 'Purple', which is singular. 'back then' shows the past. So the verb is 'was'.","'were' is tempting because 'colours' comes just before the blank, but 'a blend of rich red and blue colours' is only extra information about purple.","The answer is (N) was."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g3"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q36","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 36","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":36,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (36) ++prefer++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"preferred"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error. Look at the helping verb in front: 'I **had** ___ staying indoors'.","Rule: had + past participle makes the past perfect tense, for something that was true before another past time (before the camping trip).","The past participle of 'prefer' is 'preferred'. Double the 'r' because the stress is on the last syllable: pre-FER.","The correct word is 'preferred'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q37","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 37","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":37,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (37) ++unkumftible++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"uncomfortable"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means not feeling pleasant or relaxed, which fits sleeping in a tent without air conditioning.","Build it from parts: un + comfort + able. The base word is 'comfort', spelt with a 'c' and an 'o'.","The ending is '-able', not '-ible'.","The correct word is 'uncomfortable'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q38","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 38","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":38,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (38) ++drastiquely++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"drastically"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means in a sudden and very big way.","It comes from the adjective 'drastic'. Adjectives ending in '-ic' add '-ally' to become adverbs: basic, basically; dramatic, dramatically.","The 'k' sound is spelt 'c', not 'qu'.","The correct word is 'drastically'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q39","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 39","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":39,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (39) ++imerssed++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"immersed"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. To be 'immersed' in books is to be so deeply interested in them that you notice nothing else.","The word has a double 'm' and a single 's': im + mersed.","A related word is 'immersion'.","The correct word is 'immersed'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q40","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 40","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":40,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (40) ++glory++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"glorious"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error (wrong word form). 'glory' is a noun.","The blank describes the noun 'masterpiece', so we need an adjective.","Change the 'y' to 'i' and add '-ous': glory, glorious (like fury, furious).","The correct word is 'glorious'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q41","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 41","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":41,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (41) ++soothe++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"soothing"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error (wrong word form). 'soothe' is a verb meaning to make someone calm.","The blank describes the noun 'sound', so we need an adjective. A sound that calms you is a 'soothing' sound.","Drop the 'e' before adding '-ing': soothe, soothing.","The correct word is 'soothing'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q42","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 42","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":42,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (42) ++in++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"to","accept":["into"]},"explanation":["This is a grammar error: the wrong preposition.","The set phrase is 'lull someone **to** sleep', meaning to make someone fall asleep gently. 'in sleep' does not show the change from being awake to being asleep.","'lulled me into sleep' is also used and shows the same change.","The correct word is 'to'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q43","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 43","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":43,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["grammar"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (43) ++awake++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"awoke"},"explanation":["This is a grammar error (wrong tense). 'The next morning' in this story is in the past, and the other verbs are in the past (went, changed, lulled).","'awake' is an irregular verb: awake, awoke, awoken.","The simple past is 'awoke'.","The correct word is 'awoke'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q44","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 44","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":44,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (44) ++plithora++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"plethora"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. A 'plethora' of something is a very large amount or variety of it.","The first syllable is spelt with an 'e': ple-tho-ra.","The correct word is 'plethora'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q45","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 45","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":45,"topic":"editing","subtypes":["spelling"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Write the correct word for (45) ++krushial++.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"crucial"},"explanation":["This is a spelling error. The word means extremely important.","It begins with a 'c', and the 'shul' sound at the end is spelt '-cial', as in special, social and official.","The correct word is 'crucial'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g4"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q46","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 46","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":46,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (46) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"vary","accept":["differ"]},"explanation":["The sentence needs a verb: 'School meals around the world ___ greatly.'","The next sentence starts with 'However, they all share a common goal', so this sentence must say the meals are not the same.","'vary' and 'differ' both mean 'are different from one another'. The subject 'meals' is plural, so there is no 's' on the verb.","The answer is 'vary' (or 'differ')."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q47","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 47","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":47,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (47) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"starts","accept":["begins","commences"]},"explanation":["Clues: 'with an appetiser, followed by a main dish ... Next ... Finally'. The meal is described in order, and an appetiser is the first course.","So the meal 'starts with' an appetiser. 'begins' means the same.","'The meal' is singular and the passage is in the present tense, so the verb needs an 's'.","The answer is 'starts'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q48","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 48","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":48,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (48) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"consisting"},"explanation":["'a main dish ___ of protein like salmon or lamb': the blank is followed by 'of' and tells us what the dish is made up of.","'consist of' means 'be made up of'. After the noun 'dish' we use the -ing form: a main dish consisting of protein.","The answer is 'consisting'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q49","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 49","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":49,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (49) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"array","accept":["abundance"]},"explanation":["Clue: 'an' tells us the word begins with a vowel sound, and it is followed by 'of vitamins and minerals'.","The meal has many parts (appetiser, main dish, cheese, bread, dessert), so it gives many kinds and plenty of nutrients.","'an array of' means a wide range of. 'an abundance of' means plenty of.","The answer is 'array' (or 'abundance')."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q50","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 50","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":50,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (50) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"produce","accept":["ingredients"]},"explanation":["Clues: 'locally grown, fresh ___ – vegetables, fruits and mushrooms – obtained from nearby farms'. The words between the dashes are examples of the missing word.","'produce' (a noun) means food that is grown on farms, such as fruit and vegetables.","'ingredients' also fits, because the lunches 'use' these things to make the meals.","The answer is 'produce'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q51","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 51","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":51,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (51) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"drawn"},"explanation":["The blank is followed by 'up': 'menus are usually ___ up by a nutritionist'.","The phrasal verb 'draw up' means to prepare and write out a plan or a list.","The sentence is passive (are + past participle), so we need the past participle of 'draw', which is 'drawn'.","The answer is 'drawn'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q52","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 52","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":52,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (52) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"experience","accept":["experiences","options","choices"]},"explanation":["'offer the most diverse dining ___': we need a noun for what canteens offer to students who eat there.","Clue: 'diverse' means with a lot of variety, and the next sentence lists Chinese, Indian, Malay and Western food.","A 'dining experience' is the whole experience of eating at a place. 'dining options' or 'choices' also fit, since students choose from many stalls.","The answer is 'experience'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q53","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 53","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":53,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (53) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"from","accept":["among"]},"explanation":["'Students can choose ___ various stalls': the verb 'choose' takes the preposition 'from' when a range of things is given.","Example: 'You can choose from three flavours.'","The answer is 'from'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q54","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 54","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":54,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (54) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"educate","accept":["guide","advise"]},"explanation":["'implemented to ___ students on making healthier food and beverage choices': we need a base verb (after 'to') that goes with 'students on'.","A school programme teaches. To 'educate someone on' a topic is to teach them about it.","'teach' does not fit well because we say 'teach students **about**' or 'teach students **to**', not 'teach students on'.","The answer is 'educate'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q55","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 55","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":55,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (55) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"part"},"explanation":["'As ___ of this initiative' is a set phrase.","'As part of' means 'as one piece of a bigger plan'. Serving wholegrains, fruits and vegetables is one piece of the programme.","The answer is 'part'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q56","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 56","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":56,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (56) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"undergo","accept":["attend","receive"]},"explanation":["'they ___ mandatory training': 'they' are the canteen vendors, and 'mandatory' means they must do it.","To 'undergo training' is to go through it. This is the usual word for training, treatment or a test that someone has to go through.","'they' is plural and the passage is in the present tense, so there is no 's' on the verb.","The answer is 'undergo'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q57","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 57","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":57,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (57) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"comply"},"explanation":["'to ensure that they ___ with nutrition guidelines': the blank is followed by 'with', and guidelines are rules to follow.","To 'comply with' rules means to obey them.","'follow' is tempting, but it is not used with 'with' (we say 'follow the guidelines').","The answer is 'comply'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q58","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 58","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":58,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["prepositions"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (58) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"with"},"explanation":["'Snacks sold have to come ___ the Healthier Choice symbol.'","'come with' means to have something attached or included. The snacks must have the symbol on them.","The answer is 'with'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q59","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 59","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":59,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (59) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"flavoured","accept":["sweetened"]},"explanation":["'drinks artificially ___ by sugar are strongly discouraged': the drinks are compared with 'plain water', and the clue is 'sugar'.","Sugar is added to change the taste of a drink. 'flavoured' means given a taste, and 'sweetened' means made sweet.","We need a past participle (-ed) because something is done to the drinks.","The answer is 'flavoured' (or 'sweetened')."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q60","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 60","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":60,"topic":"comprehension-cloze","subtypes":["contextual-vocabulary"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Fill in blank (60) with a suitable word.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"increases","accept":["raises","heightens"]},"explanation":["'They are often high in salt, which ___ the risk of heart disease.' Too much salt is bad for the heart, so it makes the risk greater.","'increases' means makes bigger. 'raises' means the same here.","'which' stands for 'salt' (or for being high in salt), which is singular, and the passage is in the present tense, so the verb needs an 's'.","The answer is 'increases'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g5"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q61","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 61","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":61,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["none-of"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"These towers are not connected by bridges.\nNone of ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"None of these towers are connected by bridges.","accept":["None of these towers is connected by bridges."]},"explanation":["'None of' means 'not one of' and already carries the negative meaning, so drop 'not'. Writing 'None of these towers are not connected' would be a double negative and change the meaning.","Keep the rest of the sentence the same: 'these towers' follows 'None of', then the verb and 'connected by bridges'.","Keep the present tense. With 'none of' + a plural noun, 'are' is commonly used and 'is' is also accepted.","Answer: None of these towers are connected by bridges."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q62","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 62","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":62,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["passive-voice"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The lucky customer chose the last carton of mangoes.\nThe last ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The last carton of mangoes was chosen by the lucky customer."},"explanation":["The new sentence starts with the thing that received the action ('The last carton of mangoes'), so it must be in the passive voice.","Passive voice: object + form of 'to be' + past participle + by + doer.","Tense stays the same: 'chose' is simple past, so use 'was' (the subject 'carton' is singular, even though 'mangoes' is plural). The past participle of 'choose' is 'chosen', not 'chose'.","Answer: The last carton of mangoes was chosen by the lucky customer."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q63","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 63","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":63,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["noun-form"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The girl pronounced the difficult word accurately to impress her teacher.\nThe girl's accurate ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The girl's accurate pronunciation of the difficult word impressed her teacher."},"explanation":["After the possessive 'The girl's' and the adjective 'accurate' we need a noun. Change the verb 'pronounced' into its noun: 'pronunciation'. Watch the spelling: there is no 'o' before the 'u' (not 'pronounciation').","The adverb 'accurately' has already become the adjective 'accurate' in the starter.","Link the noun to its object with 'of': 'pronunciation of the difficult word'.","The noun phrase is now the subject, so finish with a verb: it 'impressed her teacher'. Answer: The girl's accurate pronunciation of the difficult word impressed her teacher."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q64","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 64","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":64,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["reported-speech"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"\"I won't be joining you for the celebration tomorrow,\" Vivien told her parents.\nVivien told her parents that ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Vivien told her parents that she would not be joining them for the celebration the next day.","accept":["Vivien told her parents that she would not be joining them for the celebration the following day.","Vivien told her parents that she wouldn't be joining them for the celebration the next day.","Vivien told her parents that she wouldn't be joining them for the celebration the following day."]},"explanation":["This is direct speech changed to reported speech. Remove the quotation marks.","Tense moves one step back: 'won't' (will not) becomes 'would not'.","Pronouns change to fit the speaker: 'I' is Vivien, so 'she'; 'you' is her parents, so 'them'.","Time words change: 'tomorrow' becomes 'the next day' or 'the following day'.","Answer: Vivien told her parents that she would not be joining them for the celebration the next day."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q65","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 65","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":65,"topic":"synthesis","subtypes":["participle-phrase"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"The mountain trail was steep and challenging. The hikers reached the summit successfully.\nUndeterred by ____________________.\n(Rewrite as one sentence using the word(s) given. The meaning must stay the same.)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Undeterred by the steep and challenging mountain trail, the hikers reached the summit successfully.","accept":["Undeterred by the steep and challenging mountain trail, the hikers successfully reached the summit.","Undeterred by the steep and challenging mountain trail the hikers reached the summit successfully."]},"explanation":["'Undeterred by' means 'not discouraged by'. It must be followed by a noun phrase, the thing that could have put them off.","Turn the first sentence into a noun phrase by moving the adjectives in front of the noun: 'the steep and challenging mountain trail'. Do not keep 'was'.","Put a comma after the phrase. The very next words must be the people who were undeterred: 'the hikers'.","Keep the rest unchanged. Answer: Undeterred by the steep and challenging mountain trail, the hikers reached the summit successfully."]}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q66","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 66","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":66,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"In Paragraph 1, Colin went to a countryside farm. Provide two reasons why Colin expected \"**a month of boredom**\". (line 4, paragraph 1)","parts":[{"label":"i","prompt":"First reason:","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"He would not be able to play computer games in the comfort of his air-conditioned bedroom."},"explanation":["Paragraph 1 says what made his city holidays 'busy and exciting': being out with his friends, or 'playing computer games in the comfort of his air-conditioned bedroom'.","On a countryside farm he would have neither of these, so he expected to be bored.","Write each reason as something he would miss on the farm. This reason: he could not play computer games in the comfort of his air-conditioned bedroom."]},{"label":"ii","prompt":"Second reason: (The two reasons may be given in either order.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"He would not be able to go out with his friends."},"explanation":["The other thing that kept him busy in the city: 'If he was not out with his friends...'.","His friends were in the city, so on the farm he could not go out with them.","The marker looks for two different reasons, one about computer games and one about his friends. Do not split one reason into two."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q67","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 67","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":67,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["character-and-feelings"],"format":"short","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Circle the word that **best** describes the characters' feelings.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Colin \"wrinkled his nose at the earthy smell\" (lines 5-6, paragraph 2)\naghast / bored / disgusted\n(Type the word you choose.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"text","value":"disgusted"},"explanation":["People wrinkle their noses when they find a smell unpleasant. Colin did not like the earthy smell clinging to his shoes.","'disgusted' means feeling strong dislike for something unpleasant.","'aghast' is too strong: it means filled with shock or horror. 'bored' is what he expected to feel later, but it has nothing to do with a smell.","The answer is 'disgusted'."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"\"Then, she greeted him warmly\" (line 10, paragraph 3)\nnostalgic / satisfied / thrilled\n(Type the word you choose.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"text","value":"thrilled"},"explanation":["Grandma greeted him 'warmly' and said, 'Welcome, Colin. It's been a long time since you were here.' She had not seen him for a long time and was very happy that he had come.","'thrilled' means extremely pleased and excited.","'nostalgic' is tempting because she mentions the past, but it means missing the past with a little sadness. Here she is happy about the present and eager to show him something.","The answer is 'thrilled'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q68","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 68","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":68,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","vocabulary-in-context"],"format":"structured","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Explain **in your own words** what Grandma was doing before Colin arrived.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"She was pulling out weeds from among her crops."},"explanation":["Find it in paragraph 2: Colin saw 'a figure bent over, her hands moving busily, plucking out invasive stems that competed with the crops for nutrients and space'. The figure was Grandma.","'In your own words' means you must not copy the passage. Change the key words.","'plucking out' can become 'pulling out' or 'removing'. 'invasive stems that competed with the crops for nutrients and space' are weeds (unwanted plants).","The marker looks for: she was pulling out (removing) weeds. Copying 'plucking out invasive stems' gets no mark."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q69","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 69","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":69,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","character-and-feelings"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Why did Colin eye Grandma warily? (line 12, paragraph 4)","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Colin was not sure what Grandma was going to show him, so he was cautious. He also doubted that anything on a farm could be special."},"explanation":["'warily' means in a careful, not trusting way. Look at the two questions in Colin's mind straight after: 'What was she going to show him? How special could something on the farm be?'","Point 1 (1 mark): he did not know what Grandma was going to show him, so he was cautious about it.","Point 2 (1 mark): he was sceptical (doubtful) that anything on a farm could be special or interesting. Remember that he 'expected a month of boredom'.","Turn the two questions into statements. Do not simply copy the questions from the passage."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q70","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 70","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":70,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["evidence-phrase"],"format":"short","marks":1,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Which four-word phrase from Paragraph 4 suggests an acceptance of the situation without protest?","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"text","value":"waited in quiet submission"},"explanation":["In paragraph 4, the large hen 'shoved the others aside, pecking greedily while the smaller birds waited in quiet submission'.","'submission' means giving in to someone stronger, and 'quiet' shows they did not fight back or make a fuss. That is accepting the situation without protest.","Count exactly four words and copy them without changing anything: waited (1) in (2) quiet (3) submission (4). Writing more or fewer words loses the mark.","The answer is 'waited in quiet submission'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q71","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 71","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":71,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["true-false-with-reason"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Based on lines 9-31 (paragraphs 3 to 8), state whether each statement in the table below is true or false, then give one reason why you think so.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"Colin had visited Grandma before.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. Grandma said that it had been a long time since Colin was last there, so he had visited the farm before."},"explanation":["Paragraph 3: Grandma greeted him with 'It's been a long time since you were here.'","'since you were here' tells us he had been to the farm before, a long time ago.","So the statement is True. The reason must be evidence from the passage, not the statement repeated."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Grandma cared about Colin's feelings.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. When Grandma saw Colin grimacing at the smaller hens being pushed around by the large hen, she patted him gently on the shoulder, asked what was wrong and explained what the hens were doing to comfort him."},"explanation":["Paragraph 4 ends with 'Colin grimaced.' He was upset to see the smaller hens being pushed around.","Paragraph 5: 'Grandma patted Colin on the shoulder gently. \"What's with the frown?\" she asked', and then she explained that the smaller hens 'know how to get food despite being smaller'.","She noticed how he felt and tried to make him feel better. So the statement is True."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"According to Grandma, the smaller hens were easily frightened.\n(Type True or False, then give one reason from the passage.)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"True. Grandma called out to Colin that the hens were not as bold as the large one."},"explanation":["Paragraph 8: when Colin wanted to sprint after the hens that had escaped, Grandma called, 'Wait! They're not as bold as the large one!'","'not as bold' means more timid. She was warning him that these hens scare easily, so chasing them would only drive them further away.","So the statement is True, and the reason is what Grandma said about them."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q72","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 72","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":72,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["cause-and-effect"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Explain clearly what had caused the hens to escape.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"The strong wind tugged at the coop door and made it swing open. When the rain poured down, the hens were frightened and ran out through the open door."},"explanation":["Find it in paragraph 6: 'Wind whipped across the farm, rattling fences and tugging at the coop door. With a loud creak, the door swung open. Rain poured down, and the hens burst out in a flurry of feathers and panicked clucks.'","'Explain clearly' for 2 marks needs the chain of events, not just one cause.","Point 1 (1 mark): the wind tugged at the coop door until it swung open.","Point 2 (1 mark): the rain poured down and the hens panicked ('panicked clucks'), so they rushed out of the open coop."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q73","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 73","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":73,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["referencing"],"format":"structured","marks":3,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Look at the table below. What do the word(s) in the left column refer to in the passage? Write your answers in the column on the right.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"this (line 11, paragraph 3)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The hens feeding and fighting over the grains (with the smaller hens still managing to get their food).","accept":["the hens feeding and fighting","the hens feeding and fighting over the grains","the hens feeding and jostling for the grains"]},"explanation":["Here 'this' points forward. Grandma says, 'Today, I'll show you something different. You don't see **this** happening in the city.'","Read on to see what she showed him: at the chicken coop, the hens were 'jostling for the grains', the large hen shoved the others aside, and the smaller hens got their food when it turned away.","'this' is something 'happening', so the answer must be an event, not just 'the hens'.","The answer is the hens feeding and jostling (fighting) for the grains."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"the right moment (line 31, paragraph 8)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The moment when the larger hen turned away from the grains.","accept":["when the larger hen turned away","when the larger hen turned away from the grains","when the large hen turned away","when the larger one turned away"]},"explanation":["Colin 'remembered the smaller hens at feeding time – how they had waited, watched, and chosen **the right moment**'.","Go back to feeding time in paragraph 5: he 'noticed the smaller hens pecking at the grains when the larger one turned away'.","So the right moment for the smaller hens to eat was when the larger hen was not looking.","The answer is when the larger hen turned away (from the grains)."]},{"label":"c","prompt":"nothing (line 39, paragraph 10)","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"The large hen not moving from the field (it did not come towards the coop).","accept":["the larger hen did not move from the field","the large hen did not move from the field","the large hen did not move"]},"explanation":["'For a long moment, **nothing** happened.' Ask: what was Colin waiting for?","At the end of paragraph 9, 'The large one, however, remained in the field, tense and alert', and Colin stood still, waiting.","The next sentence shows what finally happened: 'Then, slowly, the hen tilted its head...'. So before that, the large hen did not move.","The answer is the large hen staying where it was in the field and not moving."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q74","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 74","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":74,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","factual"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Explain clearly how Colin lured the large hen back to its coop.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"Colin spread grains in a trail that led towards the coop as bait, then waited patiently without rushing. The hen noticed the grains and followed them step by step, eating them, until it crossed into the coop."},"explanation":["To 'lure' is to attract an animal by offering something it wants. Look in paragraph 10 for what attracted the hen.","'the hen tilted its head, noticing the grains that led towards the coop. Step by step, it followed the bait.' 'bait' tells us the grains were put there on purpose to draw the hen in.","Point 1 (1 mark): Colin laid out grains leading towards the coop as bait.","Point 2 (1 mark): the hen noticed the grains and followed them into the coop, while Colin waited and 'resisted the urge to rush'."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-b-q75","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 2 Booklet B Question 75","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"b","number":75,"topic":"comprehension","subtypes":["inference","evidence-phrase"],"format":"structured","marks":2,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Complete the information below.","parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"One lesson Colin learnt from the visit is ____________________.","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"to be patient","accept":["to be patient","patience","to be patient and not impulsive","to be patient."]},"explanation":["Look at Grandma's last words: 'nothing teaches you how to wait like the chickens!' Knowing how to wait is patience.","It also matches what Colin realised in paragraph 8: 'Being impulsive would not help.'","The answer is to be patient (not to act impulsively)."]},{"label":"b","prompt":"Cite a piece of evidence to support your answer in (a).","marks":1,"answer":{"type":"self","value":"Instead of being impulsive and sprinting after the hens, Colin guided them slowly. When the large hen followed the bait towards the coop, he resisted the urge to rush so that he would not scare it away."},"explanation":["The evidence must show Colin being patient, and it must match your answer in (a).","Paragraph 9: 'Instead of chasing, he began guiding.' He slowed his steps and steered the hens gently.","Paragraph 10: 'Colin waited with bated breath' and 'Colin resisted the urge to rush' while the large hen followed the bait step by step.","Either piece of evidence is accepted. Say what Colin did, not only what Grandma said."]}],"group":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g6"},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-p1-q1","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 1 Question 1","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"p1","number":1,"topic":"writing","subtypes":["situational-writing"],"format":"structured","marks":14,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Part 1: Situational Writing\nThe pictures below show Vincent, Leah and Siti having a conversation. Study the pictures carefully.\n[[img1]]\n++Your Task++\nImagine you are Vincent.\n**Write an email to your form teacher, Mrs Wong, to propose the ideas for the project you and your classmates have discussed.**\nYou are to refer to the pictures and information on page 2 (shown above) for your email. The bullet point underlined below requires you to come up with your own information.\nIn your email, include the following key information:\n• the name and objective of the Values-in-Action project\n• how the activity was decided\n• the items to be purchased and by when you hope to get them\n• ++one way the class plans to interact with the elderly++\nYou may reorder the points. Remember to write in complete sentences.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There is no single model answer. Check your writing against the points below."},"explanation":["Purpose: to propose the project ideas to Mrs Wong and ask for her help to get the items. Say this in the opening lines.","Audience and tone: Mrs Wong is your form teacher, so the email is formal and polite. Begin 'Dear Mrs Wong', use no slang or short forms like 'gonna', and thank her at the end.","Context: you are Vincent, writing after the discussion with Leah and Siti on 17 August 2026. Write as 'I' and 'we', and sign off as Vincent with your class.","Point 1, name and objective: the project is called \"Bring a Smile\". Siti preferred 'the latter', which means the second of the two names, so it is not \"Paper Project Crane\". The objective is to bring cheer to the residents of Joy Elderly Home.","Point 2, how the activity was decided: the class chose to fold 1000 paper cranes because all of them have folded paper cranes before, so they should be able to finish quickly (and the residents will love the colourful display).","Point 3, items and date: the items to be purchased are coloured paper and nylon string. Do not ask for tape, because the leftover tape from the last project should be enough. Ask to have the items by 20 August, to give the class ample time to fold the cranes. Vincent first said 31 August, but Siti then suggested 20 August and gave the reason.","Point 4, your own information: give one sensible way the class will interact with the elderly, for example teaching the residents to fold a crane, chatting with them while hanging up the cranes, or singing songs for them.","Format and language: a short subject line, greeting, clear paragraphs, a polite closing request and sign-off. Write in complete sentences and check tenses, spelling and punctuation. Marks: 6 for task fulfilment, 8 for language and organisation."]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-p1-q1-img1","alt":"Two cartoon panels set in a school library, dated 17 August 2026. Three pupils are talking: Vincent (a boy, on the left), Leah (a girl, in the middle) and Siti (a girl holding a book, on the right). Panel 1. Vincent: As part of our Values-in-Action project, let's fold 1000 paper cranes to bring cheer to the residents of Joy Elderly Home! Leah: Great idea, Vincent! Since all of us have folded paper cranes before, we should be able to finish them quickly. I'm sure the residents will love the colourful display. Let's call it \"Paper Project Crane\" or \"Bring a Smile\". Siti: Leah, I prefer the latter. We'll need tape, coloured paper and nylon string to make and hang the cranes. Panel 2. Vincent: The leftover tape from our last project should be enough. I'll email Mrs Wong to help us get the rest of the items by 31 August. Leah: Perhaps, we should also share how our class plans to interact with the elderly. Siti: I agree! We should get the items by 20 August to give ourselves ample time to fold the cranes."}]},{"id":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-p1-q2","cite":"2026 Prelim Tao Nan Paper 1 Question 2","year":2026,"exam":"Prelim","school":"Tao Nan","section":"p1","number":2,"topic":"writing","subtypes":["continuous-writing"],"format":"structured","marks":36,"difficulty":2,"stem":"Part 2: Continuous Writing\nWrite a composition of ++at least 150 words++ about **something precious.**\nThe pictures are provided to help you think about this topic.\nYour composition must be based on at least one of these pictures.\n[[img1]]\nConsider the following points when you plan your composition:\n• What was precious?\n• Why was it precious?\nYou may use the points in any order and include other relevant points as well.","parts":[{"label":"","answer":{"type":"self","value":"There is no single model answer. Check your writing against the points below."},"explanation":["Understand the topic: 'something precious' is something of great value to you. It need not cost much. It can be an object, a person, time, a memory or a friendship. Your story must show clearly what was precious and why.","Picture 1 (watch): a watch that is a gift or a keepsake from a loved one, which is lost, broken or almost given up; or the watch can stand for time, such as precious time spent with someone before they move away.","Picture 2 (pupils with an elderly woman): precious time or a bond with a grandparent or a resident of a home; the precious stories and advice she shares; realising that her company matters more than games or gadgets.","Picture 3 (card with a sunflower): a handmade card from a friend, parent or teacher that is kept for years because of the words inside or the moment it was given.","Plan a story arc: introduction (set the scene and the precious thing), build-up (why it matters), problem (it is lost, damaged, taken for granted, or time is running out), climax (what the character does and feels), resolution.","Show why it is precious through actions, thoughts and feelings, not just by saying 'it was precious to me'.","End by returning to the topic: what the character learnt about what is truly precious. Use at least one picture, write at least 150 words, and check paragraphing, tenses and vocabulary. Marks: 18 for content, 18 for language and organisation."]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-p1-q2-img1","alt":"Three pictures, one below the other. Picture 1: a wristwatch showing about four o'clock. Picture 2: a boy and a girl in school uniform smiling and talking with an elderly woman with glasses who is sitting in a wheelchair; the girl is holding the woman's hands. Picture 3: a folded greeting card standing up, with a sunflower drawn on the front."}]}],"groups":{"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g1":{"text":"For each question from 16 to 20, choose the word closest in meaning to the underlined word(s).\n\nKiri was on a trip to Cambodia with his granddaughter, Sophea. After months of planning, their dream was finally __(16: realised)__ as they travelled to Kampong Thom, where he grew up. __(17: Entranced)__ by the beautiful fields and the distant clamour of roosters crowing, Sophea walked to the river where her grandfather had once spent hours fishing.\nThe duo trekked __(18: laboriously)__ up the hill, perspiration dotting their brows. They could see the vast countryside stretching before them from the summit. The view mirrored __(19: to a great extent)__ the stories Kiri had told her of his childhood — untouched, vivid, and full of quiet wonder. Kiri was confident that although time had passed, the bond he shared with Sophea would __(20: stand the test of time)__ with each cherished memory. This trip would be etched in their hearts and minds forever."},"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g2":{"text":"Study the poster (Text 1) and the extract from a school talk (Text 2) and answer questions 21 to 25.\n\n++Text 1++\nThe text below shows a poster for an event.\n[[img1]]\n++Text 2++\nThe extract below is taken from a school talk.\nA strong community makes us feel safe and connected, and it all begins at home. The family serves as the foundation of a healthy support system and spending quality time together lays the groundwork for overcoming life's challenges.\nFamily time offers invaluable benefits: it strengthens emotional bonds, boosts confidence, and reinforces our sense of self-worth. For children, it cultivates a robust sense of identity, helping them grow into confident and secure adults.\nThere are countless ways to enjoy quality family time. Indoors, a lively game night can fill the house with laughter and joy. Outdoors, a simple picnic in the park can create cherished memories while allowing us to admire nature.\nAs the saying goes, \"Home is where the heart is.\" When family bonds are strong, they form the heart of a thriving community. Together, we support and uplift one another. In this way, we grow stronger.","images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g2-img1","alt":"Poster. Heading: MOVIE AT THE PARK. Tagline (partly blanked out in the scan; the full line is quoted in Question 24): 'Ready for a magical night under the stars with your loved ones?' Picture: an outdoor screen between trees and loudspeakers, with families sitting on picnic mats and beanbags. On the screen, a lone small figure stands facing a large, dark, spooky manor at night. Text below: 'One brave boy... An abandoned manor in the woods... Many mysteries to unravel... With every step deeper, the danger closes in... Will he escape?' Cast: LEO GRAHAM as Jamie; MAX HORTON as Inspector Grey; JASPER LIN as the Gardener. FILM: The Mystery of Maplewood Manor. Based on a true story by Felicity Vale. Produced by Elliot Drake. DATE & TIME: Friday, 24 October 2025, 7:30 pm. VENUE: Willow Park. Brought to you by Willow Film Club. Register at www.willowfilmclub.com"}]},"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g3":{"text":"There are 10 blanks, numbered 26 to 35, in the passage below. From the list of words given, choose the most suitable word for each blank. Write its letter (A to Q) in the blank. The letters (I) and (O) have been omitted to avoid confusion during marking.\n**EACH WORD CAN BE USED ONLY ONCE.**\n[[table1]]\n\nWithin the Singapore Botanic Gardens stands a living landmark of national pride. Native __(26)__ Singapore, the Tembusu Tree can grow up to 40 metres tall. Its most distinctive feature – a gently curled, low-hanging branch – reaches outward as if offering a reminder of its presence to __(27)__ who walk past. Also known affectionately as the \"Five-dollar Tree\", this is the tree that graces the back of the note. Few would expect such __(28)__ iconic image to be not only real but also __(29)__ accessible to the public. The image is so ingrained in our everyday life that people often overlook the significance __(30)__ the symbol.\nThis heritage tree is believed to have existed __(31)__ the Botanic Gardens was officially founded in 1859. Close to two centuries old, this tree __(32)__ been admired by generations, some of whom return just to marvel at its resilience. __(33)__ its thick, weathered trunk and an expansive canopy of branches, the tree represents the enduring spirit of a nation. Standing unshaken by storms __(34)__ remaining deeply grounded in its values, it never forsakes the roots that define it. The Tembusu Tree is truly a symbol of what Singapore takes pride in – __(35)__ anchored and forward-looking. Such is the spirit of the country.\nAdapted from https://www.nparks.gov.sg/","tables":[{"id":"table1","rows":[["(A) a","(D) being","(G) having","(K) some","(N) to"],["(B) an","(E) had","(H) on","(L) still","(P) while"],["(C) behind","(F) has","(J) since","(M) those","(Q) with"]]}]},"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g4":{"text":"Each of the underlined words contains either a spelling or grammatical error. Write the correct word in each of the boxes.\n\nGood morning! I am Daryl, your Head Prefect. I am thrilled to share with you a meaningful project that could really benefit from your __(36: invovment)__. Collaborating with the Computer Club, the prefects will be organising a two-day computer __(37: litracy)__ workshop for the elderly folks in our neighbourhood. This __(38: inntaitive)__ is our first outreach effort to bridge the digital divide __(39: by)__ the young and old generations. Thus, we are inviting committed and __(40: emphatick)__ student volunteers to guide the elderly participants.\nIt is easy for us to take such a skill set for granted __(41: because)__ for someone who did not grow up with digital technology, __(42: those)__ can be rather intimidating. Do you know how lonely it can be when you can't connect to the digital world around us? By sharing your knowledge and __(43: applies)__ your skills, you'll not only empower our seniors, but also sharpen your __(44: comunikation)__ and coaching skills. If you're ready to step up and give back, sign up at the booth just outside the General Office today. We look forward to __(45: seen)__ you at the workshop! Thank you.\nAdapted from Inspire, Amazing Stories"},"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g5":{"text":"Fill in each blank with a suitable word.\n\nThe Museum of Toys is nestled in the heart of our city. Driven by his passion for design and art, Marcus, an enthusiastic toy __(46)__, often imagined a space where visitors from all __(47)__ of life could rediscover the past, and rekindle childhood memories of playing with favourite figurines and stuffed toys. His dream finally came true in 2007 when he successfully set up this museum, __(48)__ his personal collection from more than forty countries.\nOffering a fascinating journey through time, the museum displays toys from the 1840s. Toys __(49)__ action figures, wind-up robots and miniature cars awe children and adults __(50)__. These toys were carefully designed to move or make sounds when __(51)__ up with a key in the winding slot, showing the creativity of the toy designers. __(52)__ of the highlights of the museum is the mechanical toy, which can be powered by winding a key, __(53)__ a button, or pushing a lever, __(54)__ of relying on batteries.\n\"These toys were not mere ornaments. In fact, they were companions during our childhood days,\" said the museum owner, his __(55)__ carrying a note of nostalgia. He hoped that the place would provide visitors with a holistic experience, educating them about the stories behind each toy and how toys had gradually evolved __(56)__ the years. This emotional connection to the past can foster feelings of elation and warmth, and at the same time offer a sense of continuity between generations.\nIt is indeed amazing to note __(57)__ these toy characters were part of the children's playtime __(58)__ technology took over. Central to the museum's mission of learning and discovery, the place offers an educational experience which helps visitors __(59)__ to the past in an entertaining and informative way. Truly, the Museum of Toys is a magical place where toys come to __(60)__. Today, it continues to promote that belief.\nAdapted from https://emint.com"},"english-2025-prelim-tao-nan-g6":{"text":"Read the passage and answer questions 66 to 75.\n\n**1** Creak... The closet door groaned in protest as I tugged it open, its hinges squealing like they, too, wanted no part in what lay inside. Hanging limply before me was a rabbit costume – not the soft, storybook kind, but a garish ensemble of neon pink fur with ears that drooped like they had given up hope. Its oversized head sagged to one side. My heart sank. This was it. My debut performance. My graduation day. A room with an expectant audience... and that was what I had to wear? Taking in the **scene**, I noticed the others – fluffy sheep, dignified goats, even cows that looked halfway decent. Somehow, everyone's outfit looked far more presentable than mine. There and then, I had the urge to slip away.\n**2** \"Where are you going, Gary?\" Leon's voice stopped me. He was fiddling with the long frayed tail of his goat costume, looking as ridiculous as I felt, yet standing tall all the same. He gave a look – the kind that only best friends would, halfway between challenge and comfort. \"It's okay to be nervous,\" he said, his voice filled with quiet determination. \"We all start somewhere. You've put in blood, sweat and tears for this. You're not backing out now, are you? We're all in this together.\" His words hit me. The long rehearsals, the endless retakes, the body aches. Had I forgotten all **those**? I took a deep breath. Not for the costumes but the effort. Not for the pink rabbit but for the people who believed in me. I was not going to run away. Not this time.\n**3** Two days later at the full-dress rehearsal, my confidence cracked. The auditorium was buzzing with quiet chatter. My silly costume made every movement a chore; each hop felt unnatural, blown out of proportion. I shuffled out, my heart pounding like a drum. The spotlight lit the stage as if it were preparing to expose my secret. I began my scene. Then came the giggles. Small at first, then spreading like wildfire. My shoulders slumped when I stumbled over a line. Laughter. More laughter. The gnawing doubt clawed at me – was that failure? What a disaster! If there were a trapdoor, I would gladly vanish. Exiting the stage after my part, my oversized foot caught on a mess of wires. Like a puppet with its strings cut, I tripped and landed on my knees. Laughter exploded. **Lying there, humiliation scorched my face.** All of a sudden, something changed. I remembered Leon's words. \"We all start somewhere.\" I knew I had to face my fears. Slowly, the nerves faded away.\n**4** Then came the opening night. The curtain rose to thunderous applause. My stomach churned with butterflies, but this time, I welcomed them. As I hopped into the spotlight, the audience erupted into laughter – not mockery, but delight. Every exaggerated movement, every silly twitch of my ears added to the joy. I was not being laughed at. Instead, my antics were making the guests laugh. Backstage, Leon flashed me a thumbs-up. The fears were irrational. I had made peace with them.\n**5** \"See? You did it! You're a natural,\" he said when the final applause rang out. I smiled, the joy of the moment finally sinking in.\n**6** \"Thanks for talking me out of leaving. **It** isn't so bad after all,\" I replied, tugging at the floppy ears. \"This outfit still looks ridiculous.\" Leon grinned. In that pink fluff, I had learnt about courage and that the stage was not about perfection. It was about showing up, even when I was scared. My debut performance was a success."},"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g1":{"text":"For each question from 16 to 20, choose the word closest in meaning to the underlined word(s).\n\nFather had made up his mind. \"Ellen will be taking care of you. Stop your whining right now! Nothing will change my mind!\" he said with __(16: finality)__.\nI sensed my life was about to change __(17: drastically)__ when Ellen began taking charge of me. Father __(18: confirmed)__ my worst fears when he added, \"Shu Ma will leave tonight.\" They were getting rid of my Shu Ma just like other families getting rid of servants who had __(19: outlived)__ their usefulness.\n\"I don't want Ellen! I don't like her!\" I cried. \"I want Shu Ma!\"\nEver kind and patient, Ellen bit her lip and put on a brave face even in my stormiest moments. Father was less understanding. \"We'll see about that, young man,\" he growled __(20: sullenly)__. I knew then that I had better stop throwing my tantrums.\nAdapted from 'Things That Must Not Be Forgotten – A Childhood in Wartime China' by Michael David Kwan"},"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g2":{"text":"Study the poster (Text 1) and the extract from an online article (Text 2) and answer questions 21 to 25.\n\n++Text 1++\nThe text below shows a poster.\n[[img1]]\n++Text 2++\nThe extract below is taken from an online article.\nWearing the right shoes is important for every runner whether they are experienced athletes or beginners. A shoe's weight is just one of several factors runners should consider. Good running shoes do more than keep the feet comfortable. They play a key role in helping runners perform well and in preventing injuries.\nFirstly, proper running shoes provide strong support and sufficient cushioning. When a runner's feet hit the ground, the shoes help to absorb the impact. This reduces pressure on the feet, ankles and knees, making running safer. It also lowers the risk of common injuries such as sprains, muscle strain and joint pain.\nIn addition, running shoes are designed to help the body move correctly. They improve balance and support smoother, more controlled movements while running. With better stability and posture, runners can run for longer periods without discomfort.\nIn conclusion, choosing the right pair of running shoes may seem like a simple decision but that is far from the truth. Think about the features that will suit you best. Not every runner needs shoes with all sorts of fancy features. The right shoes help runners enjoy their runs, improve their performance and protect their bodies from injury.","images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g2-img1","alt":"Poster with a picture of a running shoe that has a wing at the heel. Text: Join us for the sales launch of the FALCON! DON'T RUN, FLY! Exclusively for Sports House members at the Vista City Outlet. 12 December at 10 a.m. $129.90 Members' Price. A total of 88 pairs will be released. Reservations and online purchases are not available. Six feature boxes: A) Lightweight: Makes running easier and faster. B) Cushioned Comfort: Keeps your feet protected and free from blisters. C) Grippy Soles: Helps you stay steady on wet surfaces. D) Visibility: Reflective materials keep you visible in low light. E) Durability: Built to last through many kilometres. F) Support: Provides stability for injury-free running. Below the boxes: Not every pair is made for the same purpose. Which features would work best for you? Visit www.sportshouse.sg for more information."}]},"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g3":{"text":"There are 10 blanks, numbered 26 to 35, in the passage below. From the list of words given, choose the most suitable word for each blank. Write its letter (A to Q) in the blank. The letters (I) and (O) have been omitted to avoid confusion during marking.\n**EACH WORD CAN BE USED ONLY ONCE.**\n[[table1]]\n\nFor thousands of years, people have admired ivory for its beauty and strength.\nDuring the Stone Age, ivory from the mammoth, an extinct ancestor of the elephant, __(26)__ used to make household items, tools, weapons and ornaments. Durable, smooth, easy to carve and cool __(27)__ the touch, ivory was considered a valuable material. It was __(28)__ precious that many people referred to it as \"white gold\".\nBefore plastic was invented, ivory was carved into many useful and decorative objects, __(29)__ as chopsticks, musical instruments and furniture. Even after plastic became widely available, wealthy people continued to use ivory as ornaments __(30)__ they regarded it as a symbol of wealth and prestige.\nAs demand for ivory increased, it became highly sought __(31)__. African elephants, which generally have larger and heavier tusks than __(32)__ Asian counterparts, became the main targets of poachers. Although there is now an international ban __(33)__ the ivory trade, illegal hunting still continues in many parts of the world.\n__(34)__ stronger efforts are made to protect these animals, conservationists fear that African elephants could disappear __(35)__ the next twenty years.\nProtecting endangered animals is everyone's responsibility if we want future generations to enjoy the rich diversity of wildlife.\nAdapted from \"Little Red Dot 29 Aug 2006\"","tables":[{"id":"table1","rows":[["(A) after","(D) because","(G) on","(K) their","(N) was"],["(B) and","(E) before","(H) so","(L) to","(P) when"],["(C) at","(F) into","(J) such","(M) unless","(Q) within"]]}]},"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g4":{"text":"Each of the underlined words contains either a spelling or grammatical error. Write the correct word in each of the boxes.\n\nJanet stood in the middle of the stage, starting her speech. She had often wondered why people listened spellbound whenever Dave, her brother, spoke. She was __(36: begin)__ to understand that it was not just the way he spoke but how he said it. A flick of the wrist here, a pause there, a sudden unexpected smile and his listeners were __(37: cabtivatted)__. Practising her own speech over the last week, Janet had incorporated some of his skills. She refused to give in to her __(38: nervous)__ this time.\nNow as Janet spoke about the drought in the village, she knew how to modulate her voice, when to pause for effect, even to __(39: jescher)__ a little with her hands. __(40: Eloquent)__, she talked of the poor harvest, of her father's __(41: daspparate)__ needs to make ends meet and of her father's sacrifices for his children. At only one point did she __(42: foltor)__. When she started to describe her father while standing behind the podium, she __(43: stammer)__ and could not continue.\nA __(44: fimilair)__ face had appeared in the audience. Instinctively, Janet raised her right hand and pointed in his direction. \"He's the reason why I am speaking here today!\" The audience erupted __(45: to)__ a roar. \"That's my father!\"\nAdapted from \"Rice Without Rain\" by Minfong Ho"},"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g5":{"text":"Fill in each blank with a suitable word.\n\nSingapore imports much of its food because of its small land size.\nThe country's __(46)__ land means that local farms cannot produce enough food to feed the entire population. As a result, Singapore depends heavily on __(47)__ food from many different countries. __(48)__, unexpected events such as extreme weather, global conflicts or disruptions to transport routes may make food supplies more __(49)__ to interruptions. These events could even lead to temporary food __(50)__ in some parts of the world.\nTo strengthen food security, Singapore has invested in __(51)__ farming methods that make better use of every available space. One example is vertical farming, where vegetables are grown on stacked shelves __(52)__ of across wide fields. This allows farmers to produce a larger __(53)__ of crops while using much less land.\nMany farms also rely on modern farming techniques. Sensors monitor light, temperature and humidity so that crops receive the __(54)__ amount of water and nutrients. As a result, farmers can __(55)__ the growth of their crops while avoiding unnecessary waste of water and energy.\nSome farms recycle water and use it more efficiently, helping to __(56)__ valuable natural resources. Since the vegetables are grown locally, they reach supermarkets more __(57)__, allowing consumers to enjoy greater freshness than vegetables transported __(58)__ long distances.\nAlthough local farms cannot meet the entire __(59)__ for food, they play an important role in building a more __(60)__ food supply for Singapore's future.\nUrban farming demonstrates how careful planning, technology and innovation can help Singapore prepare for future challenges.\nWritten by: Alvin Soh"},"english-2026-prelim-acsj-g6":{"text":"Read the passage and answer questions 66 to 75.\n\n**1** In the ancient kingdom of Tiwana, two cousins, Trev and Dev, trained under the kingdom's most respected sculptor, Master Govin. Although they trained together from a young age, they were very different. Trev's extraordinary natural talent quickly earned him the admiration of visitors but years of praise had made him proud. He became dismissive of weaker apprentices and was determined to prove he was the best. He was unbothered by the words and feelings of others. Dev, however, lacked Trev's natural brilliance. His early sculptures were rough and uneven but he was patient and hardworking. Whenever Master Govin corrected him, Dev listened carefully. He observed experienced craftsmen, practised tirelessly and gradually improved.\n**2** One summer, the King of Tiwana announced a competition to select a sculpture for the new Hall of Wisdom. The winner would receive a royal title and a chest of gold. While many sculptors saw it as a chance to showcase their skills, Trev saw **this** as an opportunity to earn fame.\n**3** Ignoring Master Govin's advice to focus on the craft rather than recognition, Trev chose a huge slab of marble and began carving a towering warrior dressed in elaborate armour. His sculpture was designed to impress. Dev took a different approach. Before touching his tools, he spent several days travelling through villages and marketplaces, observing ordinary people. He watched labourers working under the scorching sun and parents caring for their children. Inspired by what he had seen, he decided to sculpt an elderly farmer carrying a tired child on his shoulders.\n**4** As the weeks passed, Trev worked hastily. Whenever cracks or uneven surfaces appeared, he concealed them under carvings instead of repairing them properly. Trev was only concerned with the sculpture's appearance. He paid little attention to its durability. \"People will regard my work highly no matter what,\" he said to himself, smirking with pride. Meanwhile, Dev worked steadily. Rather than focussing on impressing others, he carefully carved the farmer's weathered hands, tired posture and gentle expression. His sculpture was simple but every detail reflected patience and care. When visitors entered the workshop to watch them work, they gravitated towards Trev's spectacular warrior. Seeing **this**, Trev mocked his cousin's ordinary subject. \"Your sculpture is not getting you anything. Your common man pales in comparison to my towering warrior,\" he scoffed. \"Sometimes,\" Dev replied calmly, \"ordinary things are what matter most.\"\n**5** Finally, the day of judgement arrived. The palace courtyard was filled with spectators who were excited to find out which sculpture would make it to the Hall of Wisdom. Both sculptures had their own set of admirers. The judges decided to assess Trev's sculpture first. However, just as they were inspecting it, a faint crack echoed through the courtyard. Moments later, a large fracture spread across the statue's chest before both of its arms broke away. Trev's face turned beetroot red and he lowered his head as the broken pieces lay scattered across the ground. The hidden flaws he had covered up had finally been exposed. Later that afternoon, the judges announced Dev as the winner. Although his sculpture lacked grandeur, it moved everyone. The judges praised the patience, craftsmanship and understanding reflected in every detail.\n**6** That evening, Trev returned to the workshop and studied Dev's sculpture. For the first time, he noticed the warmth in the farmer's eyes, the careful shaping of **his** worn hands and the quiet beauty that could only come from patience. Seeing that, realisation finally dawned on him; it was never just about the end result. Master Govin then came into the workshop and reminded him, \"Young man, the competition has never been about producing the most impressive sculpture. It was about becoming the kind of sculptor capable of creating one.\"\n**7** From that day on, Trev understood that his greatest failure had not been losing the competition but believing that success was measured by recognition alone. Years later, people no longer remembered the royal prize. Instead, they remembered the sculptor who had allowed the journey to shape both his masterpiece and his character.\nWritten by: Rubinder Kaur"},"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g1":{"text":"For each question from 16 to 20, choose the word closest in meaning to the underlined word(s).\n\nThe Singapore Zoo celebrated the birth of three Sri Lankan leopard cubs early this year. This arrival marked a significant __(16: milestone)__ as these were the first-ever cubs of their kind to be born here. As there are fewer than 800 of these leopards left in the wild, experts hope the birth of these cubs will __(17: bring up)__ the shrinking population of this endangered species. Mandai Wildlife Group curator, Mr Anand Kumar, said that witnessing the healthy cubs __(18: thrive)__ and develop distinct personalities was __(19: incredibly rewarding)__. He added that the two male cubs were more laid-back while the female cub was observed to be __(20: feisty)__. The cubs can be found in the Wild Africa exhibit every Wednesday, Saturday and Sunday.\nAdapted from www.straitstimes.com"},"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g2":{"text":"Study the poster (Text 1) and the extract from an online article (Text 2) and answer questions 21 to 25.\n\n++Text 1++\nThe text below shows a poster related to running.\n[[img1]]\n++Text 2++\nThe extract below is taken from an online article.\nFor many, running is seen as a symbol of health and vitality. Because of its health benefits, some people insist that it is the best or most important form of exercise. The truth is, there are many other ways to enjoy similar health benefits. Activities such as cycling or playing sports can also help improve heart health and strengthen the body. What matters most is staying active regularly and enjoying the process.\nRunning can be an intense activity, especially for beginners. If people run too often, too fast or for too long, they will likely experience injuries such as muscle strain or joint pain. When this happens, some individuals may feel discouraged and stop exercising altogether. Health experts often recommend matching activities with one's current fitness level. Whether it is running, walking, or playing games with friends, regularly engaging in enjoyable activities is what helps people stay healthy in the long run. In addition, maintaining a balanced diet and drinking enough water help support overall health.","images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g2-img1","alt":"Poster. At the top is a photograph of runners' legs and running shoes on a road. Title: House of Runners. Run Your Way to a Healthier Life. In a box: Want to feel stronger and more energetic? Run! Benefits: Improves heart health (picture of a heart); Strengthens muscles (picture of a flexed arm); Increases stamina (picture of a battery). Below the box: Our friendly members meet every Tuesday evening and Saturday morning for group runs. Runners of all levels come together to exercise, support one another and stay motivated. Whether you are new to running or already enjoy it, we welcome you! At the bottom: Interested? Contact Jim at 9123 4567 to sign up."}]},"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g3":{"text":"There are 10 blanks, numbered 26 to 35, in the passage below. From the list of words given, choose the most suitable word for each blank. Write its letter (A to Q) in the blank. The letters (I) and (O) have been omitted to avoid confusion during marking.\n**EACH WORD CAN BE USED ONLY ONCE.**\n[[table1]]\n\nA 25-year-old Singaporean, Daniel Goh, has gained attention online for creating Chimbridge, a Singlish dictionary. The project features humorous entries inspired __(26)__ local expressions and everyday speech commonly used in Singapore. Many of these entries include creative phrases and one of the examples __(27)__ \"lao hong biscuit\", which describes something that has become outdated over time.\nThe creator, __(28)__ started the project as a personal passion, wanted to showcase the uniqueness of Singlish __(29)__ highlight how language reflects identity in society. He believes Singlish plays __(30)__ unique role in shaping how Singaporeans communicate __(31)__ others in daily life.\nThrough Chimbridge, he hopes to provide a platform __(32)__ creative expression and cultural sharing among online users __(33)__ different walks of life. The dictionary has rapidly grown __(34)__ popularity over time, gaining attention from local and international audiences. __(35)__ success has also encouraged others to contribute ideas to the project in meaningful ways. Do refer to the Chimbridge dictionary if you ever need Singlish references.\nAdapted from: www.channelnewsasia.com","tables":[{"id":"table1","rows":[["(A) a","(D) are","(G) from","(K) its","(N) who"],["(B) an","(E) by","(H) in","(L) it's","(P) whom"],["(C) and","(F) for","(J) is","(M) or","(Q) with"]]}]},"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g4":{"text":"Each of the underlined words contains either a spelling or grammatical error. Write the correct word in each of the boxes.\n\nWhen Mr Rakcent Wong and his family made plans to travel around the world two years ago, many could not understand why they would do so. However, it was an __(36: opurtooniti)__ they did not want to miss. Over the past year, acquaintances they met have often asked if life overseas was more __(37: apeeling)__ for them. Certainly, it was. Nonetheless, travelling around the world has __(38: shows)__ the family how fortunate they already are. Singapore may be short __(39: in)__ natural resources, but it offers safety and __(40: stable)__. To them, Singapore is the best place to settle down and build a home.\nIn retrospect, the family found their world travels __(41: bearnefical)__ to them. Particularly, Mr Wong and his wife __(42: rekonaised)__ that their young son had experienced the __(43: great)__ gain among them. At a young age, he had cultivated an __(44: advencheras)__ spirit. Now that they are back in Singapore, it does not mark the end of a journey. Instead, it is __(45: simple)__ a new season in their journey together. Mr Wong is ready to build many more memories with his family.\nAdapted from: www.channelnewsasia.com"},"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g5":{"text":"Fill in each blank with a suitable word.\n\nDo students in Singapore like the food from their school canteens? For those who have a wide __(46)__ of food options to choose from, the answer is probably yes. __(47)__, for others, their experiences may not be as positive.\nAddressing this issue may not be a __(48)__ of cake, but some schools have adopted a central kitchen system to provide meals for their students. This change is largely __(49)__ to the decreasing number of canteen vendors. Many stallholders find it hard to __(50)__ with the long working hours and rising operating costs over time. As a result, many of them are __(51)__ to run food stalls in schools, despite the pressing need for canteen vendors.\nUnder the central kitchen system, meals are prepared in a main kitchen before being __(52)__ into bento boxes and delivered to schools. While this seems like a __(53)__ to the problem of having a shortage of canteen vendors in some schools, it also __(54)__ concerns among parents and students. For one, food that is transported over a long __(55)__ may not always be as fresh as it was when first cooked. Students may also have __(56)__ food choices than before, especially if only a limited menu is offered.\nTo improve the situation, central kitchens are making changes. Some work with nutritionists to __(57)__ meals are healthy and tasty. Schools also collect feedback from students so that changes to the menu can be made __(58)__ on their food preferences. It is __(59)__ noting that this system will not be implemented across all schools. It is meant to serve as an option for schools that face difficulties finding vendors, rather __(60)__ replace the traditional canteen completely. As schools continue to review this system, further improvements are expected to make school dining both convenient and enjoyable for students.\nAdapted from: The Straits Times"},"english-2026-prelim-ai-tong-g6":{"text":"Read the passage and answer questions 66 to 75.\n\n**1** Nick trudged out of the school gate with a heavy heart. It had been a dreadful day. In the morning, he had been reprimanded by his English teacher for forgetting to bring his homework. The stinging words still echoed in his mind, making his **cheeks burn with embarrassment**. During group work later in the day, his classmates had barely acknowledged his presence and brushed aside his ideas, leaving him feeling unimportant and excluded. To make matters worse, a stack of assignments awaited him, each one seeming more overwhelming than the last. By the time school ended, Nick felt utterly drained. It seemed as though nothing had gone right for him that day.\n**2** Dark clouds loomed overhead as Nick made his way towards the traffic light. Just as he stepped off the curb, a sudden downpour began. Caught off guard by **this**, Nick froze, unsure of what to do. At that moment, a gentle voice spoke beside him. \"Would you like to share my umbrella?\" Turning around, Nick saw a friendly lady with a tote bag slung on her shoulder smiling at him. Without waiting for a reply, she tilted **it** to shelter him from the rain. Nick nodded gratefully. As they crossed the road together, a warmth stirred in Nick's heart. It was a simple gesture, yet it brightened his gloomy mood.\n**3** After parting ways with the lady, Nick hurried towards the MRT station, weaving through the crowd to escape the rain. He tapped his card at the gantry and made his way onto the platform, his damp uniform clinging to his skin. Despite the **discomfort**, Nick patiently watched the train approach. He sighed deeply, trying to ease the uncertainty that filled his **burdened** heart. \"How can I complete all that homework by tonight?\" he lamented softly. Boarding the train, Nick found a seat and stared blankly at the floor, still thinking about the events of the day.\n**4** Suddenly, a commotion broke out. A commuter, who looked visibly unwell, had vomited on the train floor. Immediately, several passengers recoiled in disgust, covering their noses and moving away. Moments later, a young man stepped forward swiftly. Calmly, he approached the commuter and rendered assistance. Taking out some tissue paper, he began cleaning up the mess, his actions steady and uncomplaining. Nick watched in awe. Despite the unpleasant situation, the young man chose to help instead of turning away. Perhaps Singaporeans were not as selfish as he had thought, and kindness still existed in society.\n**5** When Nick alighted from the train, the rain had let up. As he walked home, he noticed a concerning sight – an **elderly lady was in distress.** Her electric wheelchair had run out of battery and she was stranded at the side of the path. Beside her stood a man who was struggling to push the heavy wheelchair forward. Without hesitation, Nick hurried over. \"Let me help,\" he offered. Together, they pushed the wheelchair slowly along the path. It was not easy, as the wheels resisted their efforts. However, Nick and the man persevered and they finally reached the old lady's flat. After a quick exchange, the man rushed off as he was running late for his appointment. With gratitude in her eyes, the old lady looked at Nick and uttered, \"Thank you, young man.\" Then, she added warmly, \"You have a kind heart.\" Her words lingered with him as he made his way home. For the first time that day, Nick smiled.\n**6** As he reflected on the events of the afternoon, Nick realised that while his day had started poorly, it did not have to end that way. The kindness he had witnessed, and taken part in, had shifted his perspective. Life would always have its challenges. With a lighter heart, Nick walked on, determined to face tomorrow with renewed positivity.\nWritten by: Casey Low"},"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g1":{"text":"For each question from 16 to 20, choose the word closest in meaning to the underlined word(s).\n\nWe know what number to call for an emergency. Many of us __(16: assume)__ that when we call an emergency number, help will arrive quickly, no matter where we are in the world.\nHowever in Kenya, there are more than 50 numbers for emergency services. Finding an emergency crew can be a __(17: potentially)__ lengthy process there. The ambulance may take two to three hours to arrive. To solve this problem, a mobile app called Flare __(18: compiles)__ all the available information on ambulances onto a single platform. Users of the app can choose the most appropriate services based on where the vehicle is as well as the __(19: expertise)__ of the crew.\nIt also uses real-time traffic data to help ambulances __(20: navigate)__ through the city's traffic jams. Since the use of the app, ambulance response time has gone down significantly.\nAdapted from: https://www.bbc.com/news/business-42064067"},"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g2":{"text":"Study the webpage (Text 1) and the interview (Text 2) and answer questions 21 to 25.\n\n++Text 1++\nThe text below shows a webpage from a wildlife website.\n[[img1]]\n++Text 2++\nThe excerpt below is taken from an interview with Park Officer, Ms Hazel Yoon.\n[[table1]]","tables":[{"id":"table1","rows":[["**Interviewer:**","Why is it important for visitors to behave responsibly when they see wildlife?"],["**Ms Hazel Yoon:**","Singapore's parks and nature reserves are home to many wild animals. Seeing them can be an exciting experience, but visitors should remember that these animals are not pets. We should enjoy observing them without disturbing their natural behaviour."],["**Interviewer:**","Could you tell us what can happen if people interact with wildlife in the wrong way?"],["**Ms Hazel Yoon:**","When animals become used to being around people, they may come too close to visitors or search bags and picnic areas for food. This can lead to unsafe situations for both people and wildlife. Wild animals are healthiest when they continue to look for food in their natural environment instead of relying on humans to feed them."],["**Interviewer:**","What should visitors do when they come across wildlife?"],["**Ms Hazel Yoon:**","Observe the animals quietly from a safe distance and avoid touching or feeding them. If an animal appears injured or behaves in an unusual manner, report it to the relevant authorities so that trained officers can help."],["**Interviewer:**","What message would you like visitors to remember?"],["**Ms Hazel Yoon:**","Every visitor has a part to play in protecting nature. By respecting wildlife and leaving animals undisturbed, we can help to keep our parks safe and enjoyable for everyone."]]}],"images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g2-img1","alt":"Webpage at www.wildlifecare.sg. Header: WILDLIFE CARE, with menu links Home, About Us, Wildlife Tips (underlined), Contact Us. Picture of a monitor lizard and an otter beside the heading: Keep Wildlife Wild. Enjoy watching them, but do not feed them. Left box, 'Why we should not feed wildlife', with four icons and points: Animals may stop searching for their own food. Animals may approach people, expecting to be fed. Human food is not suitable for wildlife and can make them ill. Leftover food attracts pests such as rats. Right box, 'What you should do', with three icons and points: Watch animals from a safe distance. Throw rubbish into bins. Let wildlife find its own food. Below the boxes is a picture of monkeys, a tortoise, squirrels and a bird. Banner at the bottom: A little care goes a long way. Keep our wildlife healthy and truly wild!"}]},"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g3":{"text":"There are 10 blanks, numbered 26 to 35, in the passage below. From the list of words given, choose the most suitable word for each blank. Write its letter (A to Q) in the blank. The letters (I) and (O) have been omitted in order to avoid confusion during marking.\n**EACH WORD CAN BE USED ONLY ONCE.**\n[[table1]]\n\nReading is often seen as a hobby, but research suggests it may also support better health and a longer life. People who read regularly tend __(26)__ experience less stress and enjoy stronger mental mental well-being. When reading, the brain slows down and is more engaged. The calming effect created from reading, similar to relaxation exercises, __(27)__ what helps our body manage stress more effectively.\nReading also plays an important role __(28)__ keeping the brain active. As readers follow a story, they __(29)__ to remember details, understand new ideas, and imagine situations. Over time, reading helps the brain stay flexible and sharp, __(30)__ as people grow older.\nIn addition, reading helps readers understand their emotions and see situations __(31)__ different perspectives. __(32)__ improves empathy and social understanding. Since loneliness is linked to poor health, staying mentally and emotionally grounded __(33)__ reading widely can be beneficial.\nExperts say that one does not need to read for __(34)__ extended amount of time for reading to be helpful. Spending a short time each day reading books is enough to support brain health. __(35)__ through printed books or audiobooks, reading remains a simple habit that offers lasting benefits. Let's pick up a book and start reading now!\nAdapted from https://www.nationalgeographic.com/health/article/reading-longevity-brain-health","tables":[{"id":"table1","rows":[["(A) a","(D) by","(G) from","(K) in","(N) to"],["(B) an","(E) even","(H) had","(L) is","(P) whether"],["(C) are","(F) for","(J) have","(M) this","(Q) with"]]}]},"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g4":{"text":"Each of the underlined words contains either a spelling or grammatical error. Write the correct word in each of the boxes.\n\nLily dreaded visiting her grandmother's house every Sunday. While her cousins played video games, her grandmother always insisted that Lily __(36: helping)__ in the kitchen, learning traditional recipes that seemed completely __(37: irellevant)__.\n\"Why do I need to learn this old-fashioned cooking?\" Lily complained. Her grandmother smiled patiently. \"__(38: This recipes)__ carry our family's history, dear. Each ingredient __(39: telling)__ a story about our ancestors.\" Lily rolled her eyes and participated rather __(40: rilaktantly)__, following instructions without __(41: genuwin)__ interest.\nYears later, Lily went overseas to study in the university. She felt incredibly homesick. The cafeteria food tasted __(42: blanned)__ compared to her family's meals. Feeling particularly __(43: nostaljik)__, Lily decided to recreate her grandmother's dumpling recipe. __(44: Until)__ Lily tasted the dumplings, tears filled her eyes. They carried the __(45: warm)__ of home and love. She immediately called her grandmother. \"Grandma, I finally understand. Thank you for teaching me our family's treasures.\""},"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g5":{"text":"Fill in each blank with a suitable word.\n\nSingapore is a small island with limited land space. In the 1970s, as air travel became more popular worldwide, Singapore faced a serious problem. The country __(46)__ a bigger airport to handle more planes and passengers, but where __(47)__ they build it?\nIn the late 1970s, the demand __(48)__ air travel around the world rose dramatically as more people __(49)__ by air for business and holidays. As a result, the former Singapore International Airport at Paya Lebar struggled to __(50)__ up with the increase in traffic volume. However, expanding the airport would sacrifice precious land space and create noise pollution over residential areas.\nThe alternative solution was to build a completely new airport in Changi. __(51)__ an airport would be located away from the city centre and planes would fly over water __(52)__ of over residential areas. In __(53)__ to the earlier benefits mentioned, there was also the possibility of future expansion by reclaiming land from the sea.\nBuilding Changi Airport was a mammoth undertaking. The airport was planned to be at least five times __(54)__ than the airport at Paya Lebar. To __(55)__ the new airport, existing land had to be cleared and new land had to be reclaimed from the sea. Given the scale of the project, the construction process encountered countless difficulties. __(56)__ being fraught with challenges, the project still finished ahead of schedule.\nMoving an entire airport is very complex so the move from Paya Lebar to Changi was carefully planned. The hard work paid __(57)__ as the move to Changi Airport happened in just one night with the last flight leaving Paya Lebar in the evening of 30 June 1981.\nChangi Airport __(58)__ operating on 1 July 1981 with the arrival of Singapore Airlines flight SQ101 from Kuala Lumpur, marking the beginning of a new era for Singapore aviation. __(59)__ then, Changi Airport has expanded significantly, from one terminal to four, with a fifth terminal expected soon.\nToday, Changi Airport stands as one of the world's most admired airports. This proves that with foresight and perseverance, even the most difficult challenges can be __(60)__. These values continue to guide Singapore's aviation industry till today.\nAdapted from https://www.airporthistory.org/blue-concourse/singapore-changi-airport-turns-40"},"english-2026-prelim-catholic-high-g6":{"text":"Read the passage and answer questions 66 to 75.\n\n**1** Maya pushed open the heavy wooden door and stepped into the library. The paint on the towering bookshelves had flaked away in places, exposing the dark wood beneath. Cobwebs occupied the forgotten corners of the high ceiling as golden sunlight streamed through the tall windows. \"This place is seriously old,\" whispered her younger brother, Sam, his voice echoing in the vast silence. \"Welcome to the Seaside Library,\" Mrs Chen, the elderly librarian, smiled.\n**2** Stumbling across this forgotten library while exploring the older part of town during the school holidays, Maya could not wait to share her find with Sam. Unlike the clean and smooth tables in the school library, the wooden tables here bore countless scratches and stains from decades past. There were no computers humming here – just the peaceful stillness of a place that had once been well-loved and well-used.\n**3** Maya paused in front of a huge wooden cabinet with hundreds of tiny drawers. Looking at Maya's look of confusion, Mrs Chen chuckled. Mrs Chen slid one open and pulled out a small handwritten card. \"This is a card catalogue. Each card tells you where to find a book on the shelves,\" Mrs Chen explained gently. \"Before computers, this was how everyone searched for books – drawer by drawer, card by card.\" Maya tried to imagine doing this every single day, instead of simply typing into a search engine on the screen, like how she would at her school library.\n**4** \"It's a little empty for a beautiful place like this,\" Maya sighed in disappointment, looking around. \"We don't get many visitors these days since information can be found easily on the internet.\" Mrs Chen's eyes grew wistful. \"But there's something magical about holding a real book. You don't just hold the book, you share the memories, emotions and traces of the people who held it before.\"\n**5** Maya smiled politely, unsure of what she meant. She reached for the nearest book, a worn poetry collection, and cracked it open. Inside the front cover, someone had written in faded ink: Eleanor, 1943. Maya ran her thumb over the looping handwriting and felt a warm sense of connection. Whoever Eleanor was, she had held this exact book and read these exact words. The internet had never once made Maya feel that.\n**6** Across the room, Sam found a collection of handwritten letters from local residents during World War II, carefully preserved in a glass case. He did not know any of them, had never heard their names before, and yet every word written by them felt heavy and real.\n**7** As the afternoon wore on, the siblings sat at different corners, turning brittle and yellowed pages slowly, each paper giving off a warm, faint smell. Maya found herself wondering about the people who had held this same book before her. Had they sat in this same quiet room, turning these same pages, feeling the same sense of peace? Maya finally understood what Mrs Chen meant earlier – the magic of holding a real book.\n**8** \"The council wants to close this library next month,\" Mrs Chen mentioned. \"They say it's too expensive to maintain, and not enough people use it.\" Maya's heart sank. The thought of all the treasures she found disappearing – Eleanor's name, the old maps, the wartime letters – made her feel like she was losing something that could never be replaced.\n**9** \"We'll be back tomorrow,\" Maya promised. \"And we'll bring our friends too.\" Mrs Chen's face brightened with hope. \"That'd be wonderful, dear. Perhaps there's still a future for old places like this.\""},"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g1":{"text":"For each question from 16 to 20, choose the word closest in meaning to the underlined word(s).\n\nThe sports hall hummed with tension. The final minutes of the inter-school floorball championships ticked away. Wei Han faced a __(16: formidable)__ defense from the opposing team, who had remained undefeated all season. He had __(17: conscientiously)__ practised his shots every afternoon under the scorching sun at the school's floorball court. Despite his aching limbs, Wei Han's __(18: resilience)__ kept him sprinting towards the goal.\nWith seconds left, he __(19: noticed)__ the goalkeeper leaning slightly to the left. He made a shrewd move, pretending he was __(20: going for)__ a high shot before sliding the ball into the opposite corner. Wei Han felt elated as the whistle blew and his teammates swarmed around him in jubilant celebration. It was a victory that proved determination was the key to success."},"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g2":{"text":"Study the poster for a school bag (Text 1) and the extract taken from an online article (Text 2) and answer questions 21 to 25.\n\n++Text 1++\nThe text below shows an advertisement for a bag.\n[[img1]]\n++Text 2++\nThe extract below is taken from an online article.\nSchool bags are designed to be both roomy and trendy, which makes them appealing to students. Lately, more focus has been put on making school bags look good. For many students, their school bag is also a way to express their personality and personal style. Is that true for you, too?\nWhile it may seem convenient to fit everything into one bag, have you considered the weight? Carrying too many items strains your back. This may cause short-term discomfort and lead to long-term posture problems. To cushion the effect, you should carry a school bag that fits comfortably on your back. Features such as wide straps and padded backs help distribute weight more evenly. By choosing a school bag wisely, you can reduce the pressure on your back.\nUltimately, health experts recommend packing only the items needed for the day to keep your school bag light. For example, store certain items in your school locker or keep some materials under your desk if your school allows it. By planning what you bring each day and leaving out unnecessary items, you can protect your back and still have everything you need.","images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g2-img1","alt":"Advertisement. Heading: Peaks School Bag. Carry Everything in Style. Say goodbye to boring school bags. Fit your entire school day into one bag: all your books, stationery, lunch boxes and more! Get organised with a specific space for everything. No more mess or things left behind! In the middle is a picture of a backpack with a front pocket and side pockets. On its left: Suitable for students of all ages! On its right: Wide range of colours to choose from! Below: Designed with students in mind! At the bottom, beside a picture of a smiling boy in school uniform carrying a backpack: \"I love my Peaks school bag! Everything I need for school fits comfortably into it. I can also find and take out any item I need easily!\" – Ansel, primary school student"}]},"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g3":{"text":"There are 10 blanks, numbered 26 to 35, in the passage below. From the list of words given, choose the most suitable word for each blank. Write its letter (A to Q) in the blank. The letters (I) and (O) have been omitted to avoid confusion during marking.\n**EACH WORD CAN BE USED ONLY ONCE.**\n[[table1]]\n\nEvery day, we enjoy fruits without realising how many quiet changes have taken place inside them. The mystery behind fruit ripening __(26)__ still not fully understood by scientists and farmers. Most fruits are picked when they are still firm and green, as they bruise easily __(27)__ left to ripen fully on the tree. The flesh of these fruits __(28)__ been known to contain a gas called ethylene which acts as a signal to begin __(29)__ ripening process. Ethylene spreads through the cells and reaches the parts __(30)__ the seeds develop. As the level of ethylene rises, the fruit softens, turns sweeter and changes colour.\nIn storage rooms, workers __(31)__ slow down the ripening process __(32)__ keeping fruits cool and reducing the amount of ethylene in the air. Later, to speed up the ripening process, they may place unripe fruits into closed boxes with a few ripe bananas __(33)__ have already released a lot of ethylene. Similarly, people sometimes put __(34)__ unripe mango in a paper bag with a ripe banana because this simple trick speeds up the ripening process. Scientists are still studying the process of fruit ripening in the hope __(35)__ using this knowledge to reduce food waste and to keep fruit fresh for a longer period.\nThe next time you watch a hard, green fruit slowly turn soft and sweet, you are witnessing this hidden ripening process at work.","tables":[{"id":"table1","rows":[["(A) a","(D) can","(G) if","(K) of","(N) was"],["(B) an","(E) has","(H) into","(L) the","(P) where"],["(C) by","(F) have","(J) is","(M) this","(Q) which"]]}]},"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g4":{"text":"Each of the underlined words contains either a spelling or grammatical error. Write the correct word in each of the boxes.\n\nIt was Jia Wei's first day back in school. He had already regretted __(36: volentiering)__ for the student council project. He and three classmates were tasked __(37: by)__ planning an exhibition on Singapore's __(38: enviromantle)__ efforts. The principal had also made it clear that everyone __(39: are)__ expecting something impressive. As Jia Wei __(40: skrold)__ through old photographs of the Singapore harbour, he noticed a photograph of a coastline that looked nothing like the present one. The caption said it was taken before land reclamation had __(41: begin)__ in the area. He felt a sudden urge to find out what the town might lose if people only cared about __(42: sprauling)__ shopping malls and new MRT lines. __(43: Althought)__, his friends were far more interested in designing beautiful posters instead of checking their facts. After a long discussion, the group still had no clear __(44: konsept)__. Walking home, Jia Wei realised __(45: what)__ easily a city's memories could disappear beneath glass and concrete. The thought stayed with him long after he reached home."},"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g5":{"text":"Fill in each blank with a suitable word.\n\nZubir Said was the composer who wrote \"Majulah Singapura\", the national anthem of Singapore. Born in 1907 in Sumatra, Zubir grew up in a poor household where money was always __(46)__ enough but music was always present. __(47)__ a teenager, he left home and sailed to Singapore. His __(48)__ from childhood was to become a professional musician. The first __(49)__ he had was playing the violin and piano in silent movie theatres, bringing the film to __(50)__ with his music. The work was tiring and the pay was low, but these early experiences honed his skills and confidence as a composer.\nIn 1958, the City Council of Singapore invited him to __(51)__ a special song for a civic celebration. He __(52)__ the task readily. He walked along crowded streets and quiet kampungs, hoping __(53)__ would inspire him to write the perfect song. He __(54)__ the song to capture the aspirations of a young nation – its courage, unity and hope. After many revisions, he composed a clear and rousing __(55)__ that ordinary people could remember and sing with pride. The song impressed the leaders, and it was later __(56)__ as the national anthem of Singapore.\nAlthough he __(57)__ studied at prestigious music schools, Zubir kept improving his musical technique by listening carefully to other composers and reflecting on his own work. He believed that a national anthem __(58)__ not only sound grand but also encourage citizens to serve their country and care for one another.\nToday, students in school sing \"Majulah Singapura\" during assemblies and national __(59)__ like the Primary 5 National Education (NE) Show so that they are __(60)__ of the sacrifices and dreams that helped build Singapore. His story continues to spur young people to contribute in their own ways to the nation."},"english-2026-prelim-chij-olgc-g6":{"text":"Read the passage and answer questions 66 to 75.\n\n**1** Maya had always admired her grandfather's magnificent vegetable garden, which was truly the apple of his eye. It stretched across the entire back corner of their family property. Every morning at precisely six o'clock, she watched him tend to his prized tomatoes, crisp lettuce, and fragrant herbs with meticulous care, treating each plant with the gentle attention of a devoted parent nurturing his child. When her grandfather fell ill and had to stay in hospital for two weeks, Maya's heart sank as she saw her dear grandfather's plants beginning to wilt under the relentless scorching sun.\n**2** \"Mum, can I take care of Grandpa's garden while he's away?\" Maya asked earnestly, her voice trembling with emotion. Her mother gave her a concerned look. \"Are you sure you want to do it? This would be a big responsibility, Maya. Plants need daily attention, you know? It'll be an uphill task for you.\"\n**3** The next morning, Maya woke up early and rushed eagerly to the garden with nervous excitement fluttering wildly in her stomach like trapped butterflies. Although she had watched her grandfather's practised movements countless times, doing it herself the first time felt like an arduous task. The metal watering can was heavier than expected, and she was not sure how much water each plant actually needed. After soaking the thirsty plants thoroughly, she noticed the lettuce leaves looked droopy despite her efforts, making her feel deeply worried.\n**4** Feeling extremely discouraged, Maya called her best friend, Sandie, for advice. \"My grandmother grows vegetables too! Let me ask her for her opinion and I will call you back immediately,\" Sandie offered without hesitation. Within an hour, Sandie arrived breathlessly with her elderly grandmother, Mrs Chen, who began to examine the struggling garden with the sharp, keen eye of an experienced gardener.\n**5** \"You're doing remarkably well, dear, but different plants need different amount of water,\" Mrs Chen explained patiently. \"For example, the soil for lettuce should not be too wet,\" Mrs Chen said. \"Gardening requires patience. **Rome wasn't built in a day, and neither is a beautiful garden.**\"\n**6** Over the next few days, Maya took Sandie's grandmother's advice to heart, taking special care not to overwater the plants, especially the lettuce. Sandie often joined her, and together, they removed stubborn weeds and dead leaves, making the demanding work considerably easier and much more enjoyable. Sandie discovered that the aromatic herbs grew noticeably faster after they talked to them, though Maya suspected **it** was just a coincidence.\n**7** When some destructive aphids suddenly attacked the vulnerable lettuce plants, Maya's heart raced frantically. However, she refused to give up. Instead, she researched effective natural remedies online and prepared a soap solution which she sprinkled onto the affected leaves. She felt immensely proud watching the tiny green invaders disappear completely after her **treatment**. The garden slowly transformed from wilting to thriving under her dedicated care, flourishing beautifully once again.\n**8** Two weeks later, Maya's grandfather returned home to find his garden more beautiful than ever, a truly wonderful sight after his hospital stay. Tears of joy streamed down his cheeks as he saw the healthy plants and Maya's detailed notebook filled with careful observations. \"You've done **something wonderful** here,\" he said, pointing at the plot of land.\n**9** \"Sandie helped me a lot, and Mrs Chen taught us so much,\" Maya replied modestly, her cheeks glowing with pride. \"I never knew gardening required such dedication. It really taught me a lot.\"\n**10** Her grandfather smiled knowingly, his eyes sparkling with happiness. \"The best gardens grow from love and commitment, not just water and sunshine. Would you like to be my gardening partner from now on?\" Maya's face lit up with pure joy as she nodded enthusiastically. Her mother nodded and gave her a thumbs-up."},"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g1":{"text":"For each question from 16 to 20, choose the word closest in meaning to the underlined word(s).\n\nThe sports hall hummed with tension as the final minutes of the inter-school floorball championships ticked away. Wei Han had __(16: conscientiously)__ practised his shots every afternoon under the scorching sun at the school's floorball court, and now all those hours of training were about to be put to the test. Facing a __(17: formidable)__ defence from the opposing team, whose exceptional skills had helped them remain undefeated all season, he refused to back down. Despite his aching limbs, Wei Han's __(18: resilience)__ kept him sprinting towards the goal.\nWith seconds left, he __(19: caught sight of)__ the goalkeeper leaning slightly to the left. He made a shrewd move, pretending he was __(20: going for)__ a high shot before sliding the ball into the opposite corner. Wei Han cheered elatedly as the whistle blew and his teammates swarmed around him in jubilant celebration. It was a victory that proved determination was the key to success."},"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g2":{"text":"Study the poster for a school bag (Text 1) and the extract taken from an online article (Text 2) and answer questions 21 to 25.\n\n++Text 1++\nThe text below shows an advertisement for a bag.\n[[img1]]\n++Text 2++\nThe extract below is taken from an online article.\nSchool bags are essential for students, but choosing the right one and packing it wisely can protect your back while keeping you comfortable, organised and healthy every school day ahead.\nWhile it may seem convenient to fit everything into one bag, have you considered the weight? Carrying too many items strains your back. This may cause short-term discomfort and lead to long-term posture problems. To cushion the effect, you should carry a school bag that fits comfortably on your back. Features such as wide straps and padded backs help distribute weight more evenly. By choosing a school bag wisely, you can reduce the pressure on your back.\nUltimately, health experts recommend packing only the items needed for the day to keep your school bag light. For example, store certain items in your school locker or keep some materials under your desk if your school allows it. By planning what you bring each day and leaving out unnecessary items, you can protect your back and still have everything you need.","images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g2-img1","alt":"Advertisement with a picture of a backpack in the middle. Heading: Peaks School Bag. Slogan: Carry Everything in Style. Text: Say goodbye to boring school bags. Fit your entire school day into one bag — all your books, stationery, lunch boxes and more! Get organised with a specific space for everything. Left of the bag: Suitable for students of all ages! Right of the bag: Wide range of colours to choose from! Below the bag: Designed with you in mind! Bottom banner with a picture of a smiling schoolboy carrying a bag: \"I love my Peaks school bag! Everything I need for school fits comfortably into it. I can also find and take out any item I need easily!\" – Ansel, primary school student"}]},"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g3":{"text":"There are 10 blanks, numbered 26 to 35, in the passage below. From the list of words given, choose the most suitable word for each blank. Write its letter (A to Q) in the blank. The letters (I) and (O) have been omitted to avoid confusion during marking.\n**EACH WORD CAN BE USED ONLY ONCE.**\n[[table1]]\n\nEvery day, we enjoy fruit without realising how many quiet changes have taken place inside them. The mystery behind fruit ripening __(26)__ still not fully understood by scientists and farmers. Most fruits are picked when they are still firm and green, as they bruise easily __(27)__ left to ripen fully on the tree. The flesh of these fruits __(28)__ been known to contain a gas called ethylene which acts as a signal to begin __(29)__ ripening process. Ethylene spreads through the cells and reaches the parts __(30)__ the seeds develop. As the level of ethylene rises, the fruit softens, turns sweeter and changes colour.\nIn storage rooms, workers __(31)__ slow down the ripening process __(32)__ keeping the fruits cool and reducing the amount of ethylene in the air. Later, to speed up the ripening process, they may place unripe fruits into closed boxes with a few ripe bananas __(33)__ have already released a lot of ethylene. Similarly, people sometimes put __(34)__ unripe mango in a paper bag with a ripe banana because this simple trick speeds up the ripening process. Scientists are still studying the process of fruit ripening in the hope __(35)__ using this knowledge to reduce food waste and to keep fruit fresh for a longer period.\nThe next time you watch a hard, green fruit slowly turn soft and sweet, you are witnessing this hidden ripening process at work.","tables":[{"id":"table1","rows":[["(A) a","(D) can","(G) if","(K) of","(N) was"],["(B) an","(E) has","(H) into","(L) the","(P) where"],["(C) by","(F) have","(J) is","(M) this","(Q) which"]]}]},"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g4":{"text":"Each of the underlined words contains either a spelling or grammatical error. Write the correct word in each of the boxes.\n\nIt was Jia Wei's first day back in school. He had already regretted __(36: volentiering)__ for the student council project. He and three classmates were tasked __(37: by)__ planning an exhibition on Singapore's __(38: enviromantle)__ efforts. The principal also made it clear that everyone __(39: are)__ expected to do a good job. As Jia Wei __(40: skrold)__ through old photographs of the Singapore Harbour, he noticed one depicting a coastline that looked nothing like the present one. The caption said it was taken before land reclamation had __(41: begin)__ in the area. He felt a sudden urge to find out what the town might lose if people only cared about __(42: sprauling)__ shopping malls and new MRT lines. __(43: Although)__, his friends were far more __(44: in··erested)__ in designing beautiful posters instead of checking their facts. After a long discussion, the group still had no clear concept. Walking home, Jia Wei realised __(45: what)__ easily a city's memories could disappear beneath glass and concrete. The thought stayed with him long after he reached home."},"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g5":{"text":"Fill in each blank with a suitable word.\n\nZubir Said was the composer who wrote Majulah Singapura, the national anthem of Singapore. Born in 1907 in Sumatra, Zubir grew up in a poor household where money was always __(46)__ enough but music was always present. __(47)__ a teenager, he left home and sailed to Singapore. His __(48)__ from childhood was to become a professional musician. The first __(49)__ he had was playing the violin and piano in silent movie theatres, bringing the film to __(50)__ with his music. The work was tiring and the pay was low, but these early experiences honed his skills and confidence as a composer.\nIn 1958, the City Council of Singapore invited him to come __(51)__ with a special song for a civic celebration. He __(52)__ the task readily. He walked along crowded streets and quiet kampungs, __(53)__ inspiration for the perfect song. He __(54)__ the song to capture the aspirations of a young nation – its courage, unity and hope. After many revisions, he composed a rousing __(55)__ that ordinary people could remember and sing with pride. After Singapore became independent, the song was __(56)__ by the government as the national anthem of Singapore.\nAlthough he did not study at prestigious music schools, Zubir kept improving his musical technique, striving to be a better composer than __(57)__ before. He believed that a national anthem __(58)__ not only sound grand but also encourage citizens to serve their country and care for one another.\nToday, students sing Majulah Singapura during assemblies and at national __(59)__ like the Primary 5 National Education (NE) Show so that they show their __(60)__ for the sacrifices and dreams that helped build Singapore. His creation continues to spur young people to contribute in their own ways to the nation."},"english-2026-prelim-chij-st-nicholas-g6":{"text":"Read the passage and answer questions 66 to 75 in complete sentences.\n\n**1** Maya had always admired her grandfather's magnificent lettuce garden, which was truly the apple of his eye. It stretched across the entire back corner of their family property. Every morning at precisely six o'clock, she watched him tend to his prized lettuce with meticulous care, treating each plant with the gentle attention of a devoted parent nurturing his child. When her grandfather fell ill and had to stay in hospital for two weeks, Maya's heart sank as she saw her dear grandfather's plants beginning to wilt under the relentless scorching sun.\n**2** \"Mum, can I take care of Grandfather's garden while he's away?\" Maya asked earnestly, her voice trembling with emotion. Her mother gave her a concerned look. \"Are you sure you want to do it? This would be a big responsibility, Maya. Plants need daily attention, you know? It'll be **an uphill task** for you.\"\n**3** The next morning, Maya woke up early and rushed eagerly to the garden with nervous excitement fluttering wildly in her stomach like trapped butterflies. Although she had watched her grandfather's practised movements countless times, doing it herself the first time felt like an arduous task. The metal watering can was heavier than expected, and she was not sure how much water each plant actually needed. After soaking the thirsty plants thoroughly, she noticed the lettuce leaves looking droopy despite her efforts, making her feel deeply worried.\n**4** Feeling extremely discouraged, Maya called her best friend, Sandie, for advice. \"My grandmother grows vegetables too! Let me ask her for her opinion,\" Sandie offered without hesitation. Within an hour, Sandie arrived breathlessly with her elderly grandmother, Mrs Chen, who began to examine the struggling garden with the sharp, keen eye of an experienced gardener.\n**5** \"You're doing remarkably well, dear! However, different plants need different amounts of water,\" Mrs Chen said with a smile. \"For example, the soil for lettuce should not be too wet. Gardening requires patience. **Rome wasn't built in a day, and neither is a beautiful garden.**\"\n**6** Over the next few days, Maya took Sandie's grandmother's advice to heart, taking special care not to overwater the lettuce. Sandie often joined her, and together, they removed stubborn weeds and dead leaves, making the demanding work considerably easier and much more enjoyable. Sandie discovered that the plants grew faster after they talked to them, though Maya suspected **it** was just coincidence.\n**7** When some destructive aphids suddenly attacked the vulnerable lettuce plants, Maya's heart raced frantically. However, she refused to give up. Instead, she researched for natural remedies online and prepared a soap solution. Then she sprinkled the treatment onto the affected leaves. She felt immensely proud watching the tiny green invaders disappear completely after **that treatment**. The garden slowly transformed from wilting to thriving under her dedicated care, flourishing beautifully once again.\n**8** Two weeks later, Maya's grandfather returned home to find his lettuce garden more beautiful than ever, a truly wonderful sight after his hospital stay. Tears of joy streamed down his cheeks as he saw the healthy plants and Maya's detailed notebook filled with careful observations. \"You've done **something wonderful** here,\" he said, pointing at the garden.\n**9** \"Sandie helped me a lot, and Mrs Chen taught us so much,\" Maya replied modestly, her cheeks glowing with pride. \"I never knew gardening required such dedication.\"\n**10** Her grandfather smiled knowingly, his eyes sparkling with happiness. \"The best gardens grow from love and commitment, not just water and sunshine. Would you like to be my gardening partner from now on?\" Maya's face lit up with pure joy as she nodded enthusiastically. Her mother nodded and gave her a thumbs-up."},"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g1":{"text":"For each question from 16 to 20, choose the word closest in meaning to the underlined word(s).\n\nProfessor Marlo was a well-known inventor. His workshop was a __(16: cluster)__ of tubes, levers and spinning wheels. Nobody quite knew what any of it did, but the old man was rarely seen without grease on his fingers and a grin on his face.\nOne morning, his assistant, Pip, looking __(17: dead on his feet)__, approached the workshop door and froze. Something was different. In the centre of the room sat an enormous machine, its fraying wires __(18: just barely)__ holding together. A faint hum filled the air.\n\"Professor, is that safe?\" Pip asked, __(19: reluctant)__ to step any closer.\nThe old man acknowledged that the machine was, perhaps, a little __(20: dangerous)__. \"But where's the fun if there's no risk?\" he chuckled, his eyes twinkling."},"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g2":{"text":"Study the poster (Text 1) and the extract from an interview (Text 2) and answer questions 21 to 25.\n\n++Text 1++\nThe text below shows a poster encouraging the public to act against scams.\n[[img1]]\n++Text 2++\nThe extract below is taken from an interview with a cyber wellness expert.\n**Interviewer: Thank you for joining us, Ms. Sarah Lee from SafeGuard Cyber Wellness. Can you tell us more about your experience running the \"I Can Act Against Scams\" event today?**\nMs. Sarah Lee: Absolutely! It has been an eye-opening journey. We worked closely with the Singapore Police Force and National Crime Prevention Council. They conducted the Young Anti-Scam Ambassador Workshop because we realised students are a crucial line of defence in protecting their families from the latest scam tactics.\n**Interviewer: What was the most impactful part of the workshop you attended?**\nMs. Sarah Lee: The role-playing sessions! We practised how to \"Sense, Check, and Act\". It is one thing to read about scams, but another to practise saying \"no\" to a suspicious request.\n**Interviewer: Is the campaign only for tech-savvy students?**\nMs. Sarah Lee: Without a doubt, no. The roadshow at the Community Hub is open to everyone. Whether you are a student or a senior citizen, there is something for everyone.\n**Interviewer: Any final advice for our readers?**\nMs. Sarah Lee: Always verify! If a deal looks too good to be true, it probably is. Don't be afraid to report suspicious numbers. Every report helps the authorities stay one step ahead of scammers. It's about the whole community staying vigilant.\n**Interviewer: Thanks, Ms. Lee. It sounds like a very meaningful event.**\nMs. Sarah Lee: It really is. We're already looking forward to our next roadshow!","images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g2-img1","alt":"Poster. Heading in large bold capitals: I CAN ACT AGAINST SCAMS! Slogan in italics: Together, We Can Be Scam-Smart! Jointly organised by: (three logos) Singapore Police Force, National Crime Prevention Council, SafeGuard. The 3-Step Guide. 1. SENSE the red flags (red flag icon): Be wary of unusual requests for money or personal details. 2. CHECK the facts (shield icon): Verify information through official sources or the ScamShield app. 3. ACT to report (magnifying glass icon): Tell a trusted adult and report suspicious numbers to the authorities. Boxed text: Defence Tip: Add the ScamShield App to your phone right now for a reliable defence against tricksters! Young Anti-Scam Ambassador Workshop. Date: 31 October 2026. Venue: Community Hub Main Hall. Activities: Role-playing, Digital Games, and Interactive Booths! (QR code) Don't Miss Out! Complete our Scam-Awareness Quiz via the QR code above for a chance to win in our Grand Lucky Draw!"}]},"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g3":{"text":"There are 10 blanks, numbered 26 to 35, in the passage below. From the list of words given, choose the most suitable word for each blank. Write its letter (A to Q) in the blank. The letters (I) and (O) have been omitted to avoid confusion during marking.\n**EACH WORD CAN BE USED ONLY ONCE.**\n[[table1]]\n\nDolphins have long fascinated humans due to their intelligence and playfulness. Recent studies suggest that these marine mammals have distinct personalities, much like __(26)__ of humans. This natural connection often stems from stories of dolphins rescuing swimmers __(27)__ shark attacks. This is why there are now many researchers __(28)__ study these creatures, believing that their sociable nature is a survival mechanism. They live in pods and rely on one another to hunt. __(29)__, this close bond is not limited to their own species.\nDolphins often show curiosity towards humans, sometimes swimming __(30)__ oceans just to interact with boats. While it is tempting to treat them as pets, we must remember they are wild. We should admire them from afar __(31)__ avoid disrupting their natural habits. Efforts are being made to protect __(32)__ habitats from pollution and its harmful effects. __(33)__ we take action to keep the oceans clean, these magnificent creatures will face a decline in population. It is the responsibility __(34)__ everyone to ensure that dolphins continue to thrive. __(35)__ effort to protect the oceans will benefit the world's marine life. Preserving these intelligent animals and their environment is a legacy we must leave for future generations.\n**Adapted from:** Dolphins: Study finds humans share personality traits with dolphins, BBC","tables":[{"id":"table1","rows":[["(A) across","(D) every","(G) in","(K) of","(N) those"],["(B) and","(E) from","(H) into","(L) their","(P) unless"],["(C) by","(F) however","(J) its","(M) them","(Q) who"]]}]},"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g4":{"text":"Each of the underlined words contains either a spelling or grammatical error. Write the correct word in each of the boxes.\n\nLast month, Dr Sarah Chen and her team of five scientists embarked on an exciting expedition to the Arctic. They wanted to __(36: conducts)__ a study on how climate change was affecting the polar bears and their habitat. The team packed warm clothing, scientific __(37: ekuipment)__ and enough food supplies for three weeks.\nThe __(38: tempreture)__ was −30°C when they arrived at the research station. The landscape was __(39: breadthtaking)__, with endless stretches of white snow and ice. Huge icebergs floated __(40: silence)__ in the dark blue ocean, creating a magnificent sight. On their first day, the scientists spotted a family of polar bears in the __(41: deestence)__. The mother bear was teaching her two cubs how to hunt for seals. Dr Chen carefully observed their __(42: behave)__ through her binoculars and recorded detailed notes. She noticed that the bears had to travel much __(43: far)__ than usual to find food because the ice was thinner as compared to previous years.\nThe team discovered that the ice was melting faster than expected. This __(44: worry)__ them because polar bears depend on the ice to hunt and survive.\nDespite the harsh conditions and freezing winds, the team completed their research __(45: successfuly)__. They gathered valuable information that would help scientists around the world better understand the effects of global warming on Arctic wildlife."},"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g5":{"text":"Fill in each blank with a suitable word.\n\nDeep in the forests of California stand some of the most magnificent trees on Earth – the giant sequoias. These ancient giants can __(46)__ for over 3,000 years and grow to __(47)__ of more than 80 metres.\nWhat makes these trees truly special is their incredible __(48)__ to survive forest fires. While most trees would be destroyed by flames, giant sequoias have developed thick, fire-resistant bark that can be up to 60 centimetres thick. This natural armour __(49)__ the tree's living tissue from heat damage.\nSurprisingly, giant sequoias actually depend __(50)__ forest fires to reproduce. The intense heat from fires causes their cones to open and __(51)__ thousands of tiny seeds. The fire also wipes __(52)__ competing plants and creates the perfect soil conditions for new sequoia seedlings to grow.\nThese remarkable trees have a unique root system that spreads horizontally instead of growing __(53)__ underground. Although the roots extend only about two metres down, they can spread over 30 metres __(54)__ all directions. This wide network helps the massive trees stay upright __(55)__ storms and strong winds.\nGiant sequoias grow incredibly slowly, adding only about one centimetre to their diameter each year. However, they __(56)__ stop growing throughout their entire lifespan. Some of the largest specimens __(57)__ as much as 2,000 tonnes – equivalent to about 400 elephants!\nScientists believe __(58)__ climate change must be slowed down because it poses a serious __(59)__ to these ancient giants. Higher temperatures and changing rainfall patterns could make it __(60)__ for young sequoias to survive. Conservation efforts are now underway to protect these magnificent trees for future generations."},"english-2026-prelim-henry-park-g6":{"text":"Read the passage and answer questions 66 to 75.\n\n**1** Kenji Sato would never forget the night a police officer knocked on their door after midnight. His mother hurried to open it and found the police officer standing outside with his grandfather, Aoto. \"I found him wandering near the highway,\" the officer explained kindly. \"He seemed a little lost.\" Kenji's mother thanked the officer and gently guided her father back to bed. Kenji watched worriedly as his grandfather, tired and confused, shuffled down the hallway.\n**2** Aoto suffered from Alzheimer's disease. The illness often caused him to forget where he was or what time it was. Sometimes, in the middle of the night, he would quietly get out of bed and start wandering around the house.\n**3** That night had frightened the whole family. Somehow, Aoto had left the house and walked all the way towards the busy highway nearby. The next morning, Kenji noticed how exhausted his mother looked. She had been caring for her father every day, and many nights she forced herself to stay awake, listening carefully in case he tried to leave his bed again. \"If I fall asleep,\" she said tiredly, \"he might wander off.\" Kenji nodded sympathetically at his mother's struggles. There had to be a way to help her.\n**4** Ever since he was little, Kenji loved visiting his parents' laboratory. This greatly influenced his love for science and technology. Many hours were spent **there**, experimenting with small gadgets. Now he began to wonder if technology could solve his family's problem.\n**5** One evening, while checking on his grandfather, Kenji noticed something important. His grandfather slowly swung his legs over the side of the bed. A moment later, his feet pressed against the floor. Suddenly, an idea formed in Kenji's mind. If a device could detect that footstep, it could warn someone immediately. The moment his grandfather stood up, a signal could be sent to a phone, alerting the caregiver before he wandered away.\n**6** Excited by his idea, Kenji began researching how sensors worked. He quickly found that turning his idea into reality was not easy. He needed a sensor that was small and comfortable enough to wear. It took a long time to find the perfect one. Then, there was another uphill battle - he had to create a system that could send a wireless signal to a smartphone. The signal sometimes did not reach the phone even when the sensor was working fine. Some days, Kenji's father would find him poring over his notes, shoulders slumped. \"Stuck again?\" **he** would ask. Hardly glancing up, his brows furrowed, Kenji would reply, \"Not for long.\" He refused to give up.\n**7** After many months of experimenting, Kenji finally created a working **prototype**. To his father, it looked just like a regular sock. What he did not know was that it was a sock with a pressure sensor attached to the bottom. His father tried it on and was amazed at how comfortable it felt. Kenji beamed. \"That's because the sensor is so thin!\" When his father suggested for him to try attaching the sensor to other pieces of clothing, Kenji took his idea seriously.\n**8** That night, Kenji tested the invention. He helped Aoto put on the sock at bedtime. Each time Aoto stepped out of bed, the phone received a message. For the first time in a long while, his mother could rest more peacefully at night.\n**9** Caregivers from other families and many elderly care centres approached Kenji to make his special socks for their family members or patients. He happily did so. Afterwards, some caregivers told him that some of the elderly did not like wearing socks to sleep. Kenji thanked them for their feedback. Even though Kenji later received several awards for his invention, he continued to improve it. He was happy that his small idea had made a big impact on many people's lives."},"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g1":{"text":"For each question from 16 to 20, choose the word closest in meaning to the underlined words.\n\nLast Saturday, my classmates and I attended an environmental awareness talk at a community club. During the session, the speaker explained how plastic pollution was harming marine life. I was shocked to learn that many sea animals become __(16: entangled)__ in plastic waste or mistake it for food. It was __(17: alarming)__ to hear that millions of tonnes of plastic enter the oceans every year.\nAfter the talk, my friend Ben and I were inspired to make a change. We both __(18: took up)__ simple habits, such as bringing reusable water bottles, metal straws and shopping bags whenever we went out. Our teacher reminded us that every small action mattered. I realised that making these __(19: important)__ changes would help reduce plastic waste in the world.\nSince then, I have __(20: consistently)__ avoided single-use plastics and encouraged my family to do the same. Together, we can protect marine life and keep our environment clean for future generations."},"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g2":{"text":"Study the information for an event (Text 1) and the extract from a school newsletter (Text 2) and answer questions 21 to 25.\n\n++Text 1++\nThe text below provides information for an event.\n[[img1]]\n++Text 2++\nThe extract below, from a school newsletter, is a pupil's thoughts about the upcoming school park run.\nThe annual park run is not just about running. It is about coming together as a school community to get active and have fun. When we all join in, whether we are fast or slow, we learn to support one another. Even when it is tough, we keep going because we know our friends are there to cheer us on. Winning is not the only goal. It is also about self-motivation to complete the entire course from start to end and having a sense of achievement as a result.\nRunning with our classmates makes us feel like we are part of something big and exciting. We show determination, encourage one another and feel a sense of pride. Moreover, it's a great way to stay fit. With each step, we are building our endurance and growing stronger, both physically and mentally.\nSo, let's hit the trail and embrace the adventure of the park run! Together, we will run, laugh and make memories that will last a lifetime.","images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g2-img1","alt":"Information sheet with a school crest. Text: West Coast Primary School. 5th Annual Park Run. Ready for our school's Park Run? Objectives of the run: to promote the physical well-being of pupils; to foster school spirit. Date: 18 September 2026. Reporting time: 8.15 a.m. for Primary 3 to 6 pupils (Primary 1 and 2 pupils will remain in school for the Sports Carnival). Assemble at the basketball court (near the entrance) for attendance taking before setting off for everyone's favourite park, Elizabeth Gardens. Below is a map headed PARK RUN ROUTE: a loop path around a park. On the right side of the loop is START LINE (Primary 3); further back along the path, at the bottom, is START LINE (Primary 4-6). Going round the loop, the path passes Checkpoint 1 (top right), Checkpoint 2 (top middle) and Checkpoint 3 (top left), then comes down the left side to a single FINISH LINE at the bottom left. A box in the middle says: 2 teachers will be stationed at each checkpoint with a First Aid kit. A water point is available at Checkpoint 2. A box at the bottom right says: Primary 3: 1 km. Primary 4-6: 1.5 km."}]},"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g3":{"text":"There are 10 blanks, numbered 26 to 35, in the passage below. From the list of words given, choose the most suitable word for each blank. Write its letter (A to Q) in the blank. The letters (I) and (O) have been omitted to avoid confusion during marking.\n**EACH WORD CAN BE USED ONLY ONCE.**\n[[table1]]\n\nThe Changi Chapel and Museum stands as a reminder of Singapore's wartime history. Originally opened in 2001, the museum underwent __(26)__ extensive revamp to modernise itself. It serves to honour the prisoners of war (POWs) and civilians __(27)__ were held in the Changi area during the Japanese Occupation.\nThe original chapel was built __(28)__ POWs during their imprisonment. These brave individuals used whatever materials they could find, such as attap leaves and scrap metal, to construct a place of worship. The chapel provided a crucial sanctuary where prisoners __(29)__ find comfort amidst harsh conditions. Today, a faithful replica of the chapel sits in the open-air area of the museum, allowing visitors a quiet space __(30)__ reflection.\nThe main exhibition area is home __(31)__ a massive collection of personal items, photographs, and letters donated by the survivors and their families. Every artefact has __(32)__ own story of resilience __(33)__ has been preserved for future generations. For example, visitors can view secret messages hidden within everyday objects, demonstrating the creativity of the prisoners.\nAnyone who is interested in history __(34)__ make a visit to this site. It bridges the gap __(35)__ the painful past and the peaceful present, teaching the younger generation the true value of psychological defence and strength.\nAdapted from https://www.heritage.sg/changichapelmuseum","tables":[{"id":"table1","rows":[["(A) a","(D) between","(G) have","(K) on","(N) to"],["(B) an","(E) by","(H) in","(L) should","(R) which"],["(C) could","(F) for","(J) its","(M) their","(Q) who"]]}]},"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g4":{"text":"Each of the underlined words contains either a spelling or grammatical error. Write the correct word in each of the boxes.\n\nEver since Max became a teenager, his parents had given him money on his birthday instead of buying him presents. As he was __(36: turnind)__ twenty-one, he requested his mother, Andrea, to donate any money he got to charity. Andrea thought of an idea. She borrowed a video camera to __(37: documentary)__ the day. Then, she withdrew 21 ten-dollar notes from the bank and bought 21 apples.\nWhen they spotted a homeless man down the street, Andrea approached him kindly. \"Today is my son Max's 21st birthday. To __(38: sellerbrait)__, he asked me to give a gift to someone,\" she said. She then handed the man a ten-dollar note and an apple. The man smiled into the camera and __(39: aknowleged)__ the gift with a hearty, \"Happy birthday, Max!\"\nNext, Andrea repeated the __(40: jescher)__ with the men and women at a nearby charity kitchen. __(41: By)__ unison, they sang 'Happy Birthday' for the camera. Andrea then gave fifty dollars to a pizza parlour, although the staff were confused even after Andrea's __(42: explain)__. They wanted to give her a __(43: reseet)__ but she refused. Instead, she urged the owners to feed the hungry. \"Happy birthday, Max!\" the staff shouted, thanking him for his __(44: generous)__.\nAndrea's final stop was her sister's office. Laughing at the __(45: bezdar)__ idea, her sister shouted into the camera, \"Happy birthday, Max!\"\nMax loved the video and thought it was the best birthday gift ever!"},"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g5":{"text":"Fill in the blanks with a suitable word.\n\nDementia is a condition that affects the brain. It can cause problems with memory, thinking and communication. The 2023 Well-being of the Singapore Elderly (WiSE) study by the Institute of Mental Health found __(46)__ about one in 11 individuals aged 60 and above __(47)__ dementia. Many people think dementia __(48)__ causes memory loss but it can also lead to changes in behaviour and personality.\nCaring for a loved one with dementia can be overwhelming. This is especially true when there are behavioural changes and communication problems. Some people with dementia may __(49)__ restless, suspicious or easily upset. These changes can be difficult for family members and caregivers to understand. When a loved one with dementia behaves in a __(50)__ that seems unusual, it is important to remember that the person is often __(51)__ to communicate a need or feeling. __(52)__ dementia affects the brain, they may find it difficult to express themselves __(53)__ with words.\nA person with dementia may repeatedly ask for food even though they have __(54)__ eaten. This happens because they cannot __(55)__ having their meal. Others may refuse to shower or groom themselves, while some may stay __(56)__ at night and feel sleepy during the day.\nA person with dementia may use the wrong word or describe an object instead of naming it directly. They may become frustrated when they are trying to find the __(57)__ words. Frustration can affect their confidence and mood. To help people with dementia, caregivers should remain patient and supportive. Experts recommend using a gentle approach and paying __(58)__ to the person's emotions. Instead of dismissing __(59)__ concerns, caregivers should recognise how they feel and try to understand their unmet needs.\nIt is also helpful to use short and simple sentences. Most importantly, caregivers should speak calmly and offer reassurance when __(60)__. With the right support, people with dementia can continue to feel respected, valued and included in their families and communities.\nAdapted from https://www.imh.com.sg/Mental-Heal resources/Conditions-"},"english-2026-prelim-mgs-g6":{"text":"Read the passage and answer questions 66 to 75.\n\n**1** Twelve-year-old Darto sat under a banyan tree with his friends Arif and Siti. Life moved slowly in their quiet village—far too slowly for Darto, whose imagination could power a small city. If there was ever trouble, the villagers knew there was a good chance he had something to do with it. Moreover, much to their annoyance, Darto insisted on chasing and frightening their chickens.\n**2** Arif said, \"Uncle Banu has a chicken that cannot be caught. He calls it Putih.\"\n**3** \"Can't be caught? Ha! Catching Putih will be easy! Then everyone will admit I am the best chicken chaser in the village!\" Darto exclaimed, jumping to his feet, his eyes lit up.\n**4** That night, Darto, followed by his friends, crept quietly into Uncle Banu's yard. There, he saw a sleek, white bird pecking at the ground near the coop. Just then, the lights in Uncle Banu's house turned on. Darto froze, his heart beating so fast he thought it might burst. For a while nothing happened. He kept an eye on the house and made his move only when he saw the lights go out. Darto walked slowly towards Putih, his hands stretched out towards it, trying to appear as harmless as possible.\n**5** As soon as it spotted Darto, Putih fixed its gaze on him and cocked its head. Then, as though realising his true intentions, Putih bolted. Darto scrambled after the bird as it darted through the yard like a feathery lightning bolt, weaving between banana trees and leaping over piles of firewood. Darto stayed close behind, but the chicken was faster—and far cleverer. It led him into a muddy ditch, then through a thorny bush that left his arms covered in scratches. By the time he emerged, he was exhausted, bleeding and coated in mud.\n**6** \"I told you he couldn't do it!\" shouted Siti. Arif and Siti were laughing so hard they could barely breathe. Cheeks burning, Darto glared at his friends, then at the Putih, who was perched smugly on the roof of Uncle Banu's goat shed. It was clear that Putih was no ordinary chicken and could not be underestimated. However, it was he, Darto, who would have the final victory! If he could not outrun it, then he would outsmart it!\n**7** The next day, Darto borrowed a fishing net from his aunt and a handful of corn from his mother. When he reached the chicken yard, he saw a crowd of children. Unknown to Darto, word of his mission had spread through the village thanks to Arif and Siti. They cheered him on as if he were a hero in a great battle. Even Uncle Banu was there. Darto's stomach fluttered at the sight of so many eyes on him. However, Darto waved to **his audience**, smiled, and loudly declared that Putih had no chance.\n**8** Darto scattered the corn near the chicken coop and hid, holding the net at the ready. After several minutes, Putih appeared. It approached the corn cautiously, pecking at the ground, but always kept its distance. Losing patience, Darto lunged forward, throwing the net with all his might. But Putih was too far away, and the net landed uselessly on the ground. Then, with a loud squawk, it launched itself at Darto, claws outstretched. Darto dodged and ran for his life.\n**9** \"Help!\" Darto screamed, running in circles as the chicken chased him, the crowd roaring with laughter. Exhausted, Darto was finally ready to admit defeat. Head hung low, he went to Uncle Banu to apologise, expecting a scolding.\n**10** To his surprise, Uncle Banu simply laughed and ruffled his hair. \"There is no shame losing to Putih, you know. My grandfather raised it from an egg and it's been keeping me on my toes ever since.\"\n**11** Darto blinked. \"Wait . . . how old is Putih?\"\n**12** Uncle Banu smiled. \"Older than you'd believe.\"\n**13** Trying to catch Putih became something of a tradition among the children after that. Even Darto made several more attempts. Although he never did catch Putih, he did not mind. He realised that the chase was more fun than the catch. From that day on, whenever Putih strutted through the village, clucking like it owned the place, Darto could not help but smile.\n**14** Because in his village even a chicken could be a legend.\nAdapted from \"The Chicken Chase of Banyumas\" by Fendy S. Tulodo"},"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g1":{"text":"For each question from 16 to 20, choose the word closest in meaning to the underlined word(s).\n\nMany schools in Singapore are currently grappling with a severe lack of canteen vendors. Individual stallholders often find it difficult to operate with many financial __(16: constraints)__. Moreover, the rising cost of ingredients and a lack of manpower have forced many vendors to __(17: cease)__ operations. To address this issue, several schools are exploring alternatives such as the central kitchen model.\nUnder the new model, catering companies will supply meals directly to the schools. Students will be able to collect their pre-ordered food __(18: seamlessly)__ from automated machines using their smart cards. Vendors will also be able to cater to the __(19: dietary)__ needs of students more effectively. Schools believe this innovative approach will ensure that students can obtain their meals __(20: promptly)__. This will reduce the time needed to wait in queues during their recess break."},"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g2":{"text":"Study the texts and then answer questions 21 to 25.\n\n++Text 1++\nThe text below shows a poster for a school bag.\n[[img1]]\n++Text 2++\nThe extract below is taken from an online article.\nSchool bags are designed to be both functional and trendy, which makes them appealing to students. Lately, more focus has been put on making school bags look good. For many students, their school bag is also a way to express their personality and personal style. Is that true for you, too?\nWhile it may seem exciting to fit everything into one stylish bag, have you considered the impact on your health? Carrying too many items or a heavy bag strains your back. This may cause short-term discomfort and lead to long-term posture problems. To cushion the effect, you should carry a school bag that fits comfortably on your back. Features such as wide straps and padded backs help distribute weight more evenly. By choosing a school bag wisely, you can reduce the pressure on your back.\nUltimately, health experts recommend packing only the items needed for the day to keep your school bag light. For example, store certain items in your school locker or keep some materials under your desk if your school allows it. By planning what you bring each day and leaving out unnecessary items, you can protect your back and still have everything you need.","images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g2-img1","alt":"Poster. Heading: Peaks School Bag. Carry Everything in Style. Say goodbye to boring school bags! Fit your entire school day into one bag, all your books, stationery, lunch boxes and more! Get organised with a specific space for everything! No more mess or things left behind! In the middle is a picture of a backpack with a front zip pocket. On its left: Suitable for students of all ages! On its right: Wide range of colours to choose from! Below: Designed with you in mind! At the bottom, beside a photo of a boy carrying a school bag: \"I love my Peaks school bag! Everything I need for school fits comfortably into it. I can also find and take out any item I need easily!\" - Ansel, 12-year-old student"}]},"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g3":{"text":"There are 10 blanks, numbered 26 to 35, in the passage below. From the list of words given, choose the most suitable word for each blank. Write its letter (A to Q) in the blank. The letters (I) and (O) have been omitted to avoid confusion during marking.\n**EACH WORD CAN BE USED ONLY ++ONCE++.**\n[[table1]]\n\nPlastic waste is everywhere. Every day, an astonishing amount of plastic waste enough to fill 2,000 garbage trucks __(26)__ dumped into the ocean. Plastic litter can be found at the bottom of the ocean and on top of Mount Everest. Surprisingly, plastic waste has now turned __(27)__ on the backs of hermit crabs!\nHermit crabs are tiny animals with soft, curly bodies __(28)__ leave them vulnerable to predators. __(29)__ their tails are fragile, they must constantly look for empty shells to protect __(30)__ from hungry birds and fishes. Studies show 10 out of 16 land species __(31)__ been observed wearing plastic waste, such as bottle caps, and __(32)__ broken lightbulbs.\nScientists believe this unusual behaviour may be due to the declining population of sea snails, resulting __(33)__ fewer natural shells for the crabs. It is also possible that crabs choose plastic materials __(34)__ traditional shells because they are lighter in weight and easier to carry.\nWhile scientists agree that plastic pollution harms wildlife and ecosystems, it is still unclear __(35)__ hermit crabs are directly endangered by this behaviour. This shows that plastic waste can affect even small animals.\nAdapted from https://www.timeforkids.com/g56/creative-crabs","tables":[{"id":"table1","rows":[["(A) are","(D) from","(G) if","(K) it","(N) to"],["(B) because","(E) has","(H) in","(L) over","(P) up"],["(C) even","(F) have","(J) is","(M) themselves","(Q) which"]]}]},"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g4":{"text":"Each of the underlined words contains either a spelling or grammatical error. Write the correct word in each of the boxes.\n\nLast week, I went to a local theatre to see a performance of Chicken Little. The story followed a clear __(36: sequanse)__ of events that everyone could easily follow. It began when a small object __(37: accidental)__ hit the main character, making her think the sky was falling. Overwhelmed by fear, she quickly __(38: spreading)__ her panic all the other animals in the woods.\nI __(39: thurruhly)__ enjoyed the acting of the performers. Their high-energy __(40: seens)__ made the play realistic and comical. They cleverly used fast __(41: dielog)__ to show how confused they were as they ran around the stage. In the final act, the characters realised the importance of not __(42: jump)__ to conclusions.\nIt provided plenty of laughter while conveying a __(43: valued)__ lesson about seeking the truth. I would certainly __(44: rekkomend)__ this show to anyone who __(45: enjoying)__ a fun story with a deep meaning. The performance is a perfect example of how theatre can teach us important things while making us laugh."},"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g5":{"text":"Fill in each blank with a suitable word.\n\nBlind boxes are small toys sold in sealed packaging. Buyers do not know what they will get __(46)__ they open them. Although blind boxes feel like a modern trend, the idea is not __(47)__. In the 1950's, mystery prize tickets, also __(48)__ as Tikam-Tikam, were so common in Singapore that every street corner had a vendor selling them. The 1950's Tikam-Tikam craze led many children and even adults to extreme __(49)__ such as shoplifting just to get what they want. In __(50)__ months, such acts have also been reported by shops selling blind boxes here.\nSo __(51)__ are blind boxes so appealing? One reason is our human desire to collect. People enjoy gathering complete sets, especially when some items are __(52)__ in quantity. When a rare piece is finally found, it feels as if a difficult __(53)__ has been successfully completed. Another reason is social influence where collectors share their __(54)__ online through posts and live videos. Seeing others unbox rare items creates excitement and encourages more people to __(55)__ the trend.\nHowever, the randomness of blind boxes creates a cycle of anticipation __(56)__ to gambling. People keep buying more boxes in __(57)__ of getting a rare item, only to end up with duplicates. Over time, this cycle can be hard to __(58)__ and the behaviour may even become addictive. This leads to risky spending __(59)__ where people frequently spend more than intended or buy repeatedly without realising how quickly the cost is adding up. As a result, governments are considering rules and __(60)__ to better protect consumers, like the ban on Tikam-Tikam in 1961. Although blind boxes can be fun, it is important to know the potential harms and act responsibly.\nAdapted from https://medium.com/publiclibrarysg/the-blind-box-boom-why-everyone-is-going-crazy-for-mystery-collectibles"},"english-2026-prelim-nan-hua-g6":{"text":"Read the passage below and answer questions 66 to 75.\n\n**1** The first night of the Primary 5 camp was difficult for Ryan. As the dormitory lights dimmed, the quiet forest outside made **his heart beat faster**. Unlike Marcus, who was already snoring loudly, Ryan lay sleepless in the dark. This was his first time staying away from home, and the thin, hard mattress made him miss his own comfortable bed.\n**2** The next morning, the students headed to the High Elements course, which began with a daunting ten-metre climb. Marcus was appointed the group leader, and it was no surprise — during the practice sessions in school, he was the only one who had mastered all the safety knots. After putting on his safety gear, he checked every classmate's harness with a sharp gaze. Mr Lim, their form teacher, stood at the side observing him quietly, his clipboard tucked under his arm.\n**3** Marcus scaled the rock wall with ease, but when it was Ryan's turn, his legs began to shake like jelly. He took one look at the sheer height of the wall and froze. \"Ryan! Don't just stand there! Start climbing! Everyone else is waiting for you,\" Marcus shouted from above. Ryan felt **embarrassed**. His face turned a deep crimson as the other students watched him tremble.\n**4** Later that afternoon, the class went for a long hiking expedition. Exhausted, Ryan had slowed and was lagging behind. He often stopped to catch his breath. Marcus had a single-minded will to be the first group to reach the checkpoint. Suddenly, he snatched Ryan's water bottle. \"You're drinking too much and stopping too often!\" Marcus shouted. He shoved the bottle into his own bag and stomped off. Ryan stared wide-eyed at Marcus's back. The outburst caused the class to stop and murmur. Ryan felt his eyes moisten, but he forced himself to keep walking in silence. From the back of the group, Mr Lim saw everything but kept his distance.\n**5** By evening, Ryan was in a sullen mood. During dinner, he sat alone at the edge of the campsite. \"All Marcus cares about is that silly trophy,\" he muttered under his breath.\n**6** That night, Ryan could not sleep. He slipped out to the washroom to splash some water on his face. Near the benches, he heard soft sobbing. To his surprise, he saw Marcus sitting on the ground with his head between his knees. Marcus was holding the brass compass so tightly that his knuckles were white. Ryan froze. His first instinct was to walk away, but remembering how Marcus had treated him, he decided to stay and taunt his tormentor. However, as he watched Marcus shaking, his anger began to soften. Then he noticed an inscription on the back of the compass: Best Camper. Realising that the compass held sentimental value for Marcus, Ryan felt a lump forming in his throat.\n**7** \"It's just a camp, Marcus,\" Ryan said gently. Marcus jumped and tried to hide **++it++**. \"Go away,\" he snapped, but his voice was shaky and lacked its usual confidence. Ryan sat down nearby. \"I'm so homesick I can't even eat,\" he admitted. Marcus finally relaxed and held up the compass. \"This was my brother's. He had won 'Best Camper' three years ago. My parents expect me to be just like him, but it's a **Herculean task**.\" Ryan finally understood why Marcus had behaved the way he did — he was terrified of letting his family down.\n**8** The next morning, during the outdoor cooking session, Ryan struggled to light his fire. The tiny flame kept flickering out in the breeze, and his pot of water remained cold. Before he could give up, Marcus came over and crouched beside him. \"Stack the fuel tablets like this. It keeps the flame going,\" he said, arranging them. He used his own body to block the wind while Ryan tried again. When the fire was lit, Ryan watched the steady glow with a grateful smile.\n**9** From across the campsite, Mr Lim watched the interaction between the two boys. **He nodded approvingly, thinking that sometimes, the most important lessons at camp had nothing to do with knots or rock walls.**"},"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g1":{"text":"For each question from 16 to 20, choose the word(s) closest in meaning to the underlined word(s).\n\nCaring for a dog has many wonderful benefits. Dogs provide companionship, which helps to __(16: alleviate)__ feelings of loneliness and stress. Studies have shown that petting a dog for just ten minutes can have a significant impact on reducing anxiety levels. Dogs also encourage more physical activity among dog owners as they have to take them on __(17: regular)__ walks. Fellow dog owners can also bond and connect over __(18: anecdotes)__ of their dogs' quirky behaviours.\nHowever, it is important to remember that looking after a dog is __(19: no walk in the park)__. Before deciding to bring a dog into your home, make sure you are __(20: completely)__ prepared to commit to their needs for many years. It would be wise for prospective dog owners to consider their lifestyle, schedules and budget before making such a commitment.\nAdapted from American Kennel Club"},"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g2":{"text":"For each question from 21 to 25, choose the best answer.\n\n++Text 1++\n[[img1]]\n++Text 2++\nThe extract below is taken from an online article.\nSchool bags are designed to be both roomy and trendy, which makes them appealing to students. Lately, more focus has been put on making school bags look good. Many students use their school bags as a way to express their personality and personal style. Is that true for you, too?\nWhile it may seem exciting to fit everything into one bag, have you considered the weight? Carrying too many items strains your back. This may cause short-term discomfort and lead to long-term posture problems. To cushion the effect, you should carry a school bag that fits comfortably on your back. Features such as wide straps and padded backs help distribute weight more evenly. By choosing a school bag wisely, you can reduce the pressure on your back.\nUltimately, health experts recommend packing only the items needed for the day to keep your school bag light. For example, you can store certain items in your school locker or keep some materials under your desk if your school allows it. By planning what you bring each day and leaving out unnecessary items, you can protect your back and still have everything you need.","images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g2-img1","alt":"Poster for a school bag, with a photo of a backpack in the middle. Text: Peaks School Bag. Carry Everything in Style. Say goodbye to boring school bags! Fit your entire school day into one bag – all your books, stationery, lunch boxes and more! Get organised with a specific space for everything! No more mess or things left behind! Suitable for students of all ages! Wide range of colours to choose from! Big bags never felt so comfortable! Beside a photo of a schoolboy carrying the bag: \"I love my Peaks bag. Although it's bigger than other school bags, I bought it because it's extremely comfortable. Everything I need for school fits inside it. I can also find any item easily! – Ansel, 12-year-old student"}]},"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g3":{"text":"There are 10 blanks, numbered 26 to 35, in the passage below. From the list of words given, choose the most suitable word for each blank. Write its letter (A to Q) in the blank. The letters (I) and (O) have been omitted to avoid confusion during marking.\n**EACH WORD CAN BE USED ONLY ONCE.**\n[[table1]]\n\nLast Saturday, our school gardening club visited a community farm. For months, the club members __(26)__ been learning how vegetables are grown in small spaces.\nAt the farm, we were divided into several groups so that __(27)__ team could try a different activity. My group was asked to loosen the soil gently __(28)__ the roots were delicate. Next, the farmer showed us a tool with a short handle __(29)__ was safer for children to use.\nHe reminded us that we __(30)__ not pour too much water onto the plants. Too little water would dry them out, __(31)__ too much water would drown them. __(32)__ the end of the session, we harvested spinach and placed the stalks carefully into baskets.\nThe baskets carried by our group were heavier __(33)__ the rest because we had picked vegetables from twice as many rows compared to others. Before leaving, we thanked the farmer, who told us to wash __(34)__ hands before eating anything. We also learnt that fresh produce often tastes better __(35)__ it is eaten soon after harvesting. The visit made many of us eager to start a small class garden.","tables":[{"id":"table1","rows":[["(A) another","(D) but","(G) have","(K) shall","(N) their"],["(B) at","(E) each","(H) must","(L) than","(P) when"],["(C) because","(F) had","(J) our","(M) then","(Q) which"]]}]},"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g4":{"text":"Each of the underlined words contains either a spelling or grammatical error. Write the correct word in each of the boxes.\n\nLast month, our class organised an exhibition about how neighbourhoods in Singapore have changed over time. Our teacher reminded us that everyone __(36: have)__ an important role to play. She also said that a panel would be put near the entrance to __(37: comemoret)__ the pioneers who had contributed to the estate.\nThe hall was already buzzing __(38: in)__ activity on the morning of the exhibition. Every display board __(39: were)__ checked very carefully before the visitors arrived. A few of us were still making minor changes as we wanted the information to be both accurate and __(40: coherant)__. Later, a resident of more than fifty years noticed an old photograph of a market and told us that he had __(41: wrote)__ about the same place many years earlier in his __(42: jornal)__.\nAt the far end of the hall stood a table of artefacts that families had kindly lent to the school. They were not __(43: asppecialy)__ valuable but helped us imagine what daily life had once been like. __(44: Since)__ being tired after clearing up, many of us felt that the effort had been __(45: undoubtably)__ worthwhile. The exhibition taught us that even ordinary objects can tell meaningful stories about the past."},"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g5":{"text":"Fill in each blank with a suitable word.\n\nHave you ever noticed how some children can remember facts better after turning them into a game? At our school, a group of teachers have begun using a new approach to teaching by adding short revision games to their lessons. __(46)__ of copying notes for the entire period, students now work in small __(47)__ to answer questions, sort cards and explain ideas to one another.\nWhen students discuss possible answers, they must listen closely and think carefully about __(48)__ their ideas make sense to others. This helps them to reflect on their answers and spot any mistakes before __(49)__ them with the class. The games may also encourage quieter students to join in. They can first discuss in small groups before speaking to the whole class. __(50)__ gives them time to build their confidence step by step. In one activity, students are given clue cards to arrange in the correct __(51)__ to show how a process works. In a different activity, they earn points by __(52)__ why one answer is wrong while another is correct. Such tasks take only a few minutes, but they keep students alert and fully focused during the lesson. Teachers have noticed that after taking part in such activities, students are __(53)__ willing to ask questions. This may be because the games __(54)__ learning feel less stressful.\nOf course, revision games are beneficial only __(55)__ students continue to revise their notes and complete their homework. Fun activities can support learning, but teachers and students __(56)__ should understand that the use of such activities in lessons __(57)__ just one part of the learning process, not the whole of it. A balanced routine, with time set __(58)__ for reading, practice and discussion, is more often than __(59)__ the most effective way to prepare for an examination. When students enjoy the process of learning, they are more likely to stay __(60)__ task. Used wisely, revision games can make learning enjoyable and worth looking forward to too."},"english-2026-prelim-nanyang-g6":{"text":"Read the passage and answer questions 66 to 75.\n\n**1** When I was in Secondary Two, my form teacher once told me, in front of the whole class, that I was \"the sort of girl who could not fit anywhere\". The words were spoken lightly, almost carelessly, but they stayed with me long after the lesson ended. For the rest of the day, I carried a quiet heaviness in me, even as everyone else laughed and chatted as though nothing had happened.\n**2** At assemblies, we were supposed to line up neatly, socks pulled up, hair tied properly, badges pinned straight. Everyone around me seemed to know how to **turn themselves into the shape the school wanted**. I, unlike the others, had my shirt tucked out and dirty shoes. My shoelaces were two different shades of blue because I had replaced one missing lace with whatever I could find. To make things worse, I had been caught drawing a strange shape in the margin of the school newsletter instead of reading it. \"What is that?\" my form teacher had asked sharply, holding up **my paper**. I stared at the page. I had drawn a blob with uneven edges and tiny arms stretching out. \"An amoeba,\" I muttered. A few girls giggled. \"Exactly,\" she said. \"A single-celled organism with no proper shape. Just floating about aimlessly. That is your problem.\"\n**3** From that day on, the words followed me everywhere. Amoeba girl. Blob. Floating aimlessly. It did not help that I asked too many questions when I was supposed to accept instructions. When the others talked excitedly about becoming doctors, lawyers or scholars, I stayed silent because none of those dreams felt like mine. I liked watching people instead, noticing the way they performed a version of themselves for the world.\n**4** At home, things were not much easier. \"Can you do what you are told?\" Mother often scolded. \"Why can't you be more like your cousin? She knows how to behave.\" \"There she goes again!\" I thought bitterly as I walked away from this familiar rant. I did know how to behave. I simply could not bear pretending for too long. Since then, I began to think of myself as an amoeba. Not the insult my teacher had intended, but the real creature I had seen under a microscope in the science lab. It had no neat outline, no fixed form, and yet it was alive. It moved. It survived. On my worst days, I was exactly like that, a strange little blob drifting quietly through school, unseen and unimportant.\n**5** Then, one afternoon, something changed. It was the final week of the term. My class was engrossed in playing board games, gossiping and laughing with one another. This was interrupted by our teacher who gave us instructions to create a short performance about good behaviour. Most of my classmates **groaned and did the bare minimum**. As they argued lazily over the script, hoping to get it over with, I suddenly felt something stir inside me. Before I could stop myself, the words slipped out. \"This is boring,\" I said. \"No one will listen.\" My heart thumped in my chest as I took over the script. This was something I never imagined myself doing. I began rewriting the whole scene. In half an hour, I completed **the task**. I made the dialogue sharper and funnier. I showed them where to stand and when to pause. Somehow, everyone listened. Even the loudest girls followed my instructions.\n**6** During the performance, the audience laughed at the right moments. For the first time in school, I was not being stared at because I was wrong. I was being watched because I had made something people could feel. Afterwards, our English teacher came up to me. \"You wrote most of that, didn't you?\" she asked. I nodded, expecting criticism. Instead, she smiled. \"You notice things other people miss. That is **a gift**. You should do something with it.\"\n**7** As we packed up our props, the same girls who had groaned earlier now lingered by my desk. One of them, the loudest in the group, smiled awkwardly and asked if I would help her with her drama club audition the following week. This caught me completely off guard. Just days ago, she had been rolling her eyes at my mismatched shoelaces. Now, she was looking at me not as a joke, but as an expert. I agreed, feeling a strange warmth spread through my chest.\n**8** That night, I drew an amoeba again. But this time, I saw a creature that could adapt and find its own way forward. Perhaps not fitting in did not mean I was broken; it simply meant I was still forming. I began to imagine myself writing and directing plays. Maybe one day, these would reach a fellow amoeba who needed to know that she was not alone.\nAdapted from How Tan Siyou turned rebellion into new film 'Amoeba' by The Straits Times"},"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g1":{"text":"For each question from 16 to 20, choose the word(s) closest in meaning to the underlined words.\n\nCaptain Colt, an explorer, was on his maiden voyage. He knew the __(16: quandary)__ he and his crew were in with no sight of land and their food supplies running low. Once, a man fell overboard and __(17: floundered)__ in the waters. Thankfully, he was rescued.\nWhen an island was spotted, they had to navigate through __(18: murky)__ waters with poor visibility in the night. Colt realised the __(19: alarming)__ effect the disease-borne mosquitoes had on his crew as they came down with high fevers. He __(20: revived)__ their hopes of survival by making the difficult decision to leave the island. The next morning, they set sail in search of a new land."},"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g2":{"text":"Study the poster (Text 1) and the extract from an online article (Text 2) and answer questions 21 to 25.\n\n++Text 1++\nThe text below shows a poster.\n[[img1]]\n++Text 2++\nThe extract below is taken from an online article.\nWearing the right shoes is important for every runner, whether they are experienced athletes or beginners. Good running shoes do more than keep the feet comfortable. They play a key role in helping runners perform well and in preventing injuries.\nFirstly, proper running shoes provide strong support and sufficient cushioning. When a runner's feet hit the ground, the shoes help to absorb the impact. This reduces pressure on the feet, ankles and knees, making running safer. It also lowers the risk of common injuries such as sprains, muscle strain and joint pain.\nIn addition, running shoes are designed to help the body move correctly. They improve balance and support smoother, more controlled movements while running. With better stability and posture, runners can run more efficiently and for longer periods without discomfort.\nIn conclusion, choosing the right pair of running shoes may seem like a simple decision, but it is an important one. The right shoes help runners enjoy their runs, improve their performance and protect their bodies from injury.","images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g2-img1","alt":"Poster with a picture of a running shoe tilted as if taking off, with feather-like wing lines rising from the heel. Text: Join us for the sales launch of the FALCON! DON'T RUN, FLY! Exclusively for Sports House members at the Vista City Outlet. 12 December at 10 a.m. $129.90 Members' Price. Only 100 pairs! No reservations for customers or online purchases. Six feature boxes: A) Lightweight: Makes running easier and faster. B) Cushioned Comfort: Keeps your feet protected and free from blisters. C) Grippy Soles: Helps you stay steady on any surface. D) Visibility: Reflective materials keep you visible in low light. E) Durability: Built to last through many kilometres. F) Support: Provides stability for injury-free running. Below the boxes: Visit www.sportshouse.sg for more information."}]},"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g3":{"text":"There are 10 blanks, numbered 26 to 35, in the passage below. From the list of words given, choose the most suitable word for each blank. Write its letter (A to Q) in the blank. The letters (I) and (O) have been omitted to avoid confusion during marking.\n**EACH WORD CAN BE USED ONLY ONCE.**\n[[table1]]\n\nHave you ever felt that your home was warmer than the weather outside? A recent study has found that __(26)__ is true for many HDB flats in Singapore. Some homes trap more heat and become hotter __(27)__ the outdoor environment.\nOne main reason is poor airflow. In some homes, windows are kept closed for long periods, preventing fresh air from entering. Large pieces __(28)__ furniture and clutter can also block the movement of air; hence, trapping heat inside the flat. In addition, concrete walls and floors absorb heat during the day and slowly release __(29)__ at night, making homes feel warm even __(30)__ the sun has set.\nLiving in a hot home can be __(31)__ uncomfortable experience, making it difficult for us to sleep well or concentrate on our tasks. The elderly and families __(32)__ do not use air-conditioning often find it challenging to cope with the heat. __(33)__, there are several ways to cool down homes. We can open windows and remove clutter to improve airflow. Using fans wisely can __(34)__ help circulate cooler air around the room.\nAs Singapore's weather becomes warmer, keeping homes cool __(35)__ become increasingly important. By making these small changes, it can create a more comfortable living environment for us.\nAdapted from The Straits Times, \"How Heat Proof Is Your Home?\"","tables":[{"id":"table1","rows":[["(A) a","(D) after","(G) it","(K) than","(N) which"],["(B) an","(E) before","(H) of","(L) them","(P) who"],["(C) also","(F) however","(J) to","(M) this","(Q) will"]]}]},"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g4":{"text":"Each of the underlined words contains either a spelling or grammatical error. Write the correct word in each of the boxes.\n\nScience is my favourite subject. When I was ten, I __(36: receeved)__ a book about Marie Curie and her story inspired me greatly.\nMarie Curie was born __(37: on)__ Poland on November 7, 1867. When she was young, she loved Science too. Sadly, girls were not __(38: aloudd)__ to study at the university in her hometown. However, she refused to give up. Instead, she moved to __(39: other)__ country to continue studying and __(40: persoo)__ her dream of becoming a scientist.\nWhat impressed me the most was her __(41: deturminasion)__, never giving up on her passion for Science. Her research helped improve X-rays, allowing doctors to take more __(42: accurately)__ pictures inside the human body. __(43: In)__ this day, she is the only person who had won Nobel Prizes in two different Sciences, proving that hard work and passion can lead to __(44: xtraordinery)__ achievements. Reading about Marie Curie reminds me that __(45: successes)__ does not come easily. I hope I can be just as resilient as her and continue chasing my dreams."},"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g5":{"text":"Fill in each blank with a suitable word.\n\n\"Checkmate!\" The game is over. In schools, community clubs and homes across Singapore, children and adults __(46)__ around chessboards to challenge each other to a friendly game of chess. Some play for fun, __(47)__ others train hard for competitions. Although the rules of the game are simple to learn, every game __(48)__ different, making chess a favourite pastime for people of all ages.\nChess is one of the world's oldest __(49)__, known for its strategy and challenge. However, no one knows exactly when or __(50)__ it began. Most historians believe that chess started in northwestern India around 600 A.D. The game was first called 'chaturanga', a Sanskrit __(51)__ that refers to the four parts of the ancient Indian army: soldiers, chariots, horsemen and elephants. __(52)__ later inspired the pieces used in modern chess. As the game __(53)__ to different parts of the world, its rules gradually changed and developed __(54)__ the modern chess we know today.\nToday, chess is more than just a game. Every move requires __(55)__ planning because one wrong decision can change the outcome. As players think several moves ahead, it teaches them to solve problems and __(56)__ calm under pressure. For __(57)__, chess helps to develop critical thinking skills, concentration and patience. For adults, it keeps the mind active and encourages mental agility. These valuable skills can be applied not __(58)__ on the chessboard, but also in everyday life.\nMost importantly, chess has a unique way of bringing people __(59)__. Children can enjoy a game of chess with both __(60)__ parents and grandparents. Through their shared love of the game, families can bond and enjoy spending time with one another.\nAdapted from Origins of Chess"},"english-2026-prelim-plmgs-g6":{"text":"Read this passage below and answer questions 66 to 75.\n\n**1** \"Since this is such a beautiful day and it's Saturday,\" Jane said, reaching for the hairdryer, \"why don't we have a picnic at the nearby park?\"\n**2** As my sister and I planned our day, our large tuxedo cat, Gato, purred softly in my arms, gazing lazily at the view through our bedroom window.\n**3** When Jane turned on the hairdryer, Gato bolted in panic. In his hurry to escape, Gato's claws ripped the soft flesh on the underside of my lower left arm. Instinctively, I shoved Gato with all my strength and he landed with a thud, scurrying away almost immediately.\n**4** The cut was deep and bled profusely. After stopping the bleeding, we decided no stitches were required. Jane helped me treat and dress the wound while I insisted no amount of discomfort would interfere with our day's plans. Soon, I began searching for Gato.\n**5** \"Gato!\" I called several times, but there was no answer. The atmosphere in the house was heavy and I could not be certain of **whose guilt** I was reading. Finally, I found him huddling beneath the stairs, wide-eyed and trembling.\n**6** As I held Gato close, I felt his heart racing. Kissing him on the head, I whispered, \"It's alright. It was just an accident. I know you didn't mean to hurt me.\" I sat on the stairs holding and stroking him in my lap until he relaxed.\n**7** When he finally seemed to have gotten over it, I released him — and wondered, briefly, if I had done the same. I dashed to the store for some snacks and while waiting in line to pay, I met my neighbour who lived next door.\n**8** \"What happened to your arm?\" he asked, noting the bandage.\n**9** After I explained, he responded with a raised brow, \"Let me tell you one thing. If that had happened to me, it would be one dead cat! Accident or not!\"\n**10** I quickly left the store after paying, a sour taste in my mouth.\n**11** Several weeks later, the wound was completely healed. However, in its place was a prominent white curved scar — almost three inches in length. While many would wish for it to fade, I liked **the mark**.\n**12** I was reading in our room when Jane returned home from her piano lesson, throwing her bag down without a word. The moment she looked up and saw the book in my hand, the words flew out of her mouth.\n**13** \"I told you to use a bookmark! Why did you fold the pages? You know how much I love this book! This is exactly why I sometimes wonder if you even pay attention to anything I say! So much for being my sister.\"\n**14** I could feel the cutting words rising in my throat. However, the soft brushing of fur at my leg distracted me and I looked down. It was Gato, meowing nonchalantly, as if reminding me to remember. My right hand **traced the raised skin of the scar**.\n**15** The incident with Gato taught me that wounds seldom tell the whole story. His lashing out was **a reaction and not a malicious intent** — there is a difference between being the target and being the nearest thing at hand. People are not so different from animals in this way.\n**16** I took a slow breath and looked at Jane — **really looked at her** — and saw not anger but exhaustion behind her eyes. \"It's alright,\" I said softly. \"Do you want to talk about **it**?\"\n**17** A long silence passed before her shoulders dropped, as though something she had been carrying all day had finally been set down. \"I didn't mean what I said before. Everything had gone wrong during the piano lesson today and I've been feeling very stressed about the upcoming competition.\" Her eyes watered.\n**18** She walked over to me and took both my hands in hers. For a long moment she **simply held them and said nothing**. When she looked up, I knew I would never hold this incident against her.\nAdapted from The Scar by Kitty Chappell"},"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g1":{"text":"For each question from 16 to 20, choose the word closest in meaning to the underlined word(s).\n\nLast summer, Maya decided to embark on a journey that would forever broaden her perspective on life. She had always been curious about places far from her hometown, and this trip promised experiences that would __(16: deepen)__ her understanding of different cultures.\nOn the first day, she visited a small village nestled among rolling hills. The locals welcomed her with radiant smiles. Maya felt a sense of __(17: fascination)__ as she explored the winding streets, each corner revealing a new surprise.\nIn the evenings, she would sit by the river, watching the sunset that seemed to __(18: illuminate)__ the sky with brilliant shades of orange and pink. These moments reminded her that life was not just about achievements, but also about the beauty that surrounded her.\nOne afternoon, while hiking a steep trail, Maya encountered an elderly man who was __(19: frail)__ yet radiated a quiet wisdom. He shared inspirational stories of his youth, and Maya listened intently, knowing that these narratives would __(20: enlighten)__ her in ways books never could. By the end of the trip, Maya felt transformed.\nAdapted from The Unexpected Journey"},"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g2":{"text":"Study the poster (Text 1) and the extract from an article (Text 2) and answer questions 21 to 25.\n\n++Text 1++\nThe text below shows a poster.\n[[img1]]\n++Text 2++\nThe extract below is taken from an online article.\nWearing the right shoes is important for every runner, whether they are experienced athletes or beginners. Good running shoes do more than keep the feet comfortable. They play a key role in helping runners perform well and in preventing injuries.\nFirstly, proper running shoes provide strong support and sufficient cushioning. When a runner's feet hit the ground, the shoes help to absorb the impact. This reduces pressure on the feet, ankles and knees, making running safer. It also lowers the risk of common injuries such as sprains, muscle strain and joint pain.\nIn addition, running shoes are designed to help the body move correctly. They improve balance and support smoother, more controlled movements while running. With better stability and posture, runners can run more efficiently and for longer periods without discomfort.\nIn conclusion, choosing the right pair of running shoes may seem like a simple decision, but it is an important one. The right shoes help runners enjoy their runs, improve their performance and protect their bodies from injury.","images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g2-img1","alt":"Poster with a picture of a running shoe that has a wing at the heel. Text: Join us for the sales launch of the FALCON! DON'T RUN, FLY! Exclusively for Sports House members at the Vista City Outlet. 12 December at 10 a.m. $129.90 Members' Price. Only 100 pairs! No reservations for customers or online purchases. Six feature boxes: A) Lightweight: Makes running easier and faster. B) Cushioned Comfort: Keeps your feet free from blisters. C) Grippy Soles: Helps you stay steady on any surface. D) Visibility: Reflective materials keep you visible in low light. E) Durability: Built to last through many kilometres. F) Support: Provides stability for injury-free running. At the bottom: Visit www.sportshouse.sg for more information."}]},"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g3":{"text":"There are 10 blanks, numbered 26 to 35, in the passage below. From the list of words given, choose the most suitable word for each blank. Write its letter (A to Q) in the blank. The letters (I) and (O) have been omitted to avoid confusion during marking.\n[[table1]]\n\nHiccups are sudden, repeated sounds that happen when your body makes a small \"jump\" inside your chest. They occur __(26)__ the diaphragm, a muscle under the lungs __(27)__ helps you breathe, suddenly contracts. This quick movement makes you breathe __(28)__ air very fast. At the same time, the gap between your vocal cords snaps shut, causing __(29)__ familiar \"hic\" sound.\nThere are __(30)__ reasons why hiccups occur. You might eat or drink too quickly, swallow too much air or even laugh too hard. Sometimes, feeling nervous or excited can also make you get hiccups. In most cases, hiccups last only a few minutes and stop on __(31)__ own.\nTo get rid __(32)__ hiccups, people often try holding their breath, drinking water slowly or breathing into a paper bag. __(33)__ methods help the diaphragm relax so that your breathing goes back __(34)__ normal. Hiccups are harmless, __(35)__ they can be annoying. The sounds are a normal part of how our bodies work.","tables":[{"id":"table1","rows":[["(A) although","(D) in","(G) much","(K) their","(N) unless"],["(B) an","(E) its","(H) of","(L) these","(P) when"],["(C) by","(F) many","(J) the","(M) to","(Q) which"]]}]},"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g4":{"text":"Each of the underlined words contains either a spelling or grammatical error. Write the correct word in each of the boxes.\n\nLast Saturday, my family gathered at a bustling seafood restaurant. It was a special __(36: occassion)__ as it was Grandma's eightieth birthday. My cousin, Sarah, who cared deeply about her __(37: appearence)__, arrived wearing a stunning silk dress that shimmered under the chandeliers. However, she soon felt __(38: embrassed)__ and her confidence vanished when she realised she had accidentally __(39: wore)__ one black sandal and one navy blue slipper __(40: on)__ her frantic rush to leave home. Everyone at the table chuckled __(41: soft)__ at the sight, but Grandma gave her a warm, __(42: comfort)__ hug to make her feel better.\nDuring the lively party, Grandma __(43: receives)__ many wonderful gifts from her grandchildren, ranging from handmade cards to __(44: elegeant)__ jewellery. My brother gave her a brand-new tablet, explaining that it would be much more __(45: convinient)__ for her to video call us whenever she missed hearing our voices or seeing our faces. Grandma smiled brightly, her eyes twinkling with joy as she thanked us all for such a memorable day."},"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g5":{"text":"Fill in each blank with a suitable word.\n\nBeing a responsible member of the school community means more than just attending classes on time. It involves showing courtesy to teachers, classmates, and staff __(46)__ simple greetings, such as saying \"please\" and \"thank you\". These polite behaviours create a positive and friendly environment where __(47)__ feels valued.\nRespecting school rules is another important part of being responsible. Rules are designed to __(48)__ safety, fairness, and order for all. For instance, __(49)__ patiently for one's turn, keeping noise levels low in the library, and following instructions in the classroom are ways to demonstrate self-discipline and __(50)__ for others.\nThink about how your actions __(51)__ possibly affect other people. Helping a classmate who has dropped her books, offering to share materials, or including quieter students in group __(52)__ are all examples of thoughtfulness and kindness.\nA responsible student also takes care of both __(53)__ and shared resources. This includes returning borrowed books on time, keeping the canteen clean, and using school property __(54)__ care. Students must not forget to appreciate the efforts of those who maintain the school grounds.\nCommunication and cooperation are equally important. Listening attentively, speaking politely, and working well in teams help to strengthen existing bonds __(55)__ maintain good relationships with others. Handling disagreements __(56)__ they arise shows emotional maturity.\nWhat about diligence? Is a diligent student a responsible one? Most certainly! Completing assignments on time, and asking questions when you are in __(57)__ about a difficult concept taught in class reflect a commitment to learning.\nFinally, responsible students promote a positive school culture by participating in community activities, __(58)__ kind towards newcomers, and helping others in need. These actions will help make the school environment safe and conducive for all. By demonstrating positive __(59)__ such as graciousness and gratitude each day, students __(60)__ to a better school experience for both their peers and teachers. That way, everyone within the school community can flourish and find joy in shared everyday experiences.\nAdapted from Social Responsibility in School"},"english-2026-prelim-raffles-girls-g6":{"text":"Refer to the passage below when you answer questions 66 to 75.\n\n**1** Brian's eyes snapped open. He was unbelievably, viciously thirsty. His mouth was dry and tasted foul and sticky. His lips were cracked and seemed as if they were bleeding. His skin was all withered up. He knew the thirst and felt the burn on his face. It was mid-afternoon. The sun had come over him and cooked him while he slept. His face felt like it was on fire. **It** was going to blister and peel.\n**2** He stood, using the tree to pull himself up because there was still some pain and much stiffness and looked down towards the lake. It was water but he did not know if he could drink it. He felt the idea of drinking water from a lake was awful, but the lake was blue, looked clean and his mouth and throat raged with thirst. Would there be another water source he could drink from? Moreover, he had swallowed a lot of the water while he was swimming out of the plane and getting to shore.\n**3** Brian felt helpless in a situation consisting of a plane wreck, a badly bruised forehead, an aching body and a thirst that tore at him until he could not think. He took small steps down the bank to the lake. Along the edge, there were thick prickly grasses in the way and the water looked a little murky. He scowled in disgust at the small bugs swimming in the water and was glad to see a log extending about ten metres out into the lake, with sturdy branches sticking up, almost like strong handles.\n**4** He balanced on the log, holding himself up with the limbs, and teetered out past the weeds and muddy water. When he was out where the water was clear and he could see no bugs swimming, he kneeled on the log to drink. He thought he would just try a sip, still pondering over the lake water. However, when he brought a cupped hand to his mouth and felt the cold lake water trickle past his cracked lips and over his tongue, he could not stop.\n**5** He had never, not even on long bicycle trips in the summer, been this thirsty. He stooped and put his mouth to the lake and drank and drank, pulling it deep and swallowing great gulps of it. He drank until his stomach was swollen, until he nearly fell off the log with **it**, then he got up and staggered his way back to the bank where he was immediately sick and threw up most of the water.\n**6** However, his thirst was gone by then and the water seemed to reduce the pain in his head as well, although the sunburn still felt hot on his face. He pulled himself once more up the bank to the tall tree without branches and sat again with his back against **it**. It was hot now. The sun was high and he sat in the shade of the tree in relative comfort, but there were things he knew he had to do.\nAdapted from \"Hatchet\" by Gary Paulsen"},"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g1":{"text":"For each question from 16 to 20, choose the word(s) closest in meaning to the underlined word(s).\n\nAs the final whistle blew, Amir sat alone on the bench. He had missed the winning penalty during the inter-class football match. He felt __(16: a deep sense of embarrassment)__ as he watched his teammates pack up quietly. His best friend, Kai, noticed that he was deep in thought. Not wanting to __(17: catch him off guard)__, Kai walked over quietly with two bottles of water and spoke in a calm tone, wanting to __(18: allay)__ Amir's worries. \"We're not blaming you,\" Kai said. \"Everyone makes mistakes. What matters is that we learn from them.\" Amir was __(19: deeply)__ touched by Kai's empathy and realised that true friends __(20: stand by)__ one another during difficult times. Their friendship stood the test of time and helped restored Amir's confidence."},"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g2":{"text":"Study the texts and then answer questions 21 to 25.\n\n++Text 1++\nThe text below shows a poster for a school bag.\n[[img1]]\n++Text 2++\nThe extract below is taken from an online article.\nSchool bags are designed to be both roomy and trendy, which makes them appealing to students. Lately, more focus has been placed on making school bags look good. For many students, their school bag is also a way to express their personality and personal style. Is that true for you, too?\nWhile it may seem exciting to fit everything into one bag, have you considered the weight? Carrying too many items strains your back. This may cause short-term discomfort and lead to long-term posture problems. To cushion the effect, you should carry a school bag that fits comfortably on your back. Features such as wide straps and padded backs help distribute the weight more evenly. By choosing a school bag wisely, you can reduce the pressure on your back.\nUltimately, health experts recommend packing only the items needed for the day to keep your school bag light. For example, store certain items in your school locker or keep some materials under your desk if your school allows it. By planning what you bring each day and leaving out unnecessary items, you can protect your back and still have everything you need.","images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g2-img1","alt":"Poster. Title: Peaks School Bag. Carry Everything in Style! Say goodbye to boring school bags! Fit your entire school day into one bag, all your books, stationery, lunch boxes and more! Get organised with a specific space for everything! No more mess or things left behind! A picture of a backpack in the middle. On its left: Suitable for students of all ages! On its right: Wide range of colours to choose from! Below: Designed with you in mind! At the bottom, a picture of a smiling boy with the quote: \"I love my Peaks school bag! Everything I need for school fits comfortably into it. I can also find and take out any item I need easily!\" – Ansel, 12-year-old student"}]},"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g3":{"text":"There are 10 blanks, numbered 26 to 35, in the passage below. From the list of words given, choose the most suitable word for each blank. Write its letter (A to Q) in the blank. The letters (I) and (O) have been omitted to avoid confusion during marking.\n**EACH WORD CAN BE USED ONLY ONCE.**\n[[table1]]\n\nBook-sharing corners are becoming a common sight in neighbourhoods across Singapore. More neighbourhoods __(26)__ introduced book-sharing corners where residents can exchange books easily.\n__(27)__ traditional libraries, these corners are less structured and have fewer rules. They do not need membership cards and borrowing records, __(28)__ have fixed return dates for borrowed books. In fact, __(29)__ corner has its own selection of books, depending on what residents donate. Readers can take a book home or leave __(30)__ behind for another reader. They are also able to read the books __(31)__ their own pace and not rush to make a return. They may also donate books they no longer need, keeping the collection varied __(32)__ time.\nThe project is more __(33)__ just a way to reduce waste. It is a simple idea __(34)__ brings neighbours together through reading. __(35)__ talking about the stories they have enjoyed, they get to know one another a little better. In this way, the book-sharing corner helps to build a warmer and more connected neighbourhood.\nAdapted from The Straits Times","tables":[{"id":"table1","rows":[["(A) all","(D) each","(G) on","(K) over","(N) then"],["(B) at","(E) had","(H) one","(L) than","(P) through"],["(C) both","(F) have","(J) or","(M) that","(Q) unlike"]]}]},"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g4":{"text":"Each of the underlined words contains either a spelling or grammatical error. Write the correct word in each of the box.\n\nLast month, Hibiscus Primary School organised a Screen-Free Week to help students spend less time on their devices and interact more with others.\nTeachers planned several __(36: steemulating)__ activities that not only encouraged students to interact with classmates __(37: and)__ also helped them discover new hobbies. Moreover, setting __(38: away)__ time during recess to play or talk with their peers had forged stronger friendships. By the end of the week, many had become __(39: fermilier)__ with spending their free time away from their devices and had become less __(40: relyant)__ on them.\nThe principal noted that many students had gained confidence __(41: by)__ the activities and discussions held during the event, __(42: learnt)__ to identify new ways to spend their free time and developing greater __(43: curious)__ about trying new pastimes.\nMany teachers regarded the event as a significant __(44: akomplishment)__ because it helped pupils develop greater __(45: ristraynt)__ when managing their screen time. The event encouraged students to make wise choices about their use of devices and to spend their time more meaningfully.\nAdapted from The Little Red Dot"},"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g5":{"text":"Fill in each blank with a suitable word.\n\nThe OneMillionTrees movement was launched in Singapore in 2020. With the __(46)__ of planting one million more trees across the island by 2030, this movement reinforces Singapore's long-term __(47)__ to becoming a City in Nature. Parks, nature reserves and park connectors are common __(48)__ where trees are planted to increase greenery across the island. By planting __(49)__ trees, Singapore hopes to create a greener and more sustainable environment for __(50)__ generations.\nTrees play many important roles in the environment. They absorb carbon dioxide which helps to regulate temperatures and improve air __(51)__. Their branches and leaves also __(52)__ food and shelter for wildlife. In addition, trees allow animals to __(53)__ freely between parks and nature reserves. As a result, both wildlife and __(54)__ benefit from a healthier environment.\nThe success of the movement depends greatly on community participation. Members of the __(55)__ can contribute by taking part in educational programmes that teach proper tree care. Some schools even set up small nurseries where young __(56)__ are carefully grown before being planted in gardens. Through these hands-on activities, students gain practical __(57)__ in caring for plants and protecting the environment. As they watch the trees __(58)__ and attract different kinds of wildlife, they develop a __(59)__ appreciation of nature and understand why conserving green spaces is __(60)__.\nBy working together, Singaporeans can help create a greener and more liveable city for everyone.\nAdapted from OneMillionTrees Movement, National Parks Board (NParks)"},"english-2026-prelim-red-swastika-g6":{"text":"Refer to the passage below when you answer questions 66 to 75 in Booklet B.\n\n**1** The kindergarten classroom hummed with excitement as children streamed towards the various play corners. With lightning speed, Farid and Lina hurried to their favourite play corner. They took out plastic plates, toy fried eggs and miniature pancakes, and scattered them across a low table.\n**2** As usual, Lina handed Farid a tiny apron. With a grin, he took a bow and put on the apron. Then, he pretended to be the chef by raising his hands as if he were putting on a tall chef's hat. Lina appreciated Farid's kindness. Not many children wanted to play with someone who had hearing difficulties. Soon, the pair were immersed in their make-believe café. Farid arranged the eggs neatly onto the pancakes while Lina closed her eyes and pretended to sniff the aroma of the food.\n**3** Caught up **in the moment**, Lina reached blindly for a pancake. Her elbow clipped a tray, sending the toy food tumbling onto the floor behind her with a loud clatter. Unaware, she reached again but found only an empty space. Farid let out a short laugh before realising that Lina was tearing up and her shoulders were stiffening.\n**4** Leaning closer, Farid gently tapped her shoulder and motioned for her to turn around. As she gasped softly, he made a deliberate scooping motion with his hands. Lina's gaze followed his **movement** and her expression softened. Her fingers brushed against the scattered toy food on the floor. Nodding quickly, she crouched down to retrieve the toy food while Farid helped gather the remaining pieces. Just then, the teacher called for Farid and he left Lina to play on her own.\n**5** Moments later, a new wave of activity swept across the classroom. The teacher announced, \"Clean up! Time for snack break!\"\n**6** Chairs scraped against the floor as children hurried to return toys to their shelves. Yet Lina remained seated, oblivious to **the sudden disruption** behind her. She was enjoying her time pretending to feed a cartoon character on the wall poster in front of her.\n**7** A child rushing past accidentally knocked against the table, causing several plates to wobble dangerously. Lina glanced about uneasily, her brows furrowing as she looked across the room. Why was everyone moving so quickly?\n**8** Sensing that something was amiss, Farid's eyes swept across the room before settling on Lina. Understanding dawned on him as he realised what she had missed. He moved beside her and lightly tapped her shoulder again. As Lina looked up, he glanced at the children tidying the classroom. Then, slowly and carefully, he swept his hands together and pointed towards the toy basket.\n**9** Lina studied his face and hands. **Realisation flickered across her features**, and her tense shoulders relaxed. Farid waved to a few classmates, who came over to help. A grateful smile spread across Lina's face. As the children gathered the toy plates and placed them into the basket, Lina followed suit. Together, they finished tidying the play corner.\n**10** When they joined the line with their classmates, Lina caught Farid's eye and pressed her hand gently against her chest before extending it towards him. Farid nodded and returned her gesture. Neither of them spoke. Yet in that quiet moment, **Lina's grateful smile and Farid's quiet nod said more than words could.**\nAdapted from \"This kindergarten put children with and without hearing loss together\", CNA Insider, 14 January 2024."},"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g1":{"text":"For each question from 16 to 20, choose the word(s) closest in meaning to the underlined word(s).\n\nAziz, Bala and Cooper were bubbling with excitement. They __(16: scurried)__ about the kitchen preparing for their picnic. Sunlight streamed through the windows as they packed sandwiches, __(17: juicy)__ slices of watermelon and a flask of chilled lemonade into a __(18: sturdy)__ woven basket.\nAziz made sure to put the first aid kit that they had prepared earlier into the basket as well. \"__(19: It is good to be prepared)__!\" he said with a grin.\nBala and Cooper replied in unison, \"You're right!\" He then checked the contents __(20: thoroughly)__ before shutting it. With unwavering enthusiasm, they ensured that everything was perfect. Finally, they were ready for the picnic."},"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g2":{"text":"Study the information for an event (Text 1) and the extract from a school newsletter (Text 2) and answer questions 21 to 25.\n\n++Text 1++\nThe text below provides information for an event.\n[[img1]]\n++Text 2++\nThe extract below, from a school newsletter, is a student's journal entry about the upcoming school park run.\nThe school park run is not just about running. Whether you are a talented runner or just simply a beginner, it is about coming together as a school community to stay active and have fun. When we all join in, regardless of your running speed, we learn to support one another. Even when it is tough, we keep going because we know our friends are there to cheer us on. It is not about winning. It is about motivating oneself to complete the entire course from start to end and having a sense of achievement as a result.\nRunning with our classmates makes us feel like we are a part of one big family. We show determination, encourage one another and feel a sense of pride as a member of the school. Moreover, it is a great way to keep fit. With each step, we are building our endurance and growing stronger, both physically and mentally!\nSo, let's hit the trail and embrace the adventure of the school park run! Together, we will run, laugh and make memories that will last a lifetime!","images":[{"id":"img1","key":"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g2-img1","alt":"Event information sheet. Heading: Sunshine Primary School 5th Annual Park Run. Ready to run, bond and build lasting memories? Date: 3 September 2026. Reporting time: 8.15 a.m. In a starburst: Primary 3 to 6 students. Bullet points: To promote physical well-being. To foster school community spirit. Assemble at the hall for attendance-taking. Primary 1 and 2 will remain in school to participate in the Sports Carnival. Below is a map headed PARK RUN ROUTE AT CREST PARK. It shows one looping track. START LINE (Primary 4-6) is at the bottom of the loop. START LINE (Primary 3) is further along the track, on the right side. The track then passes Checkpoint 1 (top right), Checkpoint 2 (top middle) and Checkpoint 3 (top left) and ends at one FINISHING LINE at the bottom left. Distances: Primary 3: 1 km. Primary 4-6: 1.6 km. Note: 2 teachers will be stationed at each checkpoint with a first aid kit. A water point is available at Checkpoint 2."}]},"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g3":{"text":"There are 10 blanks, numbered 26 to 35, in the passage below. From the list of words given, choose the most suitable word for each blank. Write its letter (A to Q) in the blank. The letters (I) and (O) have been omitted to avoid confusion during marking.\n**EACH WORD CAN BE USED ONLY ONCE.**\n[[table1]]\n\nHave you heard of the Purple Parade? Every year, this event brings people together to celebrate the inclusivity __(26)__ individuals with special needs. By wearing purple to the event, it is hoped that national awareness of the needs of these people __(27)__ be raised.\nPurple is a great choice of colour – it has __(28)__ been considered the colour of royalty. In fact, not __(29)__ could wear purple! It was reserved for the upper class in the past. Some Roman emperors even forbade __(30)__ people from wearing it. Purple dye in __(31)__ days came from tiny sea snails and was extremely rare. __(32)__ was therefore extremely expensive. Purple was worn not only to show wealth __(33)__ also to signal authority, prestige and power. The Persian king, Cyrus, was known for wearing __(34)__ purple robe for royal ceremonies.\nPurple, a blend of rich red and blue colours, __(35)__ everywhere in royal life back then. Rulers wore purple robes and signed letters with purple ink. Royal children were described as being \"born in the purple\".\nDon't you think that the colour purple has such a rich history?","tables":[{"id":"table1","rows":[["(A) a","(D) any","(G) it","(K) their","(N) was"],["(B) all","(E) but","(H) its","(L) these","(P) were"],["(C) always","(F) from","(J) of","(M) those","(Q) will"]]}]},"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g4":{"text":"Each of the underlined words contains either a spelling or grammatical error. Write the correct word in each of the boxes.\n\nLast month, my family and I went camping at Corine Beach. Initially, I was not excited about the trip as I had __(36: prefer)__ staying indoors reading to spending time outdoors. Camping sounded __(37: unkumftible)__, especially since we had to sleep in a tent without any air conditioning.\nHowever, my perspective changed __(38: drastiquely)__ on the first evening. As the sun began to set, the sky was painted in shades of orange and pink. Always __(39: imerssed)__ in my books, I hardly paid attention to sunsets until that day. The __(40: glory)__ masterpiece was a sight to behold. That night, the __(41: soothe)__ sound of the waves gently crashing against the shore lulled me __(42: in)__ sleep.\nThe next morning, I __(43: awake)__, feeling refreshed. Exploring the area, we were greeted by a __(44: plithora)__ of colourful flowers, birds, and insects. My mother and I spent hours on the beach, collecting interesting shells and watching crabs scuttle across the sand. My father introduced to me the different plants and I learnt that each one plays a __(45: krushial)__ role in the ecosystem. That camping trip was truly amazing."},"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g5":{"text":"Fill in each blank with a suitable word.\n\nWhat do you usually eat during recess? School meals around the world __(46)__ greatly. However, they all share a common goal – to nourish young minds and bodies. In France, school lunches are served in several parts. The meal __(47)__ with an appetiser, followed by a main dish __(48)__ of protein like salmon or lamb. Next, students enjoy their cheese and bread. Finally, they wrap up the meal with dessert. These provide students with an __(49)__ of vitamins and minerals.\nJapan takes a unique approach to school dining. Nearly all primary school lunches use only locally grown, fresh __(50)__ – vegetables, fruits and mushrooms – obtained from nearby farms. School lunch menus are usually __(51)__ up by a nutritionist.\nSingapore's school canteens offer the most diverse dining __(52)__, reflecting the nation's multicultural heritage. Students can choose __(53)__ various stalls selling Chinese, Indian, Malay, and Western food. The Healthy Meals in Schools Programme has been implemented to __(54)__ students on making healthier food and beverage choices. As __(55)__ of this initiative, canteen vendors are required to serve wholegrains, fruits, and vegetables daily. Therefore, they __(56)__ mandatory training by the Health Promotion Board. Routine checks by schools are conducted to ensure that they __(57)__ with nutrition guidelines.\nThis programme ensures that students obtain appropriate nutrients for their growing needs. Snacks sold have to come __(58)__ the Healthier Choice symbol. Plain water is the ideal beverage choice while drinks artificially __(59)__ by sugar are strongly discouraged. Likewise, processed foods are also not recommended. They are often high in salt, which __(60)__ the risk of heart disease. Through these efforts, Singapore's schools are helping students develop lifelong healthy eating habits that will benefit them well into adulthood.\nAdapted from https://worldpopulationreview.com/country-rankings/school-lunches-around-the-world"},"english-2026-prelim-tao-nan-g6":{"text":"Read the passage and answer questions 66 to 75.\n\n**1** Colin's school holidays in the city had always been busy and exciting. If he was not out with his friends, the twelve-year-old would be playing computer games in the comfort of his air-conditioned bedroom. Thus, when his parents sent him to stay with his grandmother on a countryside farm, Colin expected **a month of boredom**.\n**2** The moment Colin stepped onto the dirt path, he wrinkled his nose at the earthy smell clinging to his shoes. The sky seemed wider, the air calmer, and even the sun rose slowly, spilling gold rays across the fields. He spotted a figure bent over, her hands moving busily, plucking out invasive stems that competed with the crops for nutrients and space.\n**3** Upon hearing him approach, she straightened up and dusted her hands on her jeans. Then, she greeted him warmly, \"Welcome, Colin. It's been a long time since you were here. Today, I'll show you something different. You don't see **this** happening in the city.\"\n**4** **Colin eyed Grandma warily**. What was she going to show him? How special could something on the farm be? Colin followed his grandmother to the chicken coop, dragging his feet as he walked. There, hens clucked loudly, jostling for the grains scattered across the ground. Suddenly, one large hen shoved the others aside, pecking greedily while the smaller birds waited in quiet submission. Colin grimaced. Why did they let it push them around?\n**5** Grandma patted Colin on the shoulder gently. \"What's with the frown?\" she asked, pointing at the chickens. \"See what they are doing? They know how to get food despite being smaller!\" Colin watched more closely and noticed the smaller hens pecking at the grains when the larger one turned away. He started to smile.\n**6** By midday, dark clouds gathered. Wind whipped across the farm, rattling fences and tugging at the coop door. With a loud creak, the door swung open. Rain poured down, and the hens burst out in a flurry of feathers and panicked clucks.\n**7** \"Grandma! The chickens!\" Colin shouted, his heart racing. The birds scattered in all directions and showed no sign of turning back – some charged towards the open field while others darted under the bushes. Colin's first instinct was to sprint after them, but they were swifter than he was.\n**8** \"Wait! They're not as bold as the large one!\" Grandma called over the rain. Colin froze. What if they never came back? His chest tightened as he breathed hard and fast. Then he remembered the smaller hens at feeding time – how they had waited, watched, and chosen **the right moment**. Being impulsive would not help.\n**9** Taking a deep breath, Colin changed his strategy. He slowed his steps and softened his movements. Instead of chasing, he began guiding. He circled behind two hens, gently steering them towards the coop. He clapped lightly, not to scare them, but to direct them. One by one, a few hens began to return. He inhaled deeply, suppressing a smile. The large one, however, remained in the field, tense and alert. Each time Colin moved too quickly, it darted farther away. Colin muttered in frustration, \"Why can't you just come back?\" He took another deep breath and stood still for a moment, thinking.\n**10** Colin waited with bated breath. For a long moment, **nothing** happened. Then, slowly, the hen tilted its head, noticing the grains that led towards the coop. Step by step, it followed the bait. Colin resisted the urge to rush. At last, the hen crossed into the coop. Colin quickly shut the door. He was drenched and muddy, yet quietly triumphant. Grandma nodded and said, \"Getting drenched is totally worth it – nothing teaches you how to wait like the chickens!\""}}};
